1795320365753200	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1hp	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`investigation` (spec shorthand `inv`) is an offline, agent-native Rust CLI and library that records evidence, competing explanations or options, and the reasoning linking them, so a fresh agent or human can resume a case without the original chat. Nothing exists yet except `notes/plan.md` (revision 4) and three reviews. The plan is explicit about where to start: \"Do not begin with a huge ontology, plugin, task runner or dynamic-method language. The first implementation should produce one complete case that a second agent can resume, with correct source lineage and no silent dropped writes. Immediately repeat the same code path for an option evaluation, because that is where falsely generic designs often break.\"\n\nMilestone A is that slice: work packages W00–W04 plus the *minimum* of W05 (CLI) and W06 (local ingest). In this tracker the minimum halves are the goals W05a and W06a; the remainders (W05b, W06b) live under Milestone B.\n\n# Goals\n- Two cases work end to end on the same store and CLI: Scenario A (intermittent request timeout, explanatory) and Scenario C (queue architecture choice, evaluative).\n- A reviewer can see exact support and assumptions, and read at title / summary / full / evidence depth.\n- A second agent can resume from committed state alone.\n- Material cases close with a minimal truthful Retrospective; others may waive it with a reason.\n- Schemas are `v0-alpha` here. They freeze as `inv.command.v1` only after the A-gate pilot (goal AG).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe product hypothesis is unproven: no published controlled study shows structured evidence tracking helps agents more than a notes file. Building the smallest honest slice first lets the A-gate test that claim before the wider catalog, recorder, vault and MCP surfaces are built. Doing explanatory and evaluative cases together prevents a design that only fits hypotheses-and-experiments.\n\n# How this tracker is organized\n- Milestones are top-level goals. Work packages (W00…W16, as in plan §19.2) are child goals. Tasks are children of work packages. Every title starts with a code (`W02.07`); descriptions cross-reference by code.\n- `bn` propagates blocking down the hierarchy: a blocker on a goal gates every bone beneath it. Goal-level blockers are therefore used only for true gates (W00→W01→the rest of A; AG→Milestones B and C; C→D). Everything else is wired task to task so the parallel lanes of §19.4 can run.\n- Labels: `milestone-*`, `w??` per work package, `lane-*` per §19.4 lane, plus `adr`, `test`, `fixture`, `docs`, `security`.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] W00, W01, W02, W03, W04, W05a and W06a are complete.\n- [ ] MA.01 (the runnable proof) passes: both scenarios run through the shipped binary with real returned IDs, and a fresh agent resumes each from `brief` alone.\n- [ ] No invariant in \"Build rules\" was weakened for the demo: evidence is not a verdict; an invalid experiment is not a negative result; a late revision invalidates stale reviews; repeated formatting of one source is not independent confirmation; a lost response does not duplicate a committed update; missing bytes remain missing.\n- [ ] The milestone ends in a runnable proof, not a skeleton of directories.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the smallest thing that can answer the plan's sixth question — \"Can a new agent resume from the case, without the original chat?\" — and it is the object the A-gate measures against plain notes.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md Read this first, §19.1, §19.2, §19.3 (Milestone A), §19.4, Final handoff instruction (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["milestone","milestone-a"],"size":"xl","title":"Milestone A: undeniable vertical slice — a debugging case and an architecture-choice case on one core"}	blake3:yQConHH98v3sdmDHkTC1HPZ9jq2fevGHyrbZnLIZ4EY
1795320365845002	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2s2	{"description":"# Context & Background\nAll three reviews agreed on one thing above all: no published controlled study shows that structured evidence tracking helps agents more than free-form notes, and plain scratchpads are a strong baseline. Revision 4 therefore moved a controlled comparison to the first milestone. The README says it plainly: \"The rest of the build depends on that result.\"\n\nThe gate is a two-domain paired pilot (debugging and architecture choice, pilot-split cases) run before W07. One arm uses plain Markdown notes with normal file/search tools; the other has the same tools plus `inv`. The primary outcome is what a fresh *successor* concludes after the first session is interrupted.\n\n# Goals\n- Run the bring-your-own-source exercise: an unfamiliar investigator starts from one local source and hands off to a successor; a new hard criterion is inserted between review and acceptance; the successor says what changed and whether another measurement would affect the choice.\n- Apply a decision rule recorded as an ADR *before* the runs (W00.06): if `inv` does not beat the notes arm on false promotions or successor accuracy, cut or merge the record types agents ignored or misused before continuing.\n- Estimate between-case variability to size the release trial (W14), preregister that trial, then freeze `inv.command.v1`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nFreezing a schema is the expensive, irreversible step, so evidence comes first. A pilot cannot prove the product claim (20 runs cannot separate 90% from 80%), but it can reveal which record types agents ignore or misuse while change is still cheap, and it yields the variability estimate that a fixed \"N runs per scenario\" could not.\n\n# Gating semantics in this tracker\nPreparation tasks (harness, rubric, case families) carry no blocker on Milestone A and can proceed in the evaluation lane while A is built. Only AG.07 (run the pilot) waits for MA.01. This goal blocks Milestone B and Milestone C: nothing after the slice starts until the gate is resolved and the catalog is frozen.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Decision-rule ADR predates the first pilot run (journal/commit order shows it).\n- [ ] Pilot ran with at least two agent families or hosts, matched budgets, counterbalanced order, pinned versions and no session carryover.\n- [ ] Paired results, cost per arm and a between-case variability estimate are written up with their limitations.\n- [ ] The decision rule was applied as written; any simplification was made and recorded as an ADR.\n- [ ] `inv.command.v1` and the wider catalog are frozen and published under `schemas/`.\n- [ ] The release trial is preregistered (primary outcome, minimum useful difference, burden limit, sample size).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 12 is \"Cold-start excellence: agent UX must be measured using unfamiliar agents, not polished documentation judged by the author.\" This gate is where the project finds out, cheaply, whether it should exist in its planned shape.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md Binding decisions (Product hypothesis), §19.3 (A-gate), §19.5, §20.5, Disposition: Evaluation and sequencing (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["milestone","a-gate","lane-evaluation"],"size":"l","title":"A-gate: does it help? Paired pilot against plain Markdown notes, then freeze inv.command.v1"}	blake3:OD8WvAmX6QbRj6ITat6BkgbYnJ_hVmopRrvtYfrqrE8
1795320365920302	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1fa	{"description":"# Context & Background\nAfter the A-gate confirms (or reshapes) the core, the product has to become generic and epistemically honest. \"Generic\" means external producers in any language can record sessions, runs and artifacts through one neutral contract without `inv` knowing their names. \"Honest\" means provenance, measurement validity, source independence, criterion changes, synopsis freshness and structural blindspot review all behave correctly, including under adversarial fixtures.\n\nThe plan lists Milestone B as W06–W10. In this tracker it holds W05b (the rest of the agent CLI: `frontier`, full `next`, `search`, `explain`, `doctor`, docs), W06b (the rest of ingest: lineage, independence, remaining selectors, classification), W07 (neutral recorder), W08 (vault renderer; the plan also lists W08 under C), W09 (method packs) and W10 (validity, freshness, objections, blindspots, challenge reviews, retrospective digest).\n\n# Goals\n- External producer fixtures (never a named application) record runs via `capture begin/append/attach/finalize/recover` with crash/retry qualification.\n- Provenance, validity, independence, criteria changes, synopsis freshness and structural blindspot review are correct.\n- No security- or performance-specific default leaks into the core.\n- Scenario B (p99 regression) and Scenario D (contradictory sources) pass with their adversarial variants.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nDependency inversion is a binding decision: \"External tools may import/use Investigation; Investigation never imports or invokes their APIs.\" The recorder and method packs are where that is most tempting to violate, so they are built against conformance fixtures rather than a real consumer. W10 is where the \"never weaken these invariants\" list becomes machine-checked.\n\n# Gating semantics in this tracker\nBlocked by AG as a whole (plan: \"Run the A-gate before W07\"). Inside the milestone, work packages are wired task to task; there is no blanket ordering between W07, W08, W09 and W10 because the plan's lanes run in parallel.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] W05b, W06b, W07, W08, W09 and W10 are complete.\n- [ ] MB.01 (runnable proof) passes: a Rust and a Python fixture producer record with identical semantics; the four scenario families and the §17.7 drills pass deterministically.\n- [ ] §20.1 cross-domain, explanatory, measurement, evaluative, source-research and layered-retrieval checklists pass.\n- [ ] §20.3 neutral producer and method-pack checklist passes.\n- [ ] Core builds, tests, runs and exports with zero producers, executors, task trackers or credentials installed.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis milestone makes true the two closing sentences of the plan: the result \"should feel simple\", and \"Investigation never needs to know what produced the observations, or what external software will act on the decision.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): AG.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.2, §19.3 (Milestone B), §3, §11, §20.1, §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["milestone","milestone-b"],"size":"xl","title":"Milestone B: generic recording and honest knowledge (rest of W05/W06, W07–W10)"}	blake3:0ZVCaOGWEtRjV_pc6mx5EEMJE6OM0WR1YqzNym5EeZw
1795320365986457	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3q2	{"description":"# Context & Background\nMilestone C is \"the first release approved for consequential investigations\". The plan lists W08/W11/W12/W13/W14: the Obsidian casebook, retrospective reports, exports, restore, cold-agent tests across all four domains, and reviewed repair UX. In this tracker W08 sits under Milestone B (it is in the plan's B list too); C holds W11 (reports, export, archive/restore, retention, audience policy), W12 (native Bases/Canvas, reconcile, Obsidian release checks), W13 (MCP frontend), W14 (cold-agent evaluation, drills, dogfooding, tutorials) and REL (definition of done and release).\n\n# Goals\n- A human can browse a generated casebook with community plugins disabled, and nothing human-owned is ever overwritten.\n- Deterministic reports and exports render from exact committed snapshots, and audience filtering never strengthens a recommendation.\n- A workspace archive restores to an identical state on a clean machine.\n- CLI and MCP reach the same accepted domain state.\n- The preregistered sealed-set comparison against the notes arm is run and reported honestly, whatever it shows.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The fresh-agent tests in §20 are as important as durability tests: one measures whether unfamiliar agents actually know when/how to use the tool; the other whether recorded knowledge survives real errors.\" A release claim rests on sealed cases, paired differences and case-clustered intervals, not on an uncontrolled pass rate.\n\n# Gating semantics in this tracker\nBlocked by AG as a whole. There is deliberately no blanket B→C block: W11–W13 tasks are wired to the specific W08/W10 tasks they need, so the projection and evaluation lanes are not idled. REL is blocked by W11–W14 and by MB.01.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] W11, W12, W13, W14 and REL are complete.\n- [ ] §20.4 Obsidian and export fixtures pass on a recorded supported Obsidian release; headless Markdown is still valid without Obsidian.\n- [ ] §20.5 release criterion is evaluated on sealed cases per the preregistration; if inconclusive, the improvement claim is withheld and the uncertainty reported.\n- [ ] §20.6 human experience gate passes without a narrated tour.\n- [ ] §20.7 definition of done is audited item by item.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the design bar made testable: \"a fresh agent can use the tool with no bespoke instruction; its output is epistemically honest; a human can browse it with pleasure; and a second agent can trust that nothing important silently vanished.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): AG.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.2, §19.3 (Milestone C), §20.4–§20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["milestone","milestone-c"],"size":"xl","title":"Milestone C: great product — casebook, reports, restore, MCP, cold-agent trial; first release"}	blake3:I2U1OWBVCXqKIS53TrPSSngUdMJpd0PaaZTiymRwE3I
1795320366050750	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3cm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nMilestone D is explicitly optional: W15 (an Obsidian companion plugin for comparison and review interaction) and W16 (local action claims, extension SDK conveniences, pack-authoring docs, packaging and portability). The plan is blunt about the bar: \"Companion earns itself through human testing, not because plugins are fashionable\", and \"Only build after CLI and vault pass their offline gates.\"\n\n# Goals\n- If justified by the §20.6 human gate, a small TypeScript companion that reads and writes only through the typed core API, never a second planner, recorder or database.\n- If a need is demonstrated, explicit local action claims that never promise distributed exactly-once execution.\n- Platform qualification beyond Linux, with durability claims reported truthfully per platform.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe first useful version must work with no plugin, on native Obsidian features. Community plugins inherit Obsidian's full access rather than a permission sandbox, which is a concrete reason to keep the companion small and installation explicit. No reviewer examined §13 in depth, so the plan requires re-reviewing it before W15 begins (W15.01).\n\n# Gating semantics in this tracker\nBlocked by Milestone C as a whole. W16 additionally requires \"demonstrated need\" — record that evidence on the bone before starting it. External consumers integrating against the public recording API are not part of this milestone and may proceed any time after conformance fixtures exist.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] W15 and W16 are each either complete or closed with a recorded reason that the need was not demonstrated.\n- [ ] Companion gates pass: controlled mutations, cancel, revision conflict and stale-editor cases (§13.4).\n- [ ] No duplicate external effects are claimed; platform gates are correctly reported by `doctor`.\n- [ ] The core still passes every Milestone C gate with the companion absent.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps optional work honestly optional, so the core stays offline, plugin-free and domain-neutral while leaving a deliberate path for richer human review.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): MC.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §13, §14.1, §19.2 (W15, W16), §19.3 (Milestone D), §22, Disposition: Not changed (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["milestone","milestone-d"],"size":"l","title":"Milestone D: optional polish and integrations (W15 companion plugin, W16 claims/SDK/portability)"}	blake3:2TAlRZxbSmpOsb3Wz9S9fjQZP2nIyqpqSweqKN0A8yM
1795320522449655	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1a5	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe repository holds only the plan, three reviews, a README and artwork. W00 turns it into a place where the plan can be built and checked: a Rust workspace whose crate boundaries express the non-negotiable dependency direction (§3.1, §18.1), a pinned toolchain, CI, the decision records the plan demands, and conventions for deterministic fixtures.\n\n# Goals\n- Compiling workspace skeleton with `investigation-core`, `-store`, `-recording`, `-cli` (MCP crate reserved) and the `methods/ obsidian/ plugin/ schemas/ fixtures/ examples/ training/ scripts/ docs/` directories.\n- CI that runs format, lint, tests, dependency/licence audit and the neutrality guards.\n- The three ADRs that are due before W02 because they cannot change afterward without a migration, plus the wider build-policy ADR set.\n- A written support matrix: Linux on a local filesystem first; nothing claimed for macOS/Windows or network filesystems.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Parallel agents must agree on exact object/operation versions centrally; do not let each invent a different response envelope.\" ADRs and CI guards are how that agreement is kept. The crate names \"express dependency boundaries, not permission to proliferate crates\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W00: \"Domain boundaries/protocol drafts committed and tests runnable.\"\n- [ ] `cargo test` runs (even if nearly empty) locally and in CI.\n- [ ] W00.04–W00.06 are merged before any W02 code.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEverything later depends on boundaries that are enforced by the build rather than by good intentions.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: MA. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §18.1, §18.2, §19.2 (W00), §19.5 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w00","milestone-a"],"parent":"bn-1hp","size":"l","title":"W00 Foundations: repo scaffold, toolchain, CI, ADRs, support matrix, fixture conventions"}	blake3:wrDMZELRi_EseXkc03j4cMG7jR0AIcuPTj1WCYn0Zc0
1795320522610033	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-u88	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW01 fixes the things every lane must share before it can work in parallel: typed identity, shared record fields, the mutation envelope, receipts, the error envelope with exit codes, and the single operation catalog that generates help, JSON Schema, examples, MCP tool schemas and validation errors. It also drafts, as `v0-alpha`, every domain type the first two scenarios exercise: Case, Question, Source, Artifact, Observation, Hypothesis, Assumption, Option, Criterion, Comparison, Assessment, Conclusion, Decision, Review, Objection, Annotation, Retrospective, Inquiry and Run.\n\n# Goals\n- An independently compiling `investigation-core` with schema-valid examples and a minimal handoff fixture.\n- Envelope, identity and receipt semantics fixed for the first slice; names reserved for later operations.\n- CoveragePlan, authored synopses and pack extensions deliberately wait.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 1 proposed shrinking W01 to about ten types and deferring Retrospective. Revision 4 modified that: the slice exercises the closure gate, so Retrospective, Assumption, Objection and Annotation are drafted now (review 2 #3), all marked `v0-alpha`. The wider catalog freezes as `v1` only after the A-gate (AG.10), so churn here is expected and cheap. Hypothesis and Option, Assessment and Comparison, Conclusion and Decision stay distinct types: \"They should not be unified into an amorphous `node` type.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W01: \"Independently compiling core, schema-valid examples and a minimal handoff fixture.\"\n- [ ] One catalog is the only source for schema, help, examples and field-level errors; generated examples validate against their own schemas in tests.\n- [ ] No operation accepts arbitrary JSON Patch or an unrestricted \"set status\".\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Agent writes\": transactional, schema-validated JSON with explicit receipts, durable idempotency and optimistic revision checks; no parsing assistant prose to infer mutations.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.\nParent goal: MA. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5, §6.2, §6.3, §9.2–§9.6, §18.2, §18.3, §19.2 (W01), §19.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-1hp","size":"xl","title":"W01 Common envelope, identity, provenance and receipts; v0-alpha domain types and operation catalog"}	blake3:eEcrSmUKcCHEesKlE2aDCaD5yAWGk6l6fqSNJRDpHY4
1795320522707267	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-eks	{"description":"# Context & Background\nCanonical state is an append-only, hash-chained journal of immutable transaction files plus content-addressed blobs. A rebuildable SQLite index serves queries. W02 builds that store and the commit protocol, and proves it against injected crashes. Brenner Bot's pilot retrospective (plan §2.2, [B7]) is the cautionary tale: updates were silently dropped. Here a lost response must never duplicate or lose a committed update.\n\n# Goals\n- File-per-event journal (`events/<seq>.json`, sequence-only names) with SHA-256 over the exact stored bytes; no canonical-JSON step in the integrity path.\n- Blob store with exclusive-create publication; blobs durable before the event that references them.\n- The ten-step commit algorithm under a `flock`-style advisory lock, with fsync errors treated as fatal.\n- Durable idempotency keyed by `(workspace_id, actor, command_id)` and reconstructible from canonical events.\n- Deterministic replay, integrity diagnosis, and a fault-injection suite covering every row of the crash matrix.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 1 proposed a segmented append-only log; revision 4 rejected it because appended logs bring torn-tail and \"append leaves garbage\" failures and worse fsync-failure behaviour, while the performance concern was unmeasured for an agent-paced tool. Hand-rolling a journal is justified only by inspectability (grep, rsync, `sha256sum`) and it obliges serious crash testing; ADR W00.04 records that trade and W00.05 settles per-workspace versus per-case chains.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W02: \"Deterministic replay + fault-injection suite passes.\"\n- [ ] Deleting the SQLite index and rebuilding yields identical accepted state and receipts.\n- [ ] Every §20.2 persistence item that does not need later packages passes.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"A second agent can trust that nothing important silently vanished\" is a storage property before it is anything else.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.\nParent goal: MA. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2, §10.1–§10.6, §18.3, §19.2 (W02), §20.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1hp","size":"xl","title":"W02 Durable event journal, blob store, CAS, idempotent receipts and crash recovery"}	blake3:cd_UzdfzPeQwHsjTo2ok6vc0hmAXEQ2KFzpWJIMz1K4
1795320522850185	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-azj	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe explanatory loop answers \"why or how did this happen?\": collect observations, keep rival hypotheses, plan a discriminating inquiry, record external runs, assess evidence, reach a bounded conclusion, have it reviewed, and close with a retrospective. W03 implements the operation handlers, lifecycles and hard gates for that loop, and proves them on Scenario A (an intermittent request timeout).\n\n# Goals\n- Handlers for case, question, hypothesis, assumption, observation, inquiry, run, assessment, conclusion, review, objection, annotation and retrospective operations (§9.4).\n- Hard gates block *promotion* only, never preservation of evidence or a candid admission of uncertainty (§6.4).\n- Independent outcome dimensions stay independent: execution, evidence completeness, measurement validity, assessment relation (§6.3).\n- The core computes the required read-set closure for a promotion; the caller cannot omit an inconvenient dependency.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nInvestigation never executes anything, so the loop is about truthful recording: a failed run is not negative evidence, a frozen prediction cannot be silently edited, and a causal conclusion with only observational support stays `candidate`. Review 2 #4 put the per-rival expectation on the Assessment, because most evidence is not a planned test.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W03: \"Debug scenario works without special consumer and retains original evidence.\"\n- [ ] The Scenario A adversarial variant (a refuting test run on a different build) cannot produce an unqualified validation.\n- [ ] §20.1 explanatory/debugging items reachable in the slice pass.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCovers plan questions 2–5 for factual questions: what we observed, what could explain it, what would discriminate, and what we may conclude.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.\nParent goal: MA. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §5.3, §5.7, §6, §10.3, §17.1, §19.2 (W03), §20.1 (Explanatory/debugging) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-1hp","size":"xl","title":"W03 Minimal explanatory loop: observations, hypotheses, inquiries, runs, assessments, conclusions, review"}	blake3:wvJY3LrqiYOyHrlVQl8DTZboE7e8cNiVP11p_7lOyTE
1795320522944783	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-mw0	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"A consequential *choice* is not a claim about reality.\" The evaluative loop supports a versioned decision frame: options, hard and soft criteria, evidence-backed comparisons per option-criterion cell, sensitivity to uncertain assumptions, and a decision that may be rational yet conditional. W04 implements it on the same core as W03 and proves it on Scenario C (database queue vs managed queue vs dedicated broker).\n\n# Goals\n- Option, Criterion, Comparison and Decision operations with their lifecycles and gates.\n- `unknown` is a normal cell value; an unmeasured hard requirement is `unknown`, never `fails`, and can never be treated as satisfied.\n- Criterion changes mark dependent Comparisons and Decisions `needs_review`; accepted Decisions are superseded, never rewritten.\n- The review basis includes set membership, so a hard criterion or objection inserted after review is detected (the phantom problem).\n- Weight-free Pareto status with a conservative dominance rule.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe plan repeats the slice immediately for option evaluation \"because that is where falsely generic designs often break\". Review 3 #2 called set membership \"the most consequential correctness gap any review found\", and the A-gate exercise depends on it directly. No automatic weighted winner: design principle 9 forbids epistemic theater.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W04: \"Choice scenario uses same core, no forced hypotheses.\"\n- [ ] An `evaluate` case completes with zero hypotheses, zero tests and zero conclusions.\n- [ ] Changing the hard durability threshold after acceptance turns the Comparison and Decision `needs_review` while source evidence stays intact.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"First-class evaluation\": add Option, Criterion and Comparison for decisions and trade studies; do not pretend options are factual hypotheses.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.\nParent goal: MA. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §5.5, §6.2–§6.4, §10.3, §15.1, §15.3, §17.3, §19.2 (W04), §20.1 (Evaluative/architecture) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-1hp","size":"xl","title":"W04 Minimal evaluative loop: options, criteria, comparisons, decisions, sensitivity and supersession"}	blake3:63UxTOIAaMHBztpPhyz8FtDxkKakRI9QYu_nsf6eArA
1795320523032494	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3nm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nMilestone A needs \"minimum W05\": enough CLI for two cold agents to create, record, read at layered depth and resume both cases, and for the A-gate pilot to be fair. This goal is that minimum. The remainder of W05 (`frontier`, full `next` ranking, `search`, `explain`, `doctor`, integration and embedded docs) is W05b under Milestone B.\n\n# Goals\n- A single binary named `investigation` (`inv` only as a non-clobbering alias; Python Invoke already installs `inv`).\n- Machine contract: `--format json`/`--json` guarantees exactly one JSON object on stdout, including for discovery and argument errors; no ANSI, pager or prompt.\n- First useful receipt within three calls after onboarding; the `start` form makes it one.\n- Layered reads (`show --depth title|summary|full`) and a byte-budgeted `brief` that never drops a mandatory caveat.\n- A `next` that returns at most three transparent, deterministic recommendations and never invents a domain test.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Do not optimize solely for a small top-level command count. A generic `record` with an undiscoverable bag of operation names is poor UX. State-aware examples and errors are part of the API contract.\" Flag-form shortcuts ship in v1 because that is what makes \"add an observation in one command\" true (review 2 #3); `start` and `receipt` come from review 3 #5.\n\n# Gating semantics\nBlocked at goal level only by W01. Individual commands are wired to the W02/W03/W04/W06a tasks they need, so the Agent UX lane can start as soon as the catalog exists.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An agent with only `investigation --help` can create a case, preserve a source, record an observation and read it back.\n- [ ] Every error says what failed, what was committed, what remains true and what to do next.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign bar, first clause: \"a fresh agent can use the tool with no bespoke instruction\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.\nParent goal: MA. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §7.1–§7.3, §8.1–§8.3, §8.7, §9.1–§9.6, §19.2 (W05), §19.3 (Milestone A: minimum W05) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-1hp","size":"xl","title":"W05a Minimum agent-native CLI for the slice: init, record, schema, start, receipt, show, brief, next, onboard"}	blake3:4FNb--tm2n3xncQ3auQL_oTIFU-MaFtMJONdwsllyCM
1795320523126087	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-d8y	{"description":"# Context & Background\nMilestone A needs \"minimum W06\": a local file or stdin stream becomes a Source and an Artifact with exact retained bytes, and an Observation can cite a precise selector that the core verifies mechanically. Lineage, claim-level independence, the remaining selector kinds, reference mode and the classification model are W06b under Milestone B.\n\n# Goals\n- `ingest` with a strict manifest (`case_id` xor `new_case`), streaming hash and copy into the blob store, and honest per-item outcomes.\n- Safe path resolution under configured import roots.\n- Decoded-text derivatives with their own digest, encoding and parser version.\n- Native validation of `whole`, `byte_range`, `text_lines` and `json_pointer`, plus `text_quote` verification returning `verified`, `ambiguous` or `not_found`.\n- `evidence read` returning a bounded excerpt clearly labeled as untrusted content.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nArtifact creation with retained bytes goes only through `ingest` or `capture`, \"to prevent nonexistent-but-verified blobs\". The `text_quote` check (review 2 #2) is the cheapest mechanical defence against fabricated quotations: it shows the words are in the source, and says explicitly that this is presence, not support.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A digest never describes bytes different from the retained bytes; source mutation during collection is detected.\n- [ ] A text Observation whose quote is `not_found` is rejected; `ambiguous` is reported.\n- [ ] A failed ingest publishes neither a case nor a Source.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"A reviewer can traverse any important conclusion to the exact retained source/selector\" starts here.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.\nParent goal: MA. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.4, §8.4, §11.1, §11.5, §16.4, §19.2 (W06), §19.3 (Milestone A: minimum W06) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w06","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1hp","size":"l","title":"W06a Minimum local ingest for the slice: byte snapshots, text derivatives, native selectors, quote checks"}	blake3:BpdmZ05KThUXB4boC0NyAKrDgxw-CYNH44GDVG5aWX4
1795320523217145	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2jz	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW05a delivered the minimum CLI for the slice. W05b completes work package W05: the epistemic frontier, the full `next` ranking with decision-changing value, stalled-case detection, reversible advice deferral, `brief` levels and semantic deltas, historical snapshot reads, `explain`, full-text `search`, authored synopses with staleness, `--read-only`, `doctor`, `integration show/apply`, embedded documentation and the §21 agent instructions, plus a performance harness for the §10.5 targets.\n\n# Goals\n- `frontier` is the complete, filterable inventory of investigable gaps at a snapshot; `next` is its ranked top three. Both come from one versioned rule engine with stable `gap_id`s.\n- Every read is snapshot- and provenance-bound; one brief uses one verified snapshot.\n- Authored free text is rendered as quoted data at every depth; only compiled-in or pinned-pack text is guidance.\n- Docs ship embedded in the binary and agree with it.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nSapper's fact/intent frontier is adopted as an *epistemic* frontier, not a task scheduler: \"A frontier item is not an external task assignment.\" Ranking exposes each factor and its source; it is a heuristic, never a computed expected information gain.\n\n# Gating semantics\nNo goal-level blocker beyond Milestone B's (AG). W13 (MCP) is blocked by this goal because the MCP `read` tool wraps these commands.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W05: \"Two cold agents navigate both modes; title/summary/full retrieval and source-bound caveats pass.\"\n- [ ] `frontier` returns the same snapshot-bound items as the unranked inventory behind `next`.\n- [ ] Warm `brief`/`next` meet or are profiled against the 250 ms p95 target at 10,000 objects and 50,000 relations.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 10, layered attention: \"preserve exhaustive truth in canonical records but make short reads useful and safe; a summary is not a new independent source.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: MB. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §7.2–§7.5, §8.3, §8.4, §8.8, §9.1, §10.5, §16.1, §18.4, §19.2 (W05), §21 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-1fa","size":"xl","title":"W05b Full agent-native CLI: frontier, complete next, search, explain, brief levels, doctor, docs, read-only"}	blake3:t1_ne3-h1iPTp24hmSF1OzQ0HY7BO875HpNUWuguFcw
1795320523317696	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1c5	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW06a made local ingest work for the slice. W06b completes work package W06: source lineage, raw versus derived references, claim-level origin independence, the remaining selector kinds, explicit reference-only imports, source assessment and withdrawal, the sensitivity model with privacy defaults, and raw-file hardening. It is proven on Scenario D (three references that appear to corroborate a disputed specification value but share one origin).\n\n# Goals\n- Deduplicate blob bytes only, never acquisition or observation identities.\n- Independence is recorded per claim, not per document: one article may quote a specification in one passage and report its own bench test in another.\n- `table_cell`, `time_window`, `code_span` and `external_selector`; unsupported selectors are stored `validation: unavailable`, never falsely verified.\n- Default classification `internal`, never public; derivatives inherit classification; downgrades require a reviewed transformation.\n- Bounded, traversal-safe, non-executing parsers.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 3 #3 replaced \"one acquisition family\" with claim-level origins because the earlier model lost the article's own bench test. \"Exact byte deduplication is useful, but different bytes can still share one flawed origin.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W06: \"Research scenario and duplicate-origin trap pass.\"\n- [ ] Three documents quoting one specification keep three acquisition records and one known origin for that claim.\n- [ ] An unavailable external artifact yields `source_unavailable`, never fabricated verified bytes.\n- [ ] Symlink, traversal and archive-expansion fixtures do not escape intended roots.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Provenance\": distinguish raw source, acquisition, derivation, observation, interpretation and conclusion; preserve uncertainty, errors and corrections.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: MB. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.4, §5.6, §11.1, §11.4, §11.5, §16.2, §16.4, §17.4, §19.2 (W06), §20.1 (Source research) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w06","milestone-b","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1fa","size":"xl","title":"W06b Generic ingest complete: lineage, claim-level independence, more selectors, classification"}	blake3:S_-fA6ljCSXs-VmIHAsEs5bpymdiZ86zz3ZI20Uc-74
1795320523403688	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1y1	{"description":"# Context & Background\nExternal producers — benchmark harnesses, trace analyzers, simulators, research scrapers — must be able to record sessions, runs and artifacts through one neutral contract, in any language, without `inv` knowing their names or protocols and without opening an investigative case. W07 builds `investigation-recording` (Rust library plus JSON-CLI protocol), the `capture` commands, the `producer-run.v1` envelope and cross-language conformance fixtures.\n\n# Goals\n- `capture begin/append/attach/finalize/recover`, none of which authorizes, starts or reruns an external action.\n- Per-handle positions and a precise seal, so every acknowledged item survives at its position and a late item cannot alter a finalized manifest.\n- A producer's result is retained as the producer's assertion; only an attributed Assessment or Conclusion can promote it.\n- Rust and Python fixture producers with identical semantics; crash and retry qualified.\n- No built-in adapter to any named application; migration guidance is a document, not a module.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Never hold the canonical commit lock while an external process runs.\" A producer may crash after effects but before finalization, so recovery must never assume the operation was not performed. Review 3 #8 added per-handle positions because a stream claimed complete with a silently lost middle item is indistinguishable from a complete one otherwise.\n\n# Gating semantics\nThe plan says to run the A-gate before W07; Milestone B's blocker on AG enforces that.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W07: \"Python fixture producer and Rust fixture producer identical semantics; crash/retry qualified.\"\n- [ ] The same `producer-run.v1` schema records a synthetic benchmark, a file-analysis report, a simulation and a trace-capture session without core code changes.\n- [ ] A standalone producer records one Run in `record` mode with no hypotheses, Obsidian or model installed.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Inversion of dependencies\": core depends on a versioned neutral producer/recording contract only.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: MB. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.2, §3.5, §3.6, §11.2–§11.8, §17.6, §19.2 (W07), §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1fa","size":"xl","title":"W07 Neutral recording producer API: capture begin/append/attach/finalize/recover, conformance"}	blake3:sQGSOz6tN57fk8cDc3hJjuck23-mJeuarTXlK1IqSgg
1795320523491184	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-xz8	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe vault is a generated, Obsidian-compatible projection of canonical state — \"never canonical state\". W08 builds the headless Markdown renderer: `Start Here.md`, a case Overview, one note per entity with flat `inv_` properties, static Markdown tables (including the comparison matrix), blindspot and retrospective notes, the human-owned `Notes/ Boards/ Inbox/` areas, an ownership manifest, and drift detection. Native `.base` files, Canvas and reconciliation are W12.\n\n# Goals\n- \"Manage the casebook, not confiscate the entire vault\": render or delete only files proven owned; never recursively erase foreign files.\n- Deterministic output: an identical snapshot produces identical owned bytes.\n- Human notes preserved byte for byte; direct edits to generated notes are drift, not transactions.\n- Useful with community plugins disabled and with Obsidian absent.\n- Untrusted evidence and authored text rendered inert; restricted material kept out unless an approved derivative exists.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe vault is a child directory so Obsidian Sync or ordinary Git behaviour \"does not silently publish the canonical raw journal or private captures\". The renderer returns a path/hash/content plan that an ownership-aware publisher applies, which is what makes drift detection and dry runs possible.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W08: \"Layered casebook with community plugins disabled; gaps/lessons visible, drift detected, notes preserved.\"\n- [ ] Render failure advertises partial/stale state while the CLI still reports current canonical state.\n- [ ] A projection conflict never blocks unrelated evidence ingestion.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign bar, third clause: \"a human can browse it with pleasure\" — without the casebook ever becoming a second source of truth.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: MB. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2, §12.1–§12.4, §12.7, §16.1, §16.2, §18.3, §19.2 (W08), §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-1fa","size":"xl","title":"W08 Native vault renderer: overview, entity notes, static tables, ownership manifest, drift"}	blake3:dFVfyXV5wLsH1o-y32c9iRtSqdmQhCGB0N-LYSahoJk
1795320523590497	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3fk	{"description":"# Context & Background\nDifferent questions need different rigor, \"never one compulsory ritual\". Method packs carry that guidance as versioned, declarative, non-executable data: guidance cards, contextual triggers, optional namespaced properties, validation-policy proposals, views and report templates. `general` is the only compulsory pack and ships built in. `debugging`, `performance`, `source-research`, `decision-analysis` and `security-research` ship as optional examples on the same public schema; none is special-cased in the core.\n\n# Goals\n- A pack schema and inert loader that validate before use and pin id, version and digest into the case.\n- All 23 guidance cards of §2.4, from `frame` to `close`, including the revision-4 cards `intervene`, `causal-structure`, `reference-class` and `premortem`.\n- `inv next` shows one relevant card rather than the whole corpus; `inv guide NAME` shows a card on request.\n- A new pack can add a card and optional fields without modifying Rust record types.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA pack \"cannot execute commands, load binary code, contact services, or grant authority\", and cannot change canonical serialization, integrity checks, evidence trust rules, atomicity or access control. Code execution would enlarge the trust surface (§22), so there is no DSL that runs code. Pack rules may require fields on *promotion* but never prevent recording raw evidence.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W09: \"Contextual cards teach challenge and method learning; packs never execute code or alter canonical invariants.\"\n- [ ] Existing cases do not silently adopt newer pack revisions.\n- [ ] An uninstalled or unknown pack does not prevent generic recording, inspection or export.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 4, \"Methods serve decisions\", and the warning that \"Research must not become ritual.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: MB. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.4, §4.3, §11.6, §18.3, §19.2 (W09), §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-1fa","size":"l","title":"W09 Declarative method packs: schema, built-in general pack with 23 guidance cards, optional example packs"}	blake3:q9lol7vaqr-VTkWFesbTaMUzu_qRtRsDGgdmwBMKH5s
1795320523678383	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3kh	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW10 is where the plan's \"never weaken these invariants\" list becomes machine-checked: measurement validity per observation or channel, source independence, material assumptions, retractions, review freshness, objections, the read-only structural `blindspots` audit and scoped challenge Reviews. This tracker also places here the journal-derived retrospective digest, lesson admission and consultation tracking, likelihood with Brier scoring, trust-level inheritance for authored text, opt-in write-side guardrails, weights with ranges, optional coverage plans, Scenario B (p99 regression) and the six §17.7 adversarial drills.\n\n# Goals\n- No missing-control or stale-reviewed result is ever displayed as valid.\n- `inv blindspots` reports recorded structural gaps with stable, rule-versioned `gap_id`s and never certifies completeness.\n- Reviews declare `context_isolation`; same-session self-review is shown as low independence; same-model-family reviews form one dependence group.\n- Retrospective lessons must cite journal-derived digest items or record IDs.\n- The core invents no p-value, confidence interval, causal claim or score.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A clean audit never certifies that no blindspots exist.\" Deterministic checks cover recorded structure only; semantic gaps are for an attributed reviewer. Typed `causal_role` tags were deferred (review 2: agents fill fields whether or not they can justify them); the `causal-structure` card and the `failure_only_selection` check were adopted instead.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W10: \"Gap audit and adversarial fixtures; no missing-control or stale-reviewed result displayed as valid.\"\n- [ ] An audited case with no detected issue displays \"no structural gap identified under checks X\", never \"verified correct\".\n- [ ] §20.1 measurement and layered-retrieval/blindspot/retrospective checklists pass.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign bar, second clause: \"its output is epistemically honest\".\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: MB. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.6, §5.7, §6.3–§6.5, §8.5, §8.6, §14.2, §14.3, §15.1, §15.2, §16.1, §17.2, §17.7, §19.2 (W10), §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-1fa","size":"xl","title":"W10 Honest knowledge: validity, freshness, objections, blindspots, challenge reviews, retro digest"}	blake3:u06tAp1RacqbjoTvlOa3rvKZ_V1tXKXR_uMp4ao8Bd4
1795320523776842	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-65s	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW11 turns committed state into things people keep and share: a handoff for a fresh agent, case and analysis reports, an ADR-like decision record, a method retrospective, a curated review vault, a full workspace archive with verified restore, and a producer bundle. It also implements the audience policy that governs every one of them, and retention: withdrawal, truthful availability, and (if it can be qualified) physical reclamation.\n\n# Goals\n- Every report renders deterministically from an exact committed snapshot with no model call.\n- Audience policy is positive inclusion, not a best-effort scrub; effective state and caveats are computed against the full snapshot *before* filtering.\n- A restored archive on a clean machine yields the same case state and evidence digests.\n- Retention applies to the acquisition or Artifact reference first, never directly to a digest.\n- Review export and recovery archive are different products with different guarantees.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Filtering must not strengthen a recommendation\" (review 3 #9): a public decision whose decisive objection is restricted must be withheld or carry an approved caveat. Review 3 #10 moved retention to the reference because blobs deduplicate across cases. Ordinary withdrawal \"is not erasure. State that clearly in commands and docs.\"\n\n# Gating semantics\nNo goal-level blocker beyond Milestone C's. Tasks are wired to the W08 renderer and W10 freshness/blindspot tasks they need; archive and restore need only the store.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W11: \"Restore exact replay; case/ADR reports include bounded methods lessons with links/limits and do not leak restricted evidence.\"\n- [ ] Negative and inconclusive results remain visible in every report.\n- [ ] A requested export that cannot satisfy its policy fails with exact exclusions or is explicitly labeled incomplete.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes the case portable and recoverable without making it leaky.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: MC. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.7, §14.4, §15.4, §16.2, §16.3, §16.5, §19.2 (W11), §20.2, §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w11","milestone-c","lane-projection"],"parent":"bn-3q2","size":"xl","title":"W11 Deterministic reports, handoffs, export, archive/restore, retention and audience policy"}	blake3:X8LLGeyBL0OPZLzphAJWy2QFQ8M5vxJt597JN9U0758
1795320523872064	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1q7	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW08 produced a headless Markdown casebook. W12 adds the native Obsidian layer on top: generated `.base` views filtered by exact case ID, a bounded JSON Canvas case map, `vault reconcile` with explicit dispositions, existing-vault mode behind a managed prefix, optional UI checks through the official Obsidian CLI, and a load test near 50,000 notes. Native Properties, Backlinks, Search, Bases and Canvas are sufficient for v1; no community plugin is required.\n\n# Goals\n- Fifteen shipped views (§12.5) as `.base` files with static Markdown fallback tables.\n- A mode-sensitive Canvas map with stable coordinates, labeled edges and a legend.\n- Native Bases edits change underlying Markdown properties, so they are detected as projection drift and never become accepted state.\n- Feature detection or a tested compatibility profile, with the tested Obsidian version recorded.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 1 claimed the `note.` prefix is mandatory and proposed a `sort:` key; revision 4 rejected both after checking the current syntax page (`note.inv_kind` and bare `inv_kind` are equivalent; no view sort key is documented). The lesson is the task: verify syntax against the actual supported release rather than against memory or a review.\n\n# Gating semantics\nBlocked at goal level by W08, since every W12 task builds on the renderer. Views that show blindspots or the frontier are additionally wired to W10/W05b tasks.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W12: \"Tested supported Obsidian release; headless Markdown still valid.\"\n- [ ] Supported-version Bases and Canvas open correctly; links, aliases and excerpt blocks resolve.\n- [ ] Generated-note edits, Base property edits, deletes, renames and conflict copies are diagnosed as drift.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Vault\": managed casebook projection with safe human-owned Notes/Boards/Inbox; no required community plugins.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.\nParent goal: MC. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.1, §12.3, §12.5–§12.9, §19.2 (W12), §20.4, §23.6, Disposition: Obsidian (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w12","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-3q2","size":"l","title":"W12 Native Bases and Canvas, vault reconcile, existing-vault mode, supported Obsidian release checks"}	blake3:MRK4btkyK87W_vM4WQ2u72HIZEIBxex0UeoKaUK8h9Y
1795320523967157	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-17k	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"The CLI is the reference implementation. MCP uses the same Rust command dispatch, validation, receipts, and error codes over stdio.\" The primary agent path is the CLI plus a skill or AGENTS snippet, because measured token cost for CLI use is far below a large eagerly loaded MCP tool set. MCP is a secondary transport, not a second source of truth.\n\n# Goals\n- `investigation mcp` over stdio with no HTTP listener.\n- Default `core` profile: at most seven flat-schema tools (`onboard`, `read`, `evidence_read`, `schema`, `record`, `ingest`, `receipt`) with a `tools/list` budget under 12 KiB enforced in CI.\n- Opt-in `expanded` profile with domain-grouped typed write tools for hosts with deferred tool loading.\n- Read-only and mutation annotations match real effects; no hypothetical tools advertised.\n- CLI and MCP walkthroughs reach the same accepted domain state.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 1 #9 argued for a compact default; review 2 added that the plan should target the stateless MCP revision (2026-07-28, no `initialize` handshake), so profiles are chosen by server flag and visible through `tools/list`, not negotiated in a session. Verify that revision's details against the published specification when implementing; do not rely on recollection.\n\n# Gating semantics\nBlocked at goal level by W05b (the `read` tool wraps `brief`, `show`, `explain`, `search`, `next`, `frontier`). The `blindspots`, `retrospective` and `check` actions are wired to their W10/W02 tasks.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W13: \"CLI/MCP parity and error conformance in independent tests.\"\n- [ ] `tools/list` size and tool-call accuracy are measured for both profiles (input to §20.5).\n- [ ] No tool hides an unvalidated JSON string behind a supposedly typed schema.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nReaches agent hosts that prefer MCP without forking the engine or the semantics.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b.\nParent goal: MC. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.2, §9.7, §17.7, §19.2 (W13), §20.5, §20.7, Disposition: Agent experience and interface (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w13","milestone-c","lane-agent-ux","mcp"],"parent":"bn-3q2","size":"l","title":"W13 Typed MCP frontend derived from the same catalog: compact core profile, expanded profile, parity tests"}	blake3:hvg-JZSeXaMFkPurNbCI41Qa6w1LroM4kwqlVKO9Io0
1795320524075117	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1e7	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.5 is a release gate \"structured as a controlled comparison, not a documentation review or an uncontrolled pass rate\". W14 extends the A-gate harness to all four domains and both transports, writes sealed evaluation case families, runs the preregistered trial against the plain-notes arm, runs the deterministic acceptance measures and the human experience gate, and dogfoods a real investigation. It also ships the `demo` command, tutorials and walkthroughs that the trials and humans use.\n\n# Goals\n- Sealed case families written by someone who does not work on blindspot rules or onboarding, with keys outside the agents' workspace; only sealed results count toward a release claim.\n- Primary outcome is the successor's answer, graded blind to arm against a prewritten rubric.\n- Paired differences with intervals clustered by case, per-model and per-domain breakdowns, repeat reliability and cost per arm.\n- Zero false promotions in deterministic invalid-control and stale-basis fixtures; \"zero observed\" in agent trials is always stated with its upper bound.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Twenty runs cannot separate 90% from 80% success (18/20 has a 95% Wilson interval of about 70–97%)\", so 18/20 is a smoke target only. Matching the notes arm on correctness at much higher cost is a failure. \"Don't cheat by embedding fixture-specific solutions in onboarding or source labels.\"\n\n# Gating semantics\nNo goal-level blocker beyond Milestone C's, so sealed cases and harness work can start early. W14.08 (run the release trial) is blocked by W05b–W12 completing, as the plan's W14 row requires.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W14: \"Published trials meet §20 goals; invalid outcomes, hidden caveats and phantom lesson promotion are zero.\"\n- [ ] Every failure is investigated; tuning on failures is followed by fresh held-out families.\n- [ ] Fresh-agent and human-review evidence is retained with known limitations.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the evidence the product itself would demand before promoting a conclusion about its own usefulness.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: MC. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.5, §17.7, §19.2 (W14), §20.5, §20.6, Disposition: Evaluation and sequencing (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-3q2","size":"xl","title":"W14 Cold-agent evaluation, seeded multi-domain trials, layered-read/blindspot/retrospective drills, dogfooding"}	blake3:UQyju2Q3YlunFdfN0DJZ47fNcGxNbREpIZm5yByOFXQ
1795320524163808	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3ob	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.7 lists the definition of done for the first release approved for consequential investigations. REL is the closing audit: it holds no feature work, only the evidence that the shipped binary, its documentation and its claims agree, and that what is deferred is listed rather than stubbed.\n\n# Goals\n- Every normative implemented command and example works against the shipped binary.\n- Executable examples run in fresh fixture workspaces using actual returned IDs; schematic examples are labeled.\n- The release clearly lists deferred commands and features; there are no fake success stubs.\n- Release notes distinguish demonstrated guarantees from planned work.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA release note that overstates is the same failure the product exists to prevent: a claim shown as validated without its caveats. Listing §22's deliberate deferrals protects binding decisions from being relitigated by accident.\n\n# Gating semantics\nBlocked at goal level by W11, W12, W13 and W14. REL.01 is additionally blocked by MB.01 so the Milestone B proof is part of the audit trail.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All twelve §20.7 items are checked with a pointer to evidence for each.\n- [ ] Source, schema and toolchain versions and the supported OS/filesystem are documented.\n- [ ] The published core has zero named-producer imports and is demonstrated with an independent generic producer conformance fixture.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEnds Milestone C in \"a runnable proof, not a skeleton of directories\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11, W12, W13, W14.\nParent goal: MC. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §18.4, §19.3 (Milestone C), §20.7, §22 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","release","milestone-c"],"parent":"bn-3q2","size":"m","title":"REL Release readiness: definition of done audit, documentation agreement, release notes, packaging"}	blake3:pTe2nUJ-lOTqDjnv5GQ3XR7rV5VhfVQ5Vheeoq2XSP4
1795320524247243	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-tjq	{"description":"# Context & Background\nA small optional TypeScript companion may improve review interaction inside Obsidian: a case sidebar, an alternatives matrix, a measurement inspector, decision and conclusion review dialogs, and structured actions that submit typed mutations with fresh CAS preconditions. It is built \"only after CLI and vault pass their offline gates\", and it \"never implements a second planner, recorder, agent, model client, shell, benchmark, source crawler or domain-specific schema\".\n\n# Goals\n- A bridge that spawns the configured absolute `inv` path with an argv array and JSON stdin; no shell interpolation and no local HTTP server.\n- Review and transition dialogs fetch a fresh canonical snapshot and read-set from `inv`; they never assemble authority from projected notes.\n- Install, enable and uninstall are explicit, previewable, version-pinned and reversible; Restricted Mode is never quietly disabled.\n- Mobile starts as view/annotation only; the manifest truthfully declares desktop-only capabilities.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nCommunity plugins inherit Obsidian's access rather than a fine-grained sandbox, which is a concrete reason to keep the companion small. No reviewer examined §13 in depth, so the design is re-reviewed first (W15.01). The plan's W15 row depends on \"W12, W13 or CLI bridge\": the CLI bridge is the chosen default, so W13 is not a blocker.\n\n# Gating semantics\nBlocked at goal level by W12 and, through Milestone D, by Milestone C.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W15: \"Controlled mutations/cancel/revision/stale-editor gates pass.\"\n- [ ] The generated vault still works on mobile and on desktop without the plugin.\n- [ ] Plugin setup and uninstall preserve unrelated configuration and custom configuration-directory settings.\n- [ ] Every child bone is done, or the goal is closed with a recorded reason that the companion did not earn itself.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBetter human review of comparisons and conclusions against a fresh canonical snapshot, \"not an alternative database\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W12.\nParent goal: MD. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.9, §13.1–§13.4, §19.2 (W15), §20.4, Disposition: Not changed (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-3cm","size":"xl","title":"W15 Optional Obsidian companion plugin for comparison and review interaction"}	blake3:Xlrd2Ry3rC5eUh6o-sxpWvr3tc5obbON-lns6vIIzGQ
1795320524352878	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3mg	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW16 collects optional work that waits for \"W14, demonstrated need\": explicit local action claims so cooperating local agents avoid duplicate scheduling, convenience SDKs for consumer languages on top of the conformance-tested JSON protocol, documentation for authoring method packs, and packaging plus platform qualification beyond Linux.\n\n# Goals\n- Claims are explicit mutations with actor, action, expiry and relevant revisions; `next` stays read-only.\n- Expiry of a claim tied to an external run means \"reconcile the run\", never \"safe to repeat\".\n- macOS and Windows are qualified before any equivalent durability claim is made; `doctor` reports platform gates truthfully.\n- Pack authoring is documented without creating an automatic lesson-to-pack pipeline.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Do not make a lease service a prerequisite for useful investigation.\" Scheduling stays with the external agent host or task system. \"No distributed exactly-once execution claim is permitted\", because domain idempotency deduplicates records but cannot undo an external request.\n\n# Gating semantics\nBlocked at goal level by W14 and, through Milestone D, by Milestone C. Record the demonstrated need on each child before starting it; close unneeded children with the reason.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plan acceptance for W16: \"No duplicate external effects claimed; platform gates correctly reported.\"\n- [ ] Each child is done or closed with a recorded reason.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nExtends reach and collaboration without eroding the record/execute split or overstating guarantees.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W14.\nParent goal: MD. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.2, §14.1, §19.2 (W16), §22 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["work-package","w16","milestone-d"],"parent":"bn-3cm","size":"l","title":"W16 Optional local action claims, extension SDK conveniences, pack authoring docs, packaging and portability"}	blake3:9UCu0_vuznJHWMIoZkPutwdPhXHzmBbTDg1vdTV3qu8
1795320615694497	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-382	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThere is no code yet. §18.1 gives the repository shape: `crates/investigation-core` (domain types, catalog, lifecycles, gates), `-store` (journal, blobs, index, recovery), `-recording` (neutral producer protocol), `-cli` (the binary), `-mcp` (optional stdio transport), plus `methods/ obsidian/ plugin/ schemas/ fixtures/ examples/ training/ scripts/ docs/`. The names \"express dependency boundaries, not permission to proliferate crates\".\n\n# Goals\nA compiling Cargo workspace whose crate graph matches the required direction: `core` knows domain and schema; `store` knows durability; `recording` knows public producer ingestion; CLI and MCP are frontends; the method-pack loader is inert data; the renderer reads immutable snapshots.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nDependency direction is \"non-negotiable\" (§3.1). Encoding it in the crate graph makes a violation a compile error instead of a review comment. If separate crates cause churn in a small codebase, modules may be colocated temporarily \"without reversing this logical boundary\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Create the workspace `Cargo.toml` with shared lints, edition and a workspace dependency table.\n- Create the four initial crates with a trivial public item and one test each; reserve `investigation-mcp` as an empty member or a documented placeholder.\n- Binary target is named `investigation` (never `inv`; see W05a.01).\n- Add the top-level directories with a one-line README each stating what belongs there.\n- Read any destination-repo instructions first (the plan's W00 row begins with that). Note: a `CLAUDE.md` in a parent directory says to prefer Bun for JavaScript/TypeScript tooling; that applies to `plugin/` and scripts, not to the Rust core.\n- Keep the core synchronous: no async runtime, daemon, graph database or LLM dependency.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `cargo build --workspace` and `cargo test --workspace` succeed.\n- [ ] `investigation-core` has no dependency on store, recording, CLI or MCP crates.\n- [ ] Directory layout matches §18.1 and each directory explains its purpose.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nGives every later bone a place to land and makes the architecture boundary mechanical.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.1, §18.1, §18.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"m","title":"W00.01 Scaffold the Rust workspace, crate boundaries and repository directories"}	blake3:ibLDGHw0nIoLGwR62Gfa6sfO5DvwvHGsApDknLBTvz4
1795320615898136	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1dm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe commit lock relies on `std::fs::File::lock`, stable since Rust 1.89 (confirmed for revision 4), so the minimum toolchain is fixed by a design decision, not taste. CI is also where several plan-mandated budgets and guards will later be enforced (MCP `tools/list` size, neutrality, LazyFS job).\n\n# Goals\nA pinned toolchain and a CI pipeline that every later bone can extend, so \"tests runnable\" in the W00 acceptance is true on a clean machine.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nParallel agent lanes (§19.4) only integrate continuously if the gate is automatic. A pinned toolchain keeps crash-test seeds and generated schemas reproducible.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `rust-toolchain.toml` pinned to a stable release ≥ 1.89; record the MSRV in the workspace manifest.\n- CI jobs: `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy -D warnings`, `cargo test --workspace`, and a dependency audit (advisories and licences).\n- A single local entry point (script or `just`/`make` target) that runs the same checks as CI.\n- Leave documented extension points for later jobs: crash-injection property tests (W02.15), LazyFS (W02.16), MCP size budget (W13.02), neutrality guards (W00.10).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] CI passes on the scaffold and fails on a deliberate formatting or lint error.\n- [ ] Local and CI checks are the same commands.\n- [ ] Toolchain version and MSRV are documented.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nObjective evidence, not timelines, determines readiness (§19.3); CI is where that evidence is produced.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.01.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.1, §18.2, §19.2 (W00) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"s","title":"W00.02 Pin the toolchain and set up CI: format, lint, test, audit"}	blake3:GOPY6lbxdhCv1Y-SocKYn8m7P_5CONU8X47dtTQx66w
1795320615987538	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2jx	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§18.2 names plausible starting points — `clap`, `serde`, `serde_json`, `rusqlite`, `sha2`, `uuid`, `time`, `thiserror`, `tracing` and a tested YAML emitter — and explicitly refuses to dictate versions: \"inspect current releases/security advisories at implementation time.\"\n\n# Goals\nA short, justified dependency list with pinned versions and licence/advisory status, including the places where the plan warns against a library behaviour.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nTwo plan decisions depend on library details. (1) Integrity hashes cover stored event bytes, so no canonical-JSON (RFC 8785) library is needed; Rust JCS implementations have open number-encoding bugs. (2) `tempfile`'s `persist_noclobber` must not be relied on for publication, because it can fall back to a non-atomic link-and-unlink and syncs nothing.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Check each candidate's current release, licence and advisories; record the result in `docs/` (one table).\n- Confirm `serde_json` configuration can reject duplicate keys and non-finite numbers, or note the wrapper needed (W01.04).\n- Confirm how `link(2)` / `renameat2(RENAME_NOREPLACE)` will be reached (W02.05) — `rustix`, `nix` or `libc`.\n- Choose a YAML emitter that quotes Obsidian links and hostile strings deterministically (needed by W08.03).\n- Confirm UUIDv7 generation support and bundled-SQLite FTS5 availability in `rusqlite`.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Dependency table with version, licence, purpose and advisory status is committed.\n- [ ] The audit job from W00.02 passes with the pinned set.\n- [ ] The two warned-against behaviours are called out next to the relevant dependency.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps the offline, no-daemon, no-model binary small and its durability claims honest.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.01.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §18.2, §23.8 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"s","title":"W00.03 Select, pin and audit third-party dependencies"}	blake3:DGchxjwJojyxaZdiHKcYuHRWl5kKlzcanOtHlBSksRk
1795320616068500	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1zw	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§19.5: \"Three ADRs are due before W02, because none can change afterward without a migration.\" The first is the canonical store: a hand-rolled file-per-event journal, or SQLite as the canonical store. The plan body specifies the journal; the ADR must state the requirement that justifies it.\n\n# Goals\nA recorded decision naming the requirement — inspectability and grep/rsync-friendliness, hashes checkable with `sha256sum` — and the obligation it creates: the crash testing in W02.15 and W02.16.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 2 (#9, #10) asked for this ADR because a hand-rolled journal is the riskiest storage choice in the plan; SQLite already solves atomic commit. Review 1 proposed a segmented append-only log instead, which revision 4 rejected: appended logs bring torn-tail and \"append leaves garbage\" failures, and ext4/XFS fsync-failure behaviour is worse for appends. A file per event \"either exists whole or not at all\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Write `docs/adr/` entry: context, options (file-per-event journal; segmented log; SQLite canonical), decision, consequences.\n- State the revisit condition from the plan: only on a *measured* miss of the §10.5 targets, and then by packing sealed ranges as Git does, not by switching formats.\n- List the tests the decision obliges and link W02.15/W02.16.\n- If the author concludes SQLite-canonical is better, that is a binding-decision change: it needs the observed conflict, the ADR and updated tests (§22).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] ADR merged before any W02 code.\n- [ ] It names the justifying requirement, the rejected alternatives with reasons, and the crash-testing obligation.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes the most expensive-to-reverse storage decision explicit while it is still free to change.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.01.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.1, §19.5, Disposition: Storage and durability (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a","adr","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"s","title":"W00.04 ADR: file-per-event journal versus SQLite as the canonical store (due before W02)"}	blake3:lM3NyOgxH-3UQ421ZOxN_b5jMi689xsYA_VziNFkxgw
1795320616155580	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1h5	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§10.1 says: \"Use one linear workspace sequence for v1, subject to the per-case chain ADR due before W02.\" §19.5 states the trade: a per-case chain \"turns single-case export, restore and purge (§10.7, §16.5) into ordinary file operations and removes cross-case lock contention, at the cost of atomic cross-case transactions, which the plan barely uses. Lean per-case unless the ADR finds a real cross-case transaction.\"\n\n# Goals\nA decision, with evidence, on chain scope — and a precise statement of everything it changes.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThis cannot change after W02 without a migration. It also interacts with: idempotency scope `(workspace_id, actor, command_id)`; the `start`/`new_case` ingest that creates a case and its first Source in one transaction; blob deduplication across cases and retention per reference; lessons admitted across cases; workspace archive versus case archive; and the `events/<seq>.json` layout in §4.2.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Enumerate every cross-case write in the plan (candidates: lesson admission and consultation records, `new_case` creation, workspace-level policy) and decide whether each truly needs atomicity across cases.\n- For the chosen option, write down: directory layout, lock granularity, sequence and head-hash definition in receipts (`snapshot`), idempotency index scope, what `brief --snapshot` means, and how archive, restore and purge work.\n- Record the rejected option and the condition that would reopen it.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] ADR merged before any W02 code, with the cross-case transaction inventory in it.\n- [ ] W02.02, W02.04, W02.10, W11.08 and W11.11 can be implemented from it without further decisions.\n- [ ] If the decision departs from §10.1/§4.2 wording, the plan text to update is listed.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDecides whether single-case export, restore and purge are easy or hard for the life of the format.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.01.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.1, §10.7, §16.5, §19.5, Disposition: Storage and durability (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a","adr","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"s","title":"W00.05 ADR: one hash chain per workspace versus one per case (due before W02)"}	blake3:b8_9pNAuqRgyCDzIBSnxsFxwxMmCzaJCbUOdDzdOHUA
1795320616245963	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-129	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe third pre-W02 ADR is the A-gate decision rule. §19.3: \"Record the decision rule as an ADR before the runs: if `inv` does not beat the notes arm on false promotions or successor accuracy, cut or merge the record types agents ignored or misused before continuing.\"\n\n# Goals\nA rule written before anyone has seen pilot data, specific enough that two people would apply it the same way.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThis is the project applying its own method: a frozen prediction cannot be amended after results to claim success (§6.5). Writing the rule first is the guard against rationalizing whatever the pilot shows.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Define the comparison: paired, per case, `inv` arm versus plain-Markdown-notes arm, on (a) unwarranted promotions and (b) successor answer accuracy, with cost reported alongside.\n- Define \"does not beat\" for a pilot-sized sample without pretending to statistical power the pilot lacks.\n- Define what \"ignored or misused\" means per record type (e.g. never created, created empty, created with strawman content) and how that is measured from journals.\n- State the allowed outcomes: continue; cut or merge named types; stop and rethink.\n- State what the pilot may *not* be used to claim (it is not the release evidence).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] ADR merged before W02 work starts and before AG.07.\n- [ ] Outcomes, measures and thresholds are stated without reference to observed results.\n- [ ] AG.08 can apply it mechanically.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps the product hypothesis falsifiable at the one moment when acting on a negative answer is still cheap.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.3 (A-gate), §19.5, §20.5 (Decision rule) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a","adr","lane-evaluation","a-gate"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"s","title":"W00.06 ADR: the Milestone A decision rule, recorded before any pilot run"}	blake3:0BcvShnNSTgCUBxrF9K3mAVoSXMaXJ4ZdmL6UjZRbDw
1795320616329987	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2d9	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§19.5 asks for \"concise decision records\" for a list of build policies. These six concern what the product *is*. The decisions are already made in the plan; the ADRs make them findable and state what would have to be true to revisit them.\n\n# Goals\nOne short ADR each for:\n1. Domain-neutral core versus consumer-owned integrations (no adapter to a named producer is ever compiled into `inv`).\n2. Evidence versus assessment (`supports`, `contradicts`, `satisfies`, `violates` are attributed interpretive records, not truth booleans).\n3. Explanatory versus evaluative ontologies (Hypothesis ≠ Option; Assessment ≠ Comparison; Conclusion ≠ Decision).\n4. Criterion/version freshness (a changed criterion makes dependent comparisons and decisions `needs_review`).\n5. Recording/execution split (`inv` never runs an external operation; recommendations are marked `execution: external`).\n6. Method pack security (declarative data only; no executable hooks; pinned by digest).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Parallel agents must agree on exact object/operation versions centrally\" and \"Explicitly record substantive scope changes as ADRs.\" §22 adds that a binding decision is revisited only \"for an actually observed conflict, with a recorded ADR and corresponding updated tests\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\nUse one template: context, decision, consequences, what was rejected and why, revisit condition, plan sections. Quote the plan's normative sentence in each. Keep each to about a page.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Six ADRs merged under `docs/adr/` and indexed.\n- [ ] Each names its revisit condition and the tests or guards that enforce it (e.g. W00.10 for ADR 1).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nProtects the boundaries that make the tool general-purpose from erosion by convenient shortcuts.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.01.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §1.3, §2.4, §3, §5.2, §5.5, §19.5, §22 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a","adr"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"m","title":"W00.07 ADRs: boundaries and ontology (neutral core, evidence vs assessment, record/execute split, packs)"}	blake3:zTMaZtEBZCAiMkztxW-5f5OcBM6QiAzsmhr_FmrCOxo
1795320616421503	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2mz	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe rest of the §19.5 list. As with W00.07, the plan has decided; the ADRs record the decision and its revisit condition.\n\n# Goals\nOne short ADR each for:\n1. Journal versus Markdown as source of truth (the vault is a projection, never canonical).\n2. Local concurrency versus distributed merge (writer lock, CAS, idempotency; no CRDT claim).\n3. Vault ownership (tool-owned projections; human-owned Notes/Boards/Inbox; ownership manifest).\n4. Report versus recovery archive (different products, different guarantees).\n5. Supported durability guarantees (Linux local filesystem; fsync errors fatal; network filesystems refused).\n6. Layered summary freshness (synopsis is a projection or attributed annotation; stale on material change; never corroboration).\n7. Audit-rule versioning (stable `gap_id`; rule version disclosed so a changed rule is not mistaken for new evidence).\n8. Review scope and method-retrospective lifecycle (material-case rule, waiver, supersession).\n9. Explicit, non-automatic lesson adoption (admission step; no self-amending doctrine).\n10. Executable name (`investigation`; `inv` only as a non-clobbering alias).\n11. MCP profile shape (seven-tool `core` default under 12 KiB; opt-in `expanded`).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nSeveral of these were contested across reviews (lesson adoption, MCP shape, executable name). Recording the settled position with its evidence prevents re-litigation by a later agent who has not read the Disposition table.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nSame template as W00.07. Where the Disposition of reviews records a reason, cite the review number and carry the reason over.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Eleven ADRs merged and indexed; none contradicts the plan body.\n- [ ] The ADR index lists all §19.5 topics with their bone or file.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDurable, discoverable reasons are exactly what this product argues investigations should leave behind.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.01.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.3, §8.5, §8.6, §9.7, §10.2, §10.8, §12.1, §15.4, §19.5 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a","adr"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"m","title":"W00.08 ADRs: storage, projection and product policy (source of truth, vault ownership, lessons, MCP shape)"}	blake3:zdWvpvOphMczPWcUu63enjMgujo8aZvXDRxnL9sN2jM
1795320616538735	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-cst	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§10.2: \"Atomic visibility and power-loss durability are different. Document the tested filesystem/OS guarantees. Initial supported write platform is Linux on a local filesystem; qualify macOS/Windows before claiming equivalent durability. Network shares and cloud-synchronized canonical state are unsupported.\"\n\n# Goals\nA single document that states, per platform and filesystem, what is supported, what is tested, and what is explicitly not claimed — the source `doctor` (W05b.10) and release notes (REL.03) quote.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n§20.7 requires that \"source/schema/toolchain versions and supported OS/filesystem are documented\" and that release notes \"distinguish demonstrated guarantees from planned work\". A matrix written up front keeps later claims from drifting upward. Hashes establish equality with a recorded digest; they never amount to \"tamper-proof\" (§10.6).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Columns: platform, filesystem, read support, write support, durability evidence (which tests), known limits.\n- Rows at minimum: Linux ext4/XFS/btrfs local; Linux tmpfs (tests only); NFS/SMB/FUSE (refused: `RENAME_NOREPLACE` returns `EINVAL` on NFS and some FUSE servers, and locking is unreliable); macOS APFS and Windows NTFS (unqualified until W16.04); cloud-synced folders (unsupported).\n- State the same-user threat model: restrictive permissions where supported, \"without implying protection against arbitrary same-user processes\".\n- Mark every cell \"planned\" until a test bone lands; update as W02.15/W02.16 complete.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Matrix committed under `docs/` and linked from the README.\n- [ ] No cell claims a guarantee without naming the test that demonstrates it.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 5, truthful states, applied to the product's own guarantees.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.01.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.2, §10.6, §18.4, §20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a","docs","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"s","title":"W00.09 Write the support matrix and durability-claims document"}	blake3:5oVCkQ6oB_d9oveZAHHtTgL6OmTW0mAElXrJLhNNNSg
1795320616636678	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2sm	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§3.6 promises \"No secret knowledge of any consumer in binary, default case schema, help text, tests, or packaged method cards.\" §20.1 requires that \"Nothing in default `inv` help, on-disk default workspace, schema or binary requires a named external tool, target, permission, principal or security concept.\" §20.3 requires core compilation and tests to pass \"with zero installed producers, executors, task trackers, or environment credentials\".\n\n# Goals\nAutomated guards that fail the build when the core learns about a named producer, grows a security-specific default, or reverses a crate dependency.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe plan grew out of security-tool references (Sapper, nvwabase) and deliberately generalized. Regression toward that vocabulary is the most likely way neutrality is lost, and it would happen one convenient default at a time.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Crate-graph test: `core` depends on none of the other workspace crates; `store` and `recording` do not depend on CLI/MCP.\n- Deny-list scan over generated help text, schemas, the default workspace created by `init`, and the built-in `general` pack: named external tools and a small list of domain-specific terms that must not appear outside optional packs and docs.\n- A CI job that runs the full test suite in a minimal environment with no network and no extra tools on `PATH`.\n- Make the deny-list a reviewed data file with a comment explaining each entry; the scan starts trivially passing and gains teeth as artifacts appear.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Introducing a core dependency on `investigation-cli` fails CI.\n- [ ] Introducing a deny-listed term into help or a default schema fails CI with the offending path.\n- [ ] The suite passes offline with no producers installed.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Domain: General-purpose. No required target system, security concept, implementation work item, or external product.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.02.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.1, §3.6, §20.1 (Cross-domain invariants), §20.3, §20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a","test","security"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"s","title":"W00.10 CI guards for domain neutrality and dependency direction"}	blake3:TfmlCSZjlPQRFJ4Ra9YJU9BLiK0FInc8u6IpyZWH2nU
1795320616733954	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3ac	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW00's acceptance is \"Domain boundaries/protocol drafts committed and tests runnable.\" All scenarios must ship \"as deterministic offline fixtures with expected outcomes, counterevidence and invalid-result variants\" (§17). §20.7 requires that the release \"clearly lists deferred commands/features; no fake success stubs\", and §22 lists sixteen deliberate deferrals with entry conditions.\n\n# Goals\n- Conventions for fixtures so four scenario families and many variants stay deterministic and reviewable.\n- Draft protocol documents (mutation envelope, response and error envelopes, `producer-run.v1`) committed as the shared reference the lanes work from until the catalog generates them.\n- A deferred-features register seeded from §22.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"CLI snippets showing IDs are examples; generate live IDs in shipped demos.\" Fixtures therefore need a convention for seed bytes versus generated IDs from day one. A register of deferrals lets an implementer \"proceed without asking the user to select a DB, plugin, runtime, scoring formula or dedicated domain. Those are decided.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `fixtures/README`: directory per scenario and variant; seed source bytes are committed and immutable; expected outcomes are expressed as properties, not as hard-coded generated IDs; development, pilot and sealed evaluation material are kept apart (sealed keys never live in this repository's agent-visible tree — see AG.04).\n- `docs/protocol/`: the §9.3, §9.5, §9.6 and §11.2 examples, each marked as *schematic* where it contains placeholders.\n- `docs/deferred.md`: the §22 table verbatim with entry conditions, plus the revision-4 deferrals (typed `causal_role` tags, selector/receipt exporters, PDF export, semantic embeddings, event-text purge).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Fixture conventions, protocol drafts and the deferred register are committed.\n- [ ] Every schematic example is labeled as schematic.\n- [ ] The register is linked from the README and referenced by REL.03.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nShared drafts stop each parallel lane from inventing its own envelope, and the register keeps scope honest.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.01.\nParent goal: W00. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md Build rules, §17, §19.2 (W00), §20.7, §22 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w00","milestone-a","docs","fixture"],"parent":"bn-1a5","size":"s","title":"W00.11 Fixture conventions, protocol draft documents and the deferred-features register"}	blake3:kSwYZQPuHzmS5bTats--lsYhAFRBFOsDYkqdsZZBbY4
1795320735876582	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1hh	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Every accepted object has an immutable typed ID... Use typed prefixes plus UUIDv7 for identity; never use a title or path as identity.\" Examples in the plan: `case_…`, `q_…`, `cmp_…`, `tx_…`.\n\n# Goals\nOne ID type used by every record, transaction, run and capture handle, with parsing, formatting and prefix resolution.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nTitles change and paths move; identity must not. UUIDv7 gives rough creation order for humans, but \"UUIDv7 IDs are ordered only within one process, so ordering comes from the sequence number alone.\" They also embed a millisecond creation time, which matters for exports (W11.13).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- A prefix registry covering every v0-alpha type plus transactions, runs, handles, checkpoints and imports; unknown prefixes are parse errors.\n- Structured output always returns full IDs. Short unique prefixes may be accepted interactively; an ambiguous prefix is an error that lists the candidates.\n- ID generation sits behind an injectable source so tests and crash simulations are replayable (W01.17).\n- Never derive ordering or trusted time from an ID.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Round-trip parse/format for every prefix; wrong-prefix references are rejected with the expected type named.\n- [ ] Ambiguous short ID returns an error with candidates and commits nothing.\n- [ ] A test seeds the generator and gets identical IDs across runs.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nStable identity is what lets a title change without breaking a link, a review, or a vault filename.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §9.5, §10.1, §15.4, §23.8 (RFC 9562) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"s","title":"W01.01 Typed identifiers: prefixes plus UUIDv7, full IDs in output, unambiguous short-ID resolution"}	blake3:NkwrNYlqhTGhziPJBUlfWoySnTnn-ib5R2LE0CdFYYI
1795320735964131	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3bf	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEvery record stores \"common IDs, actor claims, UTC acceptance sequence, revisions, schema version, classification and provenance\". Timestamps are UTC RFC 3339 with explicit precision; source timestamp, offset and clock quality are retained separately.\n\n# Goals\nA shared header type (or trait) that every domain record embeds, so freshness, CAS, attribution and classification work uniformly.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Use sequence/revision for causality, not wall-clock ordering.\" Three times can differ and must not be conflated: `recorded_at` (local acceptance), `observed_at` (claimed observation time) and `acquired_at` (collection). \"Unknown values are explicit `null` or a typed `unknown` state, never invented zeroes.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Fields: `id`, `case_id`, `schema_version`, creating actor and `recorded_at`, current `revision`, history pointers, `classification` (`public | internal | restricted`, default `internal`) with reason/tags, provenance refs, `extensions.<namespace>` (reserved, inert until W09.06).\n- A typed `Unknown`/`Known<T>` representation that serializes distinctly from zero or empty.\n- Clock-quality enum for source times; never promote a source timestamp into ordering.\n- Every readable record exposes a stable display title (bounded length, control characters and markup stripped — see W05b.13).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All v0-alpha types embed the shared header and serialize `schema_version` from the first event.\n- [ ] Serialization tests show unknown ≠ zero ≠ empty string.\n- [ ] New records default to `internal` classification.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 5: \"unknown, invalid, disputed, stale, conditional and superseded are first-class.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.01.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §5.2 (closing paragraphs), §16.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"m","title":"W01.02 Shared record fields: revisions, history, three timestamps with clock quality, explicit unknowns"}	blake3:xhX_uSlHbt7env2Vpwt6wm_ILmjzeIG3RjoB3ggcYYM
1795320736115492	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-26w	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`--actor` selects an attributed actor; the default can come from workspace-local configuration or the established `AGENT` environment convention. \"Never interpret this as verified identity.\" Review 2 #8 added a self-reported descriptor so reviews can show who (or what) judged.\n\n# Goals\nAn actor value (e.g. `agent:investigator-1`) plus an optional descriptor: `kind` (`human` or `agent`), model family, model version and harness.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A new actor ID is attribution, not authentication and not proof of independence.\" The descriptor is a claim, like the actor string, but it enables two honest behaviours later: same-model-family reviews form one dependence group (W10.15), and calibration is reported per self-declared model family (W10.08).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Actor string grammar and validation; resolution order: explicit flag or envelope field, workspace-local config, `AGENT`.\n- Descriptor is optional everywhere and always labeled self-reported in output.\n- Envelope `actor` and `--actor`, if both present, must agree.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Actor resolution order is tested, including disagreement between flag and envelope.\n- [ ] Descriptor round-trips and is rendered with a \"self-reported\" label.\n- [ ] No code path treats actor inequality as independence or authorization.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps attribution useful without pretending it is identity.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.6, §9.2, §10.3, §14.2, Disposition: Reviews, retrospectives and learning (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"s","title":"W01.03 Actor attribution and the self-reported actor descriptor"}	blake3:yD7sAcCMdcHHeN3TijAyOEFvnq8WOqYn2dJ0ufGZSLs
1795320736210152	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-36c	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§10.1: \"UTF-8 only; reject duplicate JSON keys, non-finite values, and integers outside the interoperable safe range. Represent measured decimals, large counters and timestamps as documented strings (e.g., `{\\\"decimal\\\": \\\"18.70\\\", \\\"unit\\\": \\\"ms\\\"}`), which also preserves significant figures.\"\n\n# Goals\nOne strict parsing layer used by every input path (record, ingest manifest, capture, MCP), and a decimal value type for measurements.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nIdempotency compares parsed requests structurally across languages (W01.06). That is only sound if every producer's JSON parses to the same values, which floats and duplicate keys break. Decimal strings were adopted from review 1 #4 and remain worthwhile after hashing moved to stored bytes, because `18.70` and `18.7` report different precision.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Parser wrapper rejecting duplicate keys, invalid UTF-8, NaN/Infinity and integers beyond ±(2^53 − 1), with a JSON path in each error.\n- \"Unknown keys in our inputs are errors with paths; unknown fields from an external source can be preserved as uninterpreted source data instead of discarded.\"\n- `Decimal` type: validated string, unit, optional statistic and sample count, as in the §9.4 comparison example.\n- Bounded nesting depth and input size (limits defined in W01.05).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Fixtures for duplicate keys, non-finite numbers, oversized integers and invalid UTF-8 each return a bounded field error.\n- [ ] `\"18.70\"` survives a round trip byte for byte.\n- [ ] Unknown key in a versioned payload is rejected with its path.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA validated write contract is the alternative to \"parsing assistant prose to infer mutations\".\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.7, §9.3, §10.1, Disposition: Storage and durability (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"s","title":"W01.04 Strict JSON input rules and decimal-string measured values"}	blake3:4b_T3uZ1QCjpzG0FasPfhWv-l766FnAZ4vNiMM9-58Q
1795320736305021	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2cd	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Every mutation is one atomic transaction, including a one-operation transaction.\" The envelope carries `schema_version`, `command_id`, `case_id`, `actor`, `preconditions[]` and `operations[]`, each operation having `op`, optional `client_ref` and `payload`.\n\n# Goals\nThe typed envelope, its JSON Schema and its structural validation, drafted as `v0-alpha` and renamed `inv.command.v1` at AG.10.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n`client_ref` lets one batch create an object and use it (e.g. record a Retrospective and close the case in one call). It \"is never stored as durable identity\". The two reference forms — `{ \"client_ref\": \"…\" }` and `{ \"id\": \"…\", \"revision\": n }` — must be mutually exclusive in the schema so an agent cannot half-specify a reference.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Initial bounds: 128 operations, 512 KiB serialized input excluding artifact bytes, bounded nesting.\n- `case.create` has `case_id: null`; every case-scoped mutation requires one; envelope `case_id` and `--case` must agree.\n- Empty `operations` is `EMPTY_TRANSACTION` and \"does not count as progress\".\n- Reject unknown fields. \"No arbitrary JSON Patch or unrestricted 'set status' endpoint.\"\n- Accepted inputs cannot contain placeholder IDs such as `<returned-case-id>`.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Schema rejects a reference carrying both `client_ref` and `id`.\n- [ ] Over-limit operation count, size and nesting each return a specific error.\n- [ ] The §9.3 example validates once its illustrative ID is replaced by a real one.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe envelope is the single door through which every agent, producer and plugin changes canonical state.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.01, W01.03, W01.04.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.3, §9.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"m","title":"W01.05 Mutation envelope (inv.command, v0-alpha): operations, preconditions, client_ref, bounds"}	blake3:YKIyUopmpsmm12HLV85sS8RsoJYDVsoBmLw7Isc9Z5M
1795320736393978	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-28y	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`command_id` is \"an opaque caller-chosen 1–128-character identifier unique within `(workspace_id, actor)`. For retries, reuse it with identical semantic input.\" A reused key with different semantic input is `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED`.\n\n# Goals\nA precise, testable definition of \"identical semantic input\" and the comparison function, independent of storage (durable lookup is W02.10).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nHashes are over stored event bytes, so there is no canonical-JSON step; \"for idempotency, store the request and compare parsed values structurally.\" Getting this wrong either duplicates writes (too strict about formatting) or silently accepts a different request under an old key (too loose).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Semantic request = parsed envelope values, plus captured content digests for attachments in attachment order (§9.5). Input-file path and output-format options are not part of it.\n- Key order and insignificant whitespace do not matter; decimal strings compare as strings.\n- A `request_hash` may be stored as an optimization, but equality is decided structurally.\n- Cross-language fixtures: the same logical request serialized by different writers must compare equal; a changed byte in an attachment must not.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Reordered keys and changed whitespace compare equal; any changed value compares different.\n- [ ] Same path with changed bytes under one key is a mismatch.\n- [ ] Fixture set is reusable by W07.11 conformance.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInvariant: \"losing a response must not duplicate a committed update.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.05.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.3, §9.5, §10.1, §18.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"s","title":"W01.06 Idempotency semantics: command_id scope and structural request comparison"}	blake3:2gdi2lppwA-GYy_2Qu4zIc70jJsuBXSs2jnRuPzDMgk
1795320736484140	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-um1	{"description":"# Context & Background\nA committed write returns `ok`, `result: committed`, `command_id`, `transaction_id`, `snapshot {sequence, head_hash}`, `created[]` (with `client_ref`, `id`, `revision`), `updated[]`, `projection {index, vault}`, `warnings[]` and `next[]`.\n\n# Goals\nThe response type and schema for every successful command, read or write.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA receipt must distinguish three things that fail independently: canonical commit, index health and vault freshness. \"A retry returns the original transaction and created IDs with `result: already_committed`; current projection health may be reported separately.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `next[]` entries carry a `kind` (e.g. `local_read`) and an argv array using the real executable name and actual IDs.\n- Read responses carry `snapshot`, and layered reads add `basis_revision`, `freshness` and `depth` (W05a.08).\n- \"Hash placeholders in specification examples are notation, not accepted digest values. Production output returns real values.\"\n- Receipts must be reconstructible from canonical events (W02.10), so nothing in a receipt may exist only in process memory.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Schema published in the catalog; the §9.5 example shape validates with real values.\n- [ ] A post-commit vault failure is a warning on an `ok: true`, `committed` response, not an error.\n- [ ] `already_committed` returns byte-identical created IDs and revisions.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Accepted writes return durable receipts\" — the line agents are told to rely on.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.05.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.5, §10.2 (step 10) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"s","title":"W01.07 Success envelope and receipts (inv.response): committed, already_committed, projection health"}	blake3:fTqry66ODnlGpHc8OPbK0iFijUXYDPj5Bi7yeUJEbN8
1795320736583949	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1lq	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"An error should tell the agent what failed, what was committed, what remains true, and what to do next.\" The envelope has `ok: false`, `result: rejected`, `commit_status`, and `error {code, path, message, expected, actual, retry, repair {argv}}`.\n\n# Goals\nA closed set of error codes with documented meaning, exit-code mapping and a repair hint for each.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Never say 'not committed' unless established.\" If durability cannot be determined the response is `commit_status: unknown`, writes stop, and the caller is told to retry or look up using the *same* command ID. A vague or optimistic error here is how duplicated or lost writes happen.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Exit codes: 0 success or identical retry; 2 invalid input/schema/usage; 3 missing or ambiguous workspace/case/object/input; 4 revision conflict or idempotency-key mismatch; 5 domain transition obligation unsatisfied; 6 integrity failure; 7 IO/dependency failure or uncertain durability.\n- Seed codes named in the plan: `REVISION_CONFLICT`, `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED`, `EMPTY_TRANSACTION`, `CASE_REQUIRED`, `WORKSPACE_BUSY`, `BUDGET_TOO_SMALL`, `RETROSPECTIVE_REQUIRED`, `RETROSPECTIVE_STALE`.\n- Every error carries a JSON `path` where applicable and a `repair` that is a real argv or a named schema to inspect.\n- A failure after a durable commit \"must identify that commit if the process can still respond\".\n- No interactive prompt in machine mode; no generic \"something went wrong\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every code has an entry in the catalog with exit code, `commit_status` behaviour and one repair example.\n- [ ] Tests assert that each rejection leaves state unchanged.\n- [ ] The four example messages in §8.7 are reproducible from real conditions.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nRepairability is an acceptance measure in §20.5: \"Ordinary schema/CAS error resolved without bespoke intervention.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.07.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.7, §9.6 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"m","title":"W01.08 Error envelope, error-code taxonomy, exit codes 0–7 and commit_status semantics"}	blake3:gYvQ4XEwIGDzYjeyi5Hkql8NDzkjvmtjNOi5VnBBQuk
1795320736680537	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1h7	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"One operation catalog generates CLI help, JSON Schema, MCP tool schemas, minimal examples and field-level validation errors.\" The plan's Brenner Bot lesson and its \"discovery must not become a treasure hunt\" section both depend on this being a single source.\n\n# Goals\nA catalog data structure in `investigation-core` where each operation is declared once and everything else is derived.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nHand-maintained help, schemas and examples drift, and a drifting example teaches an agent to send invalid payloads. Generating from one catalog also makes CLI/MCP parity (W13.05) a property of the build rather than a test burden.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nEach entry declares: purpose and **when to use it**; required and optional inputs; mutation/IO effects; valid lifecycle states; one minimal valid example and one relevant error with its repair; outputs and IDs created; idempotency and revision rules; related next operations; and a workflow stage for `schema --list` grouping.\n- Payload types derive JSON Schema; \"avoid deeply nested `oneOf` unions\" where a flat form works, because the MCP default profile needs flat schemas.\n- Examples are stored as data and validated against their schema in tests; examples that need IDs are parameterized so demos fill in real ones.\n- \"A valid payload never requires the agent to invent unknown IDs, a successful oracle, fake confidence, or an execution receipt.\"\n- Reserved names for operations implemented later are listed as `deferred`, never as stubs that return success.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Adding an operation in one place makes it appear in schema output, help, examples and validation.\n- [ ] CI fails if any catalog example does not validate.\n- [ ] Deferred operations are discoverable as deferred and are rejected clearly if invoked.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes the write API self-teaching, which is the precondition for cold-start use.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.05, W01.08.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.2, §9.4, §18.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"l","title":"W01.09 Operation catalog framework: one source for schema, help, examples, validation and MCP tools"}	blake3:3xhZd4wHy8GvyCRFKcNs6w0f0XU2N-k4-5j7Ww3T9s0
1795320736786824	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-241	{"description":"# Context & Background\nA Case is a \"bounded inquiry or neutral recording session\"; a Question is \"something answerable whose resolution has value\". A workspace holds many cases and a case may hold many questions.\n\n# Goals\nTyped definitions for both, including lifecycle state machines.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nMode (`explain`, `measure`, `evaluate`, `explore`, `record`) is \"guidance, not a hard type system\": it shapes onboarding and advice but never changes record types, and a case may combine a causal investigation with a later decision. `explore` is the default so raw observations are welcome before framing is complete.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Case: title, mode, objective/question, scope, decision relevance, exclusions, stop condition, policy and method pins, lifecycle (`active`, `paused`, `closed`), closure outcome (`answered`, `chosen`, `inconclusive`, `abandoned`, `superseded`), optional `expectations` (expected answer or choice, expected effort, what would be surprising).\n- In `explore` at first capture, scope and stop condition may be explicitly unknown; `record` mode may be minimal.\n- Supported stop reasons from §6.6, with residual risks and reopening conditions.\n- Question: wording, context, scope, importance, parent, disposition (`open`, `answered`, `inconclusive`, `deferred`, `superseded`).\n- A `record`-mode case can later be amended into a full case without rewriting Run or Artifact IDs.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] State machines reject illegal transitions with a named blocker.\n- [ ] A minimal `record`-mode case and a minimal `explore` case both validate.\n- [ ] Mode change is representable only as an attributed amendment with reasons.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Cases\": explanatory, empirical, exploratory and evaluative questions without forcing an experiment lifecycle.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.02.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.3, §5.2, §6.2, §6.6, §8.6, §9.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"m","title":"W01.10 v0-alpha types: Case and Question, with modes, lifecycles, closure outcomes and expectations"}	blake3:qDlufZDEmvMmBsJ4olhJaLe50DJkkdKoHwwou63jbAM
1795320736881927	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ue	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"A benchmark report, cited design doc, and third-party assertion are sources; measurements and extracted statements are observations.\" An Artifact is \"bytes or explicit external reference\".\n\n# Goals\nTypes for the evidence layer and the versioned discriminated union of selectors that binds a claim to exact bytes.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe whole product rests on being able to walk from a conclusion to a precise selector on retained bytes. The selector union must be extensible (`external_selector`) so new source formats never require changing the core's public model.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Source: producer/publisher, acquisition, identity, time, access, verification limits; origin fields for later claim-level independence (W06b.05).\n- Artifact: digest, size, format, source, transforms, availability (`retained`, `external`, `partial`, `missing`, later `intentionally_removed`), sensitivity; original versus named derivative.\n- Observation: value/statement/units, source selector, extraction method, quality, limitations, `evidence_kind` (`observational` | `interventional`), variables changed.\n- Selector variants: `whole`, `byte_range` (half-open, zero-based), `text_lines` (one-based inclusive, on a named decoded-text derivative), `text_quote` (exact + optional prefix/suffix), `json_pointer` (RFC 6901), `table_cell`, `time_window`, `code_span`, `external_selector` (namespaced, schema URI/version). Each carries a validation state including `unavailable`.\n- Text-based Observations carry a positional selector **and** a `text_quote`; numeric and binary evidence mark the quote `not_applicable`.\n- Types only here; verification is W06a.04/W06a.05/W06b.01.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All nine selector variants serialize with a version tag and validation state.\n- [ ] An Observation without a selector must be an explicitly labeled assertion.\n- [ ] Availability states are distinct from integrity states.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInvariant: \"missing bytes remain missing\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.02.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2–§5.4, §5.6, §6.3, §11.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"m","title":"W01.11 v0-alpha types: Source, Artifact, Observation and the evidence selector union"}	blake3:SCvjNuq2s0lT7-FxyUrgcdtoeI81IjyWWCWCB9WYDbQ
1795320736989833	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-dcb	{"description":"# Context & Background\nA Hypothesis is a \"candidate factual explanation or claim\". An Assumption is an \"unestablished premise carrying a consequence\". An InquiryPlan is a \"planned inspection, experiment, benchmark or source check\". A Run is \"one recorded attempt/execution/inspection, including documentary or code searches\".\n\n# Goals\nTypes and lifecycles for the explanatory loop's planning and execution records.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nHypotheses \"can overlap; no enforced mutually exclusive/exhaustive probabilities\". A Run's execution result is \"independent of measurement validity\", so those are separate fields from the start; conflating them is how a tool timeout becomes \"the system timed out\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Hypothesis: statement, applicability, possible falsifiers, rivals/assumptions; lifecycle `proposed`, `active`, `rejected_within_scope`, `retired`. Evidentiary standing is derived from assessments, not stored as a flag.\n- Assumption: claim, status, materiality, affected hypotheses/comparisons/decisions.\n- InquiryPlan: purpose, method, controls, limits, desired observations; mode exploratory or confirmatory. Confirmatory adds a per-rival predicted-outcome table, evidence/holdout boundary, declared prior exposure and a stop/analysis rule. Lifecycle `draft`, `frozen`, `retired`.\n- Run: exact plan ref or exploration intent, producer, tool/environment, timings, result assertion, artifacts, execution status (`declared`, `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`, `blocked`, `unknown`), evidence completeness (`complete`, `partial`, `missing`, `unknown`), `evidence_kind`, variables changed, optional `search_coverage`.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two overlapping hypotheses on one question validate.\n- [ ] A Run can be `completed` with evidence `partial`, and `failed` with evidence `complete`.\n- [ ] A confirmatory plan type cannot be constructed without the per-rival table field present (possibly empty until freeze).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEncodes \"an invalid experiment is not a negative result\" in the type system.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.02.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §6.2, §6.3, §6.5 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"m","title":"W01.12 v0-alpha types: Hypothesis, Assumption, InquiryPlan and Run"}	blake3:qUdB2BNpSkCzncujJx0ury0hb8LycTan1WwB0fA7QQw
1795320737097345	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1vc	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"A hypothesis tries to explain a fact; an option is something someone could choose; a comparison evaluates an option on a criterion; ... a decision expresses a choice.\"\n\n# Goals\nTypes and lifecycles for the evaluative loop.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nKeeping these separate from the explanatory types is a binding decision. A hard criterion with an unknown result \"cannot be treated as satisfied, nor traded against arbitrary soft-score weights\", so `unknown` must be a first-class comparison outcome, not an absence.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Option: description, context, feasibility, dependencies, tradeoff notes; lifecycle `proposed`, `eligible`, `ruled_out`, `withdrawn`, `superseded`; a ruled-out option requires a reason and a criterion link.\n- Criterion: `hard | soft`, units/scale with declared scale anchors, preferred direction, limits/threshold, weighting rationale if any; lifecycle `draft`, `active`, `retired`.\n- Comparison: exact option and criterion revisions, outcome (`meets`, `fails`, `partially_meets`, `unknown`, `not_applicable`), value+unit or qualitative basis, evidence refs, assumption refs, method, limitations, uncertainty, rationale; lifecycle `draft`, `assessed`, `challenged`, `superseded`.\n- Decision: choice (which may be `defer`), compared alternatives, criteria set version, evidence, tradeoffs, consequences, reopen trigger, what the decider expects and when to check; lifecycle `proposed`, `accepted`, `superseded`; accepted content immutable.\n- Fields for the decision Question binding and applicability links (used by W04.05).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The §9.4 `comparison.add` example validates with real IDs.\n- [ ] A Decision of `defer` with reasons validates.\n- [ ] Subjective preferences are representable as preferences, distinct from measurements.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"A comparative case distinguishes options, hard constraints, measured attributes, subjective preferences, and unknowns.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.02.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §5.3, §5.5, §6.2, §6.3, §9.4 (comparison and decision contracts) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"m","title":"W01.13 v0-alpha types: Option, Criterion, Comparison and Decision"}	blake3:cjgAa1oj0s3PZK0s2Jc6x09ZwMkGLI_KvdlyivZ_oyo
1795320737198232	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-19g	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"An assessment interprets evidence; a conclusion reports what is warranted.\" A Review is an attributed review of a case, conclusion, comparison or decision. An Objection is an \"attributed, specific challenge\" whose resolution \"is its own attributed record, never a flag flip\".\n\n# Goals\nTypes and lifecycles for interpretation and challenge.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nRevision 4 fixed an internal contradiction by adding Objection as a record (reviews 2 #3 and 3 #2) and dropped the Review `draft` state: drafts live with the client. Confidence and likelihood are separate fields because ICD 203 keeps them separate and they must never appear in the same sentence.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Assessment: subject+revision, evidence refs, relation (`supports`, `contradicts`, `non_diagnostic`, `inconclusive`), per-rival expectation (`more_expected | equally_expected | less_expected | not_assessed`), rationale, assumptions, confidence/likelihood, `basis_digest`.\n- Conclusion: statement, significance, scope, support/contradictions, factor roles (`trigger`, `latent_condition`, `contributor`), review and effective disposition; lifecycle `candidate`, `review_ready`, `validated`, `rejected`, `retracted`, `superseded`.\n- Review: `kind` (`validation` | `blindspot`), exact subject and read-set, method, materials examined, source independence, objections/gaps, verdict (validation: `supports|objects|inconclusive`; blindspot: `gaps_identified|no_gap_identified_within_scope|inconclusive`), caveats, `context_isolation`, actor descriptor; lifecycle `submitted`, `superseded`.\n- Objection: exact subject revision, statement, scope, severity (`critical` | `material`), originating Review, evidence refs or explicit unknown, status `open | resolved | withdrawn`.\n- Confidence: optional `low | moderate | high | not_assessed` with rationale, limitations, what would change it, and named downgrade reasons each `ok | concern | not_assessed`. Likelihood: number in [0,1] or one of seven ICD 203 terms, allowed only with a stated resolution condition.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A blindspot Review type cannot carry a validation verdict.\n- [ ] Likelihood without a resolution condition is rejected.\n- [ ] Opposing Assessments of one subject by two actors both validate.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 9, no epistemic theater: attributed judgments instead of tool-invented scores.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.02, W01.03.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §5.7, §6.2, §6.3, §14.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"m","title":"W01.14 v0-alpha types: Assessment, Conclusion, Review and Objection, with confidence and likelihood fields"}	blake3:IHlFir0n4fZxNrk71lTT3oM6eiUVtARANuL3oEIY8mw
1795320737300825	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-8vi	{"description":"# Context & Background\nAn Annotation is a \"human/agent comment or imported note\" with \"no automatic evidentiary status\". A Retrospective is an \"attributed methodological account of an investigation\". Typed relations connect everything.\n\n# Goals\nThe remaining slice types, the edge vocabulary, and explicit reservations for what waits.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nRelations such as `supports` and `violates` are derived from attributed interpretive records; storing them as bare booleans would let \"multiple agents... overwrite each other\". W01's row says CoveragePlan, authored synopses and pack extensions wait, but their names must be reserved so the v1 freeze does not collide with them.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Annotation: subject refs, author claim, content/hash, classification, optional `role` (reserved values `synopsis` and `advice_feedback`, implemented in W05b.08 and W05b.04).\n- Retrospective: `case_ref`, `snapshot`, `methods_attempted[]`, `effective_steps[]`, `invalid_or_misleading_steps[]`, `costs_or_constraints`, `residual_unknowns[]`, `lessons[]` (guidance, applies-when, supporting refs, transfer limits), `none_observed_reason`; lifecycle `recorded`, `superseded`.\n- Relations: `addresses`, `alternative_to`, `candidate_for`, `evaluated_by`, `compares`, `satisfies`, `violates`, `depends_on_assumption`, `derived_from`, `observed_in`, `tests`, `supports`, `contradicts`, `non_diagnostic_for`, `reviews`, `supersedes`, `retracts`, `informs_decision`, `external_reference`.\n- External work-item references are typed strings/URLs with read-only provenance only (§3.4).\n- Reserve: `coverage.*`, synopsis role, `extensions.<namespace>`, `measurement.assess`, `source.assess`.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The §9.4 retrospective example validates with real IDs.\n- [ ] Interpretive edges cannot be created without an attributing record.\n- [ ] Reserved names appear in the catalog as deferred.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Retrospectives\": attributed, case-scoped, never silently installed as future rules.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.02.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §5.2, §5.5, §8.6, §9.4 (blindspot and retrospective contract), §19.2 (W01) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"m","title":"W01.15 v0-alpha types: Annotation and Retrospective; typed relation vocabulary; reserved names"}	blake3:0VKslbUNQ6JZG7Qfsddtb0ho-f9tpuva0xZWTEPZiHs
1795320737388436	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-235	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Validate all operations against a temporary in-memory state, in array order, before committing any.\" §18.3 sketches `CommandHandler::validate_and_apply(&State, Command) -> Result<AcceptedTransaction, DomainError>`.\n\n# Goals\nThe pure domain function that turns a command plus current state into an accepted transaction or a precise rejection, with no IO.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nKeeping validation pure lets the Investigation lane build and test handlers without the durable store, and lets the store call it under the lock against current state. \"Do not let callers prepare an `AcceptedTransaction` from a stale snapshot and commit without CAS.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Apply operations in order to a scratch copy of state; resolve each `client_ref` to the ID assigned earlier in the same batch; assign real IDs exactly once.\n- Check preconditions (expected revisions) and references; produce `resolved_ids` and `resulting_revisions`.\n- Promotion gates are evaluated against the **final** state of the batch, so a batch cannot validate a claim and then add a disqualifying blocker to it (hook for W04.05).\n- A malformed operation rejects the whole batch with the failing index in the error path.\n- Operation handlers register through the catalog (W01.09); this bone delivers the engine and a trivial handler, not the handlers themselves.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A batch with one bad operation commits nothing and names `operations[i]`.\n- [ ] `client_ref` forward use works; a dangling or duplicate `client_ref` is rejected.\n- [ ] The engine has no filesystem or clock dependency except through injected seams.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nAtomic, all-or-nothing writes are what let an agent trust a receipt.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.09, W01.10.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.3, §10.2 (step 5), §10.3, §18.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"m","title":"W01.16 Transaction validation engine: ordered operations against temporary state, IDs assigned once"}	blake3:re9lUZxKcvXo7V8Bymtrf8cyNUTLiRd95Svjnsn9fTM
1795320737486921	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-cjs	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW01's acceptance is \"Independently compiling core, schema-valid examples and a minimal handoff fixture.\" §18.2 asks to \"abstract time, filesystem publication boundaries and ID generation sufficiently to inject crash tests, not to create an abstract application framework.\"\n\n# Goals\nClose out W01 with the seams and artifacts that later packages build on.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nDeterministic clocks and IDs make crash-test seeds replayable and fixtures stable. A handoff fixture this early forces the question the whole product exists to answer — what does a successor need? — before the catalog hardens.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `Clock` and `IdSource` traits with real and deterministic implementations; nothing else is abstracted.\n- One example per v0-alpha operation, generated from the catalog and validated in CI.\n- Minimal handoff fixture: a hand-written transaction sequence for a tiny case (question, source, observation, hypothesis, assessment, candidate conclusion) plus the expected content a successor should be able to recover from it. It is consumed by W05a.09 (`brief`) and MA.01.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `investigation-core` compiles and tests with no other workspace crate.\n- [ ] Every catalog example validates.\n- [ ] The handoff fixture replays through the validation engine without errors.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nFixes the first-slice semantics so the parallel lanes of §19.4 can start.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.09, W01.14, W01.15.\nParent goal: W01. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §18.2, §19.2 (W01) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w01","milestone-a","lane-investigation","fixture","test"],"parent":"bn-u88","size":"s","title":"W01.17 Injectable clock and ID seams, schema-valid example set and the minimal handoff fixture"}	blake3:p5vELn3Y17MlsUY7A9duLBiTBoSIyZIy_P4AZQFOidY
1795320845943929	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3j3	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Put the commit protocol behind a small storage trait. Test it against an in-memory fake that drops un-fsynced file data and un-fsynced directory entries at any step and injects I/O errors, driven by property tests with replayable seeds.\"\n\n# Goals\nA narrow trait covering exactly the filesystem operations the commit protocol uses, a real implementation, and a fake that models what a crash can lose.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nHand-rolling a journal (ADR W00.04) is defensible only with this testing. Kill-the-process tests cannot reach \"power lost after write, before fsync\"; a fake that distinguishes written from durable data can, deterministically and fast.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Operations: create temp, write, fsync file, hard link, rename-noreplace, fsync directory, exclusive create, read, list, lock, unlink.\n- Fake model: each file has written and durable content; each directory has written and durable entries. `crash()` discards everything not durable. Any call can be scripted to fail with an injected error, including fsync.\n- Deterministic: driven by a seed; a failing seed reproduces exactly.\n- Abstract only publication boundaries, \"not to create an abstract application framework\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The fake loses a file whose data was fsynced but whose directory entry was not.\n- [ ] An injected fsync error is observable by the caller.\n- [ ] The real implementation and the fake pass one shared behavioural test suite for the non-crash paths.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the instrument that makes the durability claims testable instead of asserted.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.04, W00.05.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.2 (Crash-testing method), §18.2, §18.3, Disposition: Storage and durability (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage","test"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"m","title":"W02.01 Storage trait and a fault-injecting in-memory filesystem fake"}	blake3:2GqZjfyUVDqeQroN2_UzA_kcf-ayLwTysGxfNNGw_MI
1795320846011661	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-37u	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§4.2 fixes the layout: `investigation.toml`; `state/` (canonical: `workspace.json`, `events/`, `blobs/sha256/<prefix>/<digest>`, `checkpoints/`, `staging/<run-id>/`, `locks/commit.lock`, `tmp/`); `cache/` (rebuildable); `vault/` (projection, with human-owned `Notes/ Boards/ Inbox/`); `exports/`; `local/` (machine-specific, noncanonical).\n\n# Goals\nLibrary support for creating and opening a workspace, including the immutable genesis record that identifies the workspace and schema family.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe vault is a *child* directory \"so Obsidian Sync/ordinary Git behavior does not silently publish the canonical raw journal or private captures\". Network filesystems are refused because `RENAME_NOREPLACE` returns `EINVAL` on NFS and some FUSE servers and locking is unreliable there.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Create directories with restrictive permissions where supported, \"without implying protection against arbitrary same-user processes\".\n- Write genesis; the first transaction follows it. Layout details that depend on chain scope follow ADR W00.05.\n- Detect filesystem type at `init` (and expose the check for `doctor`); refuse network and known-unsafe FUSE mounts with a clear error.\n- Workspace discovery: ascend from CWD to the nearest `investigation.toml`; never scan the home directory.\n- `init` \"never installs plugins, modifies a harness, contacts a service, or auto-registers a model\".\n- Keep a path ownership manifest location under `local/` for the renderer (W08.02).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A fresh workspace opens, verifies genesis and reports sequence 0.\n- [ ] Init on a refused filesystem type fails without leaving partial directories (tested via an injectable detector).\n- [ ] `state/`, `cache/`, `local/` and `vault/` roles are documented in the generated `investigation.toml` comments.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Local use does not require a model, server, Obsidian, Internet, or another tool.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.05, W02.01.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2, §9.1 (init), §10.1, §10.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"m","title":"W02.02 Workspace layout, genesis record and init core, refusing network filesystems"}	blake3:h8Dl9uzVmBfdQlIo5k48-og_5garJKoo9k10qJaKidA
1795320846080802	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-6o2	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"A short advisory lock serializes commits across local processes: `flock` semantics via `std::fs::File::lock` (stable since Rust 1.89) on a dedicated lock file that is never renamed or deleted. Never use POSIX record locks (`fcntl(F_SETLK)`), which are released when any descriptor for the file closes.\"\n\n# Goals\nA lock guard with bounded wait that returns retryable `WORKSPACE_BUSY` when unavailable.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nOne serialized writer makes cross-record transactions straightforward. The `fcntl` trap is subtle: any library that opens and closes the lock file elsewhere in the process would silently release the lock.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `state/locks/commit.lock` created at init and never replaced.\n- Acquire with bounded wait and backoff; expose wait time in diagnostics.\n- Hold only for steps 3–8 of the commit algorithm. \"Case reads and file ingestion need not hold the lock while doing expensive work\", and it is never held while an external process runs.\n- Document what it is not: \"The lock provides local serialization, not authorization between users, a guarantee against hostile same-user processes, or distributed coordination.\"\n- A separate renderer lock (W08.01) and per-handle capture serialization (W07.04) are different locks.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two processes contending produce one holder and one `WORKSPACE_BUSY` after the bound.\n- [ ] Opening and closing another descriptor on the lock file in the holder does not release the lock.\n- [ ] A crashed holder's lock is released by the OS and the next writer proceeds.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Collaboration\": multi-process local writer lock, CAS and idempotency.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.01.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.1, §10.2 (step 2), §10.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"s","title":"W02.03 Workspace commit lock using File::lock on a dedicated, never-renamed lock file"}	blake3:5WI-PAvz313RdfTOpoo5_nsvCBvJs6nhMm396IyPG_s
1795320846148393	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-45i	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEach immutable transaction file contains: `format_version, workspace_id, transaction_id, sequence, previous_hash, actor, recorded_at, command_id, request_hash, preconditions, accepted_operations, resolved_ids, resulting_revisions`. It is named `events/<seq>.json` with no hash in the name.\n\n# Goals\nSerialization and parsing of event files and computation and verification of the chain.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A transaction's hash is SHA-256 of the exact bytes of its event file\", checkable with `sha256sum`. The file does not contain its own hash; the hash lives in the next event's `previous_hash`, in the receipt and in the index, and \"the head hash is computed on read\". Immutable bytes are already canonical, so there is no canonical-JSON step (review 2 #9 superseded review 1's float-canonicalization fixes). Sequence-only names let exclusive create enforce one event per sequence \"as a second line of defense behind the lock\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Zero-padded sequence filenames (`000000000001.json`).\n- `schema_version` on every event from event 1.\n- Ordering comes from the sequence number alone; UUIDv7 and wall-clock times never order events.\n- Imported and finalized-run manifests are immutable Artifact bytes referenced by events; `cache/imports/` copies are rebuildable conveniences.\n- A finalization manifest lists its covered artifacts and pre-finalization snapshot and excludes itself and its own transaction hash, to avoid recursive hashing.\n- Never normalize or rewrite artifact bytes.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `sha256sum events/N.json` equals `previous_hash` in event N+1 and the receipt's `head_hash` for N.\n- [ ] Parsing rejects an event whose internal `sequence` disagrees with its filename.\n- [ ] Round-trip preserves bytes exactly.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 8, inspectability: \"exact source revisions, evidence selectors, immutable accepted history, explicit audit receipts.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.04, W00.05, W01.05.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.1, §4.2, Disposition: Storage and durability (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"m","title":"W02.04 Event file format and hash chain: sequence-only names, SHA-256 over stored bytes"}	blake3:h8xDZI60h2TaBwH3N68J0TYYkDIFpDPsYdh7dkQkPew
1795320846216894	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ur	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPublication is: write the whole file to a temp file on the same filesystem, fsync it, publish with `link(2)` or `renameat2(RENAME_NOREPLACE)` — \"never plain `rename(2)`\" — then fsync the directory. \"An existing target is a fork and an integrity failure, never an overwrite.\"\n\n# Goals\nOne audited primitive used for both events and blobs.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Any fsync error is fatal. Do not retry it and do not report success: after a failed fsync, ext4 and XFS can mark pages clean that never reached disk, so a retried fsync may falsely succeed.\" Do not rely on `tempfile`'s `persist_noclobber`, \"which can fall back to a non-atomic link-and-unlink and syncs nothing\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Implement over the storage trait (W02.01) so the fake can crash between every step.\n- On fsync failure: return an error that maps to `commit_status: unknown`, set a process-wide \"stop writing\" state, and require tail re-verification on next start (W02.12).\n- On `EEXIST` for an event: integrity failure (fork). On `EEXIST` for a blob: verify existing bytes match the digest (W02.06).\n- Temp files live in `state/tmp/` on the same filesystem; orphans are cleaned only after a grace period.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] With the fake, a crash at each step leaves either no target or a complete durable target — never a partial one.\n- [ ] An injected fsync error is never retried into success and blocks further writes.\n- [ ] Publishing over an existing event sequence fails without modifying it.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n§20.2: \"An injected fsync error stops writes with `commit_status: unknown` and is never retried into a success.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.01, W00.03.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.2 (steps 6–7), §10.4, Disposition: Storage and durability (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"m","title":"W02.05 Durable exclusive-create publish primitive; any fsync error is fatal"}	blake3:Mf0D331w4YLYta_ez1zhYuH_bXRImPbyu38gV_8LDxM
1795320846286056	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-c67	{"description":"# Context & Background\nArtifacts are immutable bytes stored at `state/blobs/sha256/<prefix>/<digest>`. \"Blobs are durable before the event that references them. Missing staged blobs prevent commit.\"\n\n# Goals\nStream bytes in with bounded memory, compute SHA-256 and size, and publish immutably.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nBlob bytes deduplicate; acquisition and observation identities never do (W06b.03). A crash after blob publication but before the event leaves an unreferenced immutable blob — harmless, and reusable by the retry.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Stream to a private temp file while hashing; publish with the W02.05 primitive.\n- On `EEXIST`, verify the existing bytes match the digest rather than trusting the name.\n- \"Prevent writable hardlinks into canonical blobs\": published blobs are read-only and never linked from a producer-writable path.\n- Read API returns bytes plus a verification result; a digest mismatch on read is an integrity failure, not a silent fallback.\n- No garbage collection here. Reclamation is conservative and policy-driven (W11.11); \"Never silently GC a referenced artifact.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A multi-gigabyte synthetic stream ingests with bounded memory.\n- [ ] Publishing identical bytes twice yields one blob and no error.\n- [ ] A corrupted blob is detected on read and by `check`.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"A digest never describes different retained bytes.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.05.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2, §10.1, §10.2 (step 6), §10.4, §11.1, §11.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"m","title":"W02.06 Content-addressed blob store with streaming hash and verified publication"}	blake3:c8SZKdi5YSqOwlyASfj8Pm3a7uG3zhQ6ONAEyaflXc8
1795320846356411	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-yxm	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§10.2 specifies ten steps. This bone assembles them into the `Journal::commit` path that every mutation, ingest and capture uses.\n\n# Goals\nA single commit path with a clearly defined commit boundary: the successful durable publication of the event file.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Never validate against a stale index.\" Validation happens against state brought up to the verified tail, under the lock. Everything after the durable event (index update, vault render) is derived and may fail without un-committing.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n1. Parse and bound-check the envelope outside the lock; for ingestion, stream bytes to private temp files and compute digests first.\n2. Acquire the commit lock (bounded; `WORKSPACE_BUSY`).\n3. Find the last committed transaction; bring state through it, verifying the tail.\n4. Look up `(workspace_id, actor, command_id)`: identical request returns the original receipt; different request is rejected.\n5. Validate revisions, required read-set, references and every operation against temporary state; assign IDs once.\n6. Publish needed blobs (durable first).\n7. Write, fsync and exclusively publish the event; fsync the directory. Fsync error is fatal.\n8. Update the disposable index in one SQLite transaction with its applied sequence/head.\n9. Release the lock; render derived views synchronously or mark them pending.\n10. Return a receipt distinguishing canonical commit, index health and vault freshness.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A commit is observable by a second process only after step 7.\n- [ ] Index failure after step 7 yields a committed receipt with degraded `projection.index`.\n- [ ] The lock is not held during step 1 or step 9 rendering.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the mechanism behind every receipt an agent is told to trust.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16, W02.03, W02.04, W02.05, W02.06.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.2, §18.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"l","title":"W02.07 Commit algorithm end to end: validate under lock, publish event, update index, return receipt"}	blake3:fKlm0ZtBBX3RM2ax2q55iSX9Ol6PyPJ-aCBkg-K3yZo
1795320846424212	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1yb	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"No two committed files may share one sequence. Duplicate sequences, gaps, unexpected parents, unknown required event versions, and digest mismatch are explicit integrity failures, never sorted away.\"\n\n# Goals\nDeterministic replay of the journal into in-memory state, and a verifier that reports each kind of damage precisely.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A journal fork or missing canonical artifact is not fixed by pretending it never existed.\" The failure must be diagnosable without an invented repair, and reads that need current state must fail rather than quietly serve stale conclusions.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Replay from genesis (or a verified checkpoint) applying accepted operations; the result is the same state the validation engine would have produced.\n- Tail verification is cheap enough to run on every commit (step 3).\n- Diagnostics carry `severity`, `code`, `object`, `reason` and structured repair options.\n- Distinguish: structurally valid, bytes verified, externally referenced/unavailable — the states `check` reports (W02.14).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Fixtures for missing event, corrupt event, duplicate sequence, mismatched parent hash and future required schema each produce a distinct diagnostic and exit code 6.\n- [ ] Replay of the same journal twice yields identical state.\n- [ ] No fixture is \"repaired\" automatically.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA chain \"detects internal inconsistencies relative to a known head\"; this is the detector.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.04.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.1, §10.6, §9.6, §20.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"m","title":"W02.08 Journal replay, tail verification and integrity-failure diagnosis"}	blake3:5YD1YMwYftyw91lwMG18qiWwBiGuiKgWTiXXBWRu-SE
1795320846490229	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2bs	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Use SQLite for object projections, typed edges, revision dependencies, idempotency lookup, full-text search, generic import cursors, and derived recommendation indexes. Store database/schema/applied-head metadata.\"\n\n# Goals\n`cache/index.sqlite` as a disposable projection that is always either current, advertised as stale, or rebuilt.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Rebuilding the database from the journal and blobs must restore the same accepted state and command receipts.\" The index is never canonical and never synchronized as such. \"Do not let a read transparently return old conclusions after a failed rebuild: advertise the snapshot and health, or fail if correctness requires current state.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Tables: objects (per type, current and historical revisions), typed edges, revision dependency index (subject → dependents with materiality), command receipts, applied-head metadata. FTS tables are added by W05b.07.\n- Apply each committed transaction in one SQLite transaction recording applied sequence and head hash.\n- On open: compare applied head with the journal tail; replay the gap or rebuild.\n- WAL mode is acceptable for a local derived index; set busy timeouts.\n- Rebuild is a full replay into a fresh database swapped in atomically.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Delete the index, rebuild, and compare: identical accepted state and receipts.\n- [ ] A deliberately stale index is detected and never used for validation.\n- [ ] Every read response carries the snapshot it was served from.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Canonical storage\": append-only events plus immutable artifacts; rebuildable SQLite index.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.08, W01.15.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.5, §10.2 (step 8), §20.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"l","title":"W02.09 SQLite derived index: projections, typed edges, revision dependencies, rebuild from journal"}	blake3:wvtRjmubC6mF8-z9o-F5x7u4aflF07MnNcpKpozCkqs
1795320846561324	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-qet	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Acknowledgments and created IDs are reconstructible from canonical events. The idempotency index cannot be solely a cache that forgets after restart.\"\n\n# Goals\nLookup of `(workspace_id, actor, command_id)` that survives restart, index deletion and rebuild, returning the original receipt.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAn agent that loses a response has only its command ID. If the mapping lived only in a cache, a rebuild would turn a safe retry into a duplicate write — the exact Brenner Bot failure the plan exists to avoid.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Each event stores `command_id` and the request (or `request_hash` plus enough to compare structurally per W01.06).\n- The index table is derived from events during replay.\n- Identical request → original receipt with `result: already_committed`. Different request → `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED`, exit 4.\n- Backend for `receipt KEY`: returns original IDs, revisions, snapshot and artifact digests from canonical-derived state; an absent key is `not_found_at_snapshot` \"because the request might still be in flight\"; an unverifiable head yields `unknown` with a repair path.\n- Scope follows ADR W00.05.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Lose the response after commit; identical retry returns the original IDs exactly once.\n- [ ] Same command ID with changed payload is rejected, including after index rebuild.\n- [ ] Receipt lookup works without the original request or bytes.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInvariant: \"losing a response must not duplicate a committed update.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.06, W02.07, W02.09.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.3, §9.5, §10.2 (step 4), §10.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"m","title":"W02.10 Durable idempotency: canonical-derived receipt lookup and reconstruction"}	blake3:FPbP4_a3NNqhlYh3SrPjtur6M9Hx-bZBTHDKGwoABG0
1795320846627515	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3ra	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Independent additions usually need no global snapshot precondition. Editing a substantive entity requires its expected revision.\"\n\n# Goals\nOptimistic concurrency at the entity level, with conflict responses an agent can act on.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"No automatic 'last agent wins.'\" A stale mutation \"returns current revisions and a bounded diff; only an identical idempotent retry is automatic.\" The repair guidance is to read and reassess, not to replay: \"do not blindly replay the old validation.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Preconditions are `{id, revision}` pairs checked under the lock against current state.\n- `REVISION_CONFLICT` carries `path`, `expected`, `actual`, `retry: reassess` and a `repair` argv that shows the current object.\n- Bounded diff: which fields changed between the expected and current revision, without dumping content.\n- Additions with no precondition proceed concurrently. The richer *required read-set* for promotions is W03.11; set membership is W04.05.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two competing amendments: one commits, one gets a conflict naming both revisions.\n- [ ] Concurrent independent additions both succeed.\n- [ ] The conflict response commits nothing and exits 4.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMultiple agents can share one durable record without clobbering each other.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.07.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.6, §10.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"s","title":"W02.11 Revision preconditions and CAS conflict responses with current revisions and a bounded diff"}	blake3:1UamAG6T6dYqOSrnEJtTXc_eGl-Y9UiNv_T6qoE_QK0
1795320846697931	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-348	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe crash matrix lists what can remain after each crash point: orphan temp files, unreferenced blobs, an event of uncertain durability, a committed event with a stale index.\n\n# Goals\nA defined start-up path that restores a trustworthy state before accepting commands.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAfter an fsync failure the process must \"on the next start re-read the tail from disk and re-verify the chain before accepting commands\". After \"event publication but uncertain directory flush\", the same command ID resolves the ambiguity; \"do not create a new command ID.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- On open: verify the tail, replay any committed-but-unindexed transactions, and report recovered state in diagnostics.\n- Temp files: remove orphans only \"after an explicit grace period\".\n- Unreferenced blobs are left alone here; only conservative, policy-driven GC may reclaim them later (W11.11).\n- Never treat `state/staging/` as disposable (it is durable capture recovery material).\n- Surface a \"writes stopped\" marker left by a fatal fsync so the cause is visible.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] After each crash point in §10.4 (via the fake), restart yields a verified head and a correct answer to \"was command K committed?\".\n- [ ] No staging directory or unreferenced blob is removed by recovery.\n- [ ] A stale index is replayed forward, not trusted.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nHonest recovery is what makes `commit_status: unknown` a resolvable state rather than a dead end.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.07, W02.08.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.2 (fsync rule), §10.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"s","title":"W02.12 Startup recovery: tail re-verification after uncertain commits and orphan cleanup"}	blake3:PSM1cID9QuLBNzTBvqzMWw1K9zjXJ-7CBhtaB7TPQAM
1795320846769323	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-w6q	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Put `schema_version` on every event from event 1. Because the chain forbids rewriting history, schema evolution is upcast-on-read.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe mechanism by which old events are read by newer code, and by which unknown future versions are refused safely.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe `v0-alpha` catalog is expected to change at the A-gate, so the upcast path is exercised early by design. \"Unknown future required versions stop writes. No silent destructive migration on ordinary `brief`.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- A versioned reader per event/operation schema with pure upcast functions to the current in-memory form; original bytes are never rewritten.\n- Unknown *optional* source metadata is \"retained opaquely rather than invented or discarded\".\n- Unknown *required* version: reads that can proceed do so with a clear degraded label; writes stop with an explicit failure naming the version.\n- Keep a small set of historical fixtures from the first committed format onward; the full migration framework is W11.15.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A journal written by an earlier schema version replays to the same logical state.\n- [ ] A journal containing a future required version refuses writes and reports exit 6 with the version.\n- [ ] Event bytes on disk are unchanged by any read.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n§3.6 compatibility promise: \"explicit failures for unsupported required versions.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.08.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.6, §10.1, §10.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"s","title":"W02.13 Schema versioning: upcast on read, unknown required versions stop writes"}	blake3:pPXFGwEVWO9NDoxozZZw_1Le7SARkdgzSl5wZUTt-K4
1795320846846433	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2r9	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`check` reports distinct states: structurally valid, bytes verified, externally referenced/unavailable, source-authenticated, and independently anchored. It never compresses these into 'tamper-proof.'\"\n\n# Goals\nA library `check` that verifies journal, blobs and index, and a targeted `repair index`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Hashes establish equality with a recorded digest... Someone controlling the whole workspace can rewrite it.\" Reporting the states separately keeps the tool from overclaiming. `check` \"does not repair automatically\"; `repair index` \"is safe because the index is disposable\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Checks: chain continuity and hashes; every referenced blob present and matching; index applied head equals journal head; staging handles consistent.\n- Each issue has `severity`, `code`, `object`, `reason` and structured repair options.\n- `repair index` rebuilds the derived database; no other repair is automatic.\n- Leave explicit, unimplemented-but-named states for `source-authenticated` and `independently anchored` (future signed checkpoints must identify key, covered snapshot and trust policy).\n- CLI wiring is trivial and lands with W05a.01.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A clean workspace reports each state separately and never the word \"tamper-proof\".\n- [ ] A missing blob is reported as missing, with the dependent records listed.\n- [ ] `repair index` restores a deleted index; `check` then passes.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTruthful integrity reporting is part of \"epistemically honest\" output.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.08, W02.09, W02.06.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.1, §9.6, §10.6 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"m","title":"W02.14 check engine with distinct integrity states, and repair index"}	blake3:mYKh4AUNJAe-o0xeGa7d81QzheQlsn63ISfMEI0i210
1795320846919674	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-31f	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§10.4 lists eight crash points with their durable result and recovery. §20.2 requires: \"Kill the writer at every publication boundary in §10.4 and recover honestly.\"\n\n# Goals\nA property-test suite that crashes the commit protocol at every step and asserts the recovery invariants.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nADR W00.04 obliges this. The invariants are the product's durability contract; if they are not tested exhaustively they are aspirations.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nGenerate random command sequences and crash/error schedules against the fake (W02.01). After each simulated crash and restart, assert:\n- No committed event is lost; no uncommitted event appears.\n- Every event references only durable blobs.\n- Retrying the same command ID returns the original IDs exactly once, or commits once if it had not committed.\n- A batch is all-or-nothing (including a Retrospective plus `case.close` batch, once those handlers exist).\n- An injected fsync error never yields a success response.\n- The rebuilt index equals a full replay.\nFailing seeds are printed and committed as regression cases.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each row of the §10.4 matrix that involves the store has at least one targeted test plus coverage by the randomized suite.\n- [ ] The suite runs in CI with a fixed seed set and a time-boxed random budget.\n- [ ] A deliberately broken publish order (event before blob) is caught.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nW02's acceptance: \"Deterministic replay + fault-injection suite passes.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.07, W02.10, W02.12.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.2 (Crash-testing method), §10.4, §20.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage","test"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"m","title":"W02.15 Crash-matrix property tests with replayable seeds"}	blake3:XS7WhmDpDxaDTwBRA4THJ358TRSttwuuxQtdKJE0m_c
1795320846993734	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-30t	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Run one CI job against a real filesystem under LazyFS.\" LazyFS is a FUSE filesystem that keeps un-fsynced data in a cache it can drop on command, simulating power loss on a real code path.\n\n# Goals\nOne CI job that runs the real storage implementation against LazyFS and checks the same invariants as W02.15.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe in-memory fake is only as good as its model. A real-filesystem job catches a fake that is too kind — for example a missing directory fsync that the fake happened not to model in one path.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Verify current LazyFS setup and fault commands against its documentation at implementation time; do not rely on recollection.\n- Scripted scenarios: clear cache after event write before fsync; after fsync before directory fsync; after blob publish before event.\n- Note the tension with W02.02: network and some FUSE filesystems are refused at `init`. The job needs an explicit, test-only override; it must not weaken the production refusal.\n- Keep the job small and non-flaky; record the kernel/FUSE versions it ran on in the support matrix (W00.09).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The job fails when a required fsync is removed from the publish primitive.\n- [ ] It passes on the real implementation.\n- [ ] The override used is impossible to enable accidentally in normal operation.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTurns \"documented tested filesystem guarantees\" into something a reader can check.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.15.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.2 (Crash-testing method), Disposition: Storage and durability (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage","test"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"m","title":"W02.16 Real-filesystem crash job under LazyFS in CI"}	blake3:prFRPip_TBN0wJX3DWlzWv_sHOS13udfzUSq9XJ4Ey8
1795320847067654	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2c7	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Multiple agents may append independent observations/assessments concurrently. Substantive updates use revisions.\"\n\n# Goals\nTests with real OS processes that exercise the lock, CAS and idempotency together.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nIn-process tests cannot show that the advisory lock actually serializes separate processes, which is the deployment the plan targets (several local agents sharing one workspace).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- N processes add independent records concurrently: all succeed, sequences are gap-free, chain verifies.\n- Two processes amend the same record from the same base revision: exactly one commits, the other receives `REVISION_CONFLICT`.\n- A process holding the lock past the bound makes others return `WORKSPACE_BUSY` (retryable), never corrupt state.\n- A reader during writes always sees a consistent snapshot and reports which one.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] §20.2: \"Concurrent independent additions succeed; competing substantive updates produce a clear CAS conflict.\"\n- [ ] No test relies on sleeps for correctness.\n- [ ] Tests run in CI without flaking across 100 iterations.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nLocal multi-agent work on one durable record is a first-class use.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.11.\nParent goal: W02. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.3, §14.1, §20.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w02","milestone-a","lane-storage","test"],"parent":"bn-eks","size":"s","title":"W02.17 Multi-process concurrency tests: concurrent additions, competing updates, busy lock"}	blake3:KEwG6D-7YmDTZBnOtXsqYbYCMMx8FR5noQsZoy7e5TQ
1795321014040532	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-syf	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`case.create`, `case.amend`, `case.pause`, `case.reopen`, `question.create` and `question.resolve` open and shape an inquiry. (`case.close` has its own gate and lives in W03.13.)\n\n# Goals\nHandlers and lifecycle rules for these operations, registered in the catalog with examples and repairs.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Creation of a Case, Observation, Source or Annotation must be cheap.\" Design principle 1 is evidence-first: \"do not block preservation because the question is unfinished.\" So `explore` is the default mode, and scope and stop condition may be explicitly unknown at first capture.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `case.create`: title, objective, mode (default `explore`), question/decision context, scope, stop condition, optional `expectations`; empty exclusions are fine; `record` mode may be minimal.\n- `case.amend`/`pause`/`reopen`: CAS revision and an attributed reason; mode change is explicit and never changes record types or IDs.\n- `question.create`: scoped answerable wording and consequence. `question.resolve`: an evidence-backed answer or an explicit inconclusive state.\n- A producer-created `record`-mode case can be amended into a full case without rewriting Run or Artifact IDs.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A case can be created with only a title and question in `explore` mode.\n- [ ] Reopen requires a reason; pause and reopen preserve history.\n- [ ] `question.resolve` without evidence refs must be explicitly inconclusive.\n- [ ] Each operation has a catalog example that runs against a fresh workspace.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPlan question 1: \"What is the precise question, and why would an answer matter?\" — without making the answer a prerequisite for saving evidence.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.3, §6.2, §6.6, §9.4 (case.*, question.*) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.01 Case and question operations: create, amend, pause, reopen, resolve"}	blake3:B-2dOx5Q62pYhc0fyqjkOto9YohIZKEZ8vGNTqVK4Ms
1795321014135438	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3gc	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`hypothesis.create/amend/disposition` and `assumption.create/assess` record candidate factual explanations and the unestablished premises that conclusions lean on.\n\n# Goals\nHandlers, lifecycles and catalog entries for both record types.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Hypotheses can overlap; no enforced mutually exclusive/exhaustive probabilities.\" A hypothesis's standing \"comes from assessments\", so `hypothesis.disposition` records a reasoned lifecycle move (`rejected_within_scope`, `retired`), not a truth value. Assumptions carry materiality so a fragile conclusion can be found mechanically later (W10.13).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `hypothesis.create`: factual explanatory candidate, scope, question, rival/falsifier if known. Rivals are linked with `alternative_to`.\n- `hypothesis.amend`: CAS; substantive changes bump the basis that dependent assessments cite.\n- `hypothesis.disposition`: CAS plus source-based rationale for standing; rejection is always \"within scope\".\n- `assumption.create`: premise, materiality, affected subject links (`depends_on_assumption`).\n- `assumption.assess`: disposition with evidence or reasons; a changed assessment of a material assumption feeds freshness (W03.12).\n- Never fabricate a hypothesis to keep a case alive; nothing here auto-creates one.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two non-exclusive hypotheses on one question coexist.\n- [ ] Disposition without rationale is rejected with a repair.\n- [ ] An assumption linked to a conclusion appears in that conclusion's dependency set.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPlan question 3: \"What explanations or options are plausible, and what assumptions matter?\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16, W01.12.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §6.2, §9.4 (hypothesis.*, assumption.*), §20.1 (Explanatory) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.02 Hypothesis and assumption operations"}	blake3:r6xRQfYnPBmnjNGv_l4RqAmUKQEGg4gCcB4Bd8tCepY
1795321014308556	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-7kl	{"description":"# Context & Background\nAn Observation is a \"bounded measured/reported datum\" tied to a source selector. `observation.add`, `observation.correct` and `observation.retract` manage it with immutable revision history.\n\n# Goals\nHandlers enforcing the \"Record observation\" gate while keeping capture cheap.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe machine-checkable requirement is: \"Source selector or explicitly labeled assertion, extraction/measurement metadata; a text-based observation also carries a `text_quote`, and `not_found` rejects it.\" The human judgment that remains is whether the statement is \"genuinely observed rather than inferred\" and whether the quote supports it — which the tool says it cannot check.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `observation.add`: narrow value or statement, units/method, selector, quality and unknowns, `evidence_kind`, variables changed. The receipt returns the quote check result.\n- Quote verification is delegated to W06a.05 through a trait so this handler is testable with a stub; a `verified` quote is labeled presence, not support.\n- `observation.correct`: new revision, history preserved, dependents marked through the dependency index.\n- `observation.retract`: changes evidentiary use, never deletes; dependents become `needs_review` (W03.12).\n- No string such as \"best\" or \"critical\" in source text is promoted to a conclusion.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An observation with no selector and no explicit assertion label is rejected.\n- [ ] A text observation whose quote is `not_found` is rejected; `ambiguous` is reported in the receipt.\n- [ ] Correction and retraction preserve every prior revision.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPlan question 2: \"What facts or observations do we actually have, from which sources and under what conditions?\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16, W01.11.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.3, §5.4, §6.4 (Record observation), §9.4 (observation.*), §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.03 Observation operations: add, correct, retract, with selector and quote requirements"}	blake3:3rcdmKq6ppLOZfbzV3j-arwiQVQeZFmKePrR1bEeyUI
1795321014404716	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3p6	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`inquiry.create/amend/freeze/retire` manage a \"versioned plan for inspect/test/benchmark/documentary research; predictions/controls required only for confirmatory empirical mode\".\n\n# Goals\nHandlers with two distinct freeze gates.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nStrong inference [R1] and severe testing [R2]: a test supports a hypothesis only to the extent it would probably have failed if the hypothesis were false. Review 1 #5 added the per-rival prediction table; a plan that predicts the same outcome for two live rivals cannot discriminate between them and should be flagged *before any run*.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Freeze exploratory: purpose, method or inspection, observation channel and a bounded stop rule.\n- Freeze confirmatory: exact question/hypothesis revisions, per-rival predicted outcomes, observation and control conditions, evidence/holdout boundary, declared prior exposure to the outcome, stop and analysis rule.\n- At freeze, compute whether two or more live rivals share an identical predicted outcome and return it as a warning (the blindspot rule `inquiry.non_discriminating_plan` reuses this in W10.11).\n- \"A frozen test is immutable.\" Changes after freeze create a successor or an explicit amendment with chronology; never an in-place edit.\n- Documentary and code-inspection plans are valid without controls.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Confirmatory freeze without a per-rival table is rejected with the missing fields named.\n- [ ] A frozen plan cannot be modified; an amendment is a new visible record.\n- [ ] Identical predictions for two live rivals produce a warning at freeze.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPlan question 4: what \"would most reduce decision-relevant uncertainty\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16, W01.12, W03.02.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §6.2, §6.4, §6.5, §9.4 (inquiry.*), §8.5, Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.04 Inquiry plan operations and freeze gates for exploratory and confirmatory plans"}	blake3:azg0Zlppn39MILuWxs6GTt3dM5McZ7xW_JNAmiCAuog
1795321014486856	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-25n	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Because Investigation does not execute the test, it cannot prove the first external packet happened after a local record.\" So it stores how strong the ordering evidence is, rather than asserting prospective testing.\n\n# Goals\nRecord chronology strength and, separately, exposure and evidence reuse, on plans and runs.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 3 #4: \"Order, prior exposure and evidence reuse are separate axes.\" A plan can be recorded before a run and still be tested on data the investigator had already seen in a dashboard. \"Never upgrade chronology from timestamps alone.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Chronology: `retrospective` (or `order_unknown`); `recorded_before_run_declaration` (local journal order only); `executor_bound` (an executor receipt binds the plan digest before execution); `externally_attested`.\n- Compute `recorded_before_run_declaration` from journal sequence, never from wall-clock times.\n- Receipts are stored as attributed data here; accepted formats (in-toto Statement in a DSSE envelope; RFC 3161 token) are documented in W07.10, and cryptographic verification is deferred.\n- Exposure: `unseen_declared`, `partly_seen`, `seen`, `unknown`, including exposure through summaries or dashboards.\n- Reserved observation units or partitions (holdout) are named on the frozen plan.\n- Linking actual inputs, exclusions and deviations at assessment time is W10.06.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A plan recorded after its run is labeled `retrospective` regardless of supplied timestamps.\n- [ ] Chronology and exposure are independent fields; strong chronology with `seen` exposure is representable.\n- [ ] No operation upgrades chronology without a receipt record.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Exploration is valid; it becomes confirmation only through new, properly specified evidence.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W03.04.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.5, §20.1 (Explanatory), Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.05 Prediction chronology strength, prior-exposure and holdout axes"}	blake3:06IOiZZoQGC_aHIB3oIrY_xycM9Vfab3MPAa_t9f6nI
1795321014570421	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2oj	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`run.start`, `run.record` and `run.reconcile` record \"one recorded attempt/execution/inspection\" performed by something outside `inv`. The streaming `capture` path (W07) builds on the same Run record.\n\n# Goals\nHandlers that keep execution status, evidence completeness and measurement validity as separate dimensions.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A benchmark process exiting 0 does not establish a sound performance result. A tool timeout does not mean the measured system timed out.\" And: \"A failed or blocked external Run cannot be interpreted as negative evidence of the question without a separate valid observation.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `run.start` references the exact inquiry plan digest/revision, or states an exploration intent.\n- `run.record`: producer identity, external reference, explicit status, environment, provenance, artifacts, honest completeness, `evidence_kind`, variables changed.\n- `run.reconcile`: attributed update for unknown or partial outcomes. \"Run events are immutable; reconcile unknown/partial outcomes through attributed updates, never by rewriting an original failed record.\"\n- \"Repeating an external operation creates a new Run.\"\n- The producer's result is stored as an attributed assertion, never as an interpretation.\n- Intermittent failures are recorded as k of n before and after a change.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A `failed` run with `complete` evidence and a `completed` run with `missing` evidence both validate.\n- [ ] Reconcile adds a revision; the original record is unchanged.\n- [ ] No run status alone changes any hypothesis or conclusion state.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInvariant: \"an invalid experiment is not a negative result.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16, W01.12, W03.04.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.2, §6.3, §9.4 (run.*), §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.06 Run operations and independent outcome dimensions"}	blake3:KaxYXAvnldt8ypS8C1mR2a1qWHT3xE9J3FYYcmO_L20
1795321014653747	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2dy	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`assessment.add/supersede/retract` record an \"evidentiary interpretation of question/hypothesis/comparison/claim with exact evidence read-set, attribution and rationale\".\n\n# Goals\nHandlers that make `supports`/`contradicts` attributed interpretive records and capture how expected the evidence is under each rival.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nACH's weakest step is the per-hypothesis \"consistent/inconsistent\" rating, where \"evidence that fits every hypothesis still gets counted as support\". Evidence strength is comparative, so \"when live rivals exist, record how expected an observation is under each rival\" (review 2 #4). It lives on the Assessment because most evidence is not a planned test.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Fields: subject+revision, evidence refs (exact revisions), relation, rationale, assumptions, optional confidence, `basis_digest`.\n- Per-rival expectation `more_expected | equally_expected | less_expected | not_assessed` for each live rival of the subject; \"Not required in `explore` mode before rivals exist.\"\n- Concurrent assessments by different actors coexist; nothing overwrites.\n- Supersede and retract preserve history and feed freshness.\n- The core never computes a score from assessments.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] With live rivals, a supporting assessment lacking per-rival expectation is accepted only with explicit `not_assessed` entries.\n- [ ] Two opposing assessments of one hypothesis are both visible.\n- [ ] An assessment citing a retracted observation revision is rejected with the current revision in the repair.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Evidence is not a verdict\": interpretation is always someone's, with reasons.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16, W01.14, W03.03.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.1, §5.2, §5.5, §6.3, §9.4 (assessment.*), Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.07 Assessment operations with per-rival expectation and basis digest"}	blake3:kVVnWYqqUY-yPRGNTZjTlqPO4ukyTwh9343fm7qUdAg
1795321014738511	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ew	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`conclusion.create`, `conclusion.amend` and `conclusion.request_review` manage a \"bounded synthesized claim about what was learned\".\n\n# Goals\nHandlers and the \"Promote conclusion to review-ready\" gate.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA Conclusion is \"a scoped causal explanation naming one or more factors with roles... not a claim that every timeout in production has that cause.\" Review 2 #12: it stays `candidate` until an interventional result with a toggle confirms it — the fix stops the failure and reverting brings it back.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Fields: statement, significance, scope/applicability, support and contradictions, alternatives addressed, limitations, what would invalidate it, factor roles (`trigger`, `latent_condition`, `contributor`). No required severity score.\n- Review-ready gate (machine-checkable): bounded claim, direct support, applicability, alternative-explanation treatment, limitations and inspectable evidence.\n- A causal Conclusion with only observational support displays as `candidate`, never `confirmed`.\n- Amend is CAS-checked; substantive amendment changes the basis and requires re-review (W03.11).\n- Gate failures return machine-readable blockers and repair guidance; they never block recording the candidate.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `request_review` on a conclusion with no linked support is rejected with named blockers.\n- [ ] A causal conclusion backed only by observational evidence cannot be shown as confirmed.\n- [ ] A credible counterhypothesis is visible on the conclusion before promotion.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPlan question 5: \"What can we conclude or choose, what remains unknown, and what would change our mind?\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W03.07.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.3, §6.2, §6.4, §9.4 (conclusion.*), §15.3, §20.1 (Explanatory) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.08 Conclusion operations, review-ready gate and the causal-candidate rule"}	blake3:g89RGir7TlBYMEDMweqN2XEfCiiEvWyiEe6EeOwli14
1795321014832787	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3fl	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`review.submit` with `kind: validation` records an attributed review; `conclusion.validate` promotes a review-ready claim; `conclusion.disposition` rejects, retracts or supersedes it.\n\n# Goals\nThe validation path with its hard gate.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe gate: \"Current submitted validation review with a supportive verdict on the exact claim basis, current material sets (§10.3), satisfied method obligations and no unresolved critical Objection.\" There is \"no `--force-validated` shortcut\", and actor-ID inequality between reviewer and author \"is merely a procedural check\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `review.submit`: exact subject basis and read-set, method, evidence seen, source dependencies, verdict (`supports|objects|inconclusive`), caveats, `context_isolation`, and the reviewer's descriptor. May share a batch with `objection.raise`.\n- `conclusion.validate`: checks the review is current for the conclusion's `basis_digest`, the required read-set is unchanged (W03.11) and no critical Objection is open (W03.10).\n- A lifecycle-only validation event \"must not immediately invalidate its own review\".\n- `conclusion.disposition`: reject/retract/supersede with blockers and read-set; history preserved.\n- Blindspot-kind reviews use the same operation but are completed in W10.14; here it is enough that a blindspot review \"never validates a finding by itself\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Validation with a review bound to an older basis is rejected.\n- [ ] Validation with an open critical Objection is rejected.\n- [ ] A blindspot review cannot satisfy the validation gate.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInvariant: \"a late revision must invalidate stale reviews.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W03.08, W03.10, W03.11.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.2, §6.4 (Validate conclusion), §9.4 (review.submit, conclusion.validate), §14.2, §14.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.09 Validation reviews and conclusion.validate / conclusion.disposition"}	blake3:1NO9N96kK7b5Bq-8mpLKUJh8f0OYITMgL_sArwpbEL4
1795321014920080	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2eo	{"description":"# Context & Background\nRevision 4 added Objection as a first-class record because the plan referenced \"unresolved critical objections\" without any way to create one (reviews 2 #3 and 3 #2).\n\n# Goals\n`objection.raise`, `objection.resolve` and `objection.withdraw`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A decision-maker cannot suppress a material objection by deleting the note.\" Resolution \"cites the exact disputed record revision, evidence, resolution rationale and any required fresh review. This is a reasoned disposition, not a silent `disputed=false` flag.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Raise: exact subject revision, statement, scope, severity (`critical` | `material`), evidence refs or explicit unknown, optional originating Review (may share a batch with `review.submit`).\n- Resolve/withdraw: exact objection revision plus attributed rationale/evidence, as its own record.\n- All three update the subject's *set of open challenges*; \"freshness flows through the dependency graph, not flags\". That set is one of the membership sets W04.05 digests.\n- Reviewers cannot erase observations; objections attach to claims, comparisons and decisions.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Raising an objection against a stale subject revision is rejected with the current revision.\n- [ ] Resolve without rationale is rejected.\n- [ ] The open-objection set of a subject is queryable at a snapshot.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps disagreement visible instead of silently resolved.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16, W01.14.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §9.4 (objection.*), §10.3, §14.3, Disposition: Agent experience and interface (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"s","title":"W03.10 Objection operations: raise, resolve, withdraw"}	blake3:UQkl8vYBO5OcE1J3yAPrzmk0nhwMIUiqcs5MYAYRLMg
1795321015026764	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-324	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Promoting a conclusion requires the relevant read-set: conclusion basis, evidence validity, critical assessments, review, scope/applicability and policy revision... This avoids accepting a validation based on evidence retracted by another agent.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe core computes the dependency closure a promotion must be current against, and distinguishes an object's CAS revision from its substantive `basis_digest`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The caller supplies expected revisions, but cannot omit an inconvenient dependency to bypass a gate.\" And: \"Distinguish the object revision used for CAS from the substantive `basis_digest` used by review... A lifecycle-only validation event records that reviewed basis and must not immediately invalidate its own review; substantive amendments change the basis and require re-review.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Define, per promotable type, which fields are substantive (claim, evidence, scope, policy) and compute `basis_digest` over them.\n- Closure for a Conclusion: its basis, each supporting/contradicting assessment and their evidence revisions, measurement-validity records where present, material assumptions, applicable policy/pack pins.\n- On promotion, compare caller-supplied expected revisions with the computed closure; a missing or changed member yields \"a repairable rejection with the current required set\".\n- \"Detect circular justification while evaluating sufficiency.\"\n- Reused for Decisions in W04.04 and extended with set membership in W04.05.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A promotion racing with an evidence retraction cannot validate against the stale read-set.\n- [ ] Omitting a required member returns the full current required set.\n- [ ] A lifecycle-only change leaves `basis_digest` unchanged; a claim edit changes it.\n- [ ] A justification cycle is detected and reported.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the mechanism that makes a \"validated\" label mean something under concurrent work.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W03.07, W02.09.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.7, §10.3, §20.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"l","title":"W03.11 Required read-set closure for promotions; basis digest distinct from the CAS revision"}	blake3:lFIp-53XHzigjGsUVvgWkeRar_xgvoBa-peziSjYSiQ
1795321015104335	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3gf	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Preserve historical lifecycle status. Compute an effective disposition for every default read and export: for example `validated / needs_review`, `validated / disputed`, or `candidate / current`.\"\n\n# Goals\nA dependency-driven overlay that every read uses, sufficient for the Milestone A scenarios. The complete engine with materiality rules is W10.05.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Never show a stale conclusion simply as 'validated,' or an affected accepted decision as unqualified 'current.'\" History is not erased when conditions change; applicability to the new context is marked unestablished.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Maintain the revision dependency index (W02.09): each assessment, review, comparison, conclusion and decision cites exact subject/evidence revisions.\n- Triggers handled in the slice: observation retraction or correction, source withdrawal, changed hypothesis basis, open critical objection, criterion revision (W04.06 plugs in here).\n- Effective states: `current`, `needs_review`, `disputed`, `conditional`; computed on read, never written over lifecycle status.\n- Every read response includes both lifecycle status and effective state.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Retracting an observation turns its dependent validated conclusion into `validated / needs_review`.\n- [ ] An open critical objection yields `disputed`.\n- [ ] Unrelated mutations do not change the effective state.\n- [ ] Historical lifecycle status remains readable.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 5, truthful states.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W03.11.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.5, §5.7, §14.3, §20.1 (Cross-domain) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.12 Effective disposition and the slice's freshness overlay"}	blake3:s8FvWigAM9onCs0U2cH96dbcY-Z2Wy1rXzSEjz6RtNU
1795321015181012	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-200	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`retrospective.record/supersede` persist an attributed, case-scoped account of method. `case.close` on a *material* case must reference a Retrospective from the same or a prior transaction.\n\n# Goals\nBoth operations and the closure gate, including the one-call atomic path.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReviews 1 #3 and 2 #6 narrowed the rule: a Retrospective is required only when the case is material — it has a promoted Conclusion, an accepted Decision, a failed/invalidated Run, a retracted/corrected source or observation, or a pack-declared trigger. Other cases close with `retrospective_waived` and a reason; `record`-only sessions are exempt. \"Do not force an agent to claim failures it did not see.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `retrospective.record` contract per §9.4; \"'No reusable lesson identified' is valid with a bounded reason\".\n- Update only by supersession; earlier retrospectives stay readable.\n- `case.close`: outcome, reason, unresolved questions, reopen condition, `retrospective_ref` (may be `{client_ref}` in the same batch) or `retrospective_waived` with reason.\n- The Retrospective must be bound to a snapshot still current for materially relevant work; otherwise `RETROSPECTIVE_STALE`.\n- Missing on a material case: reject the entire transaction with `RETROSPECTIVE_REQUIRED`, `commit_status: not_committed`, and a repair pointing at `schema retrospective.record` plus the failing revisions.\n- Lesson citation validation against the journal digest is W10.16.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Retrospective plus close in one idempotent batch commits atomically and survives kill/retry.\n- [ ] Closing a material case without one fails with an actionable repair.\n- [ ] A non-material case closes with a waiver; a `record`-mode case closes with neither.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe closing clause of the plan: leave \"a useful method retrospective for the next investigator\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W03.01, W01.15.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.4 (Close substantive case), §8.6, §9.4 (retrospective.*, case.close, closure example), §20.1, §20.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.13 Retrospective operations and the case.close gate with material-case rule and waiver"}	blake3:vDOTeLi56xAXIWMKeNVLKvb_FA_C9Z3zNLW1cCqMhH4
1795321015261054	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1bc	{"description":"# Context & Background\nAn Annotation is a \"human/agent comment or imported note\" with a subject, author claim, content/hash and classification, and \"no automatic evidentiary status\".\n\n# Goals\nThe base `annotation.add` handler. The `synopsis` and `advice_feedback` roles are reserved here and implemented in W05b.08 and W05b.04.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAgents and humans need a cheap place for a remark that is not an observation, assessment or objection. Keeping it explicitly non-evidentiary prevents commentary from being counted as support.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Subject refs (one or more records, with revisions), attributed freeform content or a snapshot of a human note with its hash, classification.\n- Content is authored free text and is treated as data wherever it is shown (W05b.13).\n- An annotation never appears in a conclusion's support set and never satisfies a gate.\n- Backs the `note TEXT --on ID` shortcut (W05a.05).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An annotation attaches to any record type and appears in its `full` view.\n- [ ] It is excluded from evidence and support counts.\n- [ ] Using a reserved role before its implementing bone lands is rejected as deferred.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCheap capture without diluting what counts as evidence.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16, W01.15.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §9.4 (annotation.add), §12.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"s","title":"W03.14 annotation.add for attributed notes with no automatic evidentiary status"}	blake3:5vAfFckJqMDsMfC5-mBkmJrSqxTAFSkpw9SK1uxFU4I
1795321015342087	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-bra	{"description":"# Context & Background\nScenario A: \"a request occasionally hangs after a deployment. Was a connection held across an async boundary, or was the scheduler/network responsible?\"\n\n# Goals\nDeterministic offline seed material and a machine-checkable description of the expected outcome properties.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nScenarios ship \"with expected outcomes, counterevidence and invalid-result variants\". The broken-clock trace exists to test the invariant that confounded evidence stays a visible gap rather than becoming negative evidence.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Seed sources: two comparable request traces (slow request holding a database connection for 28 s; fast control); connection-pool metrics; a source-code commit reference; a third trace with intentionally broken clock alignment.\n- A successful control and a failure under changed transaction behaviour, as run outputs to ingest.\n- Adversarial variant: \"the only test that appears to refute scheduler effects used a different build.\"\n- Expected properties (not hard-coded IDs): at least two causal hypotheses and a clock-alignment assumption; the unaligned trace is not interpreted; the conclusion is bounded to tested configurations or explicitly inconclusive; the adversarial variant cannot be validated unqualified.\n- Traces must contain quotable text and JSON so selectors and quote checks are exercised.\n- Synthetic data only; no named real system or tool.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Seed bytes are committed, immutable and documented with their intended role.\n- [ ] Expected properties are written as assertions a test can evaluate.\n- [ ] The fixture contains nothing security-specific or producer-specific.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nOne of the two cases Milestone A must demonstrate and the A-gate pilot draws its development material from.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.11.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17 (preamble), §17.1, §5.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation","fixture"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.15 Scenario A fixture: intermittent request timeout seed sources and expected properties"}	blake3:pULdnLP8KiL4bQ7opT5vCLqTvsvfdkUr6NlhJR-U5SQ
1795321015430588	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2z9	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW03's acceptance is \"Debug scenario works without special consumer and retains original evidence.\" §20.7 requires executable examples to \"run in fresh fixture workspaces using actual returned/generated IDs\".\n\n# Goals\nA scripted walkthrough of Scenario A's seven steps that drives the real binary and asserts the expected properties.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA library-only test would not prove the path an agent actually takes. Driving the binary with returned IDs also exercises envelopes, receipts and the ingest path together.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nScript: create the case; ingest sources; record observations before certainty; create two hypotheses and the clock-alignment assumption; create and freeze a discriminating inquiry; record the control and failure runs; add assessments with per-rival expectations; create a bounded conclusion; submit a validation review; record a retrospective and close in one batch.\nAssert:\n- Original evidence bytes are retained and digests match.\n- The broken-clock trace is never used as negative evidence.\n- A frozen prediction cannot be silently amended after results.\n- Adversarial variant: the different-build control leaves a visible comparability gap and validation is refused or qualified.\n- A truly inconclusive ending closes with a precise reopen condition.\nSteps 6–7 of §17.1 that need `blindspots` and reports are completed by W10/W11 and re-run in MB.01.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The script passes from an empty directory with no network and no extra tools.\n- [ ] Rejection steps assert both the repair hint and the absence of mutation.\n- [ ] No fake or placeholder ID appears in any submitted payload.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe first \"runnable proof\" that the explanatory loop is real.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W03.15, W03.05, W03.06, W03.09, W03.12, W03.13, W03.14, W05a.03, W06a.01, W06a.05.\nParent goal: W03. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.1, §19.2 (W03), §20.1 (Explanatory), §20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w03","milestone-a","lane-investigation","test"],"parent":"bn-azj","size":"m","title":"W03.16 Scenario A end-to-end test through the binary, including the adversarial variant"}	blake3:M7JzzWyoMs8KpN5y8cH7vz1Rbm8dS7jF6Q2hYXxF22E
1795321015623297	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-28g	{"description":"# Context & Background\nAn Option is a \"candidate action, design, choice or strategy\" — something someone could choose, not a claim about the world.\n\n# Goals\n`option.create`, `option.amend` and `option.disposition`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Track discarded options and why.\" A ruled-out option stays in the record with its reason and the criterion that excluded it, so a later reader can see the alternative was considered and can revisit it if the criterion changes.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Create: description, context, feasibility, important dependencies, tradeoff notes; link to the decision Question (`candidate_for`).\n- Amend: CAS; revisions matter because Comparisons cite exact option revisions.\n- Disposition: `eligible`, `ruled_out` (reason and criterion link required), `withdrawn`, `superseded`.\n- Encourage, do not force, a \"do nothing / retain current design\" option when relevant (guidance text, not a gate).\n- Options never require hypotheses, predictions or tests.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Ruling out an option without a reason and criterion link is rejected.\n- [ ] A ruled-out option remains readable with its reason.\n- [ ] An `evaluate` case with options and no hypotheses validates.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps alternatives, including the rejected ones, on the record.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16, W01.13.\nParent goal: W04. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §6.2, §9.4 (option.*), §15.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-mw0","size":"s","title":"W04.01 Option operations: create, amend, disposition with ruled-out reasons"}	blake3:H4tzeBlGn-VKVLVj__6Lb_v_WIT1voMFDzqF8pWpwiw
1795321015793582	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-39r	{"description":"# Context & Background\nA Criterion is an \"evaluation dimension for a choice\": hard or soft, with units or scale, preferred direction, limits and an optional weighting rationale.\n\n# Goals\n`criterion.create`, `criterion.amend` and `criterion.retire`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Define Criteria before reviewing outcomes when possible... Record any later criterion revision openly.\" Scores, if ever used, are computed against \"the criterion's declared scale anchors, not normalization to the current option set, so adding or removing an option cannot reorder the others\" (review 2 #13) — so anchors must be captured here even though scoring is W10.20.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Create: name, kind `hard|soft`, desired direction and scale with anchors, applicability (link to the decision Question), optional threshold, priority rationale.\n- Amend: CAS; classify the change as material (threshold, units, direction, kind, applicability) or editorial. Material changes \"invalidate comparisons\" through W04.06.\n- Retire: removes it from the applicable set, which is a membership change for W04.05.\n- Subjective criteria are stated as preferences, never as measurements.\n- Do not infer units or comparability.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A hard criterion without a checkable threshold or statement is rejected.\n- [ ] A material amendment produces a new revision that dependent comparisons no longer match.\n- [ ] Retire preserves history and is visible in the criteria set version.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes must-haves explicit before preferences blur them.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.16, W01.13.\nParent goal: W04. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §6.2, §9.4 (criterion.*), §15.1, Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-mw0","size":"m","title":"W04.02 Criterion operations: hard/soft kinds, scale anchors, thresholds, retire"}	blake3:_gnQ14lohmePp4WqZNh0brK9qTQbmSiWEShv0QrAtYE
1795321015971786	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2c3	{"description":"# Context & Background\nA Comparison is \"a scoped judgment of one option against one criterion\", citing exact option and criterion revisions.\n\n# Goals\n`comparison.add`, `comparison.revise` and `comparison.retract`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"If a hard requirement is unmeasured, the comparison is `unknown`, not `fails`.\" `unknown` is an expected outcome. \"Do not automatically infer the criteria units or that different workloads are comparable.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Contract: `option_ref{revision}`, `criterion_ref{revision}`, `outcome`, `value`+`unit` (decimal string) or qualitative basis, `evidence_refs[]`, `assumption_refs[]`, `method`, `limitations`, `uncertainty`, `rationale`.\n- Gate: exact revisions, evidence or an explicit assumption, evaluation method, uncertainty.\n- Units on the value must match the criterion's declared units or the mismatch is reported, never converted silently.\n- Revise creates a new revision; retract preserves history; both feed freshness for dependent Decisions.\n- A source's \"best\" or \"recommended\" is a source-authored preference, labeled as such.\n- Concurrent comparisons of one cell by different actors coexist.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The §9.4 comparison example commits against real IDs.\n- [ ] A comparison citing a stale criterion revision is rejected with the current one.\n- [ ] A comparison with neither evidence nor an explicit assumption is rejected.\n- [ ] Unit mismatch is surfaced, not coerced.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe cell is where evidence meets a requirement, with its uncertainty attached.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W04.01, W04.02.\nParent goal: W04. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2, §5.5, §6.3, §6.4 (Assess option versus criterion), §9.4 (comparison.*, comparison contract) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-mw0","size":"m","title":"W04.03 Comparison operations: one option against one criterion, unknown allowed"}	blake3:yN4MW8GGvIUKS-VSKYQXsywpw-z4uYZ82skDLX7MiJg
1795321016094720	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3v6	{"description":"# Context & Background\nA Decision records \"a chosen option or resolved question, with reasons\". It \"never becomes an empirical `validated` conclusion merely because the choice was accepted\".\n\n# Goals\n`decision.propose`, `decision.accept` and `decision.supersede`, with the acceptance gate.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA decision \"may be rational but conditional\" and \"can be `defer` with reasons, not necessarily a selected winner\". For Decisions, objections do not automatically block: \"material objections, constraint violations and uncertain comparisons must be visible, and the selected policy may demand explicit acknowledgment rather than prevent all choices under uncertainty.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Contract: exact selected option or choice, alternatives against a *versioned* criteria set, hard-constraint disposition, evidence, assumptions, tradeoffs, consequences, what the decider expects and when to check, reversibility and reopening triggers.\n- Gate (machine-checkable): candidate options and applicable active criteria, hard-constraint disposition, material unknowns, rationale, consequences, reopen trigger. An unknown hard criterion cannot be treated as satisfied; it must be named as a condition.\n- Acceptance requires the read-set (option/criterion/comparison revisions and material assumptions) per W03.11 and the membership sets per W04.05.\n- Accepted content is immutable. A substantive change is a new superseding Decision with links.\n- \"Do not conflate an accepted decision with implementation success.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Acceptance with an unacknowledged unknown hard criterion is rejected with the cell named.\n- [ ] An accepted Decision cannot be amended; supersession links both ways.\n- [ ] A conditional decision with one uncertain comparison can be accepted and preserves the condition.\n- [ ] Disqualified options are preserved with reasons.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"A decision may be rational, conditional and accepted while one comparison remains uncertain; report explicitly preserves that condition.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W04.03, W03.10, W03.11.\nParent goal: W04. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.2, §6.4 (Recommend/accept a decision), §9.4 (decision.*, decision contract), §14.3, §15.1, §15.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-mw0","size":"m","title":"W04.04 Decision operations and acceptance gate: propose, accept, supersede, defer"}	blake3:3a2zmUDkcyfMaN-yRdwP4hAM9li7e8IIUB-DWpatAIg
1795321016174632	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3k8	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"A review basis includes set membership, not only revisions. If a reviewer judged 'all applicable hard criteria' or 'no open objections', a later *insertion* changes the basis even though every ID they read keeps its revision, the database-isolation phantom problem.\" Revision 4 calls this \"the most consequential correctness gap any review found\" (review 3 #2).\n\n# Goals\nPromotions and reads detect membership changes in the sets a judgment depended on.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nRevision checks alone cannot see a new record. The A-gate exercise inserts a hard criterion between review and acceptance precisely to test this.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Bind each Decision to its decision Question; record applicability links from options and criteria to that Question, and subject links from Objections to the claim or decision.\n- A small fixed set of built-in set rules (no saved SQL), e.g. applicable active hard criteria for a question; eligible options for a question; open objections on a subject; material assumptions of a subject.\n- At review, persist each applicable rule, its version, scope, and a digest of sorted member IDs with their substantive basis digests — including empty sets.\n- The core derives the required rules; check on promotion against locked state, and again on later reads for effective disposition.\n- Changed membership → conflict before acceptance, `needs_review` after it.\n- Evaluate against the **final state of an atomic batch**.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A new in-scope hard criterion or new material Objection after review conflicts before promotion and marks an already accepted basis `needs_review`.\n- [ ] Retiring or relinking a member updates the set.\n- [ ] A criterion added to a *different* question in the same case leaves the set current.\n- [ ] Full recomputation and indexed results agree after replay.\n- [ ] A batch cannot validate a claim and then add a disqualifying blocker to it.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nWithout this, \"reviewed\" could silently stop being true while every revision number stayed the same.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W04.04, W03.11.\nParent goal: W04. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.4, §10.3, §20.2, Disposition: Storage and durability (Review basis includes set membership) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-mw0","size":"l","title":"W04.05 Set-membership review basis: detect phantom criteria and objections inserted after review"}	blake3:ZLx3OnVIo0LR_R-vxxeNKhFw6GxA4e076XXIQ1_gOqA
1795321016258559	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1tw	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Changing the criterion's threshold, priority or measurement conditions makes the comparison `needs_review`; dependent decisions inherit freshness obligations.\"\n\n# Goals\nWire criterion, option and comparison changes into the effective-disposition overlay (W03.12).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nScenario C's adversarial variant: \"change the hard durability criterion threshold after a decision. The dependent Comparison and Decision become `needs_review`; source evidence stays intact. The system must not silently preserve 'accepted and current' as an unqualified state.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Dependency edges: Comparison → exact option and criterion revisions, evidence and assumptions; Decision → its comparisons, criteria set version and assumptions.\n- Material criterion amendments (W04.02 classification) propagate; editorial ones do not, \"unless demonstrably irrelevant per dependency map\".\n- An accepted Decision is never rewritten; its effective state becomes `accepted / needs_review` and a superseding Decision is the remedy.\n- \"A later benchmark does not automatically rewrite an earlier accepted decision: it may trigger a superseding decision.\"\n- Observations and sources are untouched by a criterion change.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Changing threshold, weight or units forces affected Comparisons and Decisions into `needs_review`.\n- [ ] An editorial criterion change leaves them current.\n- [ ] The original accepted Decision remains readable with its historical status.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nApplies \"a late revision must invalidate stale reviews\" to choices as well as claims.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W04.03, W03.12.\nParent goal: W04. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.5, §14.3, §15.3, §17.3 (Adversarial variant), §20.1 (Evaluative) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-mw0","size":"m","title":"W04.06 Criterion-change freshness: comparisons and decisions become needs_review"}	blake3:DPPNiSZzHEICl-Fo9BZB0lxMwjAe0A4zKPbNMnhmwTg
1795321016338970	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-783	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Compute weight-free Pareto status for each eligible option: `disqualified_hard` (fails a verified hard criterion), `strictly_dominated`, `pareto_frontier`, or `uncertain_frontier`.\"\n\n# Goals\nA deterministic, read-only computation over recorded comparisons that needs no weights.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAdopted from review 1 #6 as the honest alternative to weighted sums. It must be conservative: \"A dominates B only if A is at least as good on every comparable criterion with current evidence and strictly better on one. Any `unknown`, `stale` or unranked-subjective cell that could favor B makes dominance indeterminate.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Inputs: eligible options, applicable active criteria, current comparisons with effective freshness.\n- `disqualified_hard` only on a verified `fails` of a hard criterion — never on `unknown`.\n- Comparable means same criterion revision, compatible units and a defined preferred direction; ordinal or subjective cells without an agreed ranking are unranked.\n- Output per option: status plus the cells that determined it and the cells that keep it uncertain.\n- Feeds `next` ranking factor 4 (W05b.02) and the comparison matrix (W08.07).\n- Never used as a gate and never presented as \"the winner\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Pareto status never prunes an option on the strength of an `unknown`, `stale` or unranked-subjective cell.\n- [ ] Adding a duplicate option does not change other options' status.\n- [ ] A small exhaustive fixture matches hand-computed statuses.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 9: no \"automatic weighted winner scores\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W04.03.\nParent goal: W04. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §7.2, §15.1 (step 5), §20.1 (Evaluative), Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-mw0","size":"m","title":"W04.07 Weight-free Pareto status with a conservative dominance rule"}	blake3:ZWsM96nno6grUKHfmoP0e-kTmfUu12__URy6sE0tb6Q
1795321016426417	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3jg	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW04 includes \"sensitivity questions\". §15.1 step 6: \"Make consequential uncertainty visible: which missing measurement, preference weight, threshold, or model assumption could reverse the decision or change Pareto status?\"\n\n# Goals\nA structured way to record sensitivity on a proposed or accepted Decision using existing record types.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A sensitivity review says what range of uncertain inputs could change the choice; it is not an autogenerated universal ranking.\" The acceptance test wants \"which uncertain assumption could plausibly reverse an option choice, without claiming a computed probability\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- A Decision carries `reversal_conditions[]`: each links an Assumption, Criterion or Comparison cell, states the range or outcome that would change the choice, and names the option that would then be preferred.\n- Sensitivity Questions (\"would the choice change at 10× traffic?\") are ordinary Questions linked to the decision with `informs_decision`.\n- Assumptions referenced this way are material by definition.\n- Reopen triggers are derived from, or at least consistent with, reversal conditions.\n- No numeric probability or computed sensitivity index.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A decision can record at least one reversal condition tied to a specific assumption and criterion.\n- [ ] The condition appears in the decision's `summary` view as a material caveat.\n- [ ] Resolving the linked assumption against the choice marks the decision `needs_review`.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTells the next investigator exactly what would change the answer.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W04.04, W03.02.\nParent goal: W04. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.4 (decision contract), §15.1 (step 6), §19.2 (W04), §20.1 (Evaluative) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-mw0","size":"s","title":"W04.08 Sensitivity records: which uncertain assumption or measurement could reverse the choice"}	blake3:T4ELKcPHLlWfKCMe5tzIxb3U0uKHDE2N494BjUg9m-E
1795321016507350	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-11l	{"description":"# Context & Background\nScenario C: \"choose between (A) database-backed queue, (B) managed queue and (C) dedicated broker for a known application. No option is universally best.\"\n\n# Goals\nDeterministic seed documents and data plus machine-checkable expected properties.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe scenario exists to prove the same core handles a choice with no forced hypotheses, and that unknown cells and changed criteria are handled honestly.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Seeds: design documents, workload projections, small benchmark data (with units, sample counts and environment), operational cost assumptions, reliability and failure-mode information.\n- One hard requirement (\"recoverable jobs under a specified failure model\") and soft criteria (operational cost, p99 enqueue latency, team complexity).\n- At least one cell with no evidence, to stay `unknown`; one cost estimate resting on an unverified traffic assumption; one source that calls an option \"best\" (to stay a preference).\n- A performance assumption whose test could reverse the choice.\n- Adversarial variant input: a changed hard durability threshold after the decision.\n- Expected properties: unknown cells remain unknown; the decision is conditional with rejected alternatives and reopen conditions; the adversarial change yields `needs_review` with evidence intact.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Seed bytes are committed and documented by role.\n- [ ] Expected properties are assertions a test can evaluate.\n- [ ] Nothing in the fixture requires a hypothesis, test or conclusion.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe second of the two Milestone A cases, and the one that breaks \"falsely generic designs\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.11.\nParent goal: W04. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17 (preamble), §17.3, §5.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation","fixture"],"parent":"bn-mw0","size":"m","title":"W04.09 Scenario C fixture: queue architecture choice seeds and expected properties"}	blake3:k9qvTytvE29OJBVJS3b0dn6LICW2BjPZmkjIWg91AkE
1795321016597982	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-22d	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW04's acceptance is \"Choice scenario uses same core, no forced hypotheses.\"\n\n# Goals\nA scripted walkthrough of Scenario C through the real binary, with the adversarial variant.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n§20.1 demands that mode `evaluate` supports \"options/criteria/comparisons/decision with zero hypotheses, zero tests and zero conclusions\". Only an end-to-end run proves no gate secretly requires them.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nScript: create an `evaluate` case with an explicit do-nothing baseline; record options and criteria; ingest sources; add per-cell comparisons with source links, leaving one cell unknown; record the sensitivity assumption; propose and accept a *conditional* decision; record a retrospective and close.\nAssert:\n- The case contains zero hypotheses, inquiries and conclusions.\n- A hard criterion with `unknown` cannot be treated as satisfied.\n- Pareto statuses match the fixture's expected values.\n- Adversarial: amending the durability threshold after acceptance turns the Comparison and Decision `needs_review`; evidence is intact; the old decision is superseded, not overwritten.\n- Phantom: inserting a new hard criterion between review and acceptance causes a conflict.\nExport of the ADR-like record and the blindspot step are completed by W11.05 and W10 and re-run in MB.01.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Passes from an empty directory, offline.\n- [ ] Uses the same binary and store as W03.16 with no scenario-specific code path.\n- [ ] Every rejection asserts the repair and the absence of mutation.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDemonstrates the binding decision that evaluation is first-class and distinct from explanation.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W04.09, W04.05, W04.06, W04.07, W04.08, W03.13, W05a.03, W06a.01.\nParent goal: W04. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.3, §19.2 (W04), §20.1 (Cross-domain, Evaluative), §20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w04","milestone-a","lane-investigation","test"],"parent":"bn-mw0","size":"m","title":"W04.10 Scenario C end-to-end test, adversarial threshold change and the zero-hypothesis invariant"}	blake3:-pWVqguPA1-BaFtmvk71PLv5z0YraMcMcvz4x4-jfM8
1795321147703614	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2qm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe shipped executable is `investigation`. `inv` is installed as an alias only where no other command has that name, because Python Invoke already installs `inv` (review 3 #5). First-use docs, repair argv and integration snippets use `investigation` or the configured executable name.\n\n# Goals\nThe `clap`-based dispatch skeleton and every global behaviour of §9.2, so individual commands only add their own logic.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAgents parse stdout. \"`--format json` (and its global alias `--json`) guarantees one JSON object on stdout, including for discovery and recoverable argument errors; resolve the format before subcommand validation.\" If format resolution happened after argument parsing, a typo would produce human text and break the caller.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `--workspace PATH` overrides CWD discovery; otherwise ascend to the nearest `investigation.toml`; never scan the home directory.\n- `--case ID`: must agree with an envelope `case_id`. Read commands may auto-select the only case; several cases with no selection return `CASE_REQUIRED` with a compact list. \"Do not guess from last UI focus.\"\n- `--actor` per W01.03.\n- Human text is the TTY default; machine callers pass the flag. Logs and progress go to stderr and never contain raw artifacts by default.\n- JSON mode: no ANSI, pager, prompt or auto-open. Text mode honours `--no-color` and `--no-pager`.\n- Exit codes from W01.08. Repair argv uses the real executable name.\n- Subcommand tree generated from the catalog where possible; wire `check` and `repair index` (W02.14) here.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An unknown subcommand with `--json` yields exactly one JSON error object and exit 2.\n- [ ] Two cases and no `--case` on a read returns `CASE_REQUIRED` listing both.\n- [ ] The binary is named `investigation`; no `inv` file is installed by default.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA predictable machine contract is the base of \"no bespoke instruction\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.08, W01.09.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md Header (Product), §9.1, §9.2, Disposition: Agent experience and interface (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"m","title":"W05a.01 CLI skeleton and global conventions: workspace/case/actor selection, JSON mode, binary name"}	blake3:KqLcp8y0Pc_pS4Bc1q7YZY8AlGI4FE8Z6e5oLbhzVBQ
1795321147781321	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3lk	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`init PATH` creates \"an isolated casework workspace and optional vault. Refuses network filesystems.\" It mutates owned local files only.\n\n# Goals\nThe CLI front for W02.02 with a receipt-style response and a useful next step.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nFirst contact sets expectations about what the tool touches. \"`inv init` never installs plugins, modifies a harness, contacts a service, or auto-registers a model\", and \"No automatic cloud sync, publish, or Git push belongs in initialization.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Create the layout, genesis and a commented `investigation.toml`; create `vault/` with empty `Notes/ Boards/ Inbox/` unless `--no-vault`.\n- Refuse an existing non-empty target unless it is already a workspace (idempotent report).\n- On a refused filesystem, explain why and suggest a local path.\n- Response `next[]`: `onboard`, and the `start` form.\n- Output states plainly which directories are canonical, disposable, machine-local and projection.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `init` then `onboard` works with no other setup.\n- [ ] Running `init` twice does not damage an existing workspace.\n- [ ] No file is written outside PATH.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nOffline, self-contained start: no model, server, network or Obsidian.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.01, W02.02.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2, §9.1 (init), §12.8 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"s","title":"W05a.02 init command: create an isolated casework workspace and optional vault"}	blake3:r5w6AsTXZ-r6loqk4abpII_KrJ4Y7Ylgi2kiRLlELY8
1795321147859216	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-293	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`record --input FILE` (or `-` for stdin) submits the mutation envelope. \"Typed domain mutations (case, observation, comparison, conclusion etc.) live in the shared `record` operation catalog.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe reference write path: parse, commit through W02.07, print the success or error envelope.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nBatches stay on `record`; shortcuts (W05a.05) and MCP (W13) build the same envelope and call the same dispatch, so there is one write implementation.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Read input with the strict parser (W01.04); enforce bounds before the lock.\n- `--case`/`--actor` flags must agree with envelope fields.\n- Print exactly one response object in JSON mode; in text mode print created IDs and the suggested next read.\n- After each write, return \"one local read using the actual workspace, case and created IDs\".\n- On uncertain durability, print `commit_status: unknown` and the instruction to retry or look up with the same command ID.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The §9.3 example commits (with a real case ID) and returns created IDs.\n- [ ] Re-submitting the same file returns `already_committed` with the same IDs.\n- [ ] A malformed operation commits nothing and exits 2 with a field path.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Agent writes\": transactional, schema-validated JSON with explicit receipts.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.01, W02.07.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.1 (record), §9.3, §9.5, §9.6 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"s","title":"W05a.03 record command: submit a typed atomic transaction from a file or stdin"}	blake3:Bga-nW3EKPHBYse3J4sp9OBgsKj2_BiCXlLXdMn6o9A
1795321147938222	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-ufj	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv schema <operation>` must include an example by default. `--schema-only` returns just the JSON Schema. `inv schema --list` is a compact index grouped by workflow stage.\"\n\n# Goals\nJust-in-time discovery of any operation, generated entirely from the catalog (W01.09).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The agent gets exact command/payload schemas at each point, not an encyclopedia.\" An unknown operation \"suggests close matches and the catalog, without changing state\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Default output: purpose, when to use, required/optional inputs, effects, valid lifecycle states, one minimal valid example, one relevant error with repair, outputs/IDs created, idempotency and revision rules, related next operations.\n- Inside a workspace, fill example IDs from real records where the state provides them; otherwise mark the example clearly as an *incomplete form* with named unresolved fields. \"Distinguish a valid executable example from an incomplete form in both JSON and prose.\"\n- `--list`: grouped by stage (frame, collect, explain, evaluate, assess, review, close, record).\n- Also serves non-record schemas: `ingest`, `capture.*` (later), response and error envelopes.\n- Close-match suggestions for typos.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every catalog operation has `schema` output whose example validates.\n- [ ] `schema --list` fits one screen and groups by stage.\n- [ ] A misspelled operation returns suggestions and exit 2 without mutation.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"An unfamiliar agent never has to guess schema or chronology requirements.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.01.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.2, §9.1 (schema), §9.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"m","title":"W05a.04 schema command: operation schema with purpose, when-to-use, minimal example and repair"}	blake3:hXzxXWO5fB1pyQGeEVAtCCZL95fSHkU5YsZkwtPTESk
1795321148012515	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2pf	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Flag-form shortcuts ship in v1 for the four commonest single-operation writes, generated from the same catalog and never hand-maintained\": `case new`, `source add PATH`, `observe TEXT --source ID [--quote TEXT]`, `note TEXT --on ID`.\n\n# Goals\nFour shortcuts that each build the standard envelope and produce one canonical event.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"This is what makes 'add an observation in one command' (§1.2) true\" (review 2 #3). Review 3 added the key detail: \"a key that appears only in the lost response cannot be used for recovery\", so a generated command ID is printed to stderr **before** submission as well as in the receipt.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Declare shortcut-to-operation mappings in the catalog; derive flags from payload fields.\n- `--command-id` optional; if omitted, generate one and print it to stderr before submitting.\n- `source add` goes through ingest (W06a.01), never around it.\n- `observe --quote` supplies the `text_quote`; the receipt reports the quote check.\n- Each shortcut prints the equivalent envelope under `--print-envelope` so agents can graduate to batches.\n- Batches are not supported in shortcut form.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each shortcut commits exactly one transaction equivalent to its `record` form.\n- [ ] The command ID is visible on stderr before the commit and resolves with `receipt`.\n- [ ] Changing a payload field in the catalog changes the shortcut's flags without hand edits.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"An investigator can add a source or observation in one command without inventing a hypothesis, plan, or confidence number.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.03, W03.01, W03.03, W03.14, W06a.01.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §1.2, §9.1 (shortcuts), Disposition: Agent experience and interface (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"m","title":"W05a.05 Flag-form shortcuts generated from the catalog: case new, source add, observe, note"}	blake3:aImv8fJ2y6zLDwODZiZjNtXmDXTg970z91DlR5vwUnE
1795321148083504	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-7d8	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`start QUESTION --attach FILE|- --command-id KEY` creates an `explore` case and preserves the supplied source in one transaction (the `new_case` ingest variant). `receipt KEY` resolves a command receipt \"without the original request or bytes\".\n\n# Goals\nThe one-call first-use path and its recovery command (review 3 #5).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n§20.5 targets a \"first useful receipt within three calls after onboarding (the `start` form makes this one)\". And stdin cannot be re-read: if the response is lost after piping a source, the agent must still learn what was committed.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `start` translates to an ingest manifest with `new_case` and one attachment; scope and stop condition are explicitly unknown; actor, acquisition channel (e.g. stdin, publisher unknown unless supplied), size and digest are recorded.\n- \"A failed import publishes neither case nor Source.\"\n- The semantic request binds declared metadata and captured content digests, so \"reusing a key with changed bytes is a mismatch even if the path string is unchanged\".\n- `receipt KEY`: for the selected workspace and actor at a verified head, return original IDs, revisions, snapshot and artifact digests; absent → `not_found_at_snapshot`; unverifiable head → `unknown` with a repair path.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Pipe a source into `start`, lose the response and discard the input: `receipt KEY` after restart/reindex returns the same case, artifact and digest.\n- [ ] A changed stream under the same key is rejected.\n- [ ] A failed initial ingest creates no empty case.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCheap capture with safe recovery: the first minute of use cannot lose or duplicate evidence.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.03, W06a.01, W02.10.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.1, §8.2, §9.1 (start, receipt), §9.5, §11.1, §20.2, Disposition: Agent experience and interface (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"m","title":"W05a.06 start and receipt: new case plus first source in one write, and lost-response recovery"}	blake3:-RGuxwlSN1pS6U-iQv2qGczX48Qw8Lt00pPnYwfmTAg
1795321148157084	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1fk	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Every readable record has a stable display title and a compact synopsis view. The synopsis is derived deterministically from selected typed fields\" by default.\n\n# Goals\nA pure function per record type producing a title and a bounded synopsis (about 700 UTF-8 bytes) from trusted typed fields.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The synopsis is not evidence or an extra vote.\" Because an appealing summary can hide a problem, \"the current effective state, strongest material caveat, relevant contradiction, and omissions remain separately generated fields; an appealing synopsis cannot hide them.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- For each of the 19 v0-alpha types: choose the fields that make a useful one-paragraph synopsis; truncate at field boundaries, never mid-string.\n- Emit alongside, never inside, the synopsis: effective state, strongest material caveat, relevant contradiction, evidence-reference IDs, summary basis (subject revision) and freshness.\n- Deterministic: same record revision and snapshot give identical bytes.\n- Authored synopses (W05b.08) later override the text only; the mandatory fields remain generated.\n- Free-text field content inside a synopsis is still data (W05b.13).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every supported record type yields stable `title` and `summary` output.\n- [ ] Every compact current Conclusion or Decision exposes material caveats and effective freshness independently of synopsis text.\n- [ ] No synopsis exceeds the byte bound; none ends mid-field.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision \"Layered retrieval\": each layer is snapshot- and provenance-bound, with caveat and freshness indicators.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W01.14, W01.15, W03.12.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §8.3, §17.7 (drills 1–2), §20.1 (Layered retrieval) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"m","title":"W05a.07 Deterministic title and synopsis projections for every record type"}	blake3:AuqWA4tDOpFECJfDzsizoYjJ6HawlG5xkAIeDpRL660
1795321148236024	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-dhh	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`show ID --depth title|summary|full` is the middle of the retrieval ladder; `evidence read` (W06a.06) is the bottom rung.\n\n# Goals\nThe three depths exactly as specified, plus `--at <revision>`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Callers must opt into `full`/`evidence`; retrieval never implies external execution.\" Depth lets an agent skim forty records cheaply and read deeply only where it matters, without losing caveats.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `title`: stable ID, type, title, effective status, stale/disputed flag, synopsis availability.\n- `summary` (default): synopsis plus the separately required material caveats, evidence-reference IDs, summary basis and freshness. \"No raw artifact dump.\"\n- `full`: complete canonical typed record, history and current revisions, assessed relations, source/selector references, method limits. \"Still no implicit raw artifact expansion\", and no secret-bearing blob is loaded.\n- All depths include `snapshot`, `basis_revision`, `freshness` and `depth` in JSON.\n- `--at <revision>` returns historical state, labeled historical.\n- Short-ID resolution with ambiguity errors (W01.01).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each depth's field set matches the table in §8.3 for every record type.\n- [ ] `full` never contains artifact bytes.\n- [ ] `--at` shows an earlier revision without altering current state.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Agents can navigate title → synopsis → full record → evidence on demand.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.01, W05a.07, W02.09.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.3 (Normative navigation depths), §9.1 (show) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"m","title":"W05a.08 show with layered depths: title, summary (default) and full; historical revision reads"}	blake3:sf6VtRVVv-fF5hmQJgUsj9LfYaqz6B-31a88bJ6Coyw
1795321148314641	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3ad	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`brief --case C --level standard --max-bytes 16000 --format json` is how a fresh agent or human resumes. It returns, in order: (1) case question, scope, decision, snapshot, status; (2) current bounded conclusions or decisions with effective disposition and constraints; (3) decisive observations/comparisons and acquisition lineage; (4) strongest material contradiction, unresolved assumptions, any uncertain hard criterion; (5) recent changes if requested; (6) up to three next actions; (7) exact IDs/commands for deeper reading and an omission summary.\n\n# Goals\nThe `standard` level with a strict budget. Levels, `--since` and `--snapshot` are W05b.05.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Budget is serialized UTF-8 bytes so behavior is deterministic across models.\" The safety rules are the point: \"Never cut a JSON string mid-field, remove a critical caveat while retaining the conclusion, or silently drop all contradictions.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Build sections in priority order; drop whole optional items when over budget and count them in the omission summary.\n- Mandatory header and caveats are never dropped; if the budget cannot hold them, return `BUDGET_TOO_SMALL` with the minimum size.\n- One brief uses one verified snapshot, including its links.\n- Bounded and safe by classification: no raw logs, large documents, file secrets or local absolute paths.\n- Section 6 calls `next` (W05a.10) when available and degrades to an empty list with a note.\n- Navigation links carry workspace and snapshot.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The W01.17 handoff fixture yields a brief from which the expected successor content is recoverable.\n- [ ] Shrinking the budget removes optional detail before any caveat; below the minimum it returns `BUDGET_TOO_SMALL`.\n- [ ] Output is valid JSON at every budget and identical for identical snapshots.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPlan question 6: a new agent resumes \"from the case, without the original chat\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.08, W03.12, W01.17.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.3 (brief), §14.4, §16.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"l","title":"W05a.09 brief: bounded resumption context with a deterministic byte budget"}	blake3:eq7_UVsXnBAz6WmR50OLtOySa5L6pOZpfxGxQ_JJ-fU
1795321148395332	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1hr	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`next` inspects recorded state and returns decision-relevant, method-aware suggestions without an LLM call. It never invents a domain test or starts a process.\" `frontier` and `blindspots` are later views over the same rules.\n\n# Goals\nA versioned rule engine that emits gaps from recorded structure, and `next` returning at most three ranked recommendations for the slice.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The tool can deterministically know that two options have not been compared against the same criterion; it cannot know which database is best.\" Rules are \"versioned deterministic projections; never let a method pack install an executable checker.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Rule engine: each rule has a stable ID and version, inspects a snapshot, and emits gaps with a stable `gap_id` (rule + subject set), subject refs, reason and status.\n- Slice rules: asserted cause without rivals; hypothesis with no assessment; observation without validity context; unfrozen confirmatory plan; partial/unknown run to reconcile; missing or unknown hard-criterion cell; incomplete option×criterion cells; stale comparison/review; disputed conclusion; material case ready to close without a Retrospective.\n- Filter by prerequisites, case status, policy and explicit deferrals. A missing external permission is `execution_requires_consumer_check`, \"never a grant or automatic denial\".\n- Rank by the eight factors of §7.2 in order, ending with stable ID/creation order; expose each factor and its source.\n- `next` is read-only. \"Return at most three recommendations by default, plus counts of blocked/deferred actions.\"\n- Imported evidence can never inject guidance.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Identical snapshots produce identical recommendations and ranks.\n- [ ] Every recommendation lists its rank reasons.\n- [ ] No rule emits a domain test, a score or a shell command.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPlan question 4, answered honestly: point at the gap, never invent the experiment.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.08, W03.09, W03.12, W04.04, W04.07.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §7.1–§7.3, §8.5, §18.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"l","title":"W05a.10 Gap-rule engine and next v0: deterministic, transparent recommendations"}	blake3:4v5WgRcTwFslv0HLOiqHdhPVpl-uYxwn3Eoo0d2wXtU
1795321148475514	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-11j	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEach recommendation must carry the §7.3 contract, and for decision-changing advice must \"show the missing observation and how each plausible outcome would change eligibility, standing or the choice\" (review 3 #6).\n\n# Goals\nComplete recommendation objects, with outcome branches for typed thresholds.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe A-gate asks a successor \"whether another measurement would affect the choice\". The tool can answer that deterministically from a typed threshold: \"a pass keeps A eligible; a fail rules it out\". \"If every branch leads to the same disposition, say so and rank the step by its narrower value.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Contract fields: stable `action_id`, kind, target IDs/revisions; plain-English purpose and expected useful result; rank reasons and uncertainty; required inputs and unmet prerequisites; cost/bounds with provenance if supplied; `external_effects: none` or `consumer_managed` with a neutral information requirement; exact CLI operation/schema to inspect; a valid minimal payload where all facts are known; named unresolved fields otherwise; stop/deferral alternatives.\n- Outcome branches come from recorded outcome branches or deterministic consequences of a typed threshold; investigator-supplied forecasts are labeled with their source; unknown branch effects produce a request for that information.\n- Compact human output shows one leading recommendation, up to two alternatives on request.\n- \"Do not emit copy-paste JSON containing fake values such as `\\\"verified\\\": true`.\"\n- Never emit an executable shell command targeting an outside service.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] For an unknown hard-criterion cell, `next` shows pass/fail branches and their effect on eligibility.\n- [ ] Incomplete forms are labeled incomplete in JSON and prose.\n- [ ] External steps are marked `consumer_managed` and contain no command line.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDecision analysis [R4]: information that cannot change the choice has no decision value — and the tool says so.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.10.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.3, §7.2 (Say what the next observation would change), §7.3, Disposition: Agent experience and interface (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"m","title":"W05a.11 Recommendation contract and outcome branches: say what the next observation would change"}	blake3:EgdwAbnWVR3MRyeUU4ibh7bHKydaiTu4846hdwNnlxI
1795321148572829	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2fd	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv`, `inv --help`, and `inv onboard --format json` must describe the mental model, when to use this tool rather than just a shell/notebook, and the next *valid* action in one screen.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe top-level help text of §8.1 and a state-aware `onboard`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Outside a workspace, onboarding explains creation; inside, it diagnoses case state and suggests a concrete next read or mutation.\" The agent \"must not be sent straight to an observation payload whose prerequisite IDs do not exist\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Help: the one-screen block from §8.1 (Start, Learn, Resume, Decide, Audit, Inspect, Record, Recover) and the closing line that tool output is a source claim and `inv` never executes outside actions. Commands not yet implemented in the slice are omitted, not stubbed.\n- Include when *not* to use it: one-shot facts and ordinary implementation tasks.\n- `onboard` states: no workspace → `init`; empty workspace → one new-case route (`start` if the investigator has a source, `case.create` if only a question); selected case → scoped ingest or observation route; observation lacking source material → ingestion step first.\n- Output uses real workspace, case and created IDs.\n- Embed a versioned core-model summary equivalent to §21.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each of the four states returns a command that succeeds when run verbatim.\n- [ ] Help fits one screen and names no unimplemented command.\n- [ ] Nothing in onboarding contains fixture-specific solutions.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n§20.5 first-use measure, and the plan's \"genuinely self-teaching interface\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.04, W05a.06, W05a.09, W05a.10.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.1, §8.2, §9.1 (onboard), §21 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"m","title":"W05a.12 onboard and top-level help: state-aware first thirty seconds"}	blake3:c22aBWYnCDThC31jjb5zkRRpGGjpIfrde1HPmgrgpHY
1795321148646708	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2kx	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW01.08 defined the error envelope. This bone makes every CLI error state-aware and actionable.\n\n# Goals\nEach error an agent can hit in the slice has a concrete, correct repair.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n§8.7's examples set the bar, e.g.: \"No mutation committed. `operations[0].payload.evidence[0].artifact_id` refers to an unknown artifact. Ingest it first or use one of these three case-local artifacts.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Unknown reference → list a few valid case-local candidates and the creation route.\n- Revision conflict → current revision and a `show` argv; \"Read revision 4 and reassess; do not blindly replay the old validation.\"\n- Gate failure (exit 5) → machine-readable blockers with the schema to inspect for each.\n- Idempotent retry → \"The command was already committed under this command ID. Returning the original created IDs.\"\n- Post-commit projection problem → committed status plus the projection command to run.\n- Always state commit status explicitly; never a generic message; never an interactive prompt in machine mode.\n- A table-driven test maps each error code to an asserted repair that actually works.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] For every slice error code, following the repair resolves the error in a test.\n- [ ] Every rejection test asserts no mutation occurred.\n- [ ] No error text contains raw artifact content.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Ordinary schema/CAS error resolved without bespoke intervention.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.03, W05a.08.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.7, §9.6, §20.5 (Repairability) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"m","title":"W05a.13 Repairable errors in the CLI: what failed, what was committed, what remains true, what next"}	blake3:LgvDLoYzUEUA7NZxQlkM2cTD0Kpl_g9XAy4jjBjJIlA
1795321148722866	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-956	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe machine contract of §9.2 and the exit codes of §9.6 are normative: \"operation names, result envelopes, revision behavior, and persistence gates are implementation requirements.\"\n\n# Goals\nA test suite that pins the contract for every slice command.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAgents and the MCP layer depend on these properties; a regression here breaks every caller silently.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- For each command and each error class: stdout is exactly one JSON object in JSON mode; stderr holds logs; no ANSI escapes; no pager; no prompt even with a TTY attached.\n- Exit code matches the table for each class.\n- Golden outputs with normalized IDs, hashes and timestamps for stable diffing.\n- Text mode: `--no-color` and `--no-pager` honoured.\n- Help and schema outputs are snapshot-tested so accidental changes are visible in review.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every slice command is covered for success and at least one failure.\n- [ ] A deliberately added `println!` to stdout fails the suite.\n- [ ] Goldens are regenerated only by an explicit command.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps the stable response/error schema promised to CLI consumers in §3.2.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05a.12, W05a.13.\nParent goal: W05a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.2, §9.6, §20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-a","lane-agent-ux","cli","test"],"parent":"bn-3nm","size":"s","title":"W05a.14 CLI contract tests: single JSON object, exit codes, no ANSI, golden outputs"}	blake3:StrU_ZtTi0KsSmDLM-tO-UZBuXAPoLGV4IeBjFZ49zk
1795321148868927	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1h4	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv ingest --input manifest.json --format json` accepts a strict manifest with `schema_version`, `command_id`, exactly one of `case_id` or `new_case`, `actor`, `source`, `content`/`paths`/`external_refs`, extraction options and sensitivity policy.\"\n\n# Goals\nLocal-file and stdin ingestion that creates Source and Artifact records with exact retained bytes, in one committed transaction.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Artifact/source creation with retained bytes is done via `ingest` or `capture` to prevent nonexistent-but-verified blobs.\" A manifest is data: \"Producer-provided metadata is not an executable instruction.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Strict manifest schema in the catalog; `schema ingest` returns a working local-file example.\n- Stream each input to a private temp file while hashing (outside the lock); detect source mutation during collection; \"Never claim integrity for bytes different from retained bytes.\"\n- Record acquisition identity, time with clock quality, producer assertion, method and gaps; unknown fields stay unknown.\n- Commit only once required blob bytes are durable. Return per-item outcomes: imported, deduplicated, unsupported, missing, rejected, with canonical IDs.\n- `new_case` creates an `explore` case and its sources in the same transaction; a failed import publishes neither.\n- Default classification `internal`; honour stricter requested classifications.\n- \"No silent fallback to reference mode after disk full.\"\n- Reference mode, re-import lineage and extraction options beyond text decoding are W06b.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Ingested bytes read back identical; digest and size match.\n- [ ] A file modified during ingestion is detected and rejected or recorded as such.\n- [ ] Identical bytes ingested twice give two acquisition records and one blob.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nSource and artifact custody: \"the exact bytes, content-addressed, with where they came from\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.07, W01.11, W06a.02.\nParent goal: W06a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.4 (Storage and input rules), §11.1, §16.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-d8y","size":"m","title":"W06a.01 ingest manifest and local snapshot into the blob store, with the new_case variant"}	blake3:0VAYPYyQ5NinbinwmGpm1mo6TDpuKWjrmszkRv8hbkA
1795321148954636	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2yd	{"description":"# Context & Background\nIngestion step 1: \"Resolve paths only under configured import roots; prevent symlink traversal/TOCTOU surprises with safe descriptor/open rules and explicit limits.\"\n\n# Goals\nA single path-opening helper used by every file-reading entry point.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAn agent may be asked by hostile evidence to ingest `../../.ssh/id_ed25519`. The tool must make that fail by construction, not by review.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Import roots configured per workspace (default: the invocation directory given explicitly; never the whole filesystem).\n- Open relative to a root directory descriptor with no-follow semantics for intermediate components (e.g. `openat2` with `RESOLVE_BENEATH` where available, with a documented fallback).\n- After open, `fstat` the descriptor and read from it — never re-open by path (closes the check/use race).\n- Reject symlink and hardlink escapes, device files and FIFOs unless explicitly streamed via stdin.\n- Explicit size limits with a clear error; stdin is a separate, bounded channel.\n- \"Treat MIME/extension as hints; do not execute imported content to identify it.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Symlink and `..` traversal fixtures do not escape the intended roots.\n- [ ] A file swapped for a symlink between check and open is not followed.\n- [ ] Errors name the root and the offending path without reading the target.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n§20.2: \"Symlink/traversal/archive expansion fixtures do not escape the intended roots.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02.02.\nParent goal: W06a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.1 (step 1), §16.4, §20.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-a","lane-storage","security"],"parent":"bn-d8y","size":"m","title":"W06a.02 Safe path resolution under configured import roots"}	blake3:K_dRDrtqXW92cKoQXtGOFjP6GMgFGdesK2ztvSkyPek
1795321149036308	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-32m	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`text_lines` and `text_quote` selectors apply to \"a named exact decoded-text derivative with its own digest, encoding and parser version\".\n\n# Goals\nCreate a text derivative Artifact from an original when requested or needed, linked by lineage.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Preserve original bytes exactly; transformations create derivatives and lineage edges.\" Line numbers and quotes are only meaningful against a specific decoding, so the derivative must be a first-class, digest-bound artifact rather than an implicit on-the-fly decode.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Decoder: UTF-8 strict by default; declared alternative encodings only from a bounded, trusted list; record encoding, newline handling and parser version.\n- The derivative is stored as its own blob with `derived_from` the original digest and the transform identity.\n- No normalization beyond the declared decoding; quotes are checked byte-exact against the derivative.\n- Undecodable input yields an explicit outcome and no derivative; byte-range selectors remain available.\n- Deterministic: same original and parser version give the same derivative digest.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A derivative's digest is stable across runs and recorded with its parser version.\n- [ ] The original blob is unchanged.\n- [ ] Invalid UTF-8 produces a clear per-item outcome.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 3: provenance is a graph, with transformations separately identified.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W06a.01.\nParent goal: W06a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.4, §11.1 (step 4), §11.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-d8y","size":"s","title":"W06a.03 Decoded-text derivatives with their own digest, encoding and parser version"}	blake3:NN3ttva4Es8vylq-jouPStevH75v8iiC4kGEKfWEtFQ
1795321149117982	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3c9	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"The core validates offsets and native selectors against retained bytes where possible.\"\n\n# Goals\nValidation and resolution of the four selector kinds the slice needs.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA selector that points outside the artifact, or at an artifact that is not retained, must not be recorded as verified. \"`whole` on a missing external reference remains unverified.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `whole`: entire exact artifact; flagged as \"sensible only for bounded relevant inputs\" above a size threshold.\n- `byte_range`: half-open, zero-based offsets on retained original bytes; bounds-checked.\n- `text_lines`: one-based inclusive span on a named text derivative (W06a.03).\n- `json_pointer`: RFC 6901 against the exact JSON artifact, using the strict parser (W01.04); record parser identity where interpretation is ambiguous.\n- Each returns a validation state: `verified`, `invalid` (with reason), or `unavailable` when bytes or a parser are absent.\n- Parsers are bounded, trusted code; \"no command execution from source metadata\".\n- A resolver API returns the selected bytes for `evidence read` (W06a.06).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Out-of-range offsets and lines are rejected with the valid range.\n- [ ] A JSON pointer to a missing member is `invalid`, not `unavailable`.\n- [ ] A selector on a non-retained artifact is `unavailable`, never `verified`.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes \"exact selector\" a checked property.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W06a.03.\nParent goal: W06a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.4, §11.5 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-d8y","size":"m","title":"W06a.04 Native selector validation: whole, byte_range, text_lines, json_pointer"}	blake3:l-HS58mv9Ul44tMqFDwc1IS5IE3MTM5kO42GVlifcAY
1795321149200399	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3l3	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`text_quote` is \"exact quoted text plus optional prefix/suffix (W3C Web Annotation `TextQuoteSelector` shape) on a named decoded-text derivative. The core checks byte-exact presence, with no normalization, and returns `verified`, `ambiguous` (more than one match without disambiguating context) or `not_found`.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe quote checker used by `observation.add` (W03.03) and the `observe --quote` shortcut.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 2 #2: language models fabricate plausible quotations. A byte-exact check is cheap and catches that mechanically. The limit is stated just as plainly: \"A verified quote shows the words are in the source. It does not show that they support the claim.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Search the derivative for the exact byte sequence; apply prefix/suffix to disambiguate.\n- Outcomes: `verified` (one match, or one after context), `ambiguous` (several), `not_found`. `not_found` rejects the operation; `ambiguous` is reported.\n- No whitespace, case or Unicode normalization. Error text may suggest the nearest near-miss location to help repair, clearly labeled as a hint.\n- Cross-check: a supplied positional selector should contain the quote; report disagreement.\n- Numeric and binary evidence mark the quote `not_applicable`.\n- The receipt carries the outcome and the wording \"presence, not support\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A text Observation whose `text_quote` is `not_found` is rejected; `ambiguous` is reported; a verified quote is labeled as presence, not support.\n- [ ] A quote differing only by whitespace is `not_found`.\n- [ ] Prefix/suffix turns an ambiguous quote into a verified one.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA mechanical floor under \"observations ... with quoted text checked mechanically\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W06a.03.\nParent goal: W06a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.4, §6.4, §8.5, §20.1 (Cross-domain), Disposition: Agent experience and interface (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-a","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-d8y","size":"m","title":"W06a.05 text_quote verification: verified, ambiguous or not_found, with no normalization"}	blake3:Df-rAEelBiDky_JJFV4l5E6XdaL6erQejJDUxw59c1Y
1795321149288667	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-184	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv evidence read ARTIFACT --selector-file selector.json` bounds decoded output and clearly marks source content as untrusted evidence. Binary data stays in files.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe explicit, opt-in bottom rung of layered retrieval.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Captured pages, logs, source code, tool output, imported notes, and packets can contain instructions addressed to an agent.\" Evidence is data, not instructions. \"Quoting with a Markdown `>` alone does not make content inert.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Resolve the selector (W06a.04); enforce an output byte bound with an explicit truncation marker and total size.\n- JSON: excerpt in a string field beside `untrusted: true`, provenance (artifact digest, selector, source), availability state.\n- Text mode: render in a safely delimited code block with a fixed non-executable language, choosing \"a delimiter longer than any matching run in the excerpt\"; never use a source-provided fence language.\n- Binary artifacts: metadata and a path hint only, never bytes on stdout.\n- Unavailable bytes return a clear unavailable state, never empty content.\n- Restricted artifacts require an explicit opt-in flag.\n- \"Retrieval must not execute HTML, JavaScript, Markdown directives, macros, or shell code.\"\n- Record the read (actor, artifact, session) for trust-level inheritance (W10.18).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An excerpt containing backtick runs and fake fences stays inside its delimiter.\n- [ ] Output never exceeds the bound; truncation is explicit.\n- [ ] A missing artifact reports unavailability rather than an empty excerpt.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n§16.1: source content \"cannot modify the command catalog, authorize a test, install a plugin, change an actor, or inject a recommendation\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W06a.04, W05a.01.\nParent goal: W06a. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.3 (evidence depth), §8.4, §9.1 (evidence read), §16.1, §16.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-a","lane-storage","security","cli"],"parent":"bn-d8y","size":"m","title":"W06a.06 evidence read: bounded excerpt marked as untrusted content"}	blake3:HbBBZZoG50QRem6K5SGz7NQj_VjV96q-KDhnIhAczLk
1795321243870444	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-nb0	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Each milestone ends in a runnable proof, not a skeleton of directories.\" Milestone A's claim: \"Two cases work: debugging and architecture choice, using the same store and CLI. A reviewer sees exact support/assumptions, layered read depths, and a second agent can resume. Material cases close with a minimal truthful Retrospective; others may waive it with a reason.\"\n\n# Goals\nOne recorded demonstration that the slice does what the milestone says, kept as evidence for the A-gate and later audits.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nW03.16 and W04.10 prove each loop mechanically. This bone adds what scripts cannot: an unfamiliar agent, given only the binary and a workspace, resumes each case from committed state. It is a smoke check, not the controlled comparison (that is AG).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Build the binary from a clean checkout; run W03.16 and W04.10 scripts; keep the resulting workspaces.\n- Reviewer walk: from a conclusion (Scenario A) and a decision (Scenario C), follow title → summary → full → `evidence read` to the exact selector, and list the assumptions shown.\n- Resume check: start a fresh agent session per case with no prior context; it may use only ordinary help and the workspace. Ask what is currently concluded or chosen, the strongest caveat and the next useful observation. Compare with the fixture's expected properties.\n- Closure check: one material case closed with a Retrospective, one non-material case closed with a waiver.\n- Invariant check against the \"Never weaken these invariants\" list, one line of evidence each.\n- Write a short proof note (what was run, versions, outcome, known limitations) under `docs/`.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Both scenario scripts pass on the same binary with no scenario-specific code path.\n- [ ] The fresh agent states the current answer/choice, strongest caveat and next step correctly for both cases, or the gaps are recorded as defects.\n- [ ] The proof note is committed with exact versions and limitations.\n- [ ] No invariant was weakened for the demo.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nIt is the object the A-gate measures; without it the pilot would be comparing notes against a prototype that does not yet hold together.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W02, W03, W04, W05a, W06a.\nParent goal: MA. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.1, §19.3 (Milestone A), §14.4, Build rules (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["milestone-a","lane-evaluation","test"],"parent":"bn-1hp","size":"m","title":"MA.01 Milestone A runnable proof: both cases on one binary, and a fresh agent resumes each"}	blake3:1mMo1SFxC92CzuJsrkCghlE38uyAh1nFN95kH1m25kc
1795321244003349	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3pr	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.5 Conditions: \"Use at least two agent families or hosts. Match each case across two arms: plain Markdown notes with normal file/search tools, and the same tools plus `inv`... Both arms get the same objective, source bytes, time/token budget and handoff boundary, discovery only through ordinary help, and no evaluator coaching. Counterbalance run order, pin model/host/tool versions and prevent session carryover.\"\n\n# Goals\nA written protocol and a harness under `training/` and `scripts/` that runs one case in one arm reproducibly: investigator session, forced interruption, fresh successor session, answer capture.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe comparison is only evidence if the arms differ in exactly one thing. Most ways of fooling oneself here are procedural: unequal budgets, a coached prompt, a successor that inherits context, or a harness that leaks the arm to graders.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Protocol document: unit of analysis is the case; arms; budgets; interruption rule; what the successor receives (the workspace or the notes directory, nothing else); what is recorded.\n- Harness: creates an isolated working directory per run; provides identical tools to both arms, adding only the `investigation` binary to one; enforces time/token budgets; kills the first session at the handoff boundary; starts a clean successor.\n- The `inv` arm gets no AGENTS snippet or skill unless the notes arm gets an equivalent-length generic note-taking hint; record the choice in the protocol.\n- Counterbalanced order per case and agent family; versions of model, host and tools written to each run's metadata.\n- Outputs: raw transcripts, final answer file, cost counters (AG.06), workspace/notes snapshot.\n- The harness never calls a model from inside `inv`; agents are external.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A dry run on a development case completes in both arms with identical budgets and separate clean sessions.\n- [ ] Run metadata pins every version and the random order assignment.\n- [ ] Nothing in the harness or prompts contains case-specific hints.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nSupplies the evidence the product hypothesis has never had.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.06.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.5 (Conditions, Primary outcome, Cost), §18.1 (training/), §19.3 (A-gate) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"l","title":"AG.01 Trial protocol and paired-arm harness: matched budgets, pinned versions, no carryover"}	blake3:WN3dSDRJzfWwG_cef9qJu93-qXDbW0OXApaghTaoeyA
1795321244096793	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2vs	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Plain scratchpads are a strong baseline.\" All three reviews asked for a notes arm; the no-tool arm is optional because \"the notes arm is the comparison that matters\".\n\n# Goals\nA precise definition of what the notes arm may use and how its handoff is delivered.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA weak baseline would make any win meaningless. The notes arm must be what a competent agent would actually do without `inv`: keep a Markdown file, quote sources, use file and search tools.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Tools: the same file read/write/search/shell tools as the `inv` arm, minus the binary.\n- A notes directory that persists to the successor; no structure imposed, no template beyond an empty `NOTES.md`.\n- Instruction parity: both arms receive the same objective text and the same sentence about handing off to a successor.\n- Decide and record whether the notes arm is told to cite sources (it must be, if the answer format requires a decisive citation).\n- Optional no-tool arm: same budget, no persistent notes; include only if cheap.\n- Have someone not building `inv` review the arm definition for fairness.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Arm definition is in the protocol with its fairness review recorded.\n- [ ] A pilot dry run shows the notes arm can produce the required answer format.\n- [ ] The only difference between arms is the presence of the binary (plus any declared, symmetric hint).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes the central comparison honest in the direction that could embarrass the project.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): AG.01.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md Binding decisions (Product hypothesis), §20.5 (Conditions), Disposition: Evaluation and sequencing (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"s","title":"AG.02 Define the plain-Markdown-notes arm so it is a strong, fair baseline"}	blake3:DXvCfEEZmoar6jvY91yCbX6Ap0QpSl77wqUS2_3-ai8
1795321244184125	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-20z	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Primary outcome: the successor's answer... a fresh successor answers what can be concluded or chosen, the decisive citation, the strongest limitation and what would change it. Both arms submit the same short answer format. Normalize citations to seed-document selectors and strip tool identifiers before graders blinded to the arm see answers.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe answer template, a rubric written before any run, and a pipeline that blinds graders.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nGrading tool mechanics would reward the tool for existing. Grading the successor's bounded answer measures what a user cares about. The rubric must allow \"every choice consistent with the stated preferences and constraints\" so it does not punish a defensible different decision.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Answer template with four fields: conclusion or choice (including \"cannot conclude\"), decisive citation, strongest limitation, what would change it.\n- Normalizer: map `inv` IDs/selectors and free-text citations alike to seed-document selectors; strip tool names, ID prefixes and formatting tells.\n- Rubric per case: acceptable answers, required caveats, unwarranted promotions, missed warranted answers. Count both error directions.\n- Tool-specific mechanics are graded separately and never mixed into the primary outcome.\n- Gate-content items: were recorded rivals real alternatives with a distinguishing prediction; do retrospectives state anything checkable (strawman rate).\n- If LLM graders are used, \"treat them as correlated, not independent\"; include a human-graded sample for agreement.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Rubrics are committed (outside agent-visible paths) before AG.07 starts.\n- [ ] A blinding test: graders cannot identify the arm above chance on a sample.\n- [ ] Both error directions are scored.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMeasures epistemic honesty of outcomes rather than activity.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): AG.01.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.5 (Primary outcome, Gate content, Decision rule), Disposition: Evaluation and sequencing (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"m","title":"AG.03 Successor answer format, prewritten rubric and arm-blinded grading pipeline"}	blake3:E0yVbiJvJ89NSvR5_1f1MQ1ABBIWSU1xAbogemjVnQc
1795321244267757	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2j1	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Split development, pilot and sealed evaluation cases by underlying case family, with all variants in the same split.\" The A-gate uses \"pilot-split cases\" in two domains.\n\n# Goals\nThe split policy and a set of pilot case families for debugging and architecture choice, distinct from the development fixtures (Scenario A and C) that builders see daily.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nIf the pilot reused the development scenarios, onboarding and rules tuned on them would leak into the result. Twins test whether the tool changes answers for the right reasons.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Policy: a family is one underlying situation; all its variants live in one split; record the split of every family in a registry. Sealed families are W14.01 and are written by someone not working on blindspot rules or onboarding.\n- Pilot families: several per domain, each with seed sources, an objective, and a rubric (AG.03).\n- Twins per family where sensible: sufficient-evidence; missing-evidence (remove a decisive source); cosmetically changed (rename an option, add a duplicate citation, flip an operative criterion).\n- Include at least one one-shot lookup \"where the right move is to skip the tool\".\n- Keys and rubrics live outside the agents' workspace.\n- Synthetic, domain-neutral content; no named real products required to solve a case.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Split registry exists and no pilot family shares a situation with a development fixture.\n- [ ] Each pilot family has an objective, seeds, twins where applicable and a rubric.\n- [ ] Enough independent families exist per domain to estimate between-case variability (number justified in the protocol).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe experimental unit is \"an independent case, not a repeat\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W00.11.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.3 (A-gate), §20.5 (Held-out case families, Paired twins) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-evaluation","fixture"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"l","title":"AG.04 Case-family split policy and pilot cases for debugging and architecture choice, with twins"}	blake3:9-Scnfdgwkw3gJGNZJjNsUUdUcq3cCrVVrn5NknUuok
1795321244350650	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-45v	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Use the bring-your-own-source exercise: an unfamiliar investigator starts from one local source and hands off to a successor; a new hard criterion is inserted between review and acceptance; the successor says what changed and whether another measurement would affect the choice.\" (Review 3, \"Implementation decision\".)\n\n# Goals\nA scripted exercise template that can be instantiated on any pilot architecture-choice family, plus the debugging-domain analogue.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThis single exercise touches the three things the slice claims to do better than notes: one-call capture from a raw source (`start`), detection of a basis change that leaves every revision number intact (W04.05), and telling the successor what a further observation would change (W05a.11).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Stage 1: the investigator receives one local source file and an objective; nothing else.\n- Stage 2: after a review is recorded (or, in the notes arm, after the investigator states the review is done), the harness injects a new hard criterion as a new source document delivered identically in both arms.\n- Stage 3: handoff. The successor must state what changed since the review and whether another measurement would affect the choice.\n- Rubric additions: did the successor notice the inserted criterion; did it avoid treating the earlier review as still current; is the \"would a measurement matter\" answer consistent with the stated thresholds.\n- Debugging analogue: a correction to a decisive observation arrives after review.\n- Injection is by evidence only, never by instruction to the agent.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Exercise runs identically in both arms with the same injected bytes at the same point.\n- [ ] Rubric items for the three successor questions are written in advance.\n- [ ] A dry run on a development family works end to end.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTests the plan's central promise: a successor can trust what is current and what silently stopped being true.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): AG.04.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.3 (A-gate), §10.3, Disposition: Evaluation and sequencing (Bring-your-own-source handoff) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-evaluation","fixture"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"m","title":"AG.05 Bring-your-own-source handoff exercise with a hard criterion inserted between review and acceptance"}	blake3:1G-xuvV_nZWgvIU-cN35YiQKfouWGhOjGoRmS8lTtJs
1795321244440074	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2fk	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Report tokens, tool calls, help/schema bytes and wall time per arm from fresh start through handoff. Matching the notes arm on correctness at much higher cost is a failure.\"\n\n# Goals\nCounters in the harness that capture burden for both arms identically.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA structured tool can win on correctness and still lose as a product if agents spend their budget reading schemas. The plan treats cost as part of the decision rule, so it must be measured from the first pilot run.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Per run: input and output tokens, number of tool calls, wall time, bytes returned by `investigation` help/schema/onboard calls, bytes of evidence read, file-preparation steps (e.g. writing an envelope file).\n- First-use metric: number of calls after onboarding until the first useful receipt.\n- Record the same counters for the notes arm (note-file bytes read and written).\n- Emit a per-run JSON record joined to case, arm, agent family and order.\n- Keep raw logs so counts can be re-derived.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every run yields a complete cost record for both sessions (investigator and successor).\n- [ ] Counters are validated against a hand-counted dry run.\n- [ ] Help/schema bytes are separable from evidence bytes.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps \"the same binary works offline and does not spend tokens on storage or quality checks\" accountable in practice.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): AG.01.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.7, §20.5 (Cost; First use and total burden; Context efficiency) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"s","title":"AG.06 Cost instrumentation: tokens, tool calls, help/schema bytes and wall time per arm"}	blake3:zbkyG_YyKTzMycglfeMxy3blDIoE8nnZ-IkfMQj1jUE
1795321244524569	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-f9r	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Before W07, run a two-domain paired pilot (debugging and architecture choice, pilot-split cases) against the plain-notes arm of §20.5.\"\n\n# Goals\nExecute the pilot exactly as the protocol says and preserve everything.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe pilot has two jobs: apply the pre-recorded decision rule, and estimate between-case variability for sizing the release trial. Both need clean execution more than they need volume.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Confirm the decision-rule ADR (W00.06) is merged and predates the first run; record its commit hash in the pilot log.\n- Freeze the binary version under test; no fixes during the pilot except documented harness bugs, which invalidate affected runs.\n- Run every pilot family in both arms for each agent family/host, counterbalanced, including the AG.05 exercise.\n- Include timeouts and crashes in the data; do not rerun selectively.\n- Grade through the blinded pipeline (AG.03).\n- Archive transcripts, workspaces, notes directories, answers, grades and cost records with a manifest.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All planned runs are accounted for: completed, timed out or invalidated with a reason.\n- [ ] At least two agent families or hosts were used with pinned versions.\n- [ ] Raw data is archived and reproducible from the manifest.\n- [ ] No run was graded by someone who knew its arm.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the experiment on which \"the rest of the build depends\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): MA.01, AG.02, AG.03, AG.04, AG.05, AG.06, W00.06.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.3 (A-gate), §20.5 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"m","title":"AG.07 Run the A-gate pilot with at least two agent families or hosts"}	blake3:suTs-vOtjNZhX3HlOlDv9dTsPT7Aka8PIR9ArriLPxw
1795321244611199	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3i1	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"If `inv` does not beat the notes arm on false promotions or successor accuracy, cut or merge the record types agents ignored or misused before continuing. Use the pilot to estimate between-case variability for the release trial.\"\n\n# Goals\nA written analysis that applies the pre-recorded rule mechanically and states what the pilot can and cannot support.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Twenty runs cannot separate 90% from 80% success.\" A pilot is not a release claim; over-reading it would be the product failing its own standard.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Paired differences per case on successor accuracy and on unwarranted promotions and missed warranted answers, with intervals clustered by case; per-model and per-domain breakdowns; repeat reliability where repeats exist.\n- Cost per arm, including first-use burden.\n- From journals in the `inv` arm: which record types and operations were never used, used emptily, or misused; strawman-rival rate; whether retrospectives said anything checkable.\n- Between-case variance estimate with its own uncertainty.\n- Apply W00.06 exactly; record the outcome (continue / simplify named types / stop and rethink) as an ADR.\n- List threats to validity and what is not claimed.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The analysis report is committed with data pointers and limitations.\n- [ ] The decision is traceable to the pre-recorded rule with no post-hoc criteria.\n- [ ] \"Zero observed\" is never reported without its upper bound.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nConverts a belief about the product into a bounded, cited conclusion — the product's own method applied to itself.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): AG.07.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.3 (A-gate), §20.5 (Sample size and reporting, Decision rule) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"m","title":"AG.08 Analyze the pilot, estimate between-case variability and apply the decision rule"}	blake3:aNqXDW7l7y52MYBLqW8QH5bhkjlZiEcf1TIhIVb7DnM
1795321244689598	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-v3a	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe decision rule's consequence: \"cut or merge the record types agents ignored or misused before continuing.\" The catalog is still `v0-alpha` precisely so this is cheap.\n\n# Goals\nThe catalog and handlers reflect the pilot outcome before anything is frozen.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 2's caution applies broadly: \"agents fill fields whether or not they can justify them.\" A field or type that pilots show is filled with boilerplate adds cost and false assurance. Removing it now costs a refactor; removing it after v1 costs a migration.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- For each type or field flagged by AG.08: decide cut, merge, make optional, or keep with a UX fix; record each decision and its evidence in an ADR.\n- Update catalog, handlers, examples, onboarding and both scenario scripts; W03.16 and W04.10 must still pass.\n- Preserve the invariants in \"Build rules\" regardless of what is cut; a cut that would weaken one requires its own ADR and updated tests.\n- If the outcome is \"continue unchanged\", write that down with the evidence; this bone is then a short confirmation.\n- If the outcome is \"stop and rethink\", close this bone with that record and raise the question with the project owner before AG.10.\n- Upcast readers (W02.13) keep pilot-era journals readable.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every flagged item has a recorded disposition.\n- [ ] Scenario scripts and the crash suite pass after changes.\n- [ ] No binding decision was changed without an ADR and tests.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"If `inv` does not help a successor more than plain notes, simplify before building further.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): AG.08.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.3 (A-gate), §19.5, Disposition: Evaluation and sequencing (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"m","title":"AG.09 Apply the pilot outcome: cut or merge misused record types, or record that none are needed"}	blake3:dsip0NGyckkZ3-FJPrV-0pZyYPnFXSHmYTJ5ypWay5k
1795321244792943	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-irt	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"The wider catalog freezes as `v1` after the Milestone A comparison.\" §19.3 ends the A-gate with: \"then freeze `inv.command.v1`.\"\n\n# Goals\nRename `v0-alpha` to `v1`, publish JSON Schemas under `schemas/`, and state the compatibility promise that now applies.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nEverything in Milestones B–D (recorder, vault, MCP, packs, external consumers) builds on these names and envelopes. §3.6's promise begins here: \"Stable versioned schema; unknown optional source metadata retained opaquely... explicit failures for unsupported required versions.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Finalize `inv.command.v1`, `inv.response.v1` and every v1 operation payload; confirm names reserved for later operations (coverage, synopsis role, extensions, measurement/source assessment, capture) do not collide.\n- Generate and commit schemas to `schemas/` from the catalog; CI checks they are in sync.\n- Add a schema-compatibility test: any later change to a v1 schema must be additive-optional or a new version.\n- Upcast path from `v0-alpha` journals is tested (W02.13).\n- Write a short \"what v1 promises\" document for consumers.\n- Announce the freeze in the ADR index; later substantive scope changes require ADRs.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `schemas/` matches the catalog and CI enforces it.\n- [ ] A breaking change to a v1 schema fails CI.\n- [ ] Pilot-era workspaces open and replay under v1 code.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nGives external producers and later lanes a stable contract at the moment the evidence says it deserves one.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): AG.09.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.6, §18.1 (schemas/), §19.2 (W01), §19.3 (A-gate), §19.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"m","title":"AG.10 Freeze inv.command.v1 and the wider catalog; publish versioned schemas"}	blake3:zNIwWjOoQAgpkXDMtWylmKLtd0IckNeRPqodt3xCsqU
1795321244879441	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1am	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Estimate between-case variability at the Milestone A pilot, then preregister the primary outcome, minimum useful difference, burden limit and sample size.\"\n\n# Goals\nA committed preregistration document that W14.08 must follow.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReviews 1 and 2 proposed fixed counts (≥5 runs per scenario per arm, about 100 per arm). Revision 4 modified this: \"A fixed per-scenario count measures repeats of one case, not other cases.\" Sample size is therefore derived from pilot variability, and fixed before the sealed set is opened.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Primary outcome: successor answer correctness per the blinded rubric.\n- Choose one release criterion in advance: \"a meaningful gain in successor correctness within the cost budget, or noninferior correctness within a stated margin with a meaningful burden reduction.\"\n- State the minimum useful difference, the noninferiority margin if used, and the burden limit.\n- Number of independent sealed case families and repeats per family, justified from the AG.08 variance estimate.\n- Analysis plan: paired differences, case-clustered intervals, per-model and per-domain breakdowns, repeat reliability, handling of timeouts.\n- What happens if results are inconclusive: \"report the uncertainty and withhold the improvement claim.\"\n- Commit the document; its hash is recorded before any sealed case is opened.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Preregistration committed with a timestamped hash prior to W14.08.\n- [ ] It contains outcome, criterion, margins, burden limit, sample size and analysis plan.\n- [ ] The sample-size derivation cites pilot data.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA frozen plan before results is the project's own standard for calling something confirmatory.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): AG.08.\nParent goal: AG. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.5 (Sample size and reporting, Decision rule), Disposition: Evaluation and sequencing (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["a-gate","lane-evaluation","docs"],"parent":"bn-2s2","size":"s","title":"AG.11 Preregister the release trial: primary outcome, minimum useful difference, burden limit, sample size"}	blake3:tTK_X1DkHJ7zu9M033KZuEwejBWByJSnxHXhcqKo714
1795321339941687	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-20w	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv frontier --case C --format json` is a read-only complete, filterable inventory of investigable gaps at a committed snapshot, while `inv next` provides a ranked top three.\" It adapts Sapper's fact/intent frontier into an *epistemic* frontier.\n\n# Goals\nThe full inventory view over the gap-rule engine built in W05a.10.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A frontier item is not an external task assignment, an instruction to execute a test, proof that the suggested method is good, or a claim that all missing alternatives were discovered.\" Treating unknowns as work tickets is exactly what the plan declines to copy.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Statuses: `ready_to_analyze`, `requires_source`, `requires_external_action`, `blocked_by_quality`, `stale_due_to_revision`, `deferred`, `resolved`.\n- Each item: exact subject/read-set, source of the gap, reason for status, prerequisites, and a safe `schema`/`show` route.\n- Flags: `--kind`, `--status`, `--limit`, snapshot-bound pagination cursors, and `--explain GAP_ID` returning the deterministic rule that emitted it.\n- \"Mark structural rule IDs/versions in the response so a changed rule is not mistaken for newly acquired evidence.\"\n- Deduplicate subjects; deferred items stay visible.\n- `inv` does not create or dispatch workers; external systems may translate items into tasks.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `frontier` returns the same snapshot-bound missing/blocked/stale items as the complete unranked inventory behind `next`, with stable rule IDs, deduplicated subjects and honest prerequisites.\n- [ ] Pagination across a concurrent write stays on the original snapshot.\n- [ ] `--explain` names the rule and version for any `gap_id`.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nShows everything unresolved without pretending to schedule it.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.3, §7.5, §9.1 (frontier), §20.1 (Layered retrieval / frontier) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"l","title":"W05b.01 frontier: complete, filterable inventory of investigable gaps at a snapshot"}	blake3:XthG_qSsOlveP7cbQNevgq_ta4qb2b8IleQTBqmTAN8
1795321340476346	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-30z	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW05a.10 shipped `next` v0. Ranking factor 4 is \"Decision-changing value: unresolved cells or assumptions that could change a hard-criterion disposition or an option's Pareto status, or that discriminate live rivals (`could_flip_choice`, `could_discriminate`, `context_only`, `unknown`).\"\n\n# Goals\nComplete the eight-factor ranking and scale it to large sparse cases.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nValue of information [R4], adopted from review 1 #6: \"information that cannot change the choice has no decision value.\" Hence: \"Deprioritize measuring soft criteria on options already hard-disqualified or strictly dominated.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Classify each gap's decision-changing value using Pareto status (W04.07) and per-rival predictions (W03.04).\n- Factors 5–7: applicability to the current question and decision deadline; user-supplied bounded cost if comparable; novelty versus previous failed or inconclusive attempts.\n- Group repeated gaps by question and criterion with snapshot-bound drill-down (needed for the 1,000 × 200 sparse fixture, W05b.15).\n- When no feasible step can affect the decision, offer a justified stop or defer.\n- Report counts of blocked and deferred actions with a pagination/query path.\n- Still a heuristic: \"not a calculated expected information gain or probability\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `next` does not suggest soft-criterion measurement for a hard-disqualified option.\n- [ ] A step whose every outcome leaves the disposition unchanged is ranked by its narrower value and says so.\n- [ ] Rank factors and their sources are present on every recommendation.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPlan question 4: what \"would most reduce decision-relevant uncertainty\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b.01.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §7.2, §10.5, §15.1, §20.1 (Evaluative), Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"m","title":"W05b.02 next, full ranking: decision-changing value, gap grouping, blocked and deferred counts"}	blake3:i2MGpM2bnism4WCMwytaEul63TPD0lINIV90mCLkMmQ
1795321340983117	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3o6	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Detect explicit lack of progress: repeated inconclusive runs with the same failed prerequisite, recurring duplicate import attempts, unchanged blockers, and exhausted candidate tests.\"\n\n# Goals\nDeterministic stall patterns surfaced through `next`/`frontier`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Report the pattern without inferring an agent's motivation.\" And: \"Never keep a case alive by fabricating a new hypothesis.\" A justified stop is a legitimate outcome.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Rules over the journal: N consecutive inconclusive runs sharing a failed prerequisite; repeated ingest of already-known acquisitions; blockers unchanged across M transactions; all planned inquiries retired or inconclusive.\n- Each pattern offers a specific move: verify the instrument, inspect raw data, narrow the question, change the observation channel, revisit a key assumption, request missing evidence, or stop with an inconclusive result.\n- Point at the `stalled` guidance card when packs are available (W09.05).\n- Thresholds are constants in the rule version, disclosed in the response.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A fixture with repeated same-prerequisite failures produces the stall finding with the journal items cited.\n- [ ] No stall rule proposes a new hypothesis or test.\n- [ ] A justified stop is among the offered moves.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nHelps a case end honestly instead of churning.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b.02.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §7.4, §2.4 (stalled card) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"s","title":"W05b.03 Stalled-investigation detection with specific, non-fabricating moves"}	blake3:8Ar2stILdNfhiUPOaPYbQJLyvznh-PhRXJAUqZFBiAc
1795321341348898	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-118	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"An investigator can defer a recurring suggestion with `annotation.add` `role: advice_feedback` (rule/version, target IDs, material basis, `defer` or `resume`, reason, optional revisit condition).\" (Review 3 #6.)\n\n# Goals\nImplement the reserved `advice_feedback` role and honour it in `next` and `frontier`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAdvice that cannot be dismissed trains agents to ignore it. But \"feedback changes only how advice is presented; the underlying gap, caveats and validation still come from canonical evidence and policy.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Payload: rule ID and version, target IDs, the material basis at the time (so it can be re-evaluated), disposition, reason, optional revisit condition.\n- A deferred gap moves to status `deferred` in `frontier` and out of the `next` top three; it stays visible.\n- \"The advice is re-evaluated on material change or explicit resumption.\"\n- Deferral never satisfies a gate and never hides a caveat in `brief` or `show`.\n- A changed rule version re-surfaces the advice with that reason.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Deferring removes the item from `next` but not from `frontier`.\n- [ ] A material change to the target re-surfaces it.\n- [ ] A deferred blocker still blocks its promotion.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps guidance useful without letting it be silenced into false assurance.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b.01.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §7.5 (Correcting advice), §9.4 (annotation.add), Disposition: Agent experience and interface (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"s","title":"W05b.04 Advice feedback: defer or resume a recurring suggestion via annotation.add"}	blake3:IGVF7DmCu7hgVPzvrcbz0z46KORJQqq935goN-wkVho
1795321341649335	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-qhf	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW05a.09 shipped `brief` at the standard level. §8.3 adds `--level compact|standard|deep`, `--since <transaction-id>` and `--snapshot TRANSACTION_ID`.\n\n# Goals\nComplete the read-time controls for `brief`, `show` and `explain`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Depth and budget are orthogonal.\" A short budget cannot remove a mandatory caveat. Historical reads (review 3 #2) let a reviewer see the graph \"as known at that head\", but \"disclosure and byte availability follow today's audience and retention policy, even for historical reads\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `compact`: essential decision/evidence/caveat index. `deep`: fuller cited summaries within the same byte budget.\n- `--since TX`: a semantic delta, not the whole case — including critical gaps, retrospective/lesson references and any stale synopsis when relevant.\n- `--snapshot TX` on `show`, `explain` and `brief`: resolve the graph at that head and label the output historical.\n- \"One brief, including its links and pagination, uses one verified snapshot.\"\n- Lists paginate with snapshot-bound cursors.\n- Documentation may give an approximate token equivalent \"without promising tokenizer accuracy\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Compact output on the 40-record fixture is at least 50% smaller than a full-record traversal and keeps every mandatory caveat.\n- [ ] `--since` after a retraction reports the newly stale items.\n- [ ] A historical read of a since-withdrawn artifact reports it unavailable.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCheap skimming that cannot lose the caveat.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.3 (brief levels, --since, --snapshot), Disposition: Storage and durability (Historical --snapshot reads) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"m","title":"W05b.05 brief levels, semantic delta since a transaction, and historical snapshot reads"}	blake3:DHRRfe81zd6w5bz5KSIMd2ohwvB9oQfIepFAH2Lrxew
1795321341995444	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-24t	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv explain ID` walks the recorded evidence/assessment chain with bounded depth; it is a deterministic presentation of committed rationale, not newly generated reasoning.\"\n\n# Goals\nA traversal that answers \"why does the record say this?\" from a conclusion, comparison or decision down to sources and selectors.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A reviewer can traverse any important conclusion to the exact retained source/selector and inspect meaningful objections.\" `explain` is that traversal as one command, with no model call.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- From the subject: assessments and comparisons with their relation and per-rival expectation; the observations they cite; each observation's artifact, selector and quote status; assumptions; open objections; reviews with freshness.\n- Bounded depth and node count with explicit \"more\" pointers; stable ordering.\n- Show contradicting and non-diagnostic items, not only support.\n- Mark stale links and unavailable artifacts.\n- Never expands raw bytes; ends each branch with the `evidence read` argv.\n- Reports justification cycles if any are present (W03.11 detects them).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] From any validated conclusion in the fixtures, `explain` reaches at least one exact selector.\n- [ ] Contradicting assessments appear in the walk.\n- [ ] Output is identical for identical snapshots and respects the depth bound.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 8, inspectability.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.3 (explain), §9.1 (show/explain), §10.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"m","title":"W05b.06 explain: bounded walk of the recorded evidence and assessment chain"}	blake3:KdAF-QvnbYusPVIgnZPzyIIfE7IVJ1BhrwYL0Byd7Vw
1795321342342278	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-c9f	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Start with SQLite full-text search over canonical summaries and approved text derivatives. Default to the selected case; global search requires `--all-cases`.\"\n\n# Goals\n`search QUERY` returning pointers, not evidence.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Search results are pointers. Claim verification requires `show` or an evidence read with a selector.\" A match in synopsis text must never look like corroboration: \"FTS may index its text for navigation, but the search response must label it `editorial_summary`, not original evidence.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- FTS5 tables in the derived index, rebuilt from the journal.\n- Result fields: object type, ID, title, bounded synopsis, synopsis basis/freshness, effective state, matching excerpt, original-source lineage, relevance reason.\n- \"Make restricted/raw material opt-in to the query.\"\n- Excerpts from source text are labeled untrusted.\n- \"Semantic embeddings are optional later. They must not be necessary for identity, correctness, deduplication, or ordinary search.\"\n- Remember the FTS index is a downstream copy for retention purposes (W11.11).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Default search never crosses cases.\n- [ ] A synopsis match is labeled `editorial_summary` and is not counted as a source hit.\n- [ ] Restricted text does not appear without the opt-in.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nNavigation that respects provenance.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2 (closing paragraph), §8.4, §9.1 (search), §20.1 (Cross-domain) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"m","title":"W05b.07 search: SQLite full-text search with labeled editorial summaries"}	blake3:wSJGVtw328K7qzxPqBuxt88TkdzSRCDxtzEHKAGdf2E
1795321342652390	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2uz	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"An explicit `annotation.add` with `role: synopsis` supplies a human/agent-authored version bound to the exact subject revision and content/source basis. It is never silently elevated to truth, and material changes render it `stale` until reviewed.\"\n\n# Goals\nImplement the reserved `synopsis` role.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA good authored summary helps the next reader; a stale one misleads them. Drill 2: \"a once-good summary says a criterion passed; a source is withdrawn or threshold changed. Summary remains in history but is marked stale and can no longer masquerade as the current answer.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- The annotation requires the exact subject revision; it is separately revisioned and editable only by a new annotation.\n- On a material subject or source change, the synopsis is `stale`; default reads fall back to the deterministic projection and say so.\n- \"Editing it does not rewrite an empirical claim or silently make old reviews current.\"\n- Mandatory caveat and freshness fields remain generated (W05a.07).\n- Actor and basis are kept; trust level is inherited (W10.18).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A source retraction or criterion revision marks the synopsis bound to the old revision `stale`; the default brief never presents it as current.\n- [ ] The synopsis is attributed, revision-bound and never counted as corroboration.\n- [ ] History retains the old synopsis.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"A summary is not a new independent source.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b.07.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §5.2, §8.3, §17.7 (drill 2), §20.1 (Layered retrieval) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"m","title":"W05b.08 Authored synopsis annotations bound to a subject revision, with staleness"}	blake3:btnEDdURdN_KnLE19KMpAepyeRaFikyjqu0XMyMdV2E
1795321342980998	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-12w	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`--read-only` (and `inv mcp --read-only`) rejects every mutating and export command at dispatch, before the commit lock, so a host can quarantine inspection of hostile raw evidence in a context that cannot write.\" (Review 1 #10.)\n\n# Goals\nA global flag enforced from catalog metadata.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The worst injection against an epistemic ledger is a schema-valid write that validates a false claim, retracts counter-evidence or downgrades classification.\" A session that reads hostile evidence can be given a binary that cannot write at all.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Each command and operation declares mutation/IO effects in the catalog (W01.09); dispatch consults them.\n- Rejection happens before workspace locking and before any file creation, with a clear error and exit code.\n- Covers `record`, shortcuts, `ingest`, `capture`, `vault render`, `export`, `repair`, `integration apply`.\n- Also settable by environment variable so a host can enforce it without trusting the agent's argv.\n- The MCP flag (W13.01) reuses the same switch.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every mutating or exporting command is rejected under `--read-only` with no filesystem change.\n- [ ] Adding a new mutating command without effect metadata fails a test.\n- [ ] Reads behave identically with and without the flag.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA simple, strong control for agents consuming adversarial evidence.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.1 (Read-only mode), §16.1, Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli","security"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"s","title":"W05b.09 --read-only mode: reject mutating and export commands at dispatch"}	blake3:x_XRS_YX77VuI_BFVydfDfQqowtJWavD9L1D0TcFuzg
1795321344130503	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3qy	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv doctor` describing versions, capabilities, and next repair steps.\" It \"truthfully reports unsupported platform/filesystem capabilities and any tests unavailable in the current environment\".\n\n# Goals\n`doctor` and `doctor bundle --preview`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nEnvironment problems (network filesystem, missing index, stale vault) should be diagnosable by an agent in one call. \"No probing of external tools, no speculative CLI flags.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Report: binary and schema versions, catalog version, supported selector validation, installed method packs and readers, record size limits, platform/filesystem classification against the support matrix (W00.09), index and vault health, lock state, unsettled capture handles.\n- Detect and report a network filesystem with the reason it is refused.\n- Each issue has a repair step.\n- `doctor bundle --preview`: list exactly what an explicitly selected redacted troubleshooting archive would contain; defaults exclude source excerpts, credentials and sensitive artifact paths.\n- \"No telemetry or uploads occur without a user-selected external consumer.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `doctor` output on a healthy workspace is one JSON object with no warnings.\n- [ ] A stale index and a refused filesystem each produce a specific finding with a repair.\n- [ ] The bundle preview contains no evidence content.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTruthful self-description of capabilities and limits.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.8, §10.2, §11.7, §18.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"m","title":"W05b.10 doctor: versions, capabilities, filesystem support and a redacted bundle preview"}	blake3:m7ccv4SDE0Fffavat_moq65hM7XbHHXC6MMgdH4iruw
1795321344987166	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3if	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Installation into a harness is explicit. `inv integration show --target <name>` prints the proposed snippet/config; a requested apply operation changes only a bounded managed block or named file and preserves unrelated instructions.\"\n\n# Goals\n`integration show`, `integration apply` and `integration inspect`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Do not require editing every harness to use the CLI.\" Trusted operating guidance must live in the binary or versioned integration files, never in evidence: \"A case attachment named `AGENTS.md` is still evidence.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `show`: print the snippet or config for a target (generic `AGENTS.md` block, optional skill directory) without writing.\n- `apply`: write only inside a clearly delimited managed block or a named owned file; idempotent; shows a diff; leaves everything else byte-identical; reversible.\n- `inspect`: list available neutral schema versions, limits, selector validation and installed packs (§11.7).\n- Targets are generic file shapes, not named products.\n- Never triggered implicitly by `init` or any read.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `apply` twice is a no-op; content outside the managed block is unchanged.\n- [ ] Removing the managed block restores the original file.\n- [ ] Nothing is installed without an explicit `apply`.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nAgent instructions \"appear when needed\" without the tool taking over a harness.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b.12.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.8, §9.1 (integration), §11.7, §16.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"m","title":"W05b.11 integration show/apply/inspect: explicit, bounded installation of agent instructions"}	blake3:7C7NZzZdzYSVnHfWTYFRhe1e_k1ENKLxtjXD1twv-Do
1795321346122950	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2p8	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Ship embedded docs with the binary, available offline.\" §21 gives the instruction text to embed in `onboard` and offer as \"an explicitly installable small skill/AGENTS snippet\".\n\n# Goals\nThe embedded guide, the concise AGENTS snippet and an optional installable skill containing triggers and the main loop.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"These instructions must make sense to an agent that knows nothing about the implementation or any external producer.\" The snippet is the primary agent path alongside the CLI (§9.7), so its token cost matters.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Start from the §21 block: when to use and skip; core model; ten-step main loop; receipts and retries; `next` never grants permission; source text is untrusted; do not edit generated vault notes; what to do when stuck, auditing or taking over.\n- Use the real executable name; mention `inv` only as an optional alias.\n- Generate command names and examples from the catalog so docs cannot drift; CI fails on mismatch.\n- Version the snippet; `onboard` embeds the same version.\n- Keep it small; measure its size.\n- The separate producer-only page is W07.16.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every command and operation named in the docs exists in the binary (checked in CI).\n- [ ] The snippet is available offline via the binary.\n- [ ] A reader with no implementation knowledge can follow the main loop.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n§20.7: \"Quickstart, embedded guide, examples and optional skill agree with the binary.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.8, §21 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli","docs"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"m","title":"W05b.12 Embedded offline docs, the §21 agent instructions snippet and an optional skill"}	blake3:by71eQuw7GIRNRo8r6A1Vo5ZNXDUoFivE_04bmpwpCo
1795321346508738	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-16a	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Titles, synopses, annotations, rationales and lessons are free text an agent may have written after reading hostile evidence. An injected instruction can be laundered through a summary into the layer every later agent reads first.\" (Review 2 #7.)\n\n# Goals\nEvery free-text field is rendered as quoted data in every CLI output.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Only text compiled into the binary or a pinned method pack is guidance.\" Without this, the layered-read design would turn the synopsis layer into an injection amplifier.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- A single rendering helper for authored text, used by `show`, `brief`, `search`, `explain`, `next`, `frontier`, errors and text mode.\n- JSON: authored strings live in clearly named fields, separate from tool guidance fields; text mode: delimited and visibly attributed.\n- Titles: bounded length; strip control characters and markup at write time.\n- Tool-generated guidance never interpolates authored text into imperative sentences.\n- Tests with instruction-shaped titles, synopses and rationales.\n- Trust-level display is added by W10.18.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An instruction embedded in a title or synopsis appears only inside a data field or delimiter in every command's output.\n- [ ] Control characters and markup in a title are rejected or stripped.\n- [ ] No output path concatenates authored text into guidance.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nProduct integrity control for agents consuming adversarial evidence — with the stated limit that labeling alone does not prevent every failure.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §16.1 (Authored text is data too), Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli","security"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"s","title":"W05b.13 Authored text is data: quoted rendering at every depth and title sanitization"}	blake3:5Y3r811JeZbp8Phx5FAy55PZ5_1iJ9xSstZOf83STvc
1795321347699706	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-28e	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Diagnostic logs include transaction/run IDs, timings, protocol/schema versions, persistence stage, error class and recovery recipe. Default logs exclude source excerpts/credentials/sensitive artifact paths.\"\n\n# Goals\nStructured logging to stderr across core, store and CLI.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nWhen a commit's durability is uncertain, the persistence stage in the log is what tells an operator which crash-matrix row they are in. Logs are also a leak channel, so the default must be safe.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `tracing` spans for commit steps 1–10, ingest, capture and render, with stage names matching §10.2.\n- Fields: transaction ID, run ID, command ID, sequence, timings, schema versions, error class.\n- Verbosity flag; never stdout.\n- A lint or test that forbids logging payload content, excerpts or absolute artifact paths at default level.\n- No telemetry.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A failed commit's log identifies the stage reached.\n- [ ] Default-level logs for the scenario scripts contain no source text.\n- [ ] JSON-mode stdout is unaffected by any verbosity setting.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes recovery recipes followable without exposing evidence.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.2, §18.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"s","title":"W05b.14 Diagnostic logging: transaction and run IDs, persistence stage, no evidence content"}	blake3:bvBS_y22t_Jm9e6FDkBMMRg5tfM8_-wF7wkp2f4MKyo
1795321348661474	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-7mc	{"description":"# Context & Background\nTargets \"on a documented ordinary workstation: warm brief/next under 250 ms p95 for 10,000 objects and 50,000 relations; warm mutation under 150 ms p95 excluding file ingestion/render; streaming artifact import with bounded memory. These are engineering targets, not measured current performance.\"\n\n# Goals\nA repeatable benchmark harness and the two synthetic fixtures the plan names.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"If the first implementation misses them, profile before adding complexity.\" Review 1's storage redesign was rejected partly because its performance concern was unmeasured; this bone produces the measurement.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Generator for a 10,000-object / 50,000-relation workspace.\n- Sparse choice fixture: 1,000 options × 200 applicable criteria (200,000 possible cells, few recorded Comparisons) — measure first-page and top-three latency including rule evaluation; plus \"an exhaustive small fixture to check grouping and completeness\".\n- Measure warm `brief`, `next`, a single-operation mutation, and index rebuild time.\n- Apply the project's own measurement discipline: record machine, build, sample count, warmup and trial order; report percentiles with their n.\n- On a miss, file a profiling task; do not change the storage format (ADR W00.04 revisit condition).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Benchmarks run with one command and emit a machine-readable report.\n- [ ] Results are compared against targets with environment details.\n- [ ] The sparse fixture's grouped `next` output is complete relative to the exhaustive small fixture.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps the tool agent-paced without speculative optimization.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b.02.\nParent goal: W05b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.5, Disposition: Agent experience and interface (Large sparse choice fixture) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w05","milestone-b","lane-agent-ux","cli","test","fixture"],"parent":"bn-2jz","size":"m","title":"W05b.15 Performance harness, §10.5 targets and the sparse 1,000 × 200 choice fixture"}	blake3:FTexGaYAPx2Bh5x0Nnk8YOA5yhzsgoxSGlPGpolFtOs
1795321434526195	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-aeb	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW06a.04 validated `whole`, `byte_range`, `text_lines` and `json_pointer`. The union also has `table_cell`, `time_window`, `code_span` and `external_selector`.\n\n# Goals\nStorage, validation where natively possible, and honest `unavailable` states otherwise.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Unsupported external selectors are stored with `validation: unavailable`, not falsely verified.\" `external_selector` exists \"so new source types do not require changes to the core's public model\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `table_cell`: named table derivative, row identity/index plus column, extraction version and digest; verify against a table derivative when one exists.\n- `time_window`: start/end, units, coordinate origin, channel/series, artifact digest; \"meaning determined by source metadata\" — structural validation only.\n- `code_span`: repository/commit/blob digest, path and line interval; \"can be external-unverified until source bytes are imported\".\n- `external_selector`: opaque, namespaced, explicit schema URI/version, validator identity if available.\n- \"A producer/reader can later attach an attributed validation/extraction record\" — add that record so validation can arrive after the fact.\n- \"The core never dynamically loads a parser or executes a command supplied in an imported manifest.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each selector round-trips with its validation state.\n- [ ] An `external_selector` with no validator is `unavailable` and is displayed as such everywhere.\n- [ ] A later attributed validation record changes the displayed state without rewriting the observation.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPrecise citation for any source format without teaching the core every format.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.4, §11.5 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"m","title":"W06b.01 Remaining selectors: table_cell, time_window, code_span, external_selector"}	blake3:ySaa1WpbGXxfnin6u5yglDn-EiCb10JmYVYNGwinUnA
1795321434621967	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-147	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Permit a reference-only object when bytes aren't retained, marked `external_unverified` or with verified digest if independently checked; don't pretend it's a complete archive.\"\n\n# Goals\nIngest `external_refs` and explicit large-file `reference` mode.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nSome evidence cannot or should not be copied (size, licence, access). The record must say so plainly: reference mode \"stores locator and expected digest/size but does not copy and cannot satisfy an offline-completeness guarantee\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Artifact availability `external` with locator, expected digest and size where known.\n- A reverify operation: read the external bytes, compare digest, record an attributed verification with time; \"Reverify before relying on external bytes.\"\n- External mutation is detected and reported; the stored digest never silently changes.\n- \"No silent fallback to reference mode after disk full\": a failed copy is a failed ingest.\n- Selectors on unverified references stay `unavailable`.\n- Exports and archives list reference-only artifacts as omissions (W11.01).\n- `inv` never fetches a URL itself; a producer supplies bytes or a verification.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An unavailable external artifact produces `source_unavailable`, not fabricated verified bytes.\n- [ ] A changed external file fails reverification and marks dependents.\n- [ ] Disk-full during copy yields a rejected ingest, not a reference.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Missing bytes remain missing.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.4 (Storage and input rules), §11.1 (step 7 and closing paragraph), §20.1 (Source research) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"m","title":"W06b.02 Reference-only imports: explicit external/unverified state, reverification, no silent fallback"}	blake3:rrZMaTQ9szrEQsUQfk1h0VcK3OB5J7SA52URkJg6q7M
1795321434722344	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-16e	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§11.4: \"Producer namespace + stable external run ID identifies a logical attempt; manifest digest identifies a version of the description, not a fresh trial.\"\n\n# Goals\nIdentity rules that keep storage deduplicated and provenance honest.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Distinct acquisitions can yield identical bytes; deduplicate blob storage only, not the acquisition or observation identities.\" And the converse: \"Reimport of changed manifest creates explicit revisions/lineage, not bogus corroboration.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Acquisition key: producer namespace plus stable external ID where supplied; otherwise each ingest is its own acquisition.\n- \"Same original acquisition imported again maps to existing canonical Source/Run IDs and reports the prior link.\"\n- A changed manifest for a known acquisition creates a new revision with lineage.\n- Two acquisitions with identical bytes share one blob and remain two provenance identities.\n- \"Source clocks and agent IDs are claims; local sequence gives transaction order, not guaranteed real-world event order or verified identity.\"\n- Generic import cursors in the index for resumable imports.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two distinct acquisitions with identical bytes remain distinct provenance identities while blob bytes deduplicate.\n- [ ] Historical or external manifest reimport is idempotent; a manifest revision is not a new trial.\n- [ ] Re-import reports the prior link in its per-item outcome.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInvariant: \"repeated formatting of one source does not create independent confirmation.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.1, §11.4, §20.1 (Cross-domain), §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"m","title":"W06b.03 Acquisition identity, deduplication and re-import lineage"}	blake3:xsPFXogrXMSSar2hJv7ARQBlqfKnfgNaGL_mwT6i4vg
1795321434813863	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1hc	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Derivatives (redaction, text extraction, plots, aggregations) link to the original acquisition and transform/version. A screenshot and JSON extraction from the same trace are not two independent confirmations.\"\n\n# Goals\nA general derivative record, extending the text derivative of W06a.03.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nWithout lineage, a chart, a redacted copy and an extracted table of one capture look like three sources. With it, independence analysis (W06b.05) and sensitivity propagation (W06b.07) can follow the edges.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Derivative record: input digests, transform identity and version, selectors used, redaction decisions if any, output digest, review basis.\n- `derived_from` edges to the original acquisition, transitively.\n- Extraction \"creates new derived artifacts/observations linked to original bytes and parser/transform identity. An extraction doesn't change the original.\"\n- Domain-specific extraction is done by a trusted reader or external producer and arrives as derived artifact plus observation.\n- \"If an original is withdrawn/erased, dependent conclusions show inspectability loss.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A derivative of a derivative traces to the original acquisition.\n- [ ] Two derivatives of one acquisition are reported as sharing an origin.\n- [ ] The original blob is byte-identical after any derivation.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 3: \"original acquisition, transformations, observations and interpretations remain separately identified.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W06b.03.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.1 (step 4), §11.4, §16.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"s","title":"W06b.04 Derivative artifacts and transformation lineage"}	blake3:ZdolLdI0-5S7iQ9MVJS3tZAiwJTTUDkPUQnZHPT0f7A
1795321434897904	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-27g	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Independence is per claim, not per document. For a stated claim, record the origin of the observation or assertion separately from the activity that collected its document.\" (Review 3 #3.)\n\n# Goals\nRecord origin roots per claim and attributed dependence judgments, and show them in reads.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"One article may quote a specification value in one passage and report its own bench test in another.\" A per-document model either over-counts the quote or loses the bench test. \"Corroboration [is] never inferred from agent count\" or citation count.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Observations carry an optional origin reference distinct from their acquisition.\n- Assessments \"may link multiple origin roots with an attributed dependence judgment (`shared`, `partly_shared`, `independent_on_stated_dimensions`, `unknown`) and its basis. An absent link means `unknown`.\"\n- Reads \"show document/acquisition counts separately from known claim origins\". \"Every acquisition stays its own record.\"\n- Record dimensions: upstream source, collection run, method, fixture/environment, known dependence groups.\n- Keep the six reliability aspects separate and never combined into a score: byte integrity; authenticity/attribution and source reliability (with `cannot_judge`); measurement validity; interpretive confidence; reproduction status; independence.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Three separately collected documents quoting one specification keep three acquisition records and one known origin for that claim; a second claim in one of them may have a different or unknown origin.\n- [ ] Quotations, extraction, screenshots and summary notes of one document do not count as independent corroboration.\n- [ ] Absent linkage displays as `unknown`, not independent.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nAnswers the README's example question: \"whether three 'independent' sources really agree\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W06b.03, W06b.04.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.6, §17.4, §20.1 (Cross-domain, Source research), Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"m","title":"W06b.05 Claim-level origin independence with attributed dependence judgments"}	blake3:aN7LEEAKGq_XqVIJ5195aLn-oMgbPBaWDauVzdcrY2U
1795321434991585	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-izz	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`source.assess` / `source.withdraw` / `artifact.withdraw`: Source authenticity/reliability with limitations; withdrawal/reason/scope; preserved immutable originals.\"\n\n# Goals\nAttributed source assessment and withdrawal that propagates freshness without deleting anything.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 1 proposed NATO Admiralty letter-number grades; revision 4 rejected them because the scales mask subjectivity and raters do not keep the axes independent. Instead source reliability (track record, competence, access) is \"judged separately from the credibility of the specific datum, with `cannot_judge` allowed\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `source.assess`: authenticity/attribution, reliability, primary/secondary status, currency, limitations — each attributed with rationale; no combined grade.\n- `source.withdraw` / `artifact.withdraw`: reason and scope; \"Withdrawals change evidentiary use, not physical deletion.\"\n- Withdrawal marks dependent observations, assessments, comparisons, conclusions and decisions through the freshness engine.\n- Command help and docs state clearly that withdrawal \"is not erasure\".\n- These are sensitive writes that W10.19 guardrails may gate.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Retraction of an original source propagates effective freshness to observations, assessments, comparisons, conclusions and decisions.\n- [ ] Withdrawn bytes remain readable under retention policy and are labeled withdrawn.\n- [ ] A primary-source erratum can invalidate a prior judgment without deleting its original record.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCorrections stay visible; history is not rewritten.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.6, §9.4 (source.*, artifact.withdraw), §14.3, §16.5, §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"m","title":"W06b.06 source.assess, source.withdraw and artifact.withdraw"}	blake3:tHFLnl3uwvUdyO54vpGRujL1BVV0lFdwsWWBMuD3Pag
1795321435084253	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3b0	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Support `public | internal | restricted` with explicit reason/tags. Default new case/artifacts to `internal`, not public; external producers can request stricter classifications.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe classification model and its propagation rules, used by every renderer and export.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A sensitive title/path/metadata or Canvas label can leak even if the raw bytes are omitted.\" Classification must therefore attach to records and metadata, not only to blobs. \"Regex-only redaction never proves a derivative safe.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Classification on cases, artifacts, records and annotations with reason/tags.\n- \"Classification normally propagates from original to derivative; downgrading requires explicit reviewed transformation.\"\n- Derivative records include input digests, transform/version, selectors, redaction decisions, output digest and review basis.\n- A query helper answering \"may this field be shown to audience X?\" for use by `brief`, `search`, MCP, vault and exports.\n- Default `brief` and MCP outputs \"do not blindly include raw logs, huge documents, file secrets or local absolute paths\".\n- No industry is special-cased.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A derivative of a restricted artifact is restricted unless a reviewed downgrade record exists.\n- [ ] A restricted record's title does not appear in default-audience output.\n- [ ] Downgrade without a reviewed transformation is rejected.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nSafe by default, without pretending labels are a security boundary.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §16.2, §19.2 (W06: privacy defaults) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage","security"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"m","title":"W06b.07 Sensitivity classification model: defaults, propagation and reviewed downgrades"}	blake3:z3VLfk-4EUz4EK4ksR8DwQ1hou5sYvqOHuG6T8MBKM0
1795321435174557	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ui	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Bound parser input sizes, nesting, decompression expansion and archive entry counts. Prevent path traversal, symlink/hardlink escapes and accidental reads outside allowed roots.\"\n\n# Goals\nEvery parser and extractor in the ingest path has explicit limits and adversarial tests.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nEvidence is frequently hostile or simply huge. \"Implement parsers and selectors in bounded, trusted code with no command execution from source metadata.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Central limits configuration: maximum input size per parser, nesting depth, decompression ratio and absolute expansion, archive entry count and per-entry size.\n- Archive handling (if any is offered): entries are extracted only to private staging, with traversal and link entries rejected.\n- \"Treat MIME/extension as hints; do not execute imported content to identify it.\"\n- Viewers: \"safe text/JSON summaries first. External viewers are explicit user actions. Never auto-play captured audio or auto-open active documents as a side effect of `brief`.\"\n- Fuzz or property tests for JSON, text decoding and any table parser.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Decompression-bomb, deep-nesting and traversal fixtures fail safely with bounded resource use.\n- [ ] No code path spawns a process based on artifact content or metadata.\n- [ ] Limits are documented and reported by `doctor`.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nLets agents hand the tool untrusted material safely.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.4, §16.4, §20.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage","security"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"m","title":"W06b.08 Raw-file hardening: parser bounds, decompression and archive limits, no execution"}	blake3:rFQMMEKqPgoH7-Cck7NY2hXq1knt8NgseYh0nHp5n0Y
1795321435265139	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3jw	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Use streaming ingestion for large captures and retain exact bytes.\" Target: \"streaming artifact import with bounded memory\".\n\n# Goals\nVerify and, where needed, fix the ingest path for very large inputs.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nTraces, packet captures and benchmark sample dumps can be many gigabytes. An ingest that buffers them would fail exactly when evidence preservation matters most.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Test with generated multi-gigabyte input from a file and from stdin.\n- Measure peak resident memory; assert a fixed bound independent of input size.\n- Confirm the commit lock is not held during hashing or copying.\n- Disk-full mid-stream leaves no partial blob and no committed reference.\n- Report progress on stderr only.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Large capture ingestion has bounded memory and preserves exact bytes.\n- [ ] Another process can commit while a large ingest is streaming.\n- [ ] An interrupted ingest leaves only cleanable temp files.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPreservation first, at any size.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.5, §16.4, §20.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage","test"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"s","title":"W06b.09 Streaming ingestion of large captures with bounded memory"}	blake3:Qdom6scvRYHkcLGTWFy3t2uxYyGvyjSHR4bmFgAJ6rU
1795321435361055	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1kv	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Unknown optional source metadata retained opaquely rather than invented or discarded.\" \"Unknown required schema versions are retained opaque, not incorrectly normalized or dropped.\"\n\n# Goals\nA consistent policy for data the core does not understand.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nTwo failure modes are both bad: dropping unknown fields loses evidence; interpreting them risks false facts. Namespaced metadata \"never changes canonical facts or methods by side effect\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Namespaced inert metadata (`metadata: {\"example-org.instrument\": {...}}`) is stored as raw data within resource bounds and returned on `full` reads, labeled uninterpreted.\n- A manifest or envelope with an unknown *required* version is stored as an opaque artifact with a usable diagnostic; nothing is mapped into typed facts.\n- Unknown keys in *our* versioned payloads remain errors (W01.04); this policy applies to source and producer content only.\n- Size bounds and a clear error when exceeded.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Unknown source schema or namespaced metadata stays opaque with a usable diagnostic.\n- [ ] Unsupported source data never silently maps to false facts.\n- [ ] Opaque metadata survives export and restore byte for byte.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nForward compatibility without guessing.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.6, §8.7, §11.1 (step 5), §11.2, §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"s","title":"W06b.10 Opaque producer metadata and unknown schema versions preserved, never invented or dropped"}	blake3:U2GkHWTPfziHrSJ13Vw47ZCbQlfrT_JDirBWd4TUNe4
1795321435455566	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1lm	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"An absence claim is only as good as the search behind it.\" A Run may carry optional `search_coverage`: \"corpus and revision bounds, queries/filters, inclusion/exclusion rules, total/examined/empty hits\" (PRISMA-style, review 1 #7).\n\n# Goals\nImplement the field on documentary and code-search Runs so absence claims can be backed.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Absence of a cited fact in one searched source does not prove the fact is false.\" Recording what was searched, including empty searches, is what turns \"no errata exist\" into a bounded statement.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Structure: corpus identity and revision bounds; queries and filters; inclusion/exclusion rules; counts of total, examined and empty hits.\n- Recorded by `run.record` or `capture finalize`; `inv` runs no search itself.\n- Observations of absence link to the search Run.\n- Provide the hook the blindspot rule `source.absence_without_search_log` (W10.12) uses.\n- Catalog example: a registry search with zero hits.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A search Run with empty results validates and is linkable from an absence observation.\n- [ ] The coverage summary appears in the Run's `summary` view.\n- [ ] Counts are stored as given; nothing is inferred.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nHonest negative results.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.1 (Systematic search reporting), §5.2 (Run), §8.5, §17.4, Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"s","title":"W06b.11 search_coverage on search Runs: corpus bounds, queries and empty results"}	blake3:9o3I0TPu0FHBB-BmzWQixwM_qXWxG4j8rMWx29AGalo
1795321435544556	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-14z	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Document a mapping to W3C Web Annotation selectors for interchange: `byte_range` → `DataPositionSelector`, `text_lines` → RFC 5147, `text_quote` → `TextQuoteSelector`, `table_cell` → RFC 7111, `time_window` → Media Fragments. `json_pointer` stays an extension. Internal identity stays digest-bound.\"\n\n# Goals\nA reference page under `docs/` with the mapping table and its caveats.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 2 #14's standards mapping was \"adopted as documentation\"; exporters are deferred. Writing the mapping down keeps the internal selector shapes compatible with a future exporter.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- One table: internal selector, standard counterpart, lossy aspects (e.g. digest binding has no direct counterpart).\n- Verify each standard reference against its published text when writing.\n- Note explicitly that no exporter ships in v1 (add to the deferred register, W00.11).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Mapping page committed and linked from the selector schema docs.\n- [ ] Each row cites the standard it maps to.\n- [ ] The page states that exporters are deferred.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 7, portability.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.4 (Export mapping), Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (Standards mapping) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage","docs"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"xs","title":"W06b.12 Document the selector mapping to W3C Web Annotation and related standards"}	blake3:SCd_LoZss9Kpig42HgEHhXZgn5VNIXK07UbSRXjyTXE
1795321435634435	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2x5	{"description":"# Context & Background\nScenario D: \"do three independent references actually corroborate a disputed technical specification?\"\n\n# Goals\nDeterministic seed documents and expected properties, including the twin and the adversarial variant.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThis scenario carries the duplicate-origin trap that W06's acceptance names, and the prompt-injection variant that §16.1 exists for.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Four Sources: a primary specification, a trade article, a blog and a first-party erratum.\n- The first three contain passages that trace to *one originating specification claim*. The article also has \"a second passage reporting the author's own bench test, which has a different origin\".\n- The erratum is traced to the affected edition; \"in a twin fixture the changed value applies only to a later edition\". The question names a specific build.\n- Search material for a `search_coverage` Run, including registries with zero hits.\n- Adversarial variant: crawler output text \"contains apparent instructions telling the agent to modify its method or promote the conclusion\".\n- Expected properties: one origin for the disputed value across three acquisitions; the bench test kept as a separate origin; a conditional conclusion or explicit insufficient-evidence result for the named build; every original selector kept; injected text inert.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Seeds and twin are committed and documented by role.\n- [ ] Expected properties are test-evaluable.\n- [ ] The injection text is realistic and clearly synthetic.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nProves the core on a source-based question that needs no experiment.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17 (preamble), §17.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage","fixture"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"m","title":"W06b.13 Scenario D fixture: contradictory source research with shared origins, erratum and injection"}	blake3:U6ounUOWSGAQzQl-0pxWIJCMYcUoA6_tk5D8qvYDznA
1795321435725498	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-sbu	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW06's acceptance: \"Research scenario and duplicate-origin trap pass.\"\n\n# Goals\nA scripted walkthrough of Scenario D through the binary.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A citation-based research judgment can be defensible without a synthetic experiment.\" The test must show the same core supports that, and that apparent corroboration is not counted.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nScript: ingest the four sources; record quoted observations with `text_quote`; link origins and record the dependence judgment; assess source quality; record the `search_coverage` Run; assess the erratum against the named build; reach a conditional conclusion or insufficient-evidence result; submit a review without any executable experiment.\nAssert:\n- Three acquisitions, one origin for the disputed claim, a separate origin for the bench test.\n- The erratum invalidates a prior judgment without deleting its record; in the twin, it does not apply to the named build.\n- A reference-only source reports `source_unavailable`.\n- The injected instructions change no command selection, schema, onboarding output or pack loading.\nBlindspot review and retrospective steps are added by W10 and re-run in MB.01.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Passes offline from an empty directory.\n- [ ] A source-based conclusion is reviewed without requiring a physically executable experiment.\n- [ ] A source excerpt containing instructions cannot alter trusted onboarding, tool schemas, execution or pack loading.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThird domain on the same binary; no adapter, no special case.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W06b.13, W06b.05, W06b.06, W06b.11, W06b.02.\nParent goal: W06b. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.4, §19.2 (W06), §20.1 (Cross-domain, Source research) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w06","milestone-b","lane-storage","test"],"parent":"bn-1c5","size":"m","title":"W06b.14 Scenario D end-to-end test: duplicate-origin trap, erratum currency, inert instructions"}	blake3:HtgxtGLU6cwt4DliE9k2i7Iu0-ndAlQWMeg6WAYejg4
1795321537286135	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1bz	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe neutral envelope `inv.producer-run.v1` carries `producer {name, version, instance}`, `external_run_id`, `intent`, `execution`, `environment`, `artifacts[]`, `assertions[]`, `controls[]`, `receipts[]` and `limitations[]`.\n\n# Goals\nThe schema, and one-shot ingestion of a completed run described by such a manifest.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Core never interprets a producer's `verdict` as a verified fact.\" The envelope \"neither executes a benchmark nor treats `reported_pass` as proof of sound methodology\". All producer assertions are stored \"as attributed source content until assessed\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Schema in the catalog with a schematic example relative to a fixture directory; `ingest` resolves actual paths (W06a.02).\n- Map to one Run plus Source/Artifact records; `execution`, evidence completeness, measurement validity and investigative interpretation stay separate.\n- `assertions[]` and `controls[]` become attributed producer claims (`validity_claim: producer_reported`), never Observations with validity or Assessments.\n- `expected_sha256` verified when present; mismatch is a per-item rejection.\n- Namespaced `metadata` preserved inertly (W06b.10).\n- Identity and re-import follow W06b.03.\n- A minimal `record`-mode case is created when no case is given.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The §11.2 example ingests against a fixture directory and yields one Run with attributed assertions.\n- [ ] A producer's claimed result remains attributed source data, never auto-promoted to a validated Conclusion.\n- [ ] Exit code 0 in `execution` does not set any measurement validity.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Runs and sources can come from any language/tool through one neutral recorder.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W06b.03, W06b.10.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.5, §11.2, §1.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"m","title":"W07.01 producer-run.v1 envelope schema and manifest ingestion as attributed producer assertions"}	blake3:YzeGIcNT8qz_zUrWlCo16HNOaZiy6lrNhYH24q2hALY
1795321537365432	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2hv	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Provide `investigation-recording`: Rust library and JSON-CLI protocol.\" §18.3 sketches `Recorder { begin, append, attach, finalize, recover }`.\n\n# Goals\nThe public Rust API that native producers link, with \"same validation and durable transactions; no duplicated evidence/journal implementation\".\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"All producer facts use the same durable acceptance/receipt semantics as investigator mutations.\" One implementation behind the library and the CLI guarantees that Rust and JSON producers cannot diverge.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Types: `BeginCapture`, `CaptureHandle`, `CaptureEvent`, `StableArtifact`, `ArtifactRef`, `FinalizeCapture`, `RecoveryState`, receipts.\n- Depends on `core` and `store` only; no dependency on CLI, MCP, Obsidian or any producer.\n- Every call takes an idempotency key and returns a durable receipt.\n- The crate never spawns a process and never holds the commit lock across caller code.\n- Semver policy and a documented minimum surface; internal store types are not re-exported.\n- \"No external producer needs a dependency on Obsidian or an existing non-recording case.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A small Rust program records a run using only this crate's public API.\n- [ ] The crate's dependency tree contains no frontend crates.\n- [ ] Public API docs state what each call does *not* do (authorize, start, rerun).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe dependency arrow points inward: producers import Investigation, never the reverse.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.2, §11.3, §18.1, §18.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"m","title":"W07.02 investigation-recording crate: the Recorder API shared by native producers"}	blake3:pJbEh_ZhJp3NhzSp0prbM1_h3VAaLrhT8aIawSY-yi8
1795321537444827	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-6oi	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`capture begin`: Atomically declare one Run, producer identity, optional InquiryPlan/external plan ref; allocate private durable staging; return `run_id`, capture handle, negotiated schema and owned paths. Does not authorize or start execution.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe first capture operation, in library and CLI form.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nDeclaring the Run before the external action lets the journal show `recorded_before_run_declaration` chronology and gives recovery something to find if the producer crashes.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- One committed transaction creating the Run (`declared`) and, if needed, a minimal `record`-mode case.\n- Allocate `state/staging/<run-id>/` with restrictive permissions; it is \"durable recovery material bound to a committed capture handle\", never disposable cache.\n- Return `run_id`, an opaque capture handle, the negotiated schema version, limits and owned staging paths.\n- Idempotent on `(producer namespace, external_run_id, command_id)`: a lost response returns the same handle.\n- Optional exact InquiryPlan digest or external plan digest.\n- Response text and schema state that begin does not authorize or start anything.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `capture begin` returns one durable `run_id` and an idempotent receipt.\n- [ ] Retried begin yields the same run and handle, not a duplicate.\n- [ ] Staging exists and is listed by `capture recover` immediately.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"A partial/failed/crashed run remains visible.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.02.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2 (staging), §11.3 (capture begin), §17.6 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"m","title":"W07.03 capture begin: declare a Run, allocate durable staging, return a handle"}	blake3:RRt9cdS-kce55PiKSaEUmU3cOkuikxj4kyXEMkkfp6w
1795321537530199	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-18z	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`capture append`: Add bounded typed lifecycle/collection facts using producer event IDs, durable idempotency and distinct reported vs accepted timestamps; do not record every high-frequency sample as a global transaction.\"\n\n# Goals\nOrdered, idempotent appends to a handle without a global transaction per item.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 3 #8: \"Every accepted append or attachment descriptor has a per-handle position and a content/request digest. Accept only the next expected position under per-handle serialization.\" Otherwise a lost middle item is undetectable.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Per-handle serialization (a handle-scoped lock, distinct from the commit lock).\n- Accept only the next expected position; \"an already-seen item key with identical content returns its original receipt, and mismatched content conflicts\".\n- \"Persist bytes, item receipt and cursor durably before acknowledging.\"\n- Typed lifecycle facts (started, phase changes, errors, cancellation), each with producer-reported time and locally accepted time.\n- Bounded item size; high-frequency samples belong in an attached artifact.\n- Appends are staged and summarized into the finalized manifest; they are not one journal event each.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Sending position 3 when 2 is missing is rejected with the expected position.\n- [ ] Retrying an accepted item with changed bytes conflicts.\n- [ ] Every acknowledged item survives a crash at its position.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nStreaming and partial recovery without pretending a stream is complete.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.03.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.3 (capture append; Per-handle positions and a precise seal), Disposition: Storage and durability (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"m","title":"W07.04 capture append: per-handle positions, producer event IDs, durable item receipts"}	blake3:nteDnNO5lPsUN5Yld5oitEkZPaZX9HUs8faDhbLB-vM
1795321537611573	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2y6	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`capture attach`: Snapshot completed artifact/stream, verify digest/size and role, bind original/derivative identities and sensitivity; no mutable reference masquerades as finalized bytes.\"\n\n# Goals\nAttach artifacts to a handle safely while the producer may still be running.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Stage outputs durably, then snapshot only stable bytes: require producer writer close/flush or a safe copy-and-verify protocol. Prevent writable hardlinks into canonical blobs.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Attachment descriptor takes the next per-handle position (same rules as W07.04).\n- Copy-and-verify: stream to staging while hashing; compare with declared digest/size when given; detect change during copy by re-stat and, where declared, by digest.\n- Role (`original-output`, derivative, log, receipt), media type as a hint, classification (default `internal`, stricter if requested).\n- Bytes move into the blob store at finalize (or earlier as unreferenced blobs); never by hardlink to a producer-writable path.\n- A declared-but-absent artifact is recorded as missing, \"not replaced by empty files\".\n- Never hold the commit lock during the copy.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A file still being written is rejected or recorded as unstable, never snapshotted as final.\n- [ ] Digest mismatch rejects the item and leaves the handle usable.\n- [ ] Missing expected artifacts remain missing/incomplete.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"External artifact mutation is detected; a digest never describes different retained bytes.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.04.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.3 (capture attach), §10.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"m","title":"W07.05 capture attach: snapshot stable bytes, verify digest, size and role"}	blake3:5KUr54RVJH9o_GvGqv1WaYSa8lRGUOJrwNIkZ3Iiih0
1795321537695958	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2um	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`capture finalize`: Freeze retained/missing artifact manifest, execution state, producer assertions, controls, receipts, per-channel completeness, and finalization status. Never auto-create a validated Conclusion.\"\n\n# Goals\nA precise seal followed by one canonical transaction.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"`capture finalize` runs under the same serialization with an expected last position and the declared artifact/channel inventory. It seals staging before publishing the canonical manifest and rejects a stale cursor for a claimed-complete stream.\" \"Distinguish `finalized` versus `execution_completed`.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Inputs: expected last position, declared artifact/channel inventory, execution state, assertions, controls, receipts, limitations.\n- If an intermediate item was lost, \"partial finalization identifies the missing position\".\n- Order: seal staging durably → publish blobs → commit the event binding the manifest digest to the Run revision.\n- The manifest is an immutable Artifact; it \"lists its covered artifacts and pre-finalization snapshot; it excludes itself and its subsequently committed transaction hash\".\n- \"The finalized manifest binds an item-key/digest/receipt map so identical retries resolve after staging is retired.\"\n- Recording finalization and evidence completeness are independent of execution success.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A claimed-complete finalize with a stale cursor is rejected.\n- [ ] Finalization after a failed process preserves the exact original error and valid partial observations; no invented overall verdict.\n- [ ] A finalized Run with `failed` execution is representable.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nFinalization states what survived, not what was hoped for.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.05.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.1 (manifests), §11.3 (capture finalize; seal), §6.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"m","title":"W07.06 capture finalize: seal staging, publish the manifest, record completeness per channel"}	blake3:d7oYvw8Eegom48Tq2Tx6V_B-kkrbOCM7zbctg8eWbWc
1795321537779271	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ff	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`capture recover`: List unsettled handles/staging, preserve partial outputs, reconcile observed producer status using same run IDs; never rerun external actions.\"\n\n# Goals\nRecovery for interrupted producers and uncertain acknowledgements.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A producer crash may occur after effects but before finalization; recovery must not assume the operation was never performed.\" \"A missing record is not proof that no external effect occurred.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- List handles that are begun and not finalized, with accepted position and seal state.\n- Given a handle: return what was accepted (positions, digests, receipts) so the producer can resume at the right position.\n- Allow finalizing as partial/interrupted with execution state `unknown`, preserving staged bytes.\n- Reconcile through the same `run_id`; \"Recovery after ambiguous capture acknowledgement resumes the same run and never repeats the outside action.\"\n- Document producer obligations (they are not `inv` commands): if durable recording was required and initialization fails, do not start a new effectful unit; if the recorder fails mid-run, keep a bounded local spool and reconcile later.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An interrupted producer returns partial/incomplete artifacts and `unknown` external effect status; recovery never schedules a rerun.\n- [ ] Recover after a lost finalize acknowledgement reports the finalized state and original receipt.\n- [ ] No recover path deletes staged bytes.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nHonest handling of the gap between \"did it happen\" and \"did we record it\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.06.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.3 (capture recover; Producer readiness), §14.1, §17.6 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"m","title":"W07.07 capture recover: list unsettled handles, preserve partial outputs, never rerun"}	blake3:xBdtMmxQp53PQhmHcD6pNfTQG9avnVm0b5FDR0sMfjg
1795321537863634	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1jhe	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"An item arriving after sealing gets a closed-handle response, and late evidence is preserved through explicit linked reconciliation.\"\n\n# Goals\nDefined behaviour for items that arrive too late and for retries after staging is gone.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA finalized manifest is immutable; quietly accepting a late item would falsify it, and dropping it would lose evidence. Linked reconciliation keeps both truths.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- After seal: any append/attach on the handle returns a closed-handle response naming the finalized manifest.\n- A reconciliation path records late evidence as a new attributed record linked to the Run (`run.reconcile` plus ingest), with its own acquisition time.\n- After staging retirement: an identical retry of an earlier item resolves from the manifest's item-key/digest/receipt map and returns the original receipt.\n- Race: the last attachment versus sealing is decided by per-handle serialization; the loser gets closed-handle.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Racing the last attachment with sealing yields either an included item or a closed-handle response, never a silent loss.\n- [ ] After staging retirement an identical retry recovers its receipt and a late item cannot alter the manifest.\n- [ ] Late evidence is visible and linked to the Run.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nImmutable manifests and no lost evidence at the same time.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.06.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.3 (Per-handle positions and a precise seal), §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"s","title":"W07.08 Late items after sealing and receipt recovery after staging retirement"}	blake3:W7nqiP8E7WSqE1oLUhc--uzNvBG2v5C-U8OpRfPDN6w
1795321537948967	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-quh8	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Run staging is durable recovery material bound to a committed capture handle; do not treat it as disposable cache or garbage collect active/uncertain sessions.\"\n\n# Goals\nLifecycle rules for `state/staging/<run-id>/`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nStaging holds the only copy of partial outputs from an interrupted run. \"Never silently drop recoverable staged evidence or sweep it through temporary-file GC.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- States: active, sealed, finalized-retirable, interrupted.\n- Retire staging only after the finalization event is durable and referenced blobs verify.\n- Orphan temp cleanup (W02.12) must skip staging entirely.\n- `check` verifies staging consistency against handles; `doctor` lists unsettled handles.\n- Archive creation refuses or defers while unsettled staging exists (W11.09).\n- Interrupted staging is kept until explicitly recovered.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] No cleanup path removes staging for a non-finalized handle.\n- [ ] Interrupting between seal and canonical publication leaves a recoverable state.\n- [ ] Retirement is idempotent and crash-safe.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPartial evidence survives the crashes that make it partial.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.06.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2, §10.4, §10.7, §11.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"s","title":"W07.09 Staging durability and retirement: never garbage-collected while active or uncertain"}	blake3:D5N6wzDclzuxop77TIj9rseQtqUfDnhc27Jrw4dEhB4
1795321538029974	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1vla	{"description":"# Context & Background\nChronology `executor_bound` means \"an executor receipt binds the plan digest before its execution. Accept an in-toto Statement in a DSSE envelope as the receipt format rather than inventing one.\" `externally_attested` is, for example, \"an RFC 3161 token, which verifies offline\".\n\n# Goals\nReceipts are carried in `receipts[]`, stored as attributed artifacts, and mapped to chronology strength by documented rules.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe standards mapping (review 2 #14) was adopted as documentation, with exporters and verification deferred. So this bone stores and labels; it does not claim cryptographic verification it has not implemented. \"A timestamp over the plan alone only dates the plan.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Accept receipt objects with a declared format; retain bytes as artifacts; parse only the fields needed to compare the bound plan digest.\n- Chronology is set to `executor_bound` only when a receipt's subject digest equals the frozen plan digest; display the verification level (`structure_checked`, not `signature_verified`).\n- `externally_attested` requires evidence binding both the plan and the ordering of the execution event.\n- Document the formats and limits; verify against the current in-toto, DSSE and RFC 3161 specifications when writing.\n- Add signature verification to the deferred register.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A receipt whose subject does not match the plan digest does not upgrade chronology.\n- [ ] Displayed chronology always shows its verification level.\n- [ ] Documentation states what is and is not verified.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Never upgrade chronology from timestamps alone.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.06.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.5, §11.2 (receipts), Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (Standards mapping) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording","docs"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"s","title":"W07.10 Executor receipts: store in-toto/DSSE and RFC 3161 receipts and document chronology mapping"}	blake3:NHe6L4gKzkam0l0Gjb6cu9dmWICVLSyoL2NETPzvq08
1795321538103390	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1za8	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"For each supported consumer-language SDK ship fixtures proving: equivalent hashes/IDs, idempotent begin/append/attach/finalize, lost responses, truncated streams, changed manifest, exact acquisition mapping, incomplete controls, namespaced metadata preservation, schema future-version refusal and crash/restart recovery.\"\n\n# Goals\nA fixture suite expressed as JSON-CLI interactions, and runners under `scripts/`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"All integrations exercised through public conformance fixtures, not internal reach-through functions.\" If the suite is the contract, any language can prove conformance without reading Rust.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Each fixture: a sequence of JSON requests to the CLI protocol, injected faults (drop response, truncate stream, restart), and expected properties of the resulting canonical state.\n- Cover every item in the §11.7 list plus structural request comparison (W01.06 fixtures).\n- Expected results are stated as properties (same run ID on retry; same artifact digests; item at position N present), with normalized IDs.\n- Runner drives any producer executable that implements a small adapter interface.\n- Version the suite with the protocol.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The suite runs against the Rust example (W07.12) and the Python example (W07.13) and passes for both.\n- [ ] Each §11.7 item maps to at least one fixture.\n- [ ] A deliberately non-idempotent producer fails the suite.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nOne authoritative, testable contract for every consumer language.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.07, W07.08, W01.06.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.6, §11.7, §18.1 (scripts/), §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording","test","fixture"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"m","title":"W07.11 Language-neutral conformance fixture suite and cross-language runner"}	blake3:-E25BoJarXOAdKS60mXGnNvqPI9wfFJAocKiN4DYNvY
1795321538183058	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1540	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW07's acceptance needs a \"Rust fixture producer\". §11.6: \"The main package must contain no builtin `example-benchmark` client/CLI adapter: it's only an example manifest.\"\n\n# Goals\nA small standalone example under `examples/` that records a synthetic run via `investigation-recording`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe example is both documentation and a conformance subject. It must stay outside the core crates so the core keeps zero producer knowledge.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- A synthetic \"benchmark\" that fabricates deterministic sample data; it performs no real external action.\n- Uses begin → append lifecycle facts → attach output → finalize, with a crash/resume mode driven by a flag.\n- Lives in `examples/`, not in any core crate; excluded from the published core package.\n- Its name is generic; neutrality guards (W00.10) still pass.\n- Implements the adapter interface for the conformance runner.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Runs offline and produces a finalized Run in a `record`-mode case.\n- [ ] Passes the conformance suite.\n- [ ] Removing the example does not affect the core build.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nShows the native path without coupling the core to any producer.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.07.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.6, §18.1 (examples/), §19.2 (W07) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording","fixture"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"s","title":"W07.12 Rust example fixture producer using the recording crate"}	blake3:NiiPfQlhExMLo_VdJA1QXL0Ywk4H-TBffVaO8_scGpE
1795321538265824	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1tcb	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW07's acceptance: \"Python fixture producer and Rust fixture producer identical semantics.\" Other languages integrate through the JSON-CLI protocol.\n\n# Goals\nA small Python example that records the same synthetic run by spawning the CLI with JSON on stdin.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA second language is the only real test that the protocol, not the Rust types, is the contract — including strict JSON rules and structural idempotency.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Standard library only; spawn `investigation` with an argv array and JSON stdin; no shell interpolation.\n- Same scenario as the Rust example so canonical results can be compared.\n- Handles a lost response by retrying with the same command ID and by calling `capture recover`.\n- Runs under a pinned interpreter via `uv` (the machine's system Python is too new for much tooling; keep this example dependency-free).\n- Implements the conformance adapter interface.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Rust and Python/JSON producer conformance yield equivalent canonical envelopes and stable source mappings.\n- [ ] The example uses no private API and no Rust code.\n- [ ] Passes the conformance suite.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Any tool in any language can record runs and artifacts.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.07.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.2 (Neutral JSON events/manifest protocol), §11.3, §18.1 (examples/), §19.2 (W07) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording","fixture"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"s","title":"W07.13 Python example fixture producer using only the JSON-CLI protocol"}	blake3:W9nokAyivvhCZnBhSqlIuvunF4itVDwBv9DWMZE0kPk
1795321538348028	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1cqr	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW07's acceptance ends \"crash/retry qualified\". §20.3 lists the cases.\n\n# Goals\nFault-injection tests for the whole capture protocol using the storage fake (W02.01).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nCapture adds a second durable structure (staging, positions, seal) next to the journal. Its crash windows are new and must be tested as rigorously as the commit path.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nTests from §20.3:\n- \"Send position 3 when 2 is missing, retry an accepted item with changed bytes, race the last attachment with sealing, and interrupt between seal and canonical publication. Every acknowledged item survives at its position.\"\n- Begin/append/attach/finalize accept idempotent retries and recover from lost acknowledgement \"without duplicate runs or original acquisitions\".\n- Crash at each step of finalize: staging sealed but no event; blobs published but no event; event durable but staging not retired.\n- Property test: random interleavings of append/attach/crash/retry preserve the invariant that acknowledged implies recoverable.\n- Verify the commit lock is never held across simulated external work.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every §20.3 capture item has a passing targeted test.\n- [ ] No interleaving produces a duplicate Run or a manifest that differs from acknowledged items.\n- [ ] Failing seeds are committed as regressions.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe recorder is only \"reusable by independent consumers\" if it is trustworthy under failure.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.08, W07.09.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.4, §11.3, §19.2 (W07), §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording","test"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"m","title":"W07.14 Capture crash and retry qualification"}	blake3:kpLNfNk-TtbyIQwNsPps37-RxWNMiEOjcFTnNkhsKR4
1795321538427576	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-10bm	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv doctor`, `inv schema capture`, and `inv integration inspect` show available neutral schema versions, record size limits, supported selector validation and optional installed reader/method packs.\"\n\n# Goals\nProducers can discover everything they need without reading source code.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"No probing of external tools, no speculative CLI flags.\" \"Never advertise hypothetical tools from an unavailable capability.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Catalog entries for `capture.begin`, `capture.append`, `capture.attach`, `capture.finalize`, `capture.recover` and `producer-run.v1`, each with a working example against fixture files.\n- `schema capture` gives an overview and the lifecycle.\n- Extend `doctor` and `integration inspect` with protocol versions and limits (item size, attachment size, positions).\n- Future-version negotiation: an unsupported required version is refused with the supported list.\n- The §17.6 command path is executable against shipped fixtures.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All fixture JSON \"uses actual accepted schema and is tested mechanically\".\n- [ ] Schema future-version refusal returns the supported versions.\n- [ ] `inspect` output matches the binary's real capabilities.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nSelf-teaching for producers, as for agents.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.06.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.7, §17.6, §9.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"s","title":"W07.15 Capability discovery and schema output for capture"}	blake3:YLVKId24X0_nkizG1E0fjjR2auAvxNJ0nfXxkAyVpbE
1795321538510934	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2iuc	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Ship a separate producer-only onboarding page with the capture contract... No investigation methodology knowledge required just to record a program run.\" §11.8 adds migration guidance for producers with legacy journals.\n\n# Goals\nTwo documents: producer onboarding and a migration recipe.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The Investigation build only supplies neutral APIs, sample manifests, fixtures and migration recipes. It ships no migration module for a named producer... This is intentional decoupling, not abandonment of shared recording.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Onboarding: initialize recording, retain the same IDs and receipts, emit typed lifecycle facts, attach stable originals, finalize or mark partial, reconcile uncertain acknowledgements; producer obligations when recording fails.\n- Migration recipe, the seven steps of §11.8: inventory generic versus instrument-specific storage; freeze historical fixtures and crash/retry semantics; build a read-only idempotent migration tool that maps old to new IDs; integrate one low-risk path with exactly one authoritative journal and no dual writes; move the remaining paths; check external budget/permission enforcement still reconciles crash-time effects; retire duplicate writers.\n- Both use generic examples only; embedded in the binary's docs.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The onboarding page alone suffices to implement the Python example.\n- [ ] The migration recipe names no producer and adds no code to the core.\n- [ ] Both are reachable offline from the binary.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nIndependent consumers can adopt the recorder without the core ever learning their names.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.15.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.8, §21 (closing paragraph) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording","docs"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"s","title":"W07.16 Producer-only onboarding page and consumer migration guidance"}	blake3:tJTnG5QG34OamFCImBS7g6O5SmwEUJzI0cnt78sPKw4
1795321538599040	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2f3s	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.3: \"The exact same `producer-run.v1` schema records a synthetic benchmark, file-analysis report, simulation, and trace-capture session without core code changes.\"\n\n# Goals\nFour small fixture manifests and capture sequences that prove genericity.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"These interfaces allow a benchmarking tool to use the same recorder as a trace analyzer, simulator, research scraper, or telephony tester.\" If any of the four needed a core change, the envelope would not be neutral.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Four fixtures with different shapes: numeric metrics with units; a document-analysis report with extracted statements; a simulation with parameters and state snapshots; a trace capture with a time series and an `external_selector`.\n- Each recorded in `record` mode with no hypotheses, Obsidian or model installed.\n- Each exercises at least one incomplete or failed variant.\n- A test asserts all four pass with the same binary and that the core crates contain no fixture-specific branch.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All four record successfully through the same schema.\n- [ ] A standalone producer can record one Run in `record` mode with nothing else installed.\n- [ ] Core compilation and tests pass with zero installed producers.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDemonstrates binding decision \"Consumer API\" with evidence rather than assertion.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.01, W07.06.\nParent goal: W07. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §11.6, §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w07","milestone-b","lane-storage","recording","test","fixture"],"parent":"bn-1y1","size":"s","title":"W07.17 One schema, four producer kinds: benchmark, file analysis, simulation, trace capture"}	blake3:pubkukgwUQUolWTm0XvaI9JgYu2vnWMI3jR-OzXyW8o
1795321682115288	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1llt	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe vault is a projection of the journal, never a second source of truth. §18.3 sketches `CaseRenderer::render(&VerifiedSnapshot, &ViewPolicy) -> RenderPlan` and says: \"The renderer returns a path/hash/content **plan** that an ownership-aware publisher applies.\"\n\n# Goals\nSplit rendering into a pure planning step and a publishing step, under a lock that is separate from the commit lock.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA pure plan can be diffed, tested for determinism and reused by review exports (W11.07) without touching a working vault. Keeping the publisher separate is what makes \"Render or delete only files proven owned\" enforceable in one place. \"A projection conflict must not block unrelated evidence ingestion\", so rendering must never hold the commit lock.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `render(snapshot, policy)` reads one verified snapshot and returns `RenderPlan { generation, entries: [{path, content, sha256}] }` with stable ordering; no filesystem access, clock or randomness inside.\n- Publisher: takes the plan plus the ownership manifest (W08.02); writes each file by temp + atomic rename inside `vault/`; never follows symlinks out of the vault.\n- `state/locks/`-independent renderer lock (advisory, in `local/`), so two renders do not interleave while commits continue.\n- `ViewPolicy` carries audience and classification rules (W08.13).\n- Full-snapshot rendering only; incremental comes in W08.15.\n- Rendering works with Obsidian absent or closed.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The same snapshot and policy yield a byte-identical plan.\n- [ ] A commit succeeds while a render is in progress.\n- [ ] The planner has no write access to the vault in tests (enforced by type or fake).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision: canonical state is the journal; the vault is a rebuildable, inspectable view.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2, §12.7, §18.1 (obsidian/), §18.3 (CaseRenderer) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"m","title":"W08.01 Renderer architecture: pure RenderPlan from a snapshot, ownership-aware publisher, renderer lock"}	blake3:al04GUITqMJLHbgS5oDZlLGobCs7dY2E1vRFOB4iZjQ
1795321682205254	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-gek5	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Keep a path ownership manifest. Render or delete only files proven owned; never recursively erase foreign files.\" The manifest contains \"snapshot sequence/head, renderer version, owned paths, and content hashes\".\n\n# Goals\n`local/render-manifest.json` and the rules every publisher operation checks against it.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Investigation should **manage the casebook, not confiscate the entire vault**.\" A renderer that deletes by directory would eventually destroy a human note. Ownership by explicit path and last-generated hash makes that impossible by construction.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Manifest record per owned path: relative path, last generated SHA-256, generation, record ID it projects.\n- Publisher rules: create only at paths absent from disk or already owned; replace only when current bytes equal the last generated hash (otherwise drift, W08.09); delete only owned paths whose bytes still match.\n- Removing a projected record removes its owned file, never its directory if foreign files remain.\n- Manifest is written atomically and published last (W08.10); a lost manifest is rebuilt conservatively — unknown files are treated as foreign.\n- Unknown files anywhere in the vault are preserved and reported, never adopted silently.\n- Path validation: generated paths come only from validated IDs; no `..`, absolute paths or separators from record text.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A foreign file placed inside `Cases/<case-id>/` survives any number of renders.\n- [ ] Deleting the manifest and re-rendering destroys no file.\n- [ ] No publisher code path performs a recursive delete.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Human Notes/Boards/Inbox remain byte-identical through render and migration.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.01.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2 (local/render-manifest.json), §12.1, §12.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"m","title":"W08.02 Path ownership manifest: publish or delete only files proven owned"}	blake3:-kp6veQ9rC32_APIkn_shaxtr7iV902mh_xz3V7R2go
1795321682293520	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2zz0	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Every generated note includes: stable ID, type, case, exact basis revision/snapshot, effective state, short claim and applicability; typed backlinks with reasons; strongest source/contradiction; measurement/evaluation limits; exact evidence selectors; and a relevant next action or reviewer blocker.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe shared note skeleton and frontmatter emitter used by every per-type note (W08.04).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Use flat `inv_` YAML properties because property types are vault-wide and the property editor doesn't model arbitrary nested objects.\" \"Filename uses immutable safe ID; H1/aliases are display titles... A title change must not break links.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Frontmatter keys as in the §12.4 example: `inv_id`, `inv_kind`, `inv_case_id`, `inv_case` (quoted wikilink), `inv_status`, `inv_effective`, `inv_revision`, `inv_render_sequence`, `inv_schema: \"inv.vault.v1\"`, `inv_updated`, `aliases`, `tags`.\n- A tested YAML emitter that always quotes strings and links; no nested objects; nested metadata is rendered as plain Markdown tables in the body.\n- Path `Cases/<case-id>/<Type>/<id>.md`; internal links are built from validated object IDs with the title as display text.\n- A prominent banner states the note is a tool-owned projection and that edits are drift; it points to `Notes/`.\n- \"Stable excerpt block anchors include exact source digest/selector; rendered Markdown line numbers are not source authority.\"\n- Sections in fixed order: summary and caveat, typed backlinks with reasons, evidence selectors, next action or blocker.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Title changes do not rename identity-based files or break links.\n- [ ] Frontmatter parses as YAML with only flat scalar/list values for every record type.\n- [ ] Every generated note carries the projection banner and its basis revision.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nNotes stay meaningful as plain Markdown with or without Obsidian.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.01.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.4, §20.4, §23.6 [O3] (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"m","title":"W08.03 Entity note template: flat inv_ frontmatter, ID filenames, aliases, tool-owned banner, excerpt anchors"}	blake3:vG3XO7EfuV2uUA1K4B5cLdviiGrZbEFN7jSykY2JhOI
1795321682491303	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-osh3	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe vault tree in §4.2 lists a folder per type: Questions, Hypotheses, Options, Criteria, Comparisons, Assumptions, Sources, Evidence, Observations, Inquiries, Runs, Assessments, Conclusions, Reviews, Objections, Retrospectives, Decisions.\n\n# Goals\nA body renderer per record type on top of the W08.03 skeleton.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nW08's package text names \"source/evidence links, hypothesis view... blindspot/retrospective notes\". Each type has different things a reader must see first: a Hypothesis its rivals and contradicting data; a Comparison its measurement and read-set; a Conclusion its effective disposition and strongest objection.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- One module per type; each chooses its headline fields and typed backlink sections (\"typed backlinks with reasons\").\n- Evidence/Artifact notes show original versus derivative, integrity, completeness and sensitivity, with selectors — never raw bytes unless an approved derivative exists (W08.08).\n- Observation notes show the quote status and that a verified quote means presence, not support.\n- Reviews and Retrospectives: \"use normal generated notes and `inv_kind` flat properties (`retrospective`, `review`), with `inv_review_kind: blindspot|validation`, `inv_basis_sequence`, `inv_freshness`, and explicit note links.\"\n- Effective state comes from the overlay (W03.12), so a stale review reads as stale on its note and on its subject's note.\n- Authored text goes through the safe renderer (W08.12).\n- Blindspot-review content appears once W10.14 records exist; the note kind is the same Review note.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every record type in the Scenario A and C fixtures renders to its folder with working links both ways.\n- [ ] A stale review is marked stale on the Review note and on the subject note.\n- [ ] No note for a restricted artifact contains its bytes.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"A reviewer can traverse any important conclusion to the exact retained source/selector.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.03.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2 (vault tree), §12.4, §12.5 (Retrospectives and blindspot Reviews paragraph) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"l","title":"W08.04 Per-type generated notes for all record types, including Review and Retrospective notes"}	blake3:maWXcYSIkqK6Gky-Z123UPcpSMkhRgccDwt3rGT8QhY
1795321682577598	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1p1o	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEach Case Overview \"begins with a bounded summary: We are investigating **Q** because it informs **D**. Our current answer/choice is **C**, within scope **S**. The strongest original evidence is **E**, the strongest outstanding objection is **U**, and the most useful next information is **N**.\"\n\n# Goals\n`Cases/<case-id>/Overview.md`, generated from the same data as `brief`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA human opening the vault should get the same honest first paragraph an agent gets from `brief`. \"Counts are secondary; contradiction and applicability should be visible above decorative graphs.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Fill the Q/D/C/S/E/U/N sentence from typed fields; an absent element is stated as absent (\"no conclusion yet\"), never left blank or invented.\n- \"Show a **title/synopsis/full-detail ladder**: first the compact answer and its caveat; then linked evidence/assessments; then exact sources. Display `summary_current|stale` and the bound revision, never imply that a synopsis is independent corroboration.\"\n- Link the most recent blindspot review and process retrospective where present.\n- One concise section chosen by what the case actually contains: Explanation (rivals, decisive and nondiagnostic observations, confounds, inquiry timeline); Measurement (baseline/variant, units, workload, controls, repetitions, validity); Evaluation (options, hard constraints, criteria, sensitivity, decision); Research (source matrix, origin overlap, contradictions, currency).\n- \"Never display security-specific dashboards by default. Don't make a comparison case display 'zero conclusions' as though it failed.\"\n- N comes from `next` (W05a.10); the exact `brief`/`frontier` commands are printed for resumption.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Scenario A renders an Explanation section; Scenario C renders an Evaluation section with no empty \"Conclusions: 0\" block.\n- [ ] The strongest outstanding objection appears above any count.\n- [ ] The Overview and `brief` agree on answer, caveat and freshness for the same snapshot.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe human casebook tells the same bounded story the agent reads.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.04.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.2, §8.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"m","title":"W08.05 Case Overview: bounded opening summary, title/synopsis/full ladder and the mode-appropriate section"}	blake3:edgZmQlbwyScFYo5aGbKsl3sRYAkHJgNhbXNFQtvcmo
1795321682676390	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-245z	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`Start Here.md` lists cases, last committed snapshots, projection health, generated/human-owned conventions, pending questions, unresolved blindspot audits, available retrospectives, outstanding reviews, and exact `inv brief`/`inv frontier` resumption commands. No plugin required.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe vault's opening note.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA person who has never seen the tool opens this file first. It must say what is tool-owned, where their own notes go, and whether what they are looking at is current.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Case list with links to each Overview, the last committed snapshot and the render generation.\n- Projection health: complete, updating, partial or drifted (from W08.09/W08.10), with the command that explains it.\n- A short conventions block: generated notes are projections; write in `Notes/`, draw in `Boards/`, drop candidates in `Inbox/`.\n- Lists of pending questions, outstanding reviews, unresolved blindspot audits and available retrospectives, each bounded with a \"more\" link; a list with nothing in it says \"none identified\", not \"none\".\n- Exact resumption commands per case using the real executable name and case ID.\n- Plain Markdown only; no embedded queries.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Fresh vault is useful with community plugins disabled.\n- [ ] Each printed command runs verbatim against the workspace.\n- [ ] A partial render is announced on this page.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n§20.6's human gate begins here: understanding the case without the original chat.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.05.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.2, §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"s","title":"W08.06 Start Here.md: cases, snapshots, projection health, ownership conventions and resumption commands"}	blake3:Ib1VngGLEt8Tit7QxVb2TYag4Sq9Hkpq8Jul2qOPmjw
1795321682768855	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3dc7	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§12.5 lists fifteen views (Questions, Hypotheses, Options, Criteria, Comparison matrix, Sources, Evidence, Measurements, Inquiries/Runs, Conclusions, Decisions, Reviews/blindspots, Retrospectives/lessons, Epistemic frontier, Timeline). \"Ship static Markdown summary tables alongside dynamic views so the files remain meaningful outside Obsidian.\"\n\n# Goals\nA generated plain-Markdown table per view under each case. The dynamic `.base` equivalents are W12.01.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Tables are the baseline.\" \"**The comparison matrix is a generated static Markdown table in v1** with source links; a specialized interactive view belongs in an optional companion, not a mandatory dependency on a community query plugin.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- One table generator per view, with the default columns from §12.5.\n- Comparison matrix: option × criterion cells showing the exact measurement or read-set link, unknowns and limits; an unrecorded cell is shown as unknown; \"no autogenerated universal score\".\n- Hard-criterion failures and Pareto status (W04.07) are stated in words, not by colour alone.\n- Reviews table: \"zero gaps means none *identified*\".\n- Epistemic frontier table from W05b.01: \"Open/blocked/stale uncertainty and prerequisite evidence, not an external task board.\"\n- Timeline distinguishes observed from recorded times and shows corrections.\n- Bounded row counts with overflow links; stable sort keys; cell text escaped (W08.12).\n- Tables filter by exact case ID.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Scenario C's matrix shows every recorded cell with a link to its Comparison and every missing cell as unknown.\n- [ ] No table contains a computed overall score or rank.\n- [ ] All tables read correctly in a plain-text viewer.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nW08 acceptance: \"Layered casebook with community plugins disabled; gaps/lessons visible.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.04, W05b.01.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.3, §12.5, Disposition: Obsidian (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"l","title":"W08.07 Static Markdown tables for the shipped views, including the comparison matrix"}	blake3:3tZX9TLRZkuFSKMBa2DoGE_6ZwR-joa5i0ZezOz-LWk
1795321682856229	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1k9z	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§12.1's ownership table: `Notes/` is human freeform, \"preserved byte-for-byte by rendering\"; `Boards/` is \"never regenerated\"; `Inbox/` holds candidate material; `Attachments/` belongs to Investigation \"for listed derivatives only... unknown files preserved\".\n\n# Goals\nGuarantee the human areas are untouched and that attachments are only approved derivatives.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nPeople will only keep notes beside a generated casebook if the generator provably never edits them. And \"Stored originals and exact source lineage stay outside projected vault unless explicit presentation policy allows a carefully reviewed derivative.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- The publisher refuses any plan entry under `Notes/`, `Boards/` or `Inbox/` (a hard check, not a convention).\n- `Attachments/<approved-derivative-id>.<ext>`: written only for derivatives with a manifest entry and a presentation policy that allows them (W06b.04, W06b.07); never originals by default.\n- Generated notes may link into `Notes/` or `Inbox/`; \"unrecorded text is not evidence until explicitly snapshotted and assessed\".\n- Document the rule that a human note relied on by an assessment is snapshotted by hash (implemented for exports in W11.14).\n- Obsidian configuration is never written by render.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A hash of `Notes/`, `Boards/` and `Inbox/` is unchanged across render, re-render and conflict handling.\n- [ ] An unknown file in `Attachments/` is preserved and reported.\n- [ ] No original restricted artifact appears in the vault.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA coherent casebook that leaves the human's thinking space alone.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.02.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.1, §16.2, §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"s","title":"W08.08 Human-owned Notes, Boards and Inbox preserved byte for byte; Attachments limited to listed derivatives"}	blake3:LXo3Vwf5zU8yO5Ga3jILpTsxJPpyKp6qfFM4ADgx42A
1795321682945489	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-13vy	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Before replacing a managed file, compare its current bytes with the last generated hash. If they differ, **leave it in place**, retain the would-be generated version in the conflicts area, mark the path drifted, and surface the issue. Renames/deletions are drift too; they do not delete canonical records.\"\n\n# Goals\nDetection and safe handling of every kind of drift. Resolution dispositions are W12.05.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Checking a box, dragging a Base card, or editing a property does not validate a conclusion or accept a decision. **Native Bases edits change underlying Markdown properties**; they must be detected as projection drift.\" The contract is honest about limits: drift detection \"preserves edits it observes but cannot guarantee preservation of every racing unsupported edit. Do not claim otherwise.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Before each replace or delete: read current bytes, compare with the manifest hash.\n- On mismatch: do not write; store the would-be content under `local/conflicts/<generation>/<path>`; record the path as drifted with kind (edited, missing, renamed, conflict-copy present).\n- Detect sync-client conflict copies next to owned files and report them without deleting.\n- Drifted paths stay drifted across renders until reconciled; other files continue to render.\n- \"No automatic Markdown-to-domain inference.\"\n- Document the supported contract: \"do not edit generated notes directly; use Notes or controlled mutations\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Generated note edits, Base property edits, deletes, renames and conflict copies are diagnosed as drift.\n- [ ] The edited file's bytes are unchanged after render and the generated version is retrievable from the conflicts area.\n- [ ] Drift on one note does not stop the rest of the case rendering.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nW08 acceptance: \"drift detected, notes preserved.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.02.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2 (local/conflicts/), §12.7, §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"m","title":"W08.09 Drift detection: edited, renamed or deleted generated files are left in place and surfaced"}	blake3:jJWoyXjFQHldzrkVSSbXI9w12v14Ke0zJQ39raVaBbQ
1795321683034927	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1zxx	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"A multi-file render is not globally atomic to a reader. Every note carries a generation/snapshot marker, and the completed manifest/index is published last. While rendering, expose `updating`/last-complete generation. After failure, mark `partial`; `brief` continues reading canonical state.\"\n\n# Goals\nHonest signalling of whether the vault on disk matches one snapshot.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA reader — or a companion plugin — that mixes notes from two generations could see a conclusion beside a review that no longer covers it. Markers let any reader detect that; they do not pretend the render was atomic.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Generation ID per render, bound to the snapshot sequence and head; `inv_render_sequence` in every note.\n- Stage plan content under `cache/renders/<generation-id>/` before publishing.\n- State file in `local/`: `complete(gen)`, `updating(from, to)`, `partial(gen, reason)`.\n- Publish order: notes, then tables, then `Start Here.md`, then the manifest last.\n- A crash mid-render leaves `updating`; the next `vault status` reports `partial` and the last complete generation.\n- Core reads (`brief`, `show`, `next`) never consult the vault.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Render failure advertises partial/stale state; the CLI still reports current canonical state.\n- [ ] Killing a render at each file leaves a state from which the next render completes.\n- [ ] A reader can tell from any single note which snapshot it reflects.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe projection never claims more currency than it has.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.01.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2 (cache/renders/), §12.7, §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"s","title":"W08.10 Render generations: per-note marker, manifest published last, updating and partial states"}	blake3:kPA-FK5EoIVIkD28U-hJI5GYz665MS1o7bi-_XwKLf8
1795321683144030	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2dyf	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`vault render/status/conflicts/reconcile`: Publish and inspect owned Obsidian projection, manage drift. Mutation: generated view only unless explicit domain mutation.\" `reconcile` is W12.05.\n\n# Goals\nThe three CLI commands that drive and inspect the projection.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nRendering is explicit and separate from committing: a commit that succeeded must not report failure because a note was open in an editor. W05a.13 already distinguishes \"committed, projection needs attention\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `vault render [--case ID]`: plan from the current verified snapshot, publish, report counts of written, unchanged, drifted and skipped paths plus the generation.\n- `vault status`: last complete generation versus current head, state (complete/updating/partial), drift count, foreign-file count.\n- `vault conflicts`: list drifted paths with kind and the location of the retained generated version.\n- All three are catalog entries with effect metadata, so `--read-only` rejects `render` (W05b.09).\n- JSON output is one object; text output is a short summary with the next command.\n- Never opens Obsidian or any viewer.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Core CLI/rendering works without Obsidian installed.\n- [ ] `vault status` reports stale after a commit and current after a render.\n- [ ] `vault render` exits successfully with drift present and lists it.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes projection health something an agent can check in one call.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.05, W08.09, W08.10.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.1 (vault render/status/conflicts/reconcile), §12.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian","cli"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"s","title":"W08.11 vault render, vault status and vault conflicts commands"}	blake3:I6KGO7VY4aIud4RP5YIWZPZ6apZTLjIC6tmPGOVvkx0
1795321683236820	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2vd3	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Render excerpts in escaped plain text or safely delimited code blocks with a fixed non-executable language; choose a delimiter longer than any matching run in the excerpt. Never use a source-provided fence language. Sanitize HTML and generated labels, and construct internal links from validated object IDs. Disable remote embeds and command-capable URI links in captured content.\"\n\n# Goals\nOne escaping library for the Markdown channel, used by every note, table and (later) Canvas label.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nObsidian renders HTML, embeds, callouts, plugin code fences and URI links. A captured log line or an agent-written title could otherwise become active content in the reader's vault. \"Authored text is data too.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Contexts with distinct escapers: YAML scalar, table cell, inline text, block excerpt, link display text, filename.\n- Block excerpts: fenced with a fixed language tag and a fence longer than any backtick/tilde run inside.\n- Neutralize raw HTML, `![[...]]` and `![](...)` embeds, wikilinks and Markdown links found in data; show URLs as inert text.\n- Never emit a link whose target comes from data; internal links come from validated IDs only.\n- Block plugin-style fences (for example query or script languages) by never passing through a source-provided language.\n- Strip control characters and bidirectional overrides from labels.\n- A hostile-content corpus drives tests for every context.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Hostile HTML, remote embeds, plugin fences, YAML values and URI text stay inert in previews.\n- [ ] A title containing `]]`, `|`, a newline and `---` cannot break frontmatter, a table or a link.\n- [ ] No generated file contains a remote embed.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nProduct integrity for humans and agents reading adversarial evidence — with the stated limit that labeling does not prevent every failure.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.03.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §16.1, §12.4, §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian","security"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"m","title":"W08.12 Safe rendering of untrusted and authored text in Markdown: fences, HTML, embeds, links, YAML"}	blake3:rFRk3Q5T3lgJ1Q-saedTy4EQXi_jNeM4LlZnRxDs-n8
1795321683325236	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1c8y	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"A sensitive title/path/metadata or Canvas label can leak even if the raw bytes are omitted. Enforce audience policy on every rendering channel.\"\n\n# Goals\n`ViewPolicy` applied to bodies, titles, aliases, filenames, table cells, counts and attachments in the working vault.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe working vault may be synced or opened by tools the workspace owner did not intend to see restricted material. And hiding an objection can make a conclusion look stronger than it is: \"Compute effective state and material support/objections against the full canonical snapshot *before* audience filtering.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Use the classification query helper from W06b.07 for every emitted field.\n- Default vault audience is `internal`; restricted records render as a placeholder that reveals no title, or are omitted, per policy.\n- Effective state and strongest caveat are computed on the full snapshot, then filtered; if the caveat cannot be shown, the dependent statement is shown with an approved general caveat or omitted — never shown clean.\n- Counts and table rows follow the same decision so totals do not reveal hidden items.\n- Filenames are IDs, so no title leaks through a path; aliases follow policy.\n- The stricter review-export policy and fresh-destination build are W11.07.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A restricted record's title appears nowhere in the vault under the default policy (body, alias, table, backlink reason).\n- [ ] A conclusion whose decisive objection is hidden is not rendered as unqualified.\n- [ ] Changing policy and re-rendering removes previously shown content from owned files.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nSafe defaults without weakening what the reader is told about uncertainty.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.04, W06b.07.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §16.2, §16.3 (Filtering must not strengthen a recommendation), §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian","security"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"m","title":"W08.13 Classification-aware projection: audience policy on every vault channel"}	blake3:sASXS9tudMSEWDi60AvDbLKlWj9krkhG00DuaEWeXpo
1795321683408294	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1v03	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW08's demonstrated acceptance: \"Layered casebook with community plugins disabled; gaps/lessons visible, drift detected, notes preserved.\" §20.4 lists the checks.\n\n# Goals\nAn automated suite over rendered fixture vaults that needs no Obsidian process.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nEverything the renderer promises is checkable from files: determinism, link integrity, ownership and inertness. Checks that need a running Obsidian (Bases and Canvas opening) are W12.07.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Render Scenario A and C (and D once available) twice from the same snapshot; compare owned bytes. \"Identical snapshot renders produce identical owned bytes, excluding explicitly local view state.\"\n- Link checker: every wikilink, alias and excerpt block anchor resolves to an existing owned file or a declared human area.\n- Rename a title, re-render, re-check links and filenames.\n- Seed human files in `Notes/`, `Boards/`, `Inbox/`; hash before and after.\n- Apply each drift kind and assert the diagnosis.\n- Assert no file depends on a community plugin: no query or script fences, no plugin-specific syntax.\n- Assert the layered ladder exists: Overview → entity note → evidence selector for each conclusion and decision.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every file-checkable §20.4 item has a passing test.\n- [ ] The suite runs offline in CI without Obsidian.\n- [ ] Golden vaults are regenerated only by an explicit command.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTurns the casebook's promises into regression tests.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.06, W08.07, W08.08, W08.11, W08.12, W08.13.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.2 (W08), §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian","test"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"m","title":"W08.14 Vault determinism, link-stability and plugins-disabled fixture tests"}	blake3:2J8SGihIo369quoOcAYEM0I1g_x60eAvvvqTNZbkz1Y
1795321683492666	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3a7l	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Render only changed files after the first implementation's full-snapshot path is correct.\"\n\n# Goals\nSkip unchanged files at publish time without changing what a full render would produce.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nRewriting every note on every render makes sync clients and Obsidian's file watcher churn, and costs time on large cases. But correctness comes first, so this is an optimization of the publisher only: the planner still produces the full plan.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Compare each plan entry's hash with the manifest hash and the on-disk bytes; write only when they differ.\n- The generation marker lives in frontmatter, so decide and document whether an otherwise unchanged note keeps its previous `inv_render_sequence` (preferred: yes, with the manifest recording the generation that last confirmed it).\n- Property test: for random sequences of commits, incremental publishing yields the same owned tree as a clean full render.\n- Measure render time on the 10,000-object fixture (W05b.15) before and after.\n- No dependency-tracking cache that could go stale; the full plan is the source of truth.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A one-record change rewrites only the files whose content changed.\n- [ ] Incremental and clean renders are byte-identical for every tested history.\n- [ ] Drift detection behaves the same in both modes.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA casebook that stays quiet on disk when nothing changed.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.14.\nParent goal: W08. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.7, §10.5 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w08","milestone-b","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-xz8","size":"s","title":"W08.15 Incremental render: write only changed files once the full-snapshot path is correct"}	blake3:qJSgjg3SsWygSxStUutcX8Iy4tOmM5Ah-QAk6gJK1ro
1795321766506058	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1toi	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"A pack describes `id`, version, applicable case modes, contextual triggers, method cards, optional namespaced properties, observation guidance, validation policy proposals, Obsidian views, and report templates. It **cannot execute commands, load binary code, contact services, or grant authority**.\"\n\n# Goals\nA versioned pack file format and a loader that treats a pack purely as data.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nGuidance has to be extensible per domain without the core learning any domain. The safe way is a closed, schema-checked vocabulary: \"Method packs are read-only declarative data validated before use; they are not implementations of Rust callbacks.\" \"No arbitrary DSL that executes code.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Pack schema (`inv.method-pack.v1`): `id`, `version`, applicable modes, cards (name, trigger reference, body sections), optional namespaced property schemas, observation guidance, policy proposals, view and report template references.\n- Triggers reference built-in gap-rule IDs and case facts from a fixed enumerated list; a pack cannot define a new executable predicate.\n- Strict parser (W01.04 rules): unknown keys rejected; size and nesting bounds; no includes, no URLs fetched, no templates with code.\n- Loader returns an immutable in-memory pack plus its SHA-256 digest over the file bytes.\n- Card bodies are plain text rendered as guidance only when the pack is built in or pinned (W09.02).\n- Lives under `methods/`; the loader depends on `core` only.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A pack with an unknown key, an executable-looking field or an oversize body is rejected with a field path.\n- [ ] Loading a pack performs no process spawn, network call or write.\n- [ ] The same file always yields the same digest and parsed value.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision: packs \"never execute code or alter canonical invariants\".\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W09. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.4, §4.3, §11.6, §18.1 (methods/), §18.3 (closing paragraph) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology","security"],"parent":"bn-3fk","size":"m","title":"W09.01 Method pack schema and inert loader: declarative data only, validated before use"}	blake3:GDJPhclR2Wlym1XSiQeTDD1Ea8Ima1N_ZKeB3DApLhk
1795321766647429	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3abq	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Method packs are non-executable data pinned by ID/version/digest, with explicit adoption.\" \"Core can ingest a pack file supplied by the user and pin its digest in the case. Existing cases do not silently adopt newer revisions.\"\n\n# Goals\nA journaled adoption record and resolution of a case's pinned packs at read time.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nIf a pack file changed on disk could change which gates apply to an already-reviewed case, history would be rewritten by editing a text file. Pinning by digest makes policy part of the case record.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- An attributed operation adopts a pack for a case: stores ID, version, digest and the pack bytes as an Artifact so the case stays self-contained offline.\n- Adoption of a newer revision is a new explicit operation with a reason; earlier reviews keep the policy version they were made under.\n- Resolution: built-in `general` is always available; other packs apply only when pinned.\n- A pinned pack whose bytes are missing or whose digest mismatches is reported as unavailable; \"An uninstalled/unknown method pack does not prevent valid generic recording, inspection or export.\"\n- \"No pack can change canonical serialization, source integrity checks, evidence trust rules, transaction atomicity or access-control decisions.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Editing a pack file on disk does not change behaviour for a case pinned to the earlier digest.\n- [ ] A case with an unknown pinned pack still records, reads and exports.\n- [ ] Adoption appears in the journal with actor, digest and reason.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n§20.3: a pack cannot \"alter historical case policy by updating on disk\".\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W09.01.\nParent goal: W09. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.4, §4.3, §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3fk","size":"s","title":"W09.02 Pack pinning and explicit adoption per case; no silent upgrade"}	blake3:CRzZqhiz-9jKFgf887GDMqKh2S7nRIEYP_2RiYZg8ZQ
1795321766745892	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-bq5l	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Ship **`general`** built-in as the only compulsory pack.\" §2.4's table defines 23 cards with a trigger and an output or warning each.\n\n# Goals\nWrite eleven cards: `frame`, `alternatives`, `source-quality`, `measure`, `baseline`, `discriminate`, `negative-result`, `delta-debug`, `intervene`, `causal-structure`, `reference-class`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nCards are where the methodology research of §2.1 reaches the agent, one at a time and at the moment it applies. Several were added by reviews: `intervene` (review 2), `causal-structure` and `reference-class` (review 1). \"Research must not become ritual\", so each card must say when it does not apply.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- One short card each: when it applies, what to record, the exact operations and schemas to use, a common mistake, and when to skip.\n- Content from the table, for example `intervene`: \"Change exactly one variable; record the reproduction rate before and after as k of n; apply the fix and see the failure stop, revert it and see it return.\"\n- `causal-structure`: confounders (control), mediators (do not block), colliders or selection filters (\"do not analyze failure-only data without a healthy baseline\"), and the severity question.\n- `reference-class`: \"Name a reference class and its base rates before weighing case evidence. Skippable with a reason.\"\n- `discriminate`: per-rival predicted outcomes (ties to W03.04).\n- Domain-neutral wording and examples; no named products.\n- Triggers reference gap-rule IDs from W05a.10.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All eleven cards load from the built-in pack and name only operations that exist in the catalog (checked in CI).\n- [ ] Each card fits one screen and states its skip condition.\n- [ ] No card instructs the agent to execute an external action through `inv`.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Contextual cards teach challenge and method learning.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W09.01.\nParent goal: W09. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.1, §2.4 (Guidance cards) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology","docs"],"parent":"bn-3fk","size":"m","title":"W09.03 Built-in general pack, part A: framing, explanation and measurement cards"}	blake3:QG6Ou7MOWyumPbHcq6t8hkQyjPTImKf9Gjtm9ZMKW9A
1795321766847800	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-wn33	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe remaining twelve cards of the built-in `general` pack.\n\n# Goals\nWrite `premortem`, `criteria`, `compare`, `sensitivity`, `independence`, `assumptions`, `review`, `blindspot`, `retrospective`, `stalled`, `handoff`, `close`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nW09's package text singles out the \"`blindspot`/`retrospective` cards\". They carry the parts of the method a deterministic rule cannot: \"what other mechanism, hidden constraint, falsifier or measurement failure might matter?\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Same card shape as W09.03.\n- `blindspot`: \"Check missing rival explanations/options, assumptions, instrument limitations, source families, unseen decision criteria and what could overturn the result; use `inv blindspots` for structural signals.\" State that a clean checklist is not completeness and that same-session self-review is low-independence.\n- `retrospective`: \"Evidence-linked methods tried, effective discriminators, invalid/misleading work, reusable lessons, transfer limits and future triggers\", starting from the process digest.\n- `premortem`: \"Assume the choice failed; trace concrete failure stories back to untested assumptions and reopen triggers. Advisory only.\"\n- `stalled`: \"Narrow question, better measurement, new source, or justified stop.\"\n- `handoff`: \"Current claim/choice, evidence, contradictions, next allowed *recording* step.\"\n- `close`: bounded outcome, residual uncertainty, reopen trigger.\n- Cards that reference W10 operations (`blindspots`, review kinds) are checked against the catalog once those exist.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All twelve cards load and pass the catalog-reference check.\n- [ ] `blindspot` and `retrospective` state their limits explicitly.\n- [ ] No card presents a count or checklist as proof of sufficiency.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTeaches honest challenge and honest closure without a mandatory second agent.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W09.01.\nParent goal: W09. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.4 (Guidance cards), §8.5, §8.6 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology","docs"],"parent":"bn-3fk","size":"m","title":"W09.04 Built-in general pack, part B: evaluation, review and process cards"}	blake3:5_l2j3-8aqg4ULgxNDDlIysx17HjUMac3vZ0vw1jlcM
1795321766949469	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2a6i	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`guide NAME` / `method list`: Contextual investigative method cards.\" And: \"`inv next` should show one relevant card, not force the agent to read the whole corpus.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe two read commands and the hook that attaches a card to a recommendation.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAn agent should meet a card because its situation triggered it, not by reading a manual. One card per recommendation keeps the cost bounded.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `guide NAME [--pack ID]`: print one card with its pack ID, version and digest; unknown names return close matches.\n- `method list`: built-in and pinned packs, their cards and one-line triggers, grouped by stage; marks unavailable pinned packs.\n- `next`: each recommendation may carry at most one `guide` reference chosen by the gap rule that produced it; pinned-pack cards take precedence over `general` for the same trigger.\n- Card text is guidance because it comes from the binary or a pinned pack; any case text shown beside it stays quoted data (W05b.13).\n- Wire the stalled patterns (W05b.03) to the `stalled` card.\n- Both commands work with no workspace (built-in pack only).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `guide blindspot` works outside a workspace.\n- [ ] A recommendation never carries more than one card.\n- [ ] A card from an unpinned, merely present pack file is never shown as guidance.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMethod teaching that arrives just in time and costs one screen.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W09.02, W09.03, W09.04.\nParent goal: W09. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.4 (closing paragraph), §7.3, §9.1 (guide / method list) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology","cli"],"parent":"bn-3fk","size":"s","title":"W09.05 guide NAME, method list and one contextual card in next"}	blake3:I_Db3TyQ3H523SiEBOoXwV2090IiAILlPVWYRhG10Xw
1795321767049391	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-36dl	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"A domain profile can propose extra optional fields under a namespaced extension object with schema; unknown producer-origin fields remain raw metadata, not executable hooks.\" The name was reserved in W01.15.\n\n# Goals\nPacks can add optional typed fields to existing record types without a change to Rust record types.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Make it possible to implement another method pack without changing Rust record types.\" A performance pack wants a warm-up count on a Run; a source-research pack wants an edition field on a Source. Those belong in a namespaced extension, validated but never interpreted by core gates.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `extensions: {\"<pack-id>\": {...}}` on record payloads; accepted only when the case pins that pack and the object validates against the pack's JSON Schema subset.\n- The schema subset is closed: scalar types, enums, bounded strings, arrays and objects; no remote `$ref`, no patterns with unbounded cost.\n- Core never branches on extension values except through declared policy inputs (W09.07).\n- Shown in `full` reads and vault notes as labeled pack fields; included in exports byte for byte.\n- An extension for an unpinned pack is rejected with a repair that names adoption; producer metadata uses the opaque path instead (W06b.10).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A new declarative method pack can add a guidance card and custom optional fields **without modifying core code**.\n- [ ] An invalid extension value is rejected with its field path.\n- [ ] Records with extensions replay after the pack file is removed from disk.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDomain depth lives in packs; the core stays general.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W09.02.\nParent goal: W09. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.4, §4.3, §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3fk","size":"m","title":"W09.06 Namespaced extension properties declared by a pack, schema-checked and inert"}	blake3:UmYT2Df6rZlLbOad4UgOSS2l1yzbpA8bBXq95hDgfyM
1795321767147961	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-24ul	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Pack rules can require fields on *promotion* (e.g., review-ready) but never prevent recording raw evidence. Machine-enforced policy inputs are schema-checkable; a prose suggestion is advisory.\"\n\n# Goals\nA small, closed set of policy inputs a pinned pack may set, consulted by the existing gates.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nSome teams want a challenge review before any consequential decision. The plan allows that only as declared data: \"A method pack can *advise* a blindspot review or require one at a declared consequential gate only through versioned schema-bound policy; no pack runs a model, scanner or command to conduct that review.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Enumerated inputs, each tied to a named gate: required fields for `conclusion.promote`; require a current blindspot review before `conclusion.validate` or `decision.accept`; additional material-case triggers for the closure Retrospective; require a distinct actor/context or explicit human confirmation for sensitive writes (consumed by W10.19).\n- Gates read the policy from the case's pinned packs at the pinned version; blockers name the pack and rule.\n- A policy input can only add requirements to promotion; a test asserts no input can reject `observation.add`, `ingest` or `capture`.\n- Policy changes apply from adoption onward; earlier accepted records are not retroactively invalidated, but freshness can surface them.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] With a pack requiring a blindspot review, `decision.accept` is blocked with a machine-readable blocker until one is current.\n- [ ] No policy input can block evidence preservation.\n- [ ] Unpinning is impossible to do silently: policy at each gate is recorded with the transaction.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Hard gates block *promotion*, never preservation of a surprising source.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W09.02.\nParent goal: W09. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.4 (closing paragraph), §6.4, §8.5, §16.1 (Write-side guardrails), §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3fk","size":"m","title":"W09.07 Pack policy inputs: require fields or reviews at promotion gates, never on recording"}	blake3:nEPfdU1t5A-khjzYK3F1iD8GeVC4aJ55wpsGaNH1hms
1795321767252378	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3v9p	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Separately ship optional examples/docs for `debugging`, `performance`, `source-research`, `decision-analysis`, and `security-research`. The packages use the same public schema; none is special-cased in the core.\"\n\n# Goals\nTwo example packs under `methods/` that exercise cards, extensions and policy inputs.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nExample packs are the proof that the pack schema is sufficient. If either needed a core change, the schema would be wrong.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `debugging`: cards specializing `discriminate`, `delta-debug` and `intervene` (bisection bookkeeping: per-commit run counts, skipped commits, re-verified endpoints); an extension for reproduction rate as k of n; a policy proposal that a causal conclusion needs an intervention or a stated reason.\n- `performance`: cards specializing `measure` and `baseline` (workload, warm-up, repetitions, environment); extensions for workload identity and sample counts; a policy proposal requiring baseline comparability fields at promotion.\n- Each pack ships with a tiny fixture case that pins it.\n- Not installed or pinned by default; documented as examples.\n- Vocabulary stays generic; neutrality guards (W00.10) pass.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Both packs load, pin and drive `next` cards in their fixture cases.\n- [ ] Neither required a change under `crates/investigation-core`.\n- [ ] Removing both leaves all core tests passing.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMilestone B: \"No false security-/performance-specific defaults leak into the core.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W09.06, W09.07.\nParent goal: W09. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.4, §17.1, §17.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology","docs"],"parent":"bn-3fk","size":"s","title":"W09.08 Optional example packs: debugging and performance"}	blake3:nTwkiqH18YKTmuLkZQ5tkPfbBPG1RgRyCXiTeht_Lhg
1795321767353639	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-25ro	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe remaining three example packs. \"No `security` mode is a privileged type in the core; any specialized methodology is an optional pack.\"\n\n# Goals\nThree more example packs under `methods/`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe security pack matters as a demonstration that a domain with strong vocabulary can be served entirely from outside the core. The other two back Scenarios C and D.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `source-research`: cards for `source-quality`, `independence` and `negative-result` applied to documents (edition, currency, origin tracing, recorded searches); an edition/currency extension on Sources.\n- `decision-analysis`: cards for `criteria`, `compare`, `sensitivity` and `premortem`; a policy proposal requiring a reopen trigger and disclosed subjective preferences at `decision.accept`.\n- `security-research`: guidance cards only, about evidence handling and scoping of claims; it grants no authority and proposes no action. \"A source containing instructions, a decision card that recommends a test, or a method pack cannot authorize a network request... or other side effect.\"\n- Each ships with a small fixture that pins it.\n- None is default.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All three load and pin without core changes.\n- [ ] The security pack contains no executable content, target, command or authority claim.\n- [ ] Core defaults and the Overview show nothing security-specific when no such pack is pinned.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nGeneral-purpose core, specialized guidance by opt-in.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W09.06, W09.07.\nParent goal: W09. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.4, §4.3, §17.3, §17.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology","docs"],"parent":"bn-3fk","size":"s","title":"W09.09 Optional example packs: source-research, decision-analysis and security-research"}	blake3:j6GfqTaYpnzKz4hbCkOIrNkn9NqayasidZI8tnc8Jvw
1795321767464287	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-ket6	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW09's acceptance: \"packs never execute code or alter canonical invariants.\" §20.3 has four method-pack items.\n\n# Goals\nA test suite of hostile and edge-case packs.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nPacks are user-supplied files that influence guidance and gates. They are the most plausible route for someone to try to smuggle behaviour into the tool.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Hostile packs: fields named like hooks or commands; card bodies containing instructions to run tools; triggers naming unknown predicates; policy inputs targeting recording operations; extension schemas with remote references; oversize and deeply nested files.\n- Assert each is rejected at load or rendered inert.\n- \"Method pack cannot create executable hooks, replace core provenance rules, grant execution permission or alter historical case policy by updating on disk.\"\n- Run the whole core suite with `methods/` containing only `general`, and with a pinned pack missing.\n- Assert pack text shown through `guide` is attributed to its pack and digest.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each §20.3 method-pack item has a passing test.\n- [ ] No hostile pack causes a spawn, network call, write outside the journal or a changed integrity rule.\n- [ ] An uninstalled/unknown method pack does not prevent valid generic recording, inspection or export.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps the extension point from becoming an attack surface.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W09.05, W09.06, W09.07.\nParent goal: W09. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.2 (W09), §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w09","milestone-b","lane-methodology","test","security"],"parent":"bn-3fk","size":"s","title":"W09.10 Method pack safety and independence tests"}	blake3:_Oba7qZiE9R5-rn7jPhpF8NptnZ8YafUE-pVwLtRcnI
1795321911076922	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-am9n	{"description":"# Context & Background\nMeasurement validity is its own outcome dimension: \"`valid`, `invalid`, `partial`, `unknown` **per observation/channel** — Do instrument/setup/controls support interpreting this measurement?\" The operation: \"`measurement.assess`: Observation/channel validity, instrument/control or equivalent verification, assessor/rationale/limits.\"\n\n# Goals\nImplement the operation and make validity visible wherever the observation is used.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A benchmark process exiting 0 does not establish a sound performance result. A tool timeout does not mean the measured system timed out.\" Validity must be a separate attributed judgment so an invalid instrument can be recorded without deleting what it produced.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Payload: observation ref and revision (optionally a channel), validity value, the instrument/control or equivalent verification relied on, assessor, rationale, limits.\n- Several assessments may coexist and disagree; the latest per assessor is current; none is deleted.\n- A producer's own `controls[]` claims stay `producer_reported` until an assessment cites them (W07.01).\n- Reads show validity beside every use of the observation; `unknown` is the default and is displayed, not hidden.\n- An `invalid` assessment feeds the freshness engine (W10.05).\n- \"Correction of an instrument invalidates affected interpretations without deleting historical observations.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A valid control under the wrong build does not qualify the target treatment measurement.\n- [ ] Marking an observation invalid leaves it readable and marks dependent assessments `needs_review`.\n- [ ] Execution status and measurement validity can differ on the same Run in every combination.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle: separate what was attempted, what survived, and whether it can be interpreted.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.3, §9.4 (measurement.assess), §20.1 (Explanatory, Performance) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.01 measurement.assess: attributed validity per observation or channel"}	blake3:RhXT2mMa06JiVGlpLlggviKv_xb1qTG6l9d4F2Kl3VE
1795321911159957	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-rc98	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.1 requires that a measurement \"includes units, workload identity, observation window, sample count or explicit unknown, environment/tool identity and limitations\", and adds the fields reviews 2 and 3 asked for.\n\n# Goals\nOptional, typed measurement fields on Observations and Runs, each allowed to be explicitly unknown.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe advisory checks in W10.03 can only do \"arithmetic on a recorded count\". If the count, order or load model is not recorded in a typed field, nothing can be flagged and the reader cannot judge comparability.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Units, workload identity, observation window, environment and tool identity, limitations.\n- Sample count, or explicit unknown.\n- \"Raw samples carry their repetition level (build, process, iteration) and order; a flat array is accepted.\"\n- Trial order: `interleaved`, `randomized`, `sequential`.\n- Load model: open or closed loop, and \"whether time is measured from intended send time\".\n- Attempt accounting: \"Started, observed, timed-out, excluded and unknown counts are preserved with their definitions.\"\n- Whether instrumentation changed between compared runs; variables changed (with `evidence_kind` from W03.06).\n- Values stay decimal strings (W01.04). No field is inferred from another.\n- Catalog examples for a benchmark and for a non-performance measurement, to keep the fields generic.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each field round-trips and may be `unknown` without rejecting the observation.\n- [ ] A flat sample array is accepted.\n- [ ] \"Repeating a benchmark creates a distinct Run with an explicit acquisition identity and shared setup dependencies.\"\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes measurement conditions inspectable, so comparability is argued from the record.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.01.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.2, §20.1 (Performance/measurement), Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.02 Measurement fields: units, workload, window, repetition level, trial order, load model, attempt counts"}	blake3:ziuiUIuV6QZn5OjZGl5lhUY8elc2GZ425vstTNj0X7g
1795321911239448	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3vti	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.1 lists flags the core raises from recorded fields. Example: \"The core flags a p99 claim with n < 299 (or p99.9 with n < 2,995) as unable to support a distribution-free 95% order-statistic bound. This is arithmetic on a recorded count, advisory, and not a universal sample floor.\"\n\n# Goals\nDeterministic advisory rules over W10.02's fields, surfaced in reads, `next` and `blindspots`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 2 proposed \"p99 needs n ≥ 299\" as a rule; revision 4 modified it, since it is \"neither a universal floor nor a fix for missing slow requests\" (review 3). So these are flags with exact wording, never gates and never statistics the core computes on raw data. \"No p-value, confidence interval, causal claim or numeric accuracy score is invented by the core.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\nRules, each with a stable ID and precise text:\n- Percentile claim without a sample count, or below the order-statistic bound.\n- Flat sample array: \"flagged as unable to support a variance analysis\".\n- \"A comparison built on A-then-B sequential runs is flagged.\"\n- \"A tail claim from a closed-loop generator without that is flagged for coordinated omission.\"\n- \"Percentiles from separate runs are never averaged; combining needs raw samples or mergeable histograms.\"\n- \"Two reported percentiles with different load distributions are not silently treated as comparable.\"\n- \"An all-attempt criterion cannot be satisfied from a completion-only percentile\" (the 95-of-100 variant).\n- Warm-up, changed instrumentation or changed request mix shown as possible confounders.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The sequential-order and closed-loop seeds of Scenario B are both flagged.\n- [ ] The missing-outcome variant cannot record `meets` for a \"99% within 25 ms\" criterion from the completion-only p99 without a blocker or flag naming the denominators.\n- [ ] No flag blocks recording; each states that it is advisory.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMechanical honesty about measurement, without the tool pretending to be a statistician.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.02.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.2 (traps and variants), §20.1 (Performance/measurement), Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.03 Advisory measurement flags: sample bounds, sequential order, coordinated omission, averaged percentiles"}	blake3:yVNfv3inClB6uJFQRzVkl2x1pd8lsL8ZexS2oEwgGzU
1795321911319115	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-4fpp	{"description":"# Context & Background\nGate: \"Claim an informative absence — Expected signal, adequate opportunity/sampling, valid instrument/control or justified equivalent.\" Scenario B's negative variant: \"'no improvement detected' with broken performance monitor or insufficient observation window cannot become evidence of equality.\"\n\n# Goals\nA structural gate for assessments that treat \"nothing was seen\" as evidence, plus support for prespecified equivalence margins.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAbsence of evidence is the commonest way a weak test becomes a strong claim. \"A legitimate zero-effect result needs adequate measurement conditions and precise detection/power limits; the system cannot fabricate a p-value.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- An Assessment or Comparison marked as resting on an absence must name: the expected signal, the opportunity or sampling that would have shown it, and a valid instrument/control assessment (W10.01) or a justified equivalent.\n- Missing any of these blocks promotion of the dependent conclusion; the absence observation itself is always recordable.\n- \"A failed or blocked external Run cannot be interpreted as negative evidence of the question without a separate valid observation.\"\n- Practical equivalence: a frozen inquiry may state \"population, unit, aggregation, replication unit, timeout/exclusion policy and a decision-relevant margin before results\". An externally supplied interval is compared with the band by arithmetic: inside supports bounded equivalence, straddling leaves it unresolved. \"The core invents neither interval.\"\n- The human question stays explicit: \"Are detection limits appropriate?\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Zero detected difference under broken instrumentation is inconclusive, not evidence of performance equality.\n- [ ] With a prespecified ±5 ms band, [−2, +3] supports bounded equivalence and [−8, +3] leaves it unresolved.\n- [ ] A margin stated after results is recorded as such and does not count as prespecified.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Honest negative results.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.01.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.4 (Claim an informative absence), §17.2 (Negative and Practical-equivalence variants), §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.04 Informative-absence gate and practical-equivalence margins"}	blake3:FHaWT2s-E1RmcdX9YjxVXCwfnLUC4geEF1-7xo1sGE0
1795321911397952	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1qr1	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Maintain a dependency index. Retraction, material correction, invalidated measurement, changed applicability or unresolved contradiction marks dependent Assessments, Comparisons, Conclusions and Decisions `needs_review` according to declared materiality and relevance.\" W03.12 built the effective-disposition overlay for the slice; this bone generalizes it.\n\n# Goals\nOne engine that computes effective freshness for every record type from the journal, for every trigger.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Never show a stale conclusion simply as 'validated,' or an affected accepted decision as unqualified 'current.'\" But over-invalidation is also a failure: reviews must go stale \"where relevant; unconnected case mutations do not needlessly invalidate them.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Dependency index in SQLite, rebuilt from the journal: subject → evidence, assumptions, criteria/option revisions, sources, reviews, synopses, coverage cells.\n- Triggers: source or artifact withdrawal; observation correction or retraction; invalid measurement assessment; criterion or option revision; assumption disposition change; objection raised; set-membership change (W04.05).\n- Use the substantive `basis_digest`, not the CAS revision: \"A lifecycle-only validation event records that reviewed basis and must not immediately invalidate its own review.\"\n- Propagate transitively with a reason chain, so a read can say *why* something needs review.\n- Outputs effective states such as `validated / needs_review`, `validated / disputed`, `candidate / current`; history is never rewritten.\n- Dependents include Reviews, Retrospectives, authored synopses (W05b.08), reports and the Overview.\n- Deterministic and incremental; a full recompute must agree with the incremental result.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Retraction of original source propagates effective freshness to observations, assessments, comparisons, conclusions, decisions, reports and case Overview.\n- [ ] Changing criterion threshold/weight/units forces affected Comparisons and Decisions into `needs_review` (unless demonstrably irrelevant per dependency map).\n- [ ] An unrelated mutation leaves existing reviews current.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPlan question: what has silently stopped being true?\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.5, §5.7, §14.3, §15.3, §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"l","title":"W10.05 Full freshness propagation: dependency index, materiality and stale-marking of every dependent"}	blake3:BbHhkeyjevgInZOxfRksuDkO0maFaVcGFYKInH1_Ce0
1795321911477208	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3meo	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**Order, prior exposure and evidence reuse are separate axes.**\" W03.05 recorded chronology strength, declared exposure and the holdout boundary on the plan. This bone covers the assessment side: \"link actual inputs, exclusions, stop rule and deviations to the frozen basis.\"\n\n# Goals\nAssessments of a frozen confirmatory plan record what was actually used and how it differs from what was frozen; reuse of already-seen data is detected through lineage.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 3 #4: a strong timestamp does not make a test prospective if the investigator had already seen the result. \"Re-extracting already-seen observations may support reproducibility, but it cannot count as a fresh prospective test. A previously collected, genuinely held-out set can qualify within its declared limits.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Assessment fields: actual input observation units or partitions, exclusions with reasons, the stop rule applied, and listed deviations from the frozen plan.\n- Compare actual inputs with the reserved holdout boundary and with previously read or assessed observations, following derivative lineage (W06b.04) so \"a renamed derivative counts as the same data\".\n- Classify the test as fresh, reuse of known outcome, or held-out within declared limits; show it beside chronology, never merged into it.\n- \"A fixed-sample analysis stopped early after a favorable look is recorded with that deviation.\"\n- A reuse classification does not reject the assessment; it limits how the result is labeled.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An executor-bound reanalysis of an already-inspected trace keeps its strong chronology *and* its known-outcome reuse.\n- [ ] A separately held-out acquisition with a frozen plan stays distinguishable.\n- [ ] An early stop is visible as a deviation on the assessment and in `explain`.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Exploration is valid; it becomes confirmation only through new, properly specified evidence.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.5 (Order, prior exposure and evidence reuse), §20.1 (Explanatory), Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.06 Evidence reuse and deviations at assessment, separate from chronology"}	blake3:taFs5WdgG1FnyNfz6f2ieuFdhyRTqNZBzdG0K8ABW4I
1795321911558064	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-lzs4	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**Confidence** is optional `low | moderate | high | not_assessed`, with a rationale, key limitations, and what would change it. It expresses the assessor's judgment about the evidentiary basis, never the probability of the claim.\"\n\n# Goals\nValidate and display confidence with its GRADE-style prompts.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 1 proposed a five-level certainty scale; revision 4 kept three levels plus named reasons because \"GRADE itself says it is not a quantitative system\". The named reasons make an agent say what it considered, without producing a score.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- \"A `moderate` or `high` value on a promoted record names the downgrade reasons considered...: validity/risk of bias, consistency across independent evidence, directness to scope, precision/sample adequacy, and search/reporting completeness. Each is `ok`, `concern` or `not_assessed`.\"\n- Enforced at promotion (review-ready, validation, decision acceptance), not at first recording.\n- `not_assessed` is always acceptable and is displayed as such.\n- Display never renders confidence as a percentage and keeps it apart from likelihood (W10.08), significance and severity.\n- No aggregate across records.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Promoting a conclusion with `high` confidence and no downgrade-reason entries is rejected with a repair.\n- [ ] A `concern` entry is visible in the `summary` view.\n- [ ] No output combines confidence values into a number.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBinding decision: no automatic scores; uncertainty stated in words with reasons.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.7, Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"s","title":"W10.07 Confidence with named downgrade reasons, never a probability"}	blake3:4yQFtQPo0lgYjT3rMs07SJtAE9_51HKz56AeLvHzg-s
1795321911638386	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-q5qu	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**Likelihood** is a separate optional field: a number in [0, 1] or one of the seven ICD 203 terms. It is allowed only on a claim with a stated resolution condition and is never shown in the same sentence as confidence.\"\n\n# Goals\nOptional likelihood, a resolution operation, and calibration reporting.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAdopted from reviews 1 #2 and 2 #5. Scoring forecasts against outcomes is the only honest use of a probability an agent states. \"A stated likelihood never satisfies or blocks a gate.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Accept likelihood only where the claim records a resolution condition; otherwise reject with a repair that suggests confidence instead.\n- ICD 203 terms are stored as terms; a documented mapping to numeric ranges is used only for scoring, with the mapping version recorded. Verify the term list against the published standard when implementing.\n- Resolution: an attributed operation records the outcome with evidence refs.\n- \"When the claim resolves, the core records the outcome and computes a Brier score (arithmetic on recorded data).\"\n- \"Calibration is reported per self-declared model family/version with the count of resolved claims.\" Small counts are shown with their n and no interpretation.\n- Display rule enforced in renderers: never in the same sentence as confidence.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A likelihood without a resolution condition is rejected.\n- [ ] Resolving claims produces the expected Brier values on a fixture.\n- [ ] No gate outcome changes when a likelihood is added, changed or removed.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps probability talk accountable instead of decorative.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.7, Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.08 Likelihood on resolvable claims, outcome recording and Brier calibration by model family"}	blake3:Y3qPTqLQxAUA2GEEJjkKLIoG4U9PhQ4C0rfUlK-11hc
1795321911721300	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-33nl	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW03.10 implemented `objection.raise/resolve/withdraw`. §14.3 states the policy: \"Critical unresolved objections block relevant **Conclusion validation** under applicable policy. For **Decisions**, material objections, constraint violations and uncertain comparisons must be visible, and the selected policy may demand explicit acknowledgment rather than prevent all choices under uncertainty.\"\n\n# Goals\nComplete the policy layer and its interaction with freshness.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nConclusions and decisions differ: a decision may be rational under an open objection, a validated claim may not ignore a critical one. \"A reviewer cannot erase original observations; a decision-maker cannot suppress a material objection by deleting the note.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `conclusion.validate`: blocked while a critical objection on the exact claim basis is open; the blocker lists it.\n- `decision.accept`: open material objections, hard-constraint violations and uncertain comparisons are listed in the receipt and stored with the decision; a pinned policy (W09.07) may require an explicit acknowledgment field.\n- `objection.resolve` \"cites the exact disputed record revision, evidence, resolution rationale and any required fresh review\" — when the resolution changes the subject's basis, earlier reviews are stale (W10.05).\n- An objection raised after validation makes the subject effectively `disputed`.\n- Withdrawal is attributed and visible; nothing flips a flag.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A credible counterhypothesis and material objection are visible before conclusion promotion.\n- [ ] A decision accepted over an open material objection shows it in every default read.\n- [ ] Resolving an objection never deletes it.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDisagreement stays on the record and affects what the tool will call validated.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.05.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §14.3, §6.4 (Validate conclusion), §9.4 (objection.*) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"s","title":"W10.09 Objection policy: critical objections block validation; decisions require visible acknowledgment"}	blake3:FV3KLiNi6w5XXVIN5jklW7CAQHj3wCsWZwKzOyT_7Y0
1795321911808163	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2u4m	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv blindspots --case C [--subject ID] --format json` is a **read-only deterministic audit** of recorded case structure. It does not call a model, alter evidence, guarantee completeness or invent a hypothesis.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe command, its envelope and the registry that the check bones (W10.11–W10.13) add rules to.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nSapper's separate blindspot role is adopted as a deterministic read view, not as a model call. The essential honesty rule: \"'0 detected structural gaps' must never display as 'investigation complete.'\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Response per §8.5: `schema_version: \"inv.audit.v1\"`, `snapshot`, `check_version` (for example `builtin-blindspot.v1`), `scope`, `checks[]` each with `check_id`, stable `gap_id`, `state`, `subject_refs` with revisions, `why`, `suggested_schema`, plus a `limits` statement.\n- Built on the gap-rule engine (W05a.10) and the frontier inventory (W05b.01); blindspot checks are a tagged subset with severity reasons and matching schema/guide commands.\n- `--subject ID` narrows to one conclusion, comparison or decision and its read-set.\n- The response lists which checks ran, so a clean result reads \"no structural gap identified under checks X\".\n- \"`gap_id` must be stable for a given snapshot/rule/subject set, with rule-version disclosure.\"\n- `next` and `frontier` surface the highest-impact gaps from the same rules.\n- Read-only; never writes a Review.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An audited case with no detected issue displays \"no structural gap identified under checks X\", **never** \"all blindspots resolved\" or \"verified correct\".\n- [ ] Identical snapshots give identical audits including `gap_id` values.\n- [ ] The response validates against the published `inv.audit.v1` schema.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nStructural gaps are found mechanically and reported with their limits.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b.01.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.5, §9.1 (blindspots), §18.2, §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology","cli"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.10 blindspots command and the inv.audit.v1 response envelope"}	blake3:6KdTFhnSOFRS_r38BNdQ_glWAaD_2Ac4uUUtSCQAzgY
1795321911887669	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2b95	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§8.5 lists the initial cross-domain checks. This bone implements those about explanation and measurement.\n\n# Goals\nDeterministic rules, from generic typed fields only.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nStrong inference [R1, R2]: \"A test supports H only to the extent it would probably have failed if H were false.\" Review 1 #5 added the plan check; review 2 #4 the per-rival expectation; review 1 the failure-only selection check (typed causal-role tags were deferred).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nRules:\n- \"A causal Conclusion lacks an acknowledged rival account or explicit reason alternatives were not useful.\"\n- \"Confirmatory empirical claims rely on invalid/missing control, baseline, instrument, sample/window or frozen-plan basis.\"\n- `inquiry.non_discriminating_plan`: \"A confirmatory InquiryPlan predicts the same outcome for two or more live rivals\" — raised at freeze, before any run.\n- \"A Conclusion's support was never assessed against a live rival, or every supporting item is `equally_expected` under a rival.\"\n- `causal.failure_only_selection`: \"rests only on failure-filtered observations with no non-failure baseline\", or \"only on observational evidence while displayed as confirmed\".\n- Comparability: a control recorded under a different build or environment than the treatment.\n- Known-outcome reuse presented as a prospective test (from W10.06).\nEach rule has an ID, version, severity reason, and a suggested schema or guide card.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A confirmatory plan with identical predicted outcomes for two live rivals is flagged before any run.\n- [ ] A causal Conclusion with only observational support displays as `candidate`, never `confirmed`.\n- [ ] Scenario A's adversarial variant (refuting test on a different build) yields the comparability gap.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nW10 acceptance: \"no missing-control or stale-reviewed result displayed as valid.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.10, W10.01, W10.06.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.1, §8.5 (initial checks), §20.1 (Explanatory), Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.11 Blindspot checks for causal and empirical claims, including non-discriminating plans"}	blake3:zl4OGjdwuB_J_KNaxe8NW92p4tX4jR2-AGza9-mS1K4
1795321911972595	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-107o	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe §8.5 checks that concern provenance and review quality.\n\n# Goals\nDeterministic rules over quote status, search coverage, origin links and review declarations.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThese catch the failures specific to document-based work and to agent self-review: fabricated or unchecked quotes, \"no errata exist\" with no search behind it, three citations of one origin, and a conclusion whose only challenge was the author rereading its own work.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nRules:\n- \"A promoted Conclusion or accepted Decision rests on a text-based Observation whose quote is absent, `ambiguous` or unchecked.\"\n- `source.absence_without_search_log`: \"An absence claim ... has no linked search Run recording corpus bounds, queries and empty results.\"\n- \"Every blindspot or validation review of a promoted subject is `same_session_self_review`.\"\n- \"Multiple citations masquerade as independent evidence despite common original provenance\" — using claim-level origins (W06b.05); an absent dependence link counts as `unknown`, not independent.\n- \"An important claimed fact has no retained/external source selector or producer attribution.\"\n- Reviews from one model family counted as one dependence group (W10.15).\n- A promoted record resting on a reference-only artifact that was never reverified.\nEach rule reports the exact records and revisions involved.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `blindspots` discovers a shared-origin source pair and an invalidated source/review from generic typed fields.\n- [ ] Scenario D's three citations produce the shared-origin finding, and the bench-test passage does not.\n- [ ] A conclusion reviewed only by same-session self-review is flagged as low-independence.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Corroboration never inferred from agent count\" or citation count.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.10, W06b.05, W06b.11, W10.15.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.5 (initial checks), §5.6, §14.2, §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.12 Blindspot checks for sources, quotes, absence claims, shared origins and review independence"}	blake3:RvgrOHnLww7JgjiL7uVQsgI2km7E9oggajDFUl5tdks
1795321912053011	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1k2r	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe remaining §8.5 checks: evaluative structure, things still affecting promoted records, and process.\n\n# Goals\nDeterministic rules for decision frames and case-level process gaps.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The tool can deterministically know that two options have not been compared against the same criterion; it cannot know which database is best.\" These rules are the first half of that sentence.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nRules:\n- \"An evaluative Decision has missing/unknown hard-criterion cells, inconsistent units, undisclosed subjective preferences, or no reopening trigger.\" The hard-cell rule uses check ID `criterion.hard_unmeasured`, as in the §8.5 example.\n- \"A material Assumption, critical Objection, stale source or retraction still affects a promoted record.\"\n- \"A case is repeatedly collecting evidence without any recorded discriminatory effect or decision consequence.\"\n- \"A material case ready for closure has no Retrospective.\"\n- A weight recorded without a range that a narrative relies on (W10.20).\n- A decision accepted while a membership change (new option or criterion) is unreviewed (W04.05).\n- Units: compare declared criterion units with comparison value units; mismatch is a gap, never an automatic conversion.\nEach suggests the schema to inspect (`comparison.add`, `assumption.assess`, `retrospective.record`).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `blindspots` discovers a missing control, an untested material assumption and an unknown hard criterion without domain-specific executors.\n- [ ] Scenario C before the recovery measurement yields `criterion.hard_unmeasured` with the right subject refs.\n- [ ] The stalled-collection rule cites the transactions it counted.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes the plan's evaluative gates visible before a decision is accepted, and afterwards.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.10.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.5 (initial checks), §15.1, §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.13 Blindspot checks for decisions, material assumptions, stalled collection and closure"}	blake3:axB6QtxaQKuOFl5DIJ84PRxv4c3IYbje1fBB_DKozyc
1795322021351048	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-n99o	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW03.09 implemented validation reviews. A blindspot review is the recorded form of a semantic challenge: \"what other mechanism, hidden constraint, falsifier or measurement failure might matter?\"\n\n# Goals\n`review.submit` with `kind: blindspot`, its verdicts, same-batch promotion of gaps, and freshness.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"**The checker cannot know all possible explanations**\". The deterministic audit (W10.10) finds structural gaps; a reviewer finds semantic ones. The record must say exactly what was looked at, because \"a review of one narrow claim is not whole-case coverage.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Payload per §9.4: `subject_ref`, `audited_snapshot`, `method`, `scope_examined`, `checks_considered`, `gaps[]` (possibly empty with a reason), `evidence_refs[]`, `assumption_refs[]`, `limitations`, plus `context_isolation` (W10.15).\n- Verdicts: `gaps_identified | no_gap_identified_within_scope | inconclusive`. \"A blindspot review never validates a finding by itself.\"\n- \"Gaps may be promoted into Questions/Assumptions/Objections through ordinary same-batch operations\", linked by `client_ref`.\n- The schema rejects any field asserting exhaustive coverage.\n- Freshness through the engine (W10.05): \"A changed material source makes dependent reviews stale; the reviewer must explicitly recheck.\"\n- The review \"never modifies a Conclusion or Decision automatically\".\n- Optional by default; a pinned pack may require one at a declared gate (W09.07).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `review.submit(kind=blindspot)` can record zero/one/many specific gaps, scope, inspected snapshot, uncertainty and exact related IDs.\n- [ ] A semantic blindspot outside the finite rules can be added as a Question/Assumption/Objection; no rule engine claims it should already have found it.\n- [ ] Withdrawing a source the review read marks it stale; an unrelated write does not.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nChallenge becomes a durable, scoped record instead of a feeling that someone checked.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.10.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §6.2 (Review), §6.4 (Blindspot review), §8.5, §9.4 (Blindspot and retrospective contract), §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.14 Blindspot review contract: review.submit kind=blindspot with scoped gaps, read-set and staleness"}	blake3:cULVU87WuhLtyMW4peqo3EWYoZwgVGCzM4NhR5S9tfk
1795322021431350	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-dba5	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"A Review declares `context_isolation`: `same_session_self_review | fresh_context_unblinded | fresh_context_blinded | external_evidence_backed`\", whether the reviewer saw the original conclusion, which artifacts and criteria were available, whether new evidence was collected, what changed, shared dependencies, and the kind of work (self-review, independent interpretation, rerun, independent-method validation).\n\n# Goals\nEnforce and display these declarations, group reviews by self-reported model family, and generate an optional blinded packet.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Same-session self-critique without new external information often fails to improve reasoning and can make it worse.\" And across models: \"a different family is **not** independent on that basis alone: models share errors across providers.\" The field is \"cheap and honest regardless\" of how strong the cited study is.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Make the declarations required on `review.submit` for both kinds, each with an explicit unknown.\n- Reads, briefs and reports flag `same_session_self_review` as low-independence.\n- Dependence groups: reviews whose actor descriptors (W01.03) share a model family form one group; display the group count beside the review count. The descriptor is \"a claim, like the actor string\".\n- Paired independent judgments: \"each is recorded before either reviewer can read the other's; journal order shows whether that held\" — compute and display that from sequence order and recorded reads.\n- Blinded packet: an export-like read that \"can omit the originating conclusion and confidence while retaining the question, evidence, and evaluation criteria. The packet records what was omitted.\"\n- \"Actor-ID inequality is merely a procedural check.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Three reviews from one model family display as one dependence group.\n- [ ] A blinded packet contains no conclusion text or confidence and lists its omissions.\n- [ ] A second judgment recorded after reading the first is not labeled independent.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Do not promise cognitive independence simply because a second model was used.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §14.2, §5.2 (Review), §8.5, Disposition: Reviews, retrospectives and learning (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.15 Review independence: context_isolation, declarations, model-family dependence groups, blinded packet"}	blake3:MJ8UiXhhIAzhatGG9kXXkxufBqNkq8L8Ltdg58COxzE
1795322021512659	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3jj0	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv retrospective --case C --draft` computes from the journal alone the facts a retrospective should rest on: invalid, failed or inconclusive runs; predictions amended after freezing; hypotheses rejected and options ruled out; reviews that went stale; conclusions superseded or retracted; revision conflicts; and the case's sequence span.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe digest, the expectation comparison, and validation that lessons cite it.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReviews 1 #3 and 2 #6: \"The closing agent may have no memory of the process, so it annotates these facts instead of recalling them.\" Without this, retrospectives become boilerplate. \"Writing expectations down beforehand is the reliable guard against hindsight bias; telling people to ignore the outcome does not work.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Deterministic digest from the journal with stable item IDs, bound to a snapshot. \"The digest covers recorded work only and says so.\"\n- Expected versus actual: compare `case.create` `expectations` and frozen predictions with outcomes; \"a missing expectation is reported as 'no expectation recorded'\".\n- Tighten `retrospective.record` (W03.13): \"Every `effective_steps`, `invalid_or_misleading_steps` and `lessons` entry cites a digest item or record ID; an uncited generic lesson fails validation.\"\n- `none_observed_reason` remains valid: \"'No reusable lesson identified' is valid with a bounded reason.\"\n- The digest is a read; it writes nothing.\n- Revision conflicts and receipts come from the journal and index, not from logs.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The digest for Scenario A lists the invalid clock-aligned trace and any amended prediction.\n- [ ] A lesson with no citation is rejected with a repair pointing at `--draft`.\n- [ ] `retrospective.record` accepts useful lessons from failed, inconclusive or successful inquiries with transfer limits; allows explicit \"no observed failures\" without fabricated claims.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nLessons \"grounded in case events rather than boilerplate\".\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.6 (Process digest; Expected versus actual), §9.4, Disposition: Reviews, retrospectives and learning (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.16 Retrospective process digest, expected-versus-actual, and lesson citation validation"}	blake3:5qXY4kcyOb9KhvX7f3RXZV_RUKFmKPPLIZ0iyHHFHLE
1795322021593641	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3m5a	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv retrospective --case C --format json` reads the current methodological record, showing evidence links and whether proposed lessons were reviewed.\" And: \"A lesson is visible outside its source case only after an explicit admission step by a human or a recorded outcome check.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe read command, the admission step, and tracking of whether admitted lessons were consulted and useful.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nLessons are agent-written free text, possibly written after reading hostile evidence; unreviewed cross-case visibility would be an injection path and a way for folklore to accumulate. \"Lessons remain **case-scoped observations about process**, not accepted general rules.\" \"Do not use retrospective counts as a quality signal.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `retrospective`: current and superseded retrospectives, bounded highlights, exact references, lesson status.\n- Admission: an attributed operation marks one lesson admitted, with who admitted it and on what basis; unadmitted lessons never appear in another case's reads.\n- Consultation: \"When a later case reads an admitted lesson, record that it was consulted and, at that case's close, whether it changed anything.\"\n- \"Lessons that are never consulted, or never useful, are shown as such.\"\n- \"Lessons carry their source case's classification and trust level.\"\n- Each lesson shows applicability, counterexamples/limits and supporting cases.\n- \"The default never automatically rewrites method packs, agent instructions, prompts or integration config.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An unadmitted lesson is invisible from every other case, including through `search --all-cases`.\n- [ ] Reading an admitted lesson records the consultation; the reading case's close asks whether it mattered.\n- [ ] Lessons do not silently modify method packs, prompts, agent settings, or canonical policy.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nLearning that transfers only by explicit, reviewable steps.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.16.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.6 (Lesson follow-through), §9.1 (retrospective), §16.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology","cli"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.17 retrospective read command, lesson admission and consultation tracking"}	blake3:Dtkzx8eOdIuCt8QxvIXds8R0r_qKe8frkvt2aYzU5dk
1795322021675089	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1mya	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Authored text records the lowest trust level among the artifacts its transaction declared as read, and among `evidence read` calls made through `inv` by that actor in the session, and shows it. `inv` cannot see what else a session consumed; say so.\"\n\n# Goals\nCompute, store and display an inherited trust level on every authored free-text record.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 2 #7: an injected instruction \"can be laundered through a summary into the layer every later agent reads first\". Showing that a synopsis was written by a session that read untrusted material lets the next reader weigh it, without claiming to detect injection.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- A small ordered trust scale for artifacts (for example trusted-local, internal, untrusted-external), set at ingest from acquisition context and adjustable by attributed assessment.\n- At commit: take the minimum over artifacts in the transaction's declared read-set and over `evidence read` calls recorded for that actor and session (W06a.06).\n- Store the level and its inputs on the record; show it at every depth and in vault notes.\n- Every display includes the stated limit: only reads made through `inv` or declared are known.\n- Session identity is a self-reported claim; absence of session data yields `unknown`, never trusted.\n- Lessons and synopses carry the level across cases (W10.17).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A synopsis written after an `evidence read` of an untrusted artifact shows that level.\n- [ ] A record with no declared reads shows `unknown`.\n- [ ] The limit statement appears wherever a trust level is shown.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA visible, honest signal for the two-stage injection path the release trial tests.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b.13.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §16.1 (Authored text is data too), Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology","security"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.18 Trust-level inheritance for authored text from declared reads and evidence reads"}	blake3:h43AVHl2Tzzk95oIgjtV_A7p7rfzbop1FVMmVyutYhE
1795322021756055	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-77n1	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"The worst injection against an epistemic ledger is a schema-valid write that validates a false claim, retracts counter-evidence or downgrades classification. Besides `--read-only`, a pack or workspace policy may require a distinct actor/context or explicit human confirmation for `conclusion.validate`, `decision.accept`, `source.withdraw`, `observation.retract` and classification downgrades. This is opt-in so solo work stays cheap.\"\n\n# Goals\nEnforce those requirements when a workspace or pinned pack policy asks for them.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 1 #10: separate promotion and suppression from the context that read hostile evidence. It must be optional, because a mandatory second actor would make the tool unusable for one agent on a small question.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Policy sources: workspace configuration and pack policy inputs (W09.07).\n- Requirement kinds: actor differs from the subject's author; context differs (declared `context_isolation` or session); an explicit human-confirmation token supplied in the envelope.\n- Human confirmation is a recorded claim: an attributed field stating who confirmed and how; `inv` does not authenticate people and says so.\n- Violations return a gate failure (exit 5) with the unmet requirement and the neutral route to satisfy it.\n- Applies to the five named operations; a test asserts no other operation can be guarded, so recording stays cheap.\n- With no policy, behaviour is unchanged.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] With the policy enabled, the session that read an untrusted artifact cannot validate the conclusion it supports.\n- [ ] With no policy, solo validation works as before.\n- [ ] A classification downgrade without the required confirmation is rejected and nothing is written.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nLimits the damage of a successful injection to things that cannot promote, suppress or declassify.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W09.07.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §16.1 (Write-side guardrails), §9.1 (Read-only mode), Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology","security"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.19 Opt-in write-side guardrails for validation, acceptance, withdrawal, retraction and downgrades"}	blake3:anDOKAu90QA968H5CWL1_Uv_5qhMPk4ehxCwzhIAjXY
1795322021836900	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-22ls	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**A weight needs a range.** A weight is accepted only with the worst and best values it trades off among the options actually considered ('the swing from 40 ms to 15 ms p99 is worth w'). A weight with an unknown range is stored as a stated preference and never enters a score.\"\n\n# Goals\nOptional weighted analysis as a user-supplied artifact with the safeguards of §15.1.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 2 #13. Weights without ranges are meaningless, and normalizing to the current option set lets an irrelevant alternative reorder the rest. \"**Do not rank everything with uncalibrated numerical weights.** A weighted score is optional user-provided analytic artifact, not the core's truth criterion.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Weight record on a criterion: value, the swing range it refers to, rationale, attributed actor; without a range it is stored as `stated_preference`.\n- A score, when requested, uses \"the criterion's declared scale anchors, not normalization to the current option set\".\n- Preserve \"all input weights, units, normalization assumptions, sensitivity and why its score should matter\" with the score artifact.\n- \"A hard constraint violation cannot be quietly compensated by a weighted soft criterion\": hard-disqualified options are excluded from any score.\n- \"Before any weighting, show the consequences table (raw values with evidence links) and flag criteria that overlap or proxy for another\" (overlap is declared by the investigator; the tool shows the declaration).\n- Changing a weight triggers freshness for dependent decisions (W10.05).\n- The core never proposes weights.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A weight without a stated range never enters a score; adding a duplicate option does not reorder the others.\n- [ ] A `hard` Criterion with an unknown result cannot be treated as satisfied, nor traded against arbitrary soft-score weights.\n- [ ] A source-authored subjective preference stays labeled preference rather than measured fact.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nLets people use numbers when they mean something, and stops the tool from manufacturing a ranking.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §15.1 (A weight needs a range), §20.1 (Evaluative), Disposition: Methodology (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.20 Weights only with stated ranges; scale-anchored scores; consequences table first"}	blake3:eNrtk15fC8YGKYpxoKTkOaHqxgVolYwRrXbhjXiY2l4
1795322021921275	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-32iy	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Use coverage only when a bounded set of opportunities or comparison cells is explicitly defined. The plan is sparse, versioned, with exact applicability/exclusions and a denominator.\" The names were reserved in W01.15; the type waited until after the A-gate.\n\n# Goals\nThe CoveragePlan/Cell type and its three operations.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A run count or number of citations is **activity**, not quality or coverage.\" A percentage is only honest with a declared denominator and plan revision. The type is optional because most cases have no finite space to cover.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `coverage.define`: bounded matrix with per-cell predicate, assessment polarity, applicability and exclusions.\n- `coverage.revise`: new plan revision; denominator history is kept.\n- `coverage.link`: attach runs, observations or comparisons to cells.\n- Cell states: `untested`, `blocked`, `attempted_inconclusive`, `observed_negative_within_scope`, `observed_positive`, `not_applicable_with_reason`, `needs_retest`.\n- Evaluation plans show the labels `assessed`, `unknown`, `excluded`, `stale`; \"no vulnerability-flavored positive/negative count\".\n- \"Any percentage includes the exact plan revision and denominator.\"\n- Freshness: \"If a measurement becomes invalid or criterion changes, dependent cells go stale/needs_retest and no longer count as confidently assessed.\"\n- Sparse storage; reject plans whose Cartesian size exceeds a stated bound, with advice to narrow.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A percentage is never displayed without its plan revision and denominator.\n- [ ] Invalidating a linked measurement moves its cell to `needs_retest`.\n- [ ] A case with no coverage plan shows no coverage section anywhere.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCoverage claims that state exactly what was and was not looked at.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §5.2 (CoveragePlan / Cell), §9.4 (coverage.*), §15.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.21 Optional coverage plans: coverage.define, coverage.revise, coverage.link with honest denominators"}	blake3:Kktra1cnraGA0YhnJlg783-4r7FqLS1dSExxvUn5FB0
1795322022003367	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-11ym	{"description":"# Context & Background\nScenario B: \"after a runtime upgrade, did a change in memory allocation cause a latency regression, and does optimization A improve p99 meaningfully?\"\n\n# Goals\nDeterministic seed data, generic producer manifests and expected properties for the scenario and all its variants.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThis is the measurement-validity scenario. Each seed exists to make one specific mistake available, so the fixture must be exact about which mistake each variant offers.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Base: \"a control and treatment benchmark with raw sample distributions, environment/build IDs, warmup periods, run order, sample sizes, and one intentionally confounded measurement with extra debug logging\", with metadata that makes warm-up and workload-mix differences noticeable.\n- All runs arrive as `producer-run.v1` manifests from a generic fixture producer (W07.01); no named tool.\n- Negative variant: no improvement detected under a broken monitor or too short a window.\n- Ordering trap: one comparison run A-then-B sequentially.\n- Coordinated-omission trap: a closed-loop capture measuring from actual send time.\n- Missing-outcome variant: 100 started, 95 finish in 10 ms, 5 unfinished at a 1 s timeout.\n- Practical-equivalence variant: prespecified ±5 ms band with supplied intervals [−2, +3] and [−8, +3].\n- Expected properties per variant, test-evaluable, stored outside agent-visible paths where used for evaluation.\n- Synthetic numbers generated from a fixed seed.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every variant is committed with a role description and regenerates byte-identically.\n- [ ] No seed names a real product.\n- [ ] Expected properties cover each trap listed in §17.2.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe second of four domains proving the core is general.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W07.01.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17 (preamble), §17.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology","fixture"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.22 Scenario B fixture: p99 regression with confounded, sequential, closed-loop and missing-outcome seeds"}	blake3:fssFFrOmYb_PlnGvqvXWrOJHwqZtG1uOHYOhMj1Kcwo
1795322022085160	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-smz9	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW10's acceptance: \"Gap audit and adversarial fixtures; no missing-control or stale-reviewed result displayed as valid.\"\n\n# Goals\nA scripted walkthrough of Scenario B through the binary.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nProves that measurement honesty comes from generic typed fields and deterministic rules, with no performance-specific code in the core.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nScript: ingest the manifests; \"assess instrument validity and comparability, distinguish process exit from meaningful measurement, identify a variance/correlation caveat, freeze a confirmatory repeat plan, decide whether effect is repeatable and bound the conclusion to the workload\"; run `blindspots`; record a blindspot review; record a retrospective from the digest.\nAssert:\n- The confounded measurement is `invalid` and cannot support promotion.\n- Positive and negative trials are both preserved.\n- Sequential-order and coordinated-omission flags are raised.\n- The negative variant ends inconclusive, not as equality.\n- The missing-outcome variant cannot satisfy the all-attempt criterion from the completion-only p99.\n- The equivalence variant resolves for [−2, +3] and stays unresolved for [−8, +3].\n- \"The blindspot review flags missing comparability/monitoring, and the Retrospective records which benchmark conditions wasted effort.\"\n- No number appears that was not supplied or is not simple arithmetic on recorded counts.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Passes offline from an empty directory with no performance-specific flag or mode.\n- [ ] Every relevant §20.1 Performance/measurement item is asserted.\n- [ ] No p-value, confidence interval, causal claim or numeric accuracy score is invented by the core.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMilestone B: validity is \"correct\", demonstrated on an empirical case.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.22, W10.03, W10.04, W10.06, W10.11, W10.14, W10.16.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.2, §19.2 (W10), §20.1 (Performance/measurement) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology","test"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.23 Scenario B end-to-end test: validity, comparability, denominators and bounded conclusions"}	blake3:GHDghpZPthCP1O66a-Ge93GgfTSK6TE-wUCqDbhTHLY
1795322022171260	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-28lh	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§17.7: \"Ship fixtures that exercise **the same new features** in every case mode, not just a security-oriented example.\" Drill 1 is the title-only cold start; drill 2 is the stale synopsis.\n\n# Goals\nTwo fixtures with deterministic expected read sets and summaries, in at least an explanatory and an evaluative variant each.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nLayered reads are only a benefit if skimming is both cheaper and safe. These drills measure the saving and prove the caveat survives.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Drill 1: \"agent sees 40 mixed records; `show --depth title`, `brief --level compact` and `frontier` identify the right question without reading raw captures. Every visible conclusion carries a caveat/status indicator.\" Record emitted bytes for the compact path and for a full-record traversal; \"measure emitted bytes and task correctness, not just compression ratio\".\n- Drill 2: \"a once-good summary says a criterion passed; a source is withdrawn or threshold changed. Summary remains in history but is marked stale and can no longer masquerade as the current answer. `full` and `evidence` show the change.\"\n- Expected outputs are pinned: read sets, summaries, rule IDs.\n- Scripted assertions here; cold-agent use of the same fixtures is W14.\n- No fixture depends on any installed agent runtime.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A 40-record fixture produces a significantly smaller useful compact brief than reading all full records, with byte counts recorded.\n- [ ] Every compact current Conclusion or Decision exposes material caveats and effective freshness independently of optional authored synopsis text.\n- [ ] After the withdrawal, the default brief never represents the old synopsis as current.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Agents can navigate title → synopsis → full record → evidence on demand\" without being misled.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b.01, W05b.05, W05b.08, W10.05.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.7 (drills 1–2), §20.1 (Layered retrieval) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology","test","fixture"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.24 Adversarial drills 1–2: title-only cold start on 40 records, and the stale synopsis"}	blake3:8k24ynghZAHyPQDI3FygBtoLkvJTEIFgsgkNxE7vGO4
1795322022252622	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-vct9	{"description":"# Context & Background\nDrill 3: \"an apparent root cause ignores a corrupted clock; a queue choice ignores an unmeasured recovery guarantee. `inv blindspots` identifies the structural omissions. An agent records a challenge Review and appropriate Question/Assumption, not an invented replacement conclusion.\" Drill 4: \"a fresh agent proposes an additional rival explanation the deterministic audit did not identify. The review adds it; the checker does not claim failure or completeness from the absence of a preexisting rule.\"\n\n# Goals\nFixtures and scripted tests for both drills, in explanatory and evaluative forms.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nTogether they pin the two sides of the blindspot contract: the audit finds what structure can show, and it stays silent — honestly — about what only a mind can propose.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Drill 3 fixtures: Scenario A variant with the misaligned clock assumption unassessed; Scenario C variant with the hard recovery criterion unmeasured. Expected audit rule IDs are pinned.\n- The scripted response records `review.submit(kind=blindspot)` with gaps plus a Question and an Assumption in one batch; assert no Conclusion or Decision is changed by the review.\n- Drill 4: a clean audit; a scripted reviewer adds a new rival via the review. Assert the audit output before and after never says \"complete\", and that the later audit does not report the earlier absence as an error.\n- Assert the subject's effective state reflects the new open challenge.\n- Fixtures are reused by W13.05 (MCP parity) and W14.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The pinned rule IDs are returned for both drill 3 variants.\n- [ ] The review never modifies a Conclusion or Decision automatically.\n- [ ] After drill 4 the new rival is visible to `next` and `frontier`.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nChallenge improves the record without the tool inventing answers.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.11, W10.13, W10.14.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.7 (drills 3–4), §8.5, §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology","test","fixture"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.25 Adversarial drills 3–4: a blindspot that changes the answer, and a semantic gap outside rule coverage"}	blake3:Bw9Ab0ThX7iQFDlMLJ_CDvEFbDaQzOEaHXqqUUfHI8E
1795322022336314	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3qm4	{"description":"# Context & Background\nDrill 5: \"an invalid benchmark produces a useful retrospective. A subsequent architecture case may *read a reviewed lesson proposal* but never has its method pack silently rewritten. Lessons with restricted original evidence must not leak into a public report.\" Drill 6: \"`retrospective.record` plus `case.close` in one transaction survives kill/retry; an attempted close without retrospective is rejected for substantive modes with a valid repair, while `record` mode remains exempt.\"\n\n# Goals\nFixtures and scripted tests for both drills.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nDrill 5 guards the learning loop against becoming self-amending doctrine. Drill 6 guards the one rule that makes closure cost a single call.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Drill 5: a measurement case closes with a retrospective whose lesson is admitted (W10.17); a later evaluative case reads it; assert consultation is recorded, pack files and pins are byte-identical before and after, and an unadmitted lesson stays invisible. The public-report leak assertion is completed in W11.16.\n- Drill 6: use the storage fake (W02.01) to kill at each commit step of the two-operation batch and retry with the same command ID; assert either both or neither exist, with the same IDs.\n- Close without a retrospective on a material case: rejected with a repair that works when followed; a non-material case closes with `retrospective_waived`; a `record`-mode case is exempt.\n- Stale retrospective: important new evidence after the retrospective requires an updated one.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Retrospective + close in one idempotent transaction works and preserves CAS semantics.\n- [ ] Closing a material case without a Retrospective fails with an actionable repair; recording-only cases remain exempt.\n- [ ] No lesson changes a method pack, prompt or setting.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Phantom lesson promotion\" stays at zero by construction.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.17.\nParent goal: W10. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.7 (drills 5–6), §8.6, §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology","test","fixture"],"parent":"bn-3kh","size":"m","title":"W10.26 Adversarial drills 5–6: lessons with limited transfer, and the atomic closure transaction"}	blake3:X2KVnTv3KchrgV8IwAMqbE5o7_2ZDAQHfZUerTwgC5I
1795322022419758	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1rg4	{"description":"# Context & Background\nMilestone B: \"External producer fixtures (not a named app) record runs; provenance, validity, independence, criteria changes, synopsis freshness, and structural blindspot review are correct. No false security-/performance-specific defaults leak into the core.\" Each milestone \"ends in a runnable proof, not a skeleton of directories\".\n\n# Goals\nOne recorded demonstration on a single binary, kept as evidence for the release audit (REL.01).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nEach package proved its own slice. This bone proves they hold together: the four scenarios, the producers and the vault on one build, with nothing domain-specific switched on.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- From a clean checkout, run the scenario scripts for A, B, C and D with the W10 steps added to A, C and D: `blindspots`, a blindspot review, digest-based retrospective.\n- Run the conformance suite against the Rust and Python example producers (W07.11) and the four-producer-kinds test (W07.17).\n- Render the vault for all four cases; run the vault fixture suite (W08.14).\n- Run drills 1–6 (W10.24–W10.26).\n- Neutrality: run the guards (W00.10); build and test with zero producers, packs other than `general`, and no Obsidian.\n- Go through §20.1 and §20.3 item by item and record evidence (test name or command) for each, marking any not yet met.\n- Re-check the invariants in \"Build rules\".\n- Write a proof note under `docs/` with versions, outcomes and known limitations.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All four scenario scripts, the conformance suite and the six drills pass on one binary.\n- [ ] Every §20.1 and §20.3 item has recorded evidence or an explicit open defect.\n- [ ] Default help, schemas and a fresh workspace contain no named external tool or security concept.\n- [ ] The proof note is committed.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEstablishes that the core is general and honest before the product layer is built on it.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b, W06b, W07, W08, W09, W10.\nParent goal: MB. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.1, §19.3 (Milestone B), §20.1, §20.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w10","milestone-b","lane-methodology","lane-evaluation","test"],"parent":"bn-1fa","size":"m","title":"MB.01 Milestone B runnable proof: generic recording and honest knowledge across four domains"}	blake3:jyHuZnLPGFi4K3jtYdnA9bzxewat6TdIA2eOCE8L3_I
1795322147386663	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3d4z	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`export --kind KIND` / `archive verify/restore`: Produce immutable snapshot report, review vault or archive.\" §15.4 defines eight kinds and says: \"All reports render deterministically from exact committed snapshots, no model call required.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe shared export pipeline every kind plugs into.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nExports are where a case leaves the tool's control. One pipeline — snapshot, policy, plan, fresh destination, manifest — makes determinism and leak prevention properties of the framework, not of each report.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `export --kind KIND --case ID [--snapshot TX] --audience A --out DIR`: resolve one verified snapshot; build a plan with the same pure-planner pattern as the vault (W08.01); write only into a new, empty destination under `exports/` or a given path.\n- Manifest per export: \"snapshot ID, renderer/schema versions, source digests, omissions, classification policy and where applicable supersession links\", plus completeness, audience and policy version.\n- \"A synopsis, blindspot list or lesson cannot be substituted for primary evidence. Reports link to exact reviewed states and annotate changed/withdrawn sources and stale review bases.\"\n- \"Negative and inconclusive results remain visible. Mark deprecated/superseded decisions instead of rewriting the past.\"\n- Effective states come from the freshness engine (W10.05).\n- \"The tool exports locally. Publishing, emailing, cloud upload, or Git push requires a separately requested integration action.\"\n- Catalog entry with effect metadata so `--read-only` rejects it. Optional inert self-contained HTML may follow Markdown; PDF is deferred.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The same snapshot, kind and policy produce byte-identical output and manifest.\n- [ ] Export refuses a non-empty destination.\n- [ ] No export path makes a network call or model call.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDeterministic, inspectable hand-offs of what the case knows.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W08.01, W10.05.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.1 (export), §15.4, §16.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection","cli"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"m","title":"W11.01 Export framework: export --kind, deterministic rendering from a snapshot, export manifests"}	blake3:ov4s_HmpgRDTK0NDLuCYUEGilkWyqD64nr6-4ffIyF4
1795322147481447	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3d96	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Export uses a positive inclusion manifest and audience policy. Check every channel: body text, labels, aliases, filenames, source URLs, Canvas cards, generated summaries, human annotations, errors/logs, attachment metadata, and attachments.\"\n\n# Goals\nA policy engine used by every export kind, stricter than the working-vault policy (W08.13).\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 3 #9: \"**Filtering must not strengthen a recommendation.** Compute effective state and material support/objections against the full canonical snapshot *before* audience filtering.\" Removing a restricted objection must never leave the conclusion looking unopposed.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- \"Audience policy is positive inclusion, not a best-effort scrub\": nothing is exported unless a rule includes it.\n- Two-phase: compute effective state, caveats and objections on the full snapshot; then decide per statement whether it can be shown with \"an approved representation of its operative caveats\". \"If even a general caveat would mislead or disclose protected facts, omit the dependent statement or refuse that report.\"\n- \"Apply the same decision to previews, summaries, search, counts and renderers.\"\n- \"Source names, hashes, IDs and even the existence of an omission need audience permission; detailed diagnostics go to a private publisher receipt.\"\n- Two manifests: an audience-facing one that leaks nothing hidden, and a private receipt listing exact exclusions.\n- \"If requested material cannot be included under the chosen policy, fail with exact exclusions or generate an explicitly incomplete review bundle—never label it complete.\"\n- Re-exporting an old snapshot uses today's policy and availability.\n- \"Regex-only redaction never proves a derivative safe\": only reviewed derivatives are included.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A public decision whose decisive objection is restricted is withheld or carries an approved caveat in every public channel.\n- [ ] Names, counts, hashes, cursors and the omission manifest leak nothing the policy hides.\n- [ ] Re-exporting an old snapshot follows today's policy.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nSharing never makes a claim look stronger or reveals what was meant to stay private.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.01, W06b.07, W08.13.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §15.4, §16.2, §16.3, §20.4, Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection","security"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"l","title":"W11.02 Audience policy engine: positive inclusion, caveats computed before filtering, every channel checked"}	blake3:fag26Lpvd1GaVYDrIGRcceBW03zAVIfjWdwk67iI1Z8
1795322147572571	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-28he	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Generate handoffs from committed state at an exact snapshot. Include objective, scope, current/effective conclusions, decisive evidence, strongest contradiction, open assumptions, available recording/reader capabilities, incomplete/unknown runs, missing comparisons, **frontier highlights, audited structural gaps and most recent challenge-review freshness**, current Retrospective/lessons when present, next action, and stop conditions.\"\n\n# Goals\n`export --kind handoff`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A fresh agent must not need the originating chat.\" The handoff is `brief` made portable, so the receiving agent or person can start without access to the workspace — and with a clear path back into it.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Two files: \"both a compact agent brief and a human case overview\", generated from one snapshot.\n- Content as listed above; \"Provide the `title`/`summary`/`full`/`evidence` escalation path for every referenced claim rather than dumping a full graph.\"\n- \"The handoff should say what to read first and what not to repeat until reconciled\" — list unknown or partial runs explicitly.\n- Frontier highlights from W05b.01, structural gaps from W10.10, challenge-review freshness from W10.14, retrospective highlights from W10.16.\n- Audience policy applies (W11.02).\n- \"`inv export --kind handoff` is local output, not a message to another person or service... nothing in the core auto-notifies contacts.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The handoff for each scenario contains every listed element or states its absence.\n- [ ] Every referenced claim has its escalation commands.\n- [ ] An unknown-outcome run appears under \"do not repeat until reconciled\".\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPlan question 6: resumption without the original chat.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.02, W05b.01, W10.10, W10.14, W10.16.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §14.4, §15.4 (handoff) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"m","title":"W11.03 handoff export: compact agent brief plus human overview at an exact snapshot"}	blake3:KMISQhKPCEZwiYlLYn7tlPNiofBs602farpQFaCaXoo
1795322148171183	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3c9f	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`case-report` (general inquiry) must contain \"Objective, methods, original observations, alternatives, supported conclusions, limits, open issues, methodological retrospective reference/highlights when substantive.\" `analysis-report` (causal/empirical cases) must contain \"Repro conditions, baselines, runs/controls, uncertainty, root-cause/measurement assessment.\"\n\n# Goals\nTwo deterministic report kinds.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA report is the form in which most people will meet a case. It must carry the same bounded claims and caveats as the record, with paths back to evidence, and must not look more settled than the case is.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Fixed section order per kind; each claim shows its effective disposition, scope, strongest contradiction and review basis.\n- Evidence is cited by source and selector, with quote status; excerpts are bounded and rendered inert.\n- `analysis-report`: runs with execution status, completeness and validity shown separately; measurement fields and advisory flags (W10.03); `evidence_kind`; k-of-n reproduction where recorded.\n- Conclusion content per §15.3: \"significance, applicability, evidence selectors, alternatives addressed, uncertainty, dissent/review, and what would invalidate it. No required severity score.\"\n- Retrospective highlights: \"a bounded highlights section with exact references\".\n- Confidence and likelihood never in the same sentence.\n- Stale or withdrawn inputs are annotated in place.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Scenario A and B reports contain every required element.\n- [ ] An inconclusive case produces a report that says so, with a reopen condition.\n- [ ] A stale-reviewed conclusion is never printed as simply validated.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Case/ADR reports include bounded methods lessons with links/limits.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.02, W10.03.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §15.3, §15.4 (case-report, analysis-report) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"m","title":"W11.04 case-report and analysis-report exports"}	blake3:10U6SrvNE1yrvu77Q8dWjr2KDsTOT2MDesb6Llvy8Kg
1795322148285933	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2o2l	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`decision-record` (evaluative cases, ADR-like) must contain \"Context, options, hard constraints, criteria, evidence, choice, tradeoffs, consequences, revisit trigger, and any unresolved blindspot/criterion warnings.\" It is the last command of the shipped tutorial path (§17.5).\n\n# Goals\n`export --kind decision-record`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nADRs are treated as \"a *recording format* for context, decision and consequences... No study shows ADRs improve decisions, so treat this as a format, not a method.\" The format is useful because teams already read it; the content must stay honest about what was unknown when the choice was made.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Sections in ADR order: context, options considered (including ruled-out ones with reasons), hard constraints and their dispositions, criteria, consequences table with evidence links, choice, tradeoffs, expected outcome and when to check, reopen trigger.\n- Embed the static comparison matrix (W08.07); unknown cells stay unknown.\n- Warnings block: open `blindspots` gaps for the decision (W10.13), stale comparisons, criteria changed since acceptance.\n- Status line uses the effective disposition; a superseded decision links forward and back.\n- Subjective preferences are labeled as preferences.\n- \"A decision may be rational, conditional and accepted while one comparison remains uncertain; report explicitly preserves that condition.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Scenario C exports a conditional decision record with its matrix and reopen conditions.\n- [ ] After the criterion-threshold change, the export shows the decision as needing review.\n- [ ] The §17.5 tutorial path ends with a non-empty decision record.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDecisions leave the tool with their conditions attached.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.02, W08.07, W10.13.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.1 (Architecture decision records), §15.1, §15.4 (decision-record), §17.5 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"m","title":"W11.05 decision-record export: ADR-style record with the comparison matrix and unresolved warnings"}	blake3:A9auq3twfphFot5YuIF330y9Uaqs0RRAHrkVny3gngg
1795322148378199	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3vo8	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`method-retrospective` must contain \"Case/snapshot, approaches attempted, effective/invalid routes, constraints, linked examples, limits, proposed reusable lessons and reviewer status; no automatic pack modification.\"\n\n# Goals\n`export --kind method-retrospective`.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nW11's package text highlights the \"**method Retrospective**\". A team may want the process lessons of a case without its evidence. That is exactly where restricted material could leak through a lesson's wording, so policy applies to lessons like any other text.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Render the current Retrospective with its digest citations (W10.16) and expected-versus-actual section.\n- Each lesson: applicability, counterexamples/limits, supporting cases, admission status, consultation history (W10.17), trust level and classification.\n- Lessons are presented as proposals; the export states that nothing was applied to any pack or instruction.\n- Lessons whose source evidence is restricted are withheld or generalized per policy (W11.02).\n- Superseded retrospectives are linked, not merged.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Restricted lessons cannot leak into public review exports.\n- [ ] Every lesson in the export shows its limits and status.\n- [ ] A case with \"no reusable lesson identified\" exports that statement and its reason.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nLessons travel with their evidence links and their limits.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.02, W10.17.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.6, §15.4 (method-retrospective), §20.1 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"s","title":"W11.06 method-retrospective export with reviewer status and transfer limits"}	blake3:V5nnawqk6qBofXvsWktu0vvsxPxNATd2Ku1x8Xlmbz0
1795322148468835	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3gx3	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`review-vault`: \"Share approved browsable casebook — Curated notes, views, explicitly allowed attachments and omitted-data manifest.\"\n\n# Goals\nA browsable vault for a stated audience, containing nothing the policy did not include.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Build review exports in a fresh destination from allowed inputs, never from the working vault, whose conflict copies may hold obsolete sensitive material.\" And: \"A redacted review vault must not quietly contain the unredacted canonical journal needed for resumption. Review export and full recovery archive are different products.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Run the vault planner (W08.01) with the export audience policy (W11.02) into an empty directory; never copy from `vault/`.\n- \"Default review bundles exclude raw canonical events, local absolute paths, credential sources, and raw captures.\"\n- Include only reviewed derivative attachments; include human notes and boards only when selected, with snapshot hashes and ownership role (W11.14).\n- Omitted-data manifest suitable for the audience; private receipt for the publisher.\n- Mark the bundle as not resumable.\n- Plain Markdown tables included so the vault reads without Obsidian.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Review export excludes raw journal/secrets and checks labels/filenames/Canvas/annotations as well as artifact bodies.\n- [ ] Stale generated files and conflict copies from the working vault never appear.\n- [ ] A mobile/offline reader can understand the exported case without shell or community plugins.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nSafe sharing of a casebook, distinct from recovery.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.02, W08.14.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §15.4 (review-vault), §16.3, §12.8, §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian","security"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"m","title":"W11.07 review-vault export built in a fresh destination from allowed inputs only"}	blake3:HCmfJ8L0NwRav_cHZ9Xh8fmShkTAE15lpRYOzT_IFj4
1795322148555998	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1ji3	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`workspace-archive`: \"Fully recover canonical state — Journal, retained blobs, schemas, owner config and selected human notes, with integrity manifest.\" \"For the first implementation, a **workspace archive** is the simplest resumable form because sequence/idempotency are workspace-wide.\"\n\n# Goals\nCreate, verify and restore a complete recovery archive.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"A synced vault alone is not a resumable investigation.\" Recovery is a different product from review export, with a different guarantee: exact replay.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Archive contents: journal events to a chosen recovery head, checkpoints, every retained blob referenced at that head, schemas, owner configuration, selected human notes as snapshots, and an integrity manifest (digests, head hash, counts, tool versions).\n- `archive verify`: check manifest, chain and blob digests without restoring.\n- `archive restore`: \"into an empty new destination and verify before publication\"; rebuild the index; run `check`.\n- \"Preserve original IDs for an exact restore; a deliberate fork gets a new workspace identity and explicit origin snapshot. Do not let two forked workspaces masquerade as one coordinated writer.\"\n- If ADR W00.05 chose per-case chains, also offer a single-case archive; otherwise \"a selected-case export can be a portable review bundle; do not call it resumable\".\n- Reference-only and intentionally removed artifacts are listed as unavailable, never fabricated.\n- Use an established container format; no new cryptosystem. Encryption, if offered, uses a reviewed external tool.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Restore exact replay: restored state, receipts and digests equal the source at the recovery head.\n- [ ] `archive verify` detects a flipped byte in any event or blob.\n- [ ] Restore refuses a non-empty destination.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe case survives the machine it was made on.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.01.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.7, §15.4 (workspace-archive), §9.1 (archive verify/restore) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection","cli"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"l","title":"W11.08 workspace-archive export with archive verify and archive restore into a new destination"}	blake3:7PI--vLGCOfvt3fxNWCiL-pg4RK3yVsvKb2DZ7UkeWo
1795322148643257	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-65pm	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Pin the chosen recovery head and its required blobs until the archive verifies and publishes or is explicitly abandoned. Serialize blob reclamation against pin creation and release; ordinary appends may continue.\"\n\n# Goals\nArchive creation that is safe against concurrent reclamation and honest about in-flight captures.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 3 #10. Without pins, a blob could be reclaimed between listing and copying, producing an archive that verifies its manifest but cannot restore. And staging holds evidence the journal does not yet reference.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- A durable pin record naming the head and digest set; created under coordination with reclamation (W11.11) and released on publish or explicit abandon.\n- A crashed archive leaves a discoverable pin that `doctor` reports and an explicit command releases.\n- Appends continue during archiving; the archive covers exactly the pinned head.\n- \"A complete recovery archive must account for active/unreconciled staging. Initially refuse/defer a complete archive until those runs are finalized or explicitly recovered as interrupted.\"\n- \"A review-only export may omit staging with a clear limitation.\"\n- \"Never silently drop recoverable staged evidence.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A reclamation attempted during an archive cannot remove a pinned blob.\n- [ ] Archive creation with an unsettled capture handle is refused with the handle listed.\n- [ ] An abandoned archive's pin can be found and released.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA recovery archive is either complete or says exactly why it is not.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.08, W07.09.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.7, §16.5, §20.2, Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"m","title":"W11.09 Archive pins and staging policy: pin the recovery head and blobs; refuse while captures are unsettled"}	blake3:olwYRZ2PNU_KhEhY3gUbx0V69wCqcfXoM1NjhtkNBDk
1795322148729334	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-27y6	{"description":"# Context & Background\n`producer-bundle`: \"Interoperability — Versioned structured records, mappings, original lineage, completeness.\"\n\n# Goals\nExport recorded Runs and their artifacts in a neutral structured form an external producer or analysis tool can read.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe recorder takes data in through one neutral schema; a symmetric way out lets independent consumers verify what was recorded and move it elsewhere, without learning the journal format.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Per Run: the finalized manifest (W07.06), `producer-run.v1`-shaped records, artifact digests and roles, completeness per channel, lineage edges, and the mapping from producer external IDs to canonical IDs.\n- Versioned schema; re-importing a bundle into another workspace follows the idempotent re-import rules (W06b.03) and creates lineage, not new trials.\n- Producer assertions remain labeled as assertions.\n- Audience policy applies to artifacts and metadata.\n- Opaque namespaced metadata is preserved byte for byte.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A bundle exported and re-imported yields equivalent canonical Runs and \"isn't a new trial\".\n- [ ] Missing artifacts are listed as missing.\n- [ ] The bundle validates against its published schema.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDesign principle 7, portability, for producers.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.01, W07.06.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §15.4 (producer-bundle), §11.2, §11.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection","recording"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"s","title":"W11.10 producer-bundle export for interoperability"}	blake3:KQ2T_AQ2NsnD-jJds7rqzEeeOSvBRMlOxAZhJqynpWM
1795322148815427	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-28pu	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Because blobs deduplicate across cases, **retention applies to the acquisition or Artifact reference first**, never directly to a digest. Commit the reference's loss of availability (`availability: intentionally_removed`, keeping digest/size/provenance) before scheduling physical reclamation; every byte read, including at a historical snapshot, follows current availability.\"\n\n# Goals\nRetention policy, a preview, and safe physical reclamation.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 3 #10. Deleting by digest would silently remove another case's evidence; reading old snapshots after removal would defeat the removal. The plan also allows an honest fallback: \"If v1 cannot qualify reclamation, ship withdrawal and truthful availability reporting first.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Case-level retention policy; an attributed operation expires a reference, committed before any unlink.\n- \"A second acquisition of identical bytes keeps its own policy, and deduplication grants no cross-case read permission.\"\n- Before unlinking a digest: \"recheck all live retention obligations, durable staging and archive pins under coordination with import and archive creation\".\n- Preview \"reports separately: the logical loss on each permitted reference, bytes still held for others, reclaimable bytes, and known downstream copies, including FTS, SQLite/WAL, old renders, previews and exports.\"\n- Purge derived copies it controls (index text, cached renders); list those it cannot.\n- \"Reclamation is retryable after interruption.\" \"Never silently GC a referenced artifact.\"\n- \"All dependent claims/exports must reflect lost inspectability.\"\n- Expired references stay auditable.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Import identical bytes into cases A and B, expire A during an archive of B, then read, restore and reindex: A's reference is unavailable even at an old head; B and the pinned archive verify.\n- [ ] Reclamation waits for the last obligation and survives interruption.\n- [ ] The preview's four quantities are reported separately.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nRemoval that is real where promised and truthful where it is not.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.09, W06b.06.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §16.5, §20.2, Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection","security"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"l","title":"W11.11 Retention per reference, availability loss committed first, retryable blob reclamation"}	blake3:_Lw_nO7OWDO_Qh6Tc5PLUv1byZp3mb-6yCnb-ZenrHc
1795322148915606	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-19ik	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"If a secret was committed in event text, adding a redaction event does not remove the old plaintext. A dedicated purge workflow must create a sanitized new storage epoch/archive... Until that workflow is implemented, diagnose the exposure and document manual/backup remediation; do not claim secure deletion or an effective purge.\"\n\n# Goals\nA diagnosis aid and clear documentation of what v1 can and cannot do about a committed secret.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAn append-only, hash-chained journal cannot forget. Saying so plainly, with a workable manual procedure, is better than a `redact` command that leaves plaintext in old events and backups.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- A read-only diagnosis: given an event or record, list where its text lives — journal event file, index and FTS rows, cached renders, vault notes, exports and archives known to the workspace.\n- Documentation: rotate the secret first; then the manual options (restore from a pre-exposure archive and replay; rebuild a sanitized workspace as a deliberate fork with a recorded mapping), and exactly \"which historical replay guarantees changed\".\n- State that \"distributed copies cannot be recalled automatically\".\n- The purge workflow itself (new storage epoch with redaction audit) goes to the deferred register with its entry condition.\n- \"OS disk encryption or an established external encrypted archive can protect storage. Do not invent a new cryptosystem.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The diagnosis lists every local location holding the event text in a fixture.\n- [ ] No command or doc uses the words \"securely deleted\" or \"purged\" for withdrawal or redaction.\n- [ ] The deferred register names the purge workflow.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTruthful limits on deletion.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §16.5 (second and third paragraphs), §10.6 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection","docs","security"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"s","title":"W11.12 Secret-exposure diagnosis and documented manual remediation; no false purge claim"}	blake3:y2M8zr5319RZtuROoFqxuMCLR_gZND7v2AwP1sZbsII
1795322149007707	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-12v0	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"UUIDv7 identifiers embed a millisecond creation time; the export manifest says so, and exports where record timing is sensitive can use remapped random IDs.\" (Review 2 #14.)\n\n# Goals\nDisclosure in every manifest, an ID-remapping option, and private snapshot mapping.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nIDs and sequence numbers are metadata that travels with every export. They can reveal when work happened and how much other activity a workspace has.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Every audience-facing manifest states that record IDs embed creation time, unless remapping was used.\n- `--remap-ids`: replace IDs with random ones consistently across the bundle (bodies, links, filenames, frontmatter); keep the mapping in the private publisher receipt only.\n- \"When a global head or sequence would expose other activity, the audience-facing manifest identifies the approved bundle, and the mapping to the canonical snapshot is kept privately.\"\n- Timestamps in exported records follow policy: exact, coarsened or omitted.\n- Remapped exports are review products; they cannot be restored as archives, and say so.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A remapped export contains no original ID or sequence number in any channel.\n- [ ] The same remapping is applied consistently, so all links resolve.\n- [ ] The disclosure sentence is present in non-remapped manifests.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMetadata is treated as content when deciding what leaves.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.01.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §15.4 (closing paragraph), Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection","security"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"s","title":"W11.13 Export identifier and timing disclosure: UUIDv7 timestamps, remapped IDs, private snapshot mapping"}	blake3:z7MqCEKDhv3SHaANwKF2OpVrAm4Eh5fMvrxzZbN6i9M
1795322149094205	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1tqh	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"When a report or assessment relies on a human note, snapshot that exact note revision/hash and record an Annotation or Source. A later uncommitted note edit cannot silently change a previous conclusion. Human boards included in an export also get snapshot hashes and a stated ownership/provenance role.\"\n\n# Goals\nA controlled way to bring a human-owned file into the record, and consistent treatment in exports and archives.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nHuman notes live outside the journal and can change at any time. If a conclusion cites one, the cited bytes must be fixed, or the conclusion's basis could move without any transaction.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- An ingest route for a path under `Notes/`, `Boards/` or `Inbox/`: retain the bytes as an Artifact with acquisition context \"human note snapshot\", then reference it from an Annotation (note snapshot role) or a Source.\n- The human file itself is never modified or moved.\n- Reads show when the live note differs from the snapshot a record relies on.\n- Exports include selected notes/boards as snapshots with hash and ownership role; archives \"snapshot included human notes as separate attributed inputs because they can change outside the journal\".\n- A snapshotted note is still authored text: quoted data, with a trust level.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Editing a note after snapshot does not change any record; the difference is reported.\n- [ ] An export lists each included human file with its hash and role.\n- [ ] Human files are byte-identical before and after snapshot and export.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nHuman thinking can become evidence only by an explicit, hashed step.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.01, W08.08.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.1, §10.7, §9.4 (annotation.add) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"s","title":"W11.14 Snapshot human notes and boards by hash when a record or export relies on them"}	blake3:4BaJH7If_1hpbsx6AvqYqS_yRy57f1mp2BM6UT46nKk
1795322149186925	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-10xc	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Migrations are versioned, reversible via backup, and tested against historical fixtures. Prefer a new projection/schema reader that retains original journal bytes; an incompatible journal conversion creates a new format with a mapping and preserves the original archive. Unknown future required versions stop writes. No silent destructive migration on ordinary `brief`.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe policy and tooling for evolving on-disk formats after v1.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nW02.13 gave upcasting readers. This bone adds what a release needs: a tested procedure, historical fixtures kept forever, and a guarantee that reads never migrate.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Historical fixture workspaces for every released format (starting with `v0-alpha` pilot data and v1) checked in CI: open, replay, `check`, render.\n- Derived state (index, vault, manifests) migrates by rebuild.\n- A journal-format change, if ever needed: an explicit `migrate` command that requires a verified archive first (W11.08), writes a new workspace with an ID mapping, and preserves the original archive.\n- Read commands never write migration results; they report \"migration available\".\n- A newer required version stops writes with a clear error and leaves reads working where safe.\n- Vault schema (`inv.vault.v1`) changes re-render; human areas stay byte-identical.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every historical fixture opens and replays under the current binary.\n- [ ] `brief` on an old-format workspace changes no file.\n- [ ] A simulated future required version blocks writes and explains why.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe compatibility promise of §3.6, kept by tests.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.08.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.7 (Migrations), §3.6 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"m","title":"W11.15 Migration framework: versioned, backup-reversible, tested against historical fixtures"}	blake3:5CQDU469xGaKX7g8g4WtvGx7kKtcgd_Zv4u16o3l7hA
1795322149280183	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2pm0	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW11's acceptance: reports \"do not leak restricted evidence\". §20.4 names the hard cases.\n\n# Goals\nAn adversarial test suite run against every export kind.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nLeaks hide in side channels: a filename, a count, an alias, a manifest entry, a stale file. Only systematic tests over every channel make \"positive inclusion\" a fact.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Fixture with canary strings planted in restricted records: titles, bodies, source names, URLs, annotations, lesson text, artifact bytes, namespaced metadata, human notes.\n- For each kind and each audience below `restricted`: export, then search every output byte, filename and manifest for canaries, original IDs where remapped, absolute paths and sequence numbers.\n- Count inference: adding a restricted record must not change any audience-visible count.\n- Caveat preservation: the decision with a restricted decisive objection is withheld or caveated in every kind.\n- Policy change: re-export an old snapshot after tightening policy.\n- Dirty working vault: plant conflict copies and stale notes; confirm the review vault does not contain them.\n- Completes the restricted-lesson assertion of drill 5 (W10.26).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Zero canaries in any export for an audience not permitted to see them.\n- [ ] Every §20.4 export item has a passing test.\n- [ ] The suite fails if a new export kind is added without being registered in it.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes safe sharing a regression-tested property.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.03, W11.04, W11.05, W11.06, W11.07, W11.13, W11.14, W10.26.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §16.3, §19.2 (W11), §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection","test","security"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"m","title":"W11.16 Export leak and caveat-preservation test suite across every channel"}	blake3:mx-a6QLV6ken_YJQzEYrcc4WiqmV9IbDYqQq8-666S0
1795322149370224	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1tk7	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.2: \"A full archive restored on a clean machine yields the same case state and retained evidence digests.\" W11's acceptance begins \"Restore exact replay\".\n\n# Goals\nAn end-to-end recovery test in a clean environment.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA backup that has never been restored elsewhere is a hope. Running the restore where nothing of the original workspace exists catches hidden dependencies on `local/`, `cache/` or absolute paths.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Build an archive from a workspace holding all four scenarios, a finalized capture, an expired reference and a reference-only artifact.\n- In a fresh container or temporary root with only the binary: `archive verify`, `archive restore`, `check`, `repair index`, `vault render`.\n- Compare with the source: accepted state, receipts (idempotent retry of an old command returns the original IDs), blob digests, effective dispositions, and rendered owned bytes.\n- Confirm the expired reference is unavailable and the reference-only artifact is still reported as external.\n- Run in CI on each supported platform from the support matrix (W00.09).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Restored state and retained evidence digests equal the source.\n- [ ] An old command ID retried against the restored workspace returns `already_committed`.\n- [ ] The test passes with no network and no files from the original machine.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n§20.7: \"Milestone C gates pass, including independent neutral producer integration and restore.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W11.08, W11.09, W11.11.\nParent goal: W11. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.2 (W11), §20.2, §20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w11","milestone-c","lane-projection","test"],"parent":"bn-65s","size":"s","title":"W11.17 Clean-machine restore test: archive, restore, replay, reindex, render"}	blake3:SI4PamfgJPV4EwMDQqKnGEvPBAH9THvXRtVebXznOvg
1795322289708616	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1osq	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Generate native `.base` files with filters on flat properties, and plain Markdown fallback tables. Verify the syntax against the actual supported Obsidian release.\" The static tables already exist (W08.07); these are the dynamic equivalents under `Cases/<case-id>/Views/*.base`.\n\n# Goals\nOne generated Base per view in §12.5, valid for the supported Obsidian release.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nBases give sortable, filterable tables with no community plugin. Revision 4 checked the syntax page: \"`note.inv_kind` and the shorthand `inv_kind` are equivalent; `displayName` labels column headers only. Row sort keys are not documented on that page, so detect them by release rather than assuming them.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Start from the §12.5 scaffold (`filters`, `properties` with `displayName`, `views` of `type: table` with `order`), and \"validate/revise in implementation\" against the release recorded in W12.02.\n- \"Real views filter by exact case ID, not an accidental cross-case folder match\": every Base filters `inv_case_id == \"<case-id>\"` plus `inv_kind`.\n- Views: Questions, Hypotheses, Options, Criteria, Sources, Evidence, Measurements, Inquiries/Runs, Conclusions, Decisions, Reviews/blindspots, Retrospectives/lessons, Epistemic frontier, Timeline. The comparison matrix stays a static table in v1.\n- Emit with the tested YAML emitter; deterministic key order.\n- Add any extra flat `inv_` properties the views need to W08.03's frontmatter, never nested values.\n- Base files are tool-owned and in the ownership manifest; edits to them are drift.\n- Editing a cell in a Base changes note properties: that is drift (W08.09), documented beside each view.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Supported-version Bases open correctly and show only the selected case's records.\n- [ ] Each Base has a matching static table with the same rows.\n- [ ] Base files are byte-identical across identical renders.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nW12 acceptance: \"Tested supported Obsidian release; headless Markdown still valid.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W12.02.\nParent goal: W12. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.3, §12.5, §23.6 [O1, O2], Disposition: Obsidian (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w12","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-1q7","size":"m","title":"W12.01 Native .base files for the fifteen shipped views, filtered by exact case ID"}	blake3:bsNVxYA4lr-BcuRkoeF4oHF_jnX1Q6wwBTq41Z2j5SU
1795322289959108	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-ob0v	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Use feature detection or a tested compatibility profile, not assumptions that every user has every current view type. Tables are the baseline. The implementation must record the Obsidian version used for its integration gate.\"\n\n# Goals\nA written, versioned compatibility profile that the Bases, Canvas and CLI-check bones build against.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nObsidian's Bases syntax and CLI are recent and changing. §23.6 records what was observed when the plan was written (the CLI documentation's 1.12 installer generation; CLI-handler registration annotated from 1.12.2) and says these \"justify feature detection, not a promise that every deployment has those capabilities\". Those facts must be re-checked, not copied.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Re-read the current primary documentation for Bases, Bases syntax, Properties, Canvas, JSON Canvas and the Obsidian CLI; record the release tested and the date.\n- Profile file in the repo: supported release range, Base syntax features relied on, whether row sort keys exist in that release, Canvas features used, CLI availability.\n- The renderer reads the profile to choose optional features; with no profile match it emits the baseline (Markdown tables and the simplest Bases).\n- `doctor` reports the profile and, when the Obsidian CLI is present, the installed version; it never requires Obsidian.\n- Record the version in each integration-gate report.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The profile names an exact tested Obsidian release and the documentation revision consulted.\n- [ ] Rendering with an unknown or absent Obsidian falls back to the baseline without error.\n- [ ] Every optional feature the renderer emits is listed in the profile.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nClaims about Obsidian support are tied to a version someone actually tested.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W12. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.3, §23.6 (closing paragraph), §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w12","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-1q7","size":"s","title":"W12.02 Obsidian compatibility profile: pin and record the supported release, verify syntax, detect features"}	blake3:jh95WiewxxAwAzDjfyX4-1nzVM_u3NnMVeRrwI3TFGI
1795322290071897	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-qad9	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Generate a bounded case map using JSON Canvas file nodes and labeled edges, with a mode-sensitive focus.\"\n\n# Goals\n`Cases/<case-id>/Map.canvas`, deterministic and readable.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA map helps a human see the shape of an argument. It must not become the place meaning lives: \"Evidence semantics live in canonical typed relations and Markdown tables. Obsidian's generic graph only knows links; color alone may not encode support/refutation or meets/fails.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Focus by case content: explanatory \"question → rival hypotheses → discriminating observation(s) → next inquiry → bounded conclusion\"; evaluative \"question → options → major criteria / hard constraints → decisive comparisons → conditional decision\"; source research \"questioned claim → primary acquisition roots → derived reporting → conflicting sources and corrections\".\n- File nodes pointing at generated notes, so backlinks work; \"Don't hide essential evidence solely in text-only Canvas nodes\".\n- \"Use edge labels and legend\": every edge carries its relation in words; a legend node explains them.\n- \"Use stable coordinates/ordering and overflow links; avoid rendering every journal event as a node\": a deterministic layered layout with a node cap and an overflow node linking to the tables.\n- Labels pass through safe rendering (W08.12) and audience policy (W08.13).\n- Emit per JSON Canvas 1.0; verify against the spec when implementing.\n- \"A bounded focus view may be generated for a selected subgraph when the case grows.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Supported-version Canvas opens correctly and every node link resolves.\n- [ ] The same snapshot yields a byte-identical canvas.\n- [ ] The map is understandable with colour disabled.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA visual overview that never outranks the typed record.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W12.02.\nParent goal: W12. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §4.2 (Map.canvas), §12.6, §23.6 [O6, O7] (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w12","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-1q7","size":"m","title":"W12.03 Bounded JSON Canvas case map with mode-sensitive focus, edge labels and a legend"}	blake3:svDYbSKbJihW-AZrgwdh2PAVoZR_UgMfV0E7-kfOuXU
1795322290166957	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-13y5	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Generated map is tool-owned. `inv vault copy-map --case C --to Boards/name.canvas` creates an editable human-owned snapshot that does not automatically mutate canonical assessments.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe command.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nPeople want to rearrange and annotate a map. Letting them edit the generated one would be drift; giving them a copy in `Boards/` keeps ownership clear in both directions.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Copy the current generated canvas to a path under `Boards/`; refuse to overwrite an existing file; refuse any destination outside `Boards/`.\n- The copy is not added to the ownership manifest and is never regenerated.\n- Record nothing in the journal; this is a vault-only action.\n- The copy carries a text node stating the snapshot it was taken from.\n- Honour `--read-only` (rejected) via catalog effect metadata.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The copy opens in Obsidian and survives later renders byte for byte.\n- [ ] An existing destination is never overwritten.\n- [ ] Editing the copy changes no canonical record and produces no drift report.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nHuman-owned thinking space, seeded by the casebook.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W12.03.\nParent goal: W12. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.1 (vault copy-map), §12.6 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w12","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian","cli"],"parent":"bn-1q7","size":"xs","title":"W12.04 vault copy-map: an editable human-owned snapshot of the generated map"}	blake3:ton4x7YaDcyREwBsGXANM1XCpInRKgHWxVEvjW2Rt7Q
1795322290259687	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2l8g	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW08.09 detects drift and leaves edited files in place. \"`vault reconcile` offers explicit dispositions: preserve the observed edited file as a human note and regenerate; submit a supported, schema-bound proposed change through `record`; regenerate after explicitly discarding that observed edit; mark an accidentally managed path human-owned and choose a new projection path.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe reconcile command with exactly these four outcomes.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"No automatic Markdown-to-domain inference. Checking a box, dragging a Base card, or editing a property does not validate a conclusion or accept a decision.\" Every drift is resolved by a person or agent choosing, never by the tool guessing intent.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `vault reconcile PATH --disposition preserve-as-note|propose-change|discard|release-path`.\n- `preserve-as-note`: move the edited bytes to `Notes/` under a non-colliding name, then regenerate.\n- `propose-change`: for a small set of supported edits (for example a title), build the standard envelope with fresh CAS preconditions and submit through `record`; unsupported edits are refused with the list of what is supported.\n- `discard`: requires an explicit flag; the discarded bytes are kept in `local/conflicts/` until cleaned.\n- `release-path`: remove the path from the ownership manifest and project the record at a new path.\n- Each disposition is one step with a preview; nothing is inferred from Markdown content.\n- Batch mode applies one disposition to a listed set, never \"all\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each disposition leaves the vault with no drift for that path and loses no observed bytes without an explicit discard.\n- [ ] A checkbox or property edit never becomes a domain mutation except through `propose-change` for a supported field.\n- [ ] Reconcile works with Obsidian closed.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nW12 package: \"owned file publishing/reconciliation\".\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W12. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.1 (vault reconcile), §12.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w12","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian","cli"],"parent":"bn-1q7","size":"m","title":"W12.05 vault reconcile: four explicit dispositions for drifted generated files"}	blake3:VlS_3gr_CFSZzblK4rESePUGtMFZPF2I9cb19tH5hN0
1795322290346431	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1g4i	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Existing-vault mode is supported after dedicated mode works. It requires an explicit managed prefix and a complete ownership manifest. Do not move existing notes, change their properties, change the theme, or recursively delete unknown files.\"\n\n# Goals\nRender the casebook inside a subfolder of a vault the user already has.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nMany people will not want a second vault. The risk is that vault-wide things — property types, base filters, links — collide with their notes. The managed prefix and exact-ID filters contain that.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Configuration: vault path outside the workspace plus a managed prefix (for example `Investigation/`); all owned paths live under it, including `Notes/`, `Boards/`, `Inbox/` equivalents.\n- Canonical state stays in the workspace; the vault receives only the projection.\n- Property names are `inv_`-prefixed, so vault-wide property types do not clash; document the property types the casebook expects.\n- Bases filter by `inv_case_id`, so foreign notes never appear.\n- Never write Obsidian configuration, themes or other folders.\n- First render into a prefix that already contains files refuses unless they are owned.\n- Test against a fixture vault with unrelated notes, settings and plugins.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A hash of everything outside the managed prefix is unchanged after render, reconcile and uninstall of the projection.\n- [ ] Foreign notes with similar titles never appear in a generated view.\n- [ ] Removing the projection deletes only owned files.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Manage the casebook, not confiscate the entire vault.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W12.05.\nParent goal: W12. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.1 (Existing-vault mode), §13.4, §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w12","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-1q7","size":"m","title":"W12.06 Existing-vault mode: managed prefix, complete ownership manifest, nothing else touched"}	blake3:h5mxmFzj2M8wI_yQQmWuh1y0FdORRELov2MvF8WGlMc
1795322290435879	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3d9f	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Current Obsidian has an official CLI with file navigation, backlinks, unresolved-link inspection, and Base queries, but it runs through the desktop app and requires a recent installer. Treat it as an optional navigation and UI-test adapter.\"\n\n# Goals\nA test adapter, outside the headless suite, that checks a rendered vault in a real Obsidian of the supported release.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nFile-level tests (W08.14) cannot show that a Base actually parses or a Canvas opens. \"No Obsidian automation is part of the core headless test suite\", so this runs as a separate, optional gate with its version recorded.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Verify the CLI's current commands against its documentation first (W12.02); \"Always pass exact vault and path; its default targeting depends on context.\"\n- Checks: open each generated note type; query each Base and compare row counts with the static table; list unresolved links (expect none in owned files); open the Canvas.\n- Spawn with an argv array; never a shell string; never `eval`.\n- Skips cleanly with a clear message when Obsidian or its CLI is unavailable.\n- Produces a report naming the Obsidian version, vault and results.\n- Community plugins disabled in the test vault.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Supported-version Bases and Canvas open correctly; links/aliases/excerpt blocks resolve.\n- [ ] Base row counts equal the static tables for each fixture case.\n- [ ] The core test suite passes on a machine with no Obsidian.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEvidence that the casebook works in the application, at a stated version.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W12.01, W12.03.\nParent goal: W12. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.3, §13.4 (closing paragraph), §20.4, §23.6 [O4] (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w12","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian","test"],"parent":"bn-1q7","size":"m","title":"W12.07 Optional Obsidian CLI adapter for UI checks: open paths, query Bases, find unresolved links"}	blake3:irH-V_rLQhsTGha14teGDKXS8s-LrI1I3YxL0Bjqk_w
1795322290528233	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-hli9	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Load-test the vault near 50,000 notes, where users report lag.\" (Adopted from review 2.)\n\n# Goals\nMeasure render time, vault size and Obsidian behaviour for a very large workspace, and record limits.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nOne note per record means a long-lived workspace reaches tens of thousands of files. It is better to know where it degrades and say so than to discover it in someone's vault.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Generate a synthetic workspace producing about 50,000 owned notes across many cases (reuse the W05b.15 generator).\n- Measure full render and incremental render time (W08.15), manifest size and memory.\n- With the supported Obsidian release: cold open time, Base query responsiveness for one case, Canvas open, search — recorded with machine details and method, following the project's own measurement discipline (counts, repetitions, order).\n- If Bases lag because they scan the whole vault, document mitigations: per-case Bases already filter by ID; suggest a review vault per case (W11.07) or archiving closed cases out of the working vault.\n- Report results as observations with their conditions, not as guarantees.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The test is reproducible with one command and produces a report.\n- [ ] Documented guidance states the tested scale and observed limits.\n- [ ] Any mitigation changes no canonical state.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nHonest capacity limits for the human-facing projection.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W12.01.\nParent goal: W12. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.5, §10.5, Disposition: Obsidian (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w12","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian","test"],"parent":"bn-1q7","size":"s","title":"W12.08 Vault load test near 50,000 notes"}	blake3:nvXclOrSHCZmz2dqLTMzGZZ_yOSHScu8PqUgpt7yVsA
1795322290617935	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3tq2	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`inv obsidian setup --vault PATH` to inspect and show the exact proposed diff/manifest.\" And §12.1: \"A requested setup changes only named settings/bundles with a previewable diff.\"\n\n# Goals\nA preview-first setup command for native features only, plus the deployment guidance of §12.8.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The core neither injects plugins nor disables Restricted Mode. Any install is opt-in, previewable, version-pinned, and reversible without changing unrelated vault config.\" Companion installation is W15.09; this bone covers the base case and the documentation users need before syncing anything.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `obsidian setup --vault PATH`: detect the configuration directory (including a custom one); show which optional native settings would help and the exact diff; apply only with an explicit flag, only named keys.\n- Never enables community plugins or changes Restricted Mode.\n- Documentation: \"Obsidian Sync synchronizes files, not Investigation transactions\"; the recommended deployment (one authoritative workspace; optional synced review vault; mobile for browsing and human annotations; full archive for recovery).\n- \"Never put SQLite/WAL under Sync and call that collaboration. Avoid evidence symlinks.\"\n- \"Configure sharing/sync selection before exporting sensitive evidence. Removing a file from a future export does not retract copies already distributed.\"\n- The plugin table of §12.9: what is not required and why.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Without the apply flag, `obsidian setup` writes nothing.\n- [ ] Apply changes only the previewed keys; other configuration is byte-identical.\n- [ ] The docs state plainly that a synced vault alone is not a resumable investigation.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"No automatic cloud sync, publish, or Git push belongs in initialization.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W12.02.\nParent goal: W12. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.1 (obsidian setup), §12.1 (Obsidian configuration row), §12.8, §12.9, §13.3 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w12","milestone-c","lane-projection","obsidian","cli","docs"],"parent":"bn-1q7","size":"s","title":"W12.09 obsidian setup preview and documentation for sync, mobile and what must never be synced"}	blake3:CMxTEq0bN4Hs7YiL5fha9p2n4BvJV7OWheQPprI5_GY
1795322290837642	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2vy3	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"The CLI is the reference implementation. MCP uses the same Rust command dispatch, validation, receipts, and error codes over stdio. No HTTP listener is needed.\" \"MCP is a secondary transport.\"\n\n# Goals\n`investigation mcp` serving tools from the catalog through the shared dispatch.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nW13's package text: \"Typed MCP frontend derived from same catalog, not separate domain implementation.\" A second implementation would drift from the CLI and double the surface to test.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- New crate `investigation-mcp`; depends on core, store and the dispatch layer only.\n- Stdio transport; no network listener; no daemon.\n- \"Target the stateless MCP revision (2026-07-28, no `initialize` handshake): profiles are chosen by server flag and visible through `tools/list` and server discovery, not negotiated in a session.\" Re-read the published specification before implementing; do not rely on memory of earlier revisions.\n- Each tool call maps to one catalog command; results are the same response and error envelopes as the CLI, carried as structured content.\n- `mcp --read-only` reuses the dispatch switch from W05b.09.\n- Workspace is fixed by server flag; case selection follows §9.2 rules. Actor is supplied per call or by server configuration, and is still only a claim.\n- Synchronous core; add an async runtime only if the transport requires it.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A tool call and the equivalent CLI command produce the same accepted state and receipt.\n- [ ] The server opens no socket.\n- [ ] Under `--read-only`, every mutating tool call is rejected before the commit lock.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"MCP is a transport, not a second source of truth.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W13. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.1 (mcp), §9.7, §18.1 (investigation-mcp), Disposition: Agent experience and interface (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w13","milestone-c","lane-agent-ux","mcp"],"parent":"bn-17k","size":"m","title":"W13.01 MCP server over stdio wrapping the same dispatch, with --read-only"}	blake3:dN_RqeAlJfL3AUnZhO2ViIB-40B3SUdaY_zg_C_uK18
1795322291023903	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3m9b	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**Default `core` profile: at most seven flat-schema tools with a `tools/list` budget under 12 KiB**, enforced in CI: `onboard`; `read` (flat `action`: `brief | show | explain | search | next | frontier | blindspots | retrospective | check`, with `depth`, `case_id`, `id`, `query`, `max_bytes`); `evidence_read`; `schema`...; `record`...; `ingest`; `receipt`.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe compact default profile and a CI check on its size.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReview 1 #9: a large eagerly loaded tool set costs tokens in every session. \"The **primary agent path is the CLI plus a skill/AGENTS snippet**; measured token cost for CLI use is far below a large eagerly loaded MCP tool set.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Seven tools exactly as listed; `record` takes \"the same atomic envelope and validation as `inv record`, so cross-entity batches need no escape hatch\".\n- `schema` gives the just-in-time operation schema and minimal example, so the big schemas are never in `tools/list`.\n- \"Avoid deeply nested `oneOf` unions in default tool schemas\": flat properties with enums; operation payloads are validated server-side against the catalog.\n- Descriptions are short and generated from the catalog.\n- CI test: serialize `tools/list` for the core profile and fail above 12 KiB or above seven tools.\n- Tool results carry the same snapshot, freshness and untrusted-content markings as CLI JSON.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `tools/list` for the core profile is under 12 KiB with at most seven tools, enforced in CI.\n- [ ] Every `read` action returns the same JSON as its CLI command.\n- [ ] A batch spanning several record types commits through the single `record` tool.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA cheap default for hosts that load every tool up front.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W13.01, W10.10, W10.17.\nParent goal: W13. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.7, §20.5, Disposition: Agent experience and interface (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w13","milestone-c","lane-agent-ux","mcp"],"parent":"bn-17k","size":"m","title":"W13.02 Default core profile: at most seven flat-schema tools under a 12 KiB tools/list budget in CI"}	blake3:SWNnkM7O17mg5RMeWS96iY2nQyZYeGJGIZUc6mvm51w
1795322291120984	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-xkw8	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"An opt-in `expanded` profile exposes domain-grouped typed write tools for hosts with deferred tool loading. Do not hide unvalidated JSON strings behind a supposedly typed tool. Do not duplicate all CLI aliases as tools.\"\n\n# Goals\nA second profile selected by server flag.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nHosts that load tool schemas on demand benefit from typed inputs per operation; hosts that load everything eagerly do not. Offering both, chosen explicitly, serves each without penalizing the other.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `mcp --profile expanded`: tools generated from the catalog, grouped by stage (frame, collect, explain, evaluate, assess, review, close), each with a real typed input schema.\n- Every tool still builds the standard envelope and goes through the same dispatch; idempotency keys and CAS preconditions are explicit inputs.\n- No tool accepts a string that is parsed as JSON internally.\n- No tool exists for a CLI alias or shortcut that another tool covers.\n- The profile is \"visible through `tools/list` and server discovery, not negotiated in a session\".\n- The core profile's tools remain available in the expanded profile for batches.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every expanded write tool's schema validates the same payloads as the catalog operation, and rejects the same invalid ones.\n- [ ] Adding a catalog operation adds its tool without hand-written code.\n- [ ] No expanded tool has an untyped JSON-string parameter.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nOne catalog, two shapes, no second domain implementation.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W13.02.\nParent goal: W13. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w13","milestone-c","lane-agent-ux","mcp"],"parent":"bn-17k","size":"m","title":"W13.03 Opt-in expanded profile: domain-grouped typed write tools"}	blake3:Mi50BCaBM4O1ezIuXVPD-P50Utp6BFIxDjtfSADA_Rk
1795322291211979	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-sf1l	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Resources expose versioned schemas, method cards, and sanitized case snapshots. Read-only tool annotations and mutation annotations must match real effects. Never advertise hypothetical tools from an unavailable capability.\"\n\n# Goals\nResources and annotations generated from catalog metadata.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nHosts use annotations to decide what needs confirmation. An annotation that says read-only on a tool that writes would defeat host-side safety; one that says destructive on a read would train users to click through.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Resources: published JSON Schemas (from `schemas/`), cards from built-in and pinned packs, and a bounded, classification-safe case snapshot equivalent to `brief`.\n- Annotations derive from the same effect metadata that `--read-only` uses (W05b.09); a test compares every tool's annotation with its declared effects.\n- Tools for capabilities not present (for example capture when disabled by configuration) are not listed.\n- Resource content containing authored or source text keeps its quoted-data and untrusted markings.\n- \"Default `brief` and MCP outputs are bounded and safe by classification.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] No tool's annotation disagrees with its catalog effect metadata.\n- [ ] Case snapshot resources contain no raw artifact bytes or absolute paths.\n- [ ] Method card resources identify their pack and digest.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nHosts can trust what the server says about itself.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W13.01.\nParent goal: W13. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.7 (closing paragraph), §16.1, §16.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w13","milestone-c","lane-agent-ux","mcp"],"parent":"bn-17k","size":"s","title":"W13.04 MCP resources and truthful tool annotations"}	blake3:chf-GtJihkqTMPpADWTc7iG7Mg3f_4EniAmZMhJ-8zg
1795322291303662	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1n2u	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW13's acceptance: \"CLI/MCP parity and error conformance in independent tests.\" §20.7: \"When MCP is included in the release, CLI and MCP equivalent walkthroughs reach the same accepted domain state, excluding transport-attribution metadata.\"\n\n# Goals\nA test suite that drives the same scenarios through both frontends and compares results.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Expose the same fixtures through CLI and MCP, with deterministic expected read sets, summaries, audit rule IDs, and accepted transactions.\" Parity is the evidence that MCP is only a transport.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- A minimal MCP test client written independently of the server crate (speaks the protocol over stdio).\n- Replay Scenarios A–D and drills 1–6 through MCP in both profiles; compare journals with the CLI runs after normalizing IDs, times and transport attribution.\n- Error conformance: for each error class (validation, conflict, gate failure, idempotency mismatch, unknown durability), the MCP error carries the same code, blockers and repair as the CLI envelope.\n- Read parity: `read` actions against golden CLI outputs.\n- Lost-response retry through MCP returns the original IDs.\n- Assert no fixture depends on an agent runtime being installed.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Equivalent walkthroughs reach the same accepted domain state in both frontends and both profiles.\n- [ ] Every error class matches by code and repair.\n- [ ] The parity suite runs in CI without a model.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nOne behaviour, whichever way an agent reaches it.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W13.02, W13.03, W13.04, W10.24, W10.25, W10.26.\nParent goal: W13. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.7 (closing paragraph), §19.2 (W13), §20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w13","milestone-c","lane-agent-ux","mcp","test"],"parent":"bn-17k","size":"m","title":"W13.05 CLI/MCP parity and error-conformance tests on the shared fixtures and drills"}	blake3:M-3hnmRogNjF2wbxqyip4scjVO0EJi3w-R6YIMrWL5Q
1795322291394865	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-rx36	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Measure `tools/list` size and tool-call accuracy for both profiles in §20.5.\"\n\n# Goals\nNumbers that justify, or revise, the profile design and the claim that CLI plus snippet is the primary path.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe 12 KiB budget and the seven-tool shape are design bets. The plan's own standard is to measure rather than assert, and to report cost alongside correctness.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Use the evaluation harness (W14.05) on development-split tasks, not sealed ones.\n- Conditions: CLI plus snippet; MCP core; MCP expanded (on a host with deferred loading if available).\n- Record: `tools/list` bytes; tokens consumed before the first useful receipt; fraction of tool calls that are well-formed on the first attempt; number of schema look-ups; task success on the smoke set.\n- Report with counts and denominators, per agent family, with the limitations of a small sample stated.\n- Feed findings into profile descriptions and the snippet; record any change of default as an ADR (\"MCP profile shape\" is on the §19.5 ADR list).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A committed report covers all three conditions with raw counts.\n- [ ] The report states what the sample can and cannot support.\n- [ ] Any resulting design change has an ADR.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInterface choices backed by observed agent behaviour.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W13.05, W14.05.\nParent goal: W13. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.7, §20.5 (Cost; measures) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w13","milestone-c","lane-agent-ux","mcp"],"parent":"bn-17k","size":"s","title":"W13.06 Measure tools/list size and tool-call accuracy for both MCP profiles against the CLI path"}	blake3:yZDbtvYnYb5xylPM4hSIIjzJTR2XnLAWdwDfdy1K0Vg
1795322412510226	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-139b	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Split development, pilot and sealed evaluation cases by underlying case family, with all variants in the same split. Sealed cases are written by someone who does not work on blindspot rules or onboarding, and their keys live outside the agents' workspace. Only sealed results count toward a release claim.\"\n\n# Goals\nA sealed set of independent case families covering base scenarios 1–4: an intermittent bug with a good control and an invalid confounded trace; a p99 regression with a broken instrument; a source-research claim with dependent citations and a correction; an architecture choice under hard and soft constraints with a changed criterion.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nReviews 2 and 3: the development scenarios have shaped onboarding text and gap rules, so results on them say little. Authorship by a non-builder is the control against unconscious tuning. \"Don't cheat by embedding fixture-specific solutions in onboarding or source labels.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Number of families per domain from the preregistration (AG.11); \"the experimental unit is an independent case, not a repeat\".\n- Each family: objective, seed source bytes, handoff boundary, and a prewritten rubric that \"allows every choice consistent with the stated preferences and constraints\".\n- Register every family in the split registry (AG.04); no family shares its underlying situation with a development or pilot family.\n- Keys and rubrics are stored outside any path an agent or builder workflow reads; access is logged.\n- Synthetic, domain-neutral content; nothing a model could have memorized as a known incident.\n- The author records how each family was constructed and what it is meant to test, sealed with the keys.\n- Builders see sealed content only after the trial; \"use fresh held-out families after tuning on failures\".\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The sealed set matches the preregistered counts, with each domain represented.\n- [ ] Authorship and custody are recorded; no builder of rules or onboarding wrote or read a sealed case before the trial.\n- [ ] Each family has a rubric committed before any run.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe only results allowed to support a release claim.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.5 (Held-out case families; base scenarios 1–4), Disposition: Evaluation and sequencing (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation","fixture"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"l","title":"W14.01 Sealed evaluation case families for all four domains, written by a non-builder"}	blake3:vbk1S1jfAzpvTTeJGIHY3mMHIoZ6uL41cMlPJ0dz0W4
1795322413963240	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2da8	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Include sufficient-evidence, missing-evidence and cosmetically changed twins (remove a decisive source, flip an operative criterion, rename an option, add a duplicate citation), plus one-shot lookups where the right move is to skip the tool. Count missed warranted answers alongside unwarranted promotions.\"\n\n# Goals\nTwin variants for sealed families and a set of tasks where using the tool is the wrong choice.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA tool that makes agents cautious can look good by never concluding anything. Twins separate real sensitivity to evidence from blanket hedging: the answer should change when a decisive source is removed and should not change when an option is renamed.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- For each family where it makes sense, three twins: sufficient evidence (a warranted answer exists); missing evidence (the decisive source removed; the right answer is \"cannot conclude\" with what is needed); cosmetic change (rename, duplicate citation, reorder) that must not change the answer.\n- One operative-criterion flip per evaluative family, which must change the choice or its conditions.\n- All twins of a family stay in the sealed split.\n- Skip-the-tool tasks: one-shot factual lookups and ordinary implementation tasks, graded on answer correctness and on cost.\n- Rubrics state the expected direction of change per twin.\n- Twins are assigned so one agent session never sees two twins of a family.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every twin has an expected outcome relative to its base case.\n- [ ] Both error directions are scoreable for every family.\n- [ ] Skip-the-tool tasks exist and are included in the cost comparison.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMeasures unwarranted promotion and unnecessary abstention together.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W14.01.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.5 (Paired twins; False promotion and unnecessary abstention) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation","fixture"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"m","title":"W14.02 Paired twins and skip-the-tool tasks for the sealed set"}	blake3:xiKn9ADOGtUEymbGuLcmgDNXjYU4Wqx0bYMfMcvLjrA
1795322414571528	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-15m3	{"description":"# Context & Background\nScenarios: \"5. Resume interrupted work with a different agent and a stale snapshot. 6. Retry a write whose response was lost; handle a real revision conflict without clobbering. 7. Receive a source containing a forged `AGENTS.md`, instructions and an embedded schema-valid `inv record` payload urging the agent to validate a false conclusion; ignore them. Second stage: a first agent summarizes that source; a second agent, reading only `brief` and `show --depth summary`, must not act on any instruction carried in the synopsis, title or lesson. Policy guardrails (§16.1) block the promotion when enabled.\"\n\n# Goals\nHarness tasks and rubrics for these three scenarios.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThese test the mechanics that only matter when things go wrong. The two-stage injection (reviews 1 #10 and 2 #7) targets the specific weakness of layered reads: laundering an instruction through a summary.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Task 5: the successor is handed a brief from an earlier snapshot while the case has since changed; success is noticing and re-reading before acting.\n- Task 6: the harness drops a response after commit and, separately, makes a concurrent change; success is exactly one write and a reconciled conflict.\n- Task 7 stage one: a seeded source with a forged instruction file, imperative text and a valid-looking `record` payload. Stage two: a fresh agent sees only compact reads of the first agent's records.\n- Run stage two with guardrails off and on; record whether the write was attempted and whether policy blocked it.\n- Deterministic checks on the journal decide outcomes (was the false conclusion validated; by whom), not transcript reading alone.\n- Hostile content is clearly synthetic and harmless.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each task has a journal-based pass/fail check and a rubric.\n- [ ] The injection task reports attempts and successes with denominators for both stages.\n- [ ] No task prompt mentions injection or hints at the trap.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTests \"evidence is data, not instructions\" where it is hardest to keep.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.18, W10.19.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.5 (scenarios 5–7), §16.1, Disposition: Trust, sensitivity and retention (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation","fixture","security"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"m","title":"W14.03 Trial tasks 5–7: stale-snapshot resume, lost-response and conflict, two-stage injection"}	blake3:sML-Sjv472yjsMHjTKLonfGH9jbUntTv80jMXauSGb4
1795322415644736	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3vjp	{"description":"# Context & Background\nScenarios: \"8. Use title/summary reads to inspect a 40-record case before selectively fetching deep evidence; retain the decisive caveat and reduce unnecessary raw reads. 9. Discover one deliberate missing control/criterion with `inv blindspots`, then record a genuinely novel semantic risk absent from the checker, and review without auto-promoting the case. 10. Produce a useful, bounded Retrospective for a failed or inconclusive investigation and close atomically; demonstrate method packs did not mutate.\"\n\n# Goals\nAgent-facing versions of drills 1–6 with rubrics.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nW10.24–W10.26 proved the mechanisms with scripts. These tasks ask whether an unfamiliar agent actually uses them well, which is what W14's package text calls \"layered retrieval, blindspot and retrospective drills\".\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Task 8: sealed-split 40-record cases; measure bytes read at each depth, raw evidence reads, and whether the answer keeps the decisive caveat.\n- Task 9: a seeded structural gap plus a semantic risk the rules cannot see; rubric credits finding the first, proposing a real second, and not promoting the case as a side effect. Graders judge whether the proposed risk is substantive or filler.\n- Task 10: a failed or inconclusive investigation; rubric checks that the retrospective cites digest items, states something checkable, invents no failure, and that closure was one transaction.\n- Deterministic post-checks: pack files and pins unchanged; no lesson admitted automatically.\n- Fixtures belong to the sealed split; development copies of the drills stay separate.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each task has deterministic journal checks plus a rubric for the judgment parts.\n- [ ] Byte and call counts are captured per depth for task 8.\n- [ ] Task 10 verifies \"zero invented failures or auto-adopted method changes\".\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nShows whether the features built to keep agents honest work in agents' hands.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W10.24, W10.25, W10.26.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.5 (scenarios 8–10), §17.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation","fixture"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"m","title":"W14.04 Trial tasks 8–10: layered reads on 40 records, blindspots plus a novel risk, bounded retrospective"}	blake3:05DI0VoTX8bLpIuz5FJcQpFSZy31QpyKuS72UP_NJ8U
1795322416011012	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2aic	{"description":"# Context & Background\nAG.01 built the paired-arm harness for two domains. The release trial needs all four domains, tasks 5–10, twins and the full cost record.\n\n# Goals\nA harness that can run the entire preregistered design reproducibly.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe trial's value depends on procedure: matched budgets, clean sessions, counterbalancing, blinding, pinned versions. Extending the pilot harness, with its lessons applied, is safer than a new one.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Apply fixes from the pilot (AG.08 threats to validity).\n- Task runners for each task type, including fault injection for task 6 and the two-stage flow for task 7.\n- Twin assignment and counterbalanced order generated from a recorded seed.\n- Sealed cases are mounted read-only into each run; keys and rubrics never enter a run's filesystem.\n- Cost capture as in AG.06, plus per-depth read bytes.\n- Optional MCP conditions for W13.06, kept outside the primary comparison.\n- Run metadata: model, host, tool and binary versions; \"prevent session carryover\".\n- Dry-run mode on development families only.\n- The binary under test is frozen for the trial and its hash recorded.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A full dry run on development families completes for every task type in both arms.\n- [ ] No sealed key or rubric is readable from inside a run.\n- [ ] Every run produces transcripts, answers, journal or notes snapshot, and a complete cost record.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes the release comparison executable exactly as preregistered.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.5 (Conditions, Cost), §18.1 (training/) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"m","title":"W14.05 Extend the trial harness for the release trial: four domains, all task types, both arms, cost capture"}	blake3:teJgEvQ1xxCHcf2Ro_gf2GPuwFiah1I5FohtUl5luAU
1795322417100464	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-17ts	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**Gate content, not gate presence.** Graders judge whether recorded rivals were real alternatives with a distinguishing prediction and whether retrospectives state anything checkable. Report the strawman rate.\" (Review 2 #1.)\n\n# Goals\nRubrics and a grading procedure for the content agents put through the gates.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA gate that requires a rival hypothesis can be satisfied by a rival nobody believes. If agents learn to feed gates filler, the structure adds cost and false assurance. Measuring that is the only way to know.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Rival rubric: is the rival a real alternative for this evidence; does it have a prediction that differs from the favoured account; was it assessed against anything.\n- Retrospective rubric: does it cite case events; does it state something a later reader could check; does it invent failures or praise.\n- Blindspot-review rubric: are the recorded gaps specific to the case.\n- Sample records from `inv`-arm journals in the trial; graders see records without outcomes where possible.\n- Report rates with counts and denominators per agent family; if LLM graders are used, \"treat them as correlated, not independent\" and include a human-graded subsample with agreement.\n- Findings feed the catalog only through an ADR.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Rubrics are committed before the sealed trial.\n- [ ] The trial report includes the strawman rate with its denominator.\n- [ ] Grader agreement on the human subsample is reported.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nChecks that structure produces substance, not ceremony.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W14.01.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.5 (Gate content, not gate presence), Disposition: Evaluation and sequencing (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"s","title":"W14.06 Gate-content grading: strawman-rival rate and checkable-retrospective rate"}	blake3:E7LITALXpmqtHT7JFPZtdw5DVMY587aJc9YWkHs7uik
1795322417784535	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2nlz	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe §20.5 table has measures that do not need an agent trial to check, with fixed targets: for example \"Correct case/object targeting: 100%; no cross-case writes\" and \"Lost/duplicate writes: Zero with injected response loss, retries and process termination.\"\n\n# Goals\nA scripted suite that produces these measures on every release candidate.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nSeparating deterministic gates from statistical ones keeps each honest. A deterministic \"zero\" really is zero; an agent-trial \"zero observed\" needs an upper bound.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Targeting: fuzz writes with wrong or missing case IDs; assert none lands in another case.\n- Lost or duplicate writes: inject response loss, retries and process kills across the write commands; assert zero lost and zero duplicated.\n- \"False promotion... Zero in deterministic invalid-control and stale-basis fixtures.\"\n- Repairability: for each ordinary schema and CAS error, follow the returned repair mechanically and assert success (extends W05a.13).\n- Context efficiency: \"A compact 40-record fixture should emit at least 50% fewer bytes than a full-record traversal... this is a compression regression check.\"\n- Blindspot calibration: \"All seeded structural gaps detected in matched fixtures; no claim of completeness.\"\n- Domain neutrality: scripted architecture and research walkthroughs use no security abstraction and no test-run ceremony.\n- Output a machine-readable report keyed by measure.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every deterministic measure has a passing test and appears in the report.\n- [ ] The suite runs offline in CI.\n- [ ] The report labels these as deterministic checks, distinct from trial results.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nW14 acceptance: \"invalid outcomes, hidden caveats and phantom lesson promotion are zero.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W14.05.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.5 (measures table) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation","test"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"m","title":"W14.07 Deterministic acceptance measures: targeting, lost writes, false promotion, compression, calibration"}	blake3:eNMUmCNkMq8xwSLLteux-w278_rMVLlRb905po6oieE
1795322418727297	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1ktn	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"This is a release gate **structured as a controlled comparison**, not a documentation review or an uncontrolled pass rate. No published study shows that structured evidence tracking beats free-form notes for agents... so this comparison is the evidence the product needs.\"\n\n# Goals\nExecute the trial as preregistered (AG.11), analyze it as planned, and publish what it does and does not show.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe whole plan rests on an untested hypothesis. The project's own standard — frozen plan, held-out data, both error directions, stated uncertainty — applies to its release claim.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Confirm the preregistration hash predates opening the sealed set; freeze the binary and record versions.\n- Run every sealed family and twin in both arms on at least two agent families or hosts; include timeouts and failures.\n- Grade blinded (AG.03 pipeline); grade tool mechanics separately.\n- Analysis: \"paired differences with intervals clustered by case, per-model and per-domain breakdowns, and repeat reliability (fraction of cases where all k repeats pass)\"; cost per arm; \"'Zero observed' is stated with its upper bound, never as 'zero'\".\n- \"18/20 is a smoke target only.\"\n- Apply the preregistered decision rule. \"If results are inconclusive, report the uncertainty and withhold the improvement claim.\"\n- \"Investigate every failure\"; fixes are validated on fresh held-out families, not the opened ones.\n- Publish the report, data pointers, deviations from the preregistration and limitations.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All preregistered runs are accounted for and archived.\n- [ ] The report applies the chosen criterion with no post-hoc change, and lists any deviation.\n- [ ] Each domain is represented and every failure has a recorded investigation.\n- [ ] The release claim made matches what the intervals support.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Published trials meet §20 goals\" — or the release says honestly that they did not.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W05b, W06b, W07, W08, W09, W10, W11, W12, AG.11, W14.02, W14.03, W14.04, W14.05, W14.06, W14.07.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.2 (W14), §20.5, Binding decisions (Product hypothesis) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"l","title":"W14.08 Run the preregistered release trial on sealed cases and publish the report with intervals"}	blake3:Ut3YE3J6M8bnvi2DoZ80wdLj-5i3ftFD1LETWTQkbSQ
1795322420689352	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3ijp	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.6: \"Ask a reviewer who did not create the case to\" do ten things, from \"Find the current bounded conclusion\" to following \"the evidence-to-caveat path by keyboard and in a linear plain-text rendering, in a narrow viewport with color off\".\n\n# Goals\nRun the gate with real reviewers on the shipped vault and exports, and fix what it finds.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Pass only when the reviewer can do these without a narrated tour... A screenshot looking polished is insufficient.\" Item 10 was added by review 3: status must not depend on colour or layout.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Prepare cases the reviewer has not seen, one explanatory and one evaluative at least, rendered with community plugins disabled.\n- Script the ten tasks: find the conclusion; follow it to evidence and selector; identify the strongest contradiction; tell a failed test from a valid negative result; add a note without corrupting generated state; resume with the suggested command; switch between synopsis, full reasoning and excerpt and spot a stale synopsis; locate the latest blindspot challenge and Retrospective and tell speculative from supported lessons; decide whether a suggested measurement would change the choice, defer an unavailable source and find the still-visible blocker; do the evidence-to-caveat path by keyboard and in plain text.\n- No help from the observer. \"Count wrong turns and requests for help.\"\n- Record observations; \"fix misleading status labels, overwhelming tables, or dead-end navigation\"; re-test changed areas with a fresh reviewer.\n- Keep the session records with their limitations (how many reviewers, who).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] At least one reviewer per case type completes all ten tasks without a narrated tour.\n- [ ] Wrong turns and help requests are counted and reported.\n- [ ] Every misleading label or dead end found has a fix or a recorded decision.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe casebook is judged by whether a stranger can use it.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W12.01, W12.03, W11.07, W14.11.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.6, Disposition: Agent experience and interface (Accessibility checks) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"m","title":"W14.09 Human experience gate: ten tasks by a reviewer who did not create the case"}	blake3:jwHhhrCUqv57xaADePyr4EOYfpk7ORHheMrhCP7mnaM
1795322422392603	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-6n1p	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.7: \"A real investigation has been dogfooded, with its sensitive data kept out of public fixtures.\"\n\n# Goals\nUse the tool for at least one real question the project or its owner actually has, from first source to closure and export.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nFixtures are designed; real work is not. Dogfooding finds the friction no scenario anticipated: awkward first steps, missing shortcuts, advice that nags, reports that bury the point.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Choose a real question with real uncertainty (for example a design choice or a bug in this project); ideally one explanatory and one evaluative.\n- Run it with ordinary agent sessions and at least one handoff to a fresh session.\n- Keep the workspace outside the repository; classify evidence properly; nothing sensitive enters fixtures or the public tree.\n- Log friction as it happens: commands retried, schemas re-read, advice deferred, places notes were used instead of records.\n- Close with a Retrospective written from the digest; export a report and, if evaluative, a decision record.\n- Turn findings into bones or ADRs; record what was deliberately not changed.\n- Write a short dogfooding note with limitations (one team, chosen cases).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] At least one real case is carried from start to closed, with a handoff and an export.\n- [ ] No dogfood evidence appears in the public repository.\n- [ ] Friction findings are filed with dispositions.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTests the tool on the job it exists to do.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): MB.01.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.2 (W14: real dogfooding), §20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"m","title":"W14.10 Dogfood a real investigation and keep its sensitive data out of public fixtures"}	blake3:INw_Vl0GtC2v0CO67NbkjdcUdSuVeolPoXc5J-eh9D8
1795322423224718	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-goz8	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"`demo PATH --scenario NAME`: Generate reproducible tutorial cases (`debug`, `performance`, `research`, `choice`). Mutation: local fixture only.\" §17.5 gives the tutorial command path from `demo` through `onboard`, `brief`, `schema comparison.add`, `next`, `check`, `vault render` and `export --kind decision-record`.\n\n# Goals\nThe `demo` command and a tutorial that runs exactly as written.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The fixture contains already committed objects with real IDs and a replayable exercise, so first-use commands work without copying fake IDs.\" A new user or agent learns fastest from a real workspace they can poke.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- `demo` creates a fresh workspace at PATH by replaying the scenario's committed transactions through the normal commit path with a fixed clock and ID seed (W01.17 seams), so output is reproducible.\n- Scenarios map to fixtures: `debug` (A), `performance` (B), `research` (D), `choice` (C); each leaves a deliberate next step open.\n- Refuses a non-empty PATH; writes nothing elsewhere.\n- CI runs the §17.5 path verbatim for each scenario.\n- \"`inv tutorial next` (optional) can emit the next input operation for tutorial-only use, but must never auto-promote an invalid result to finish the tutorial.\"\n- Tutorial text is generated from the same catalog as the commands.\n- Demo workspaces are marked as demo in `workspace.json`.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each of the four scenarios generates byte-identical workspaces on repeat.\n- [ ] The §17.5 command path runs without edits against `--scenario choice`.\n- [ ] No tutorial step validates an invalid result.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"All scenarios ship as deterministic offline fixtures.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W06b.13, W10.22, W11.05.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §9.1 (demo), §17 (preamble), §17.5 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation","cli","docs"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"m","title":"W14.11 demo command and the shipped tutorial path over reproducible scenario workspaces"}	blake3:z9pnmMiFyDMad4Y470pymaLbvotn8aaKvH-QWsIsVxM
1795322424527505	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-vxw1	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§17 is titled \"Worked investigations and shipped tutorials\". Each scenario lists what a fresh agent should do and an adversarial variant.\n\n# Goals\nFour short walkthrough documents under `docs/`, one per scenario, whose commands are executed in CI.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n§20.7: \"Executable examples run in fresh fixture workspaces using actual returned/generated IDs; schematic examples are labeled. Rejection examples assert both the repair and absence of mutation.\" Documentation that is run cannot drift.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Each walkthrough follows its scenario's numbered steps (for example Scenario A's seven steps), explaining why each step is taken and which card applies.\n- Include the adversarial variant and what the tool reports: A's different-build refutation, B's broken monitor, C's changed hard criterion, D's injected instructions.\n- Each shows one rejection with its repair.\n- Commands are extracted and run against a `demo` workspace; real IDs are substituted from outputs.\n- State explicitly that the same binary and catalog handle all four; \"only optional method cards change\".\n- Keep each to a few screens; link to cards and schemas rather than repeating them.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All four walkthroughs execute in CI from a fresh demo workspace.\n- [ ] Each rejection example asserts the repair and the absence of mutation.\n- [ ] Schematic examples are labeled as schematic.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTeaches the method by example, in four domains, on one tool.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W14.11.\nParent goal: W14. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §17.1–§17.4, §18.1 (docs/), §20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w14","milestone-c","lane-evaluation","docs"],"parent":"bn-1e7","size":"s","title":"W14.12 Method walkthroughs for the four scenarios, executed in CI"}	blake3:4cqr3BHIKH0xS4pFfNztCdIax7fsOzLqWJL1MQuz5Jg
1795322425726323	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-p7ge	{"description":"# Context & Background\nMilestone C is \"the first release approved for consequential investigations\". §20.7 lists twelve definition-of-done items; §20.1–§20.6 list the gates behind them.\n\n# Goals\nA committed audit that maps every checkbox in §20 to evidence, and a clear verdict.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Each milestone ends in a runnable proof.\" For the release, the proof is the full gate list. An item without evidence is not done, and saying so is better than a green summary.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Start from the Milestone B proof note (MB.01) and extend it to §20.4 (Obsidian and export), §20.5 (trial report, W14.08; deterministic measures, W14.07), §20.6 (human gate, W14.09) and §20.7.\n- For each item: the test, command or report that demonstrates it, on the release-candidate binary; or an explicit open defect with its disposition.\n- Specific §20.7 items to confirm: commands and examples work against the shipped binary; CLI and MCP walkthroughs reach the same state; no fake success stubs; neutral producer integration and restore pass; \"the published core has zero named-producer imports\"; fresh-agent and human-review evidence retained with limitations; drills pass; a real investigation was dogfooded (W14.10).\n- Re-check the \"Never weaken these invariants\" list and the binding decisions; any deviation needs its ADR.\n- A second person or agent reviews the audit against the plan text.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every §20 checkbox has evidence or a recorded open defect.\n- [ ] No item is marked done on the strength of a description alone.\n- [ ] The audit is committed with the binary version and hash it describes.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe release is approved by evidence, the same way the tool asks its users to conclude.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): MB.01.\nParent goal: REL. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §19.3 (Milestone C), §20.1–§20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["release","milestone-c","test"],"parent":"bn-3ob","size":"m","title":"REL.01 Definition-of-done audit: every §20 gate with recorded evidence"}	blake3:hbn6IrtJwbl4putBT_-Af2uGDEjvKINWZWHgoDdZtJE
1795322426563583	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-29bx	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.7: \"Quickstart, embedded guide, examples and optional skill agree with the binary.\" And: \"Source/schema/toolchain versions and supported OS/filesystem are documented.\"\n\n# Goals\nOne pass that makes every user-facing document true for the release candidate, enforced by CI.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nAgents follow documentation literally. A command renamed after the docs were written becomes a failed first minute for every new user.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Quickstart under `docs/`: install, `init`, `start`, `brief`, `next`, vault render, export — executed in CI.\n- Confirm generated material is current: embedded guide and AGENTS snippet (W05b.12), walkthroughs (W14.12), producer onboarding (W07.16), published `schemas/` (AG.10).\n- CI check: every command, operation and flag named in any document exists in the binary; every schema example validates.\n- Use the real executable name `investigation` in first-use text; `inv` only as the optional alias.\n- Document versions: source revision, schema versions, toolchain, and the supported OS/filesystem matrix (W00.09) with tested configurations.\n- README updated to describe what exists, not what is planned.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The docs-versus-binary check passes in CI on the release candidate.\n- [ ] The quickstart runs verbatim from an empty directory.\n- [ ] Supported platforms and filesystems are stated with how each was tested.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe self-teaching promise holds on day one.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: REL. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §8.8, §20.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["release","milestone-c","docs"],"parent":"bn-3ob","size":"s","title":"REL.02 Documentation agrees with the binary: quickstart, embedded guide, examples, skill, schemas"}	blake3:C28cXGDaznRZdPc-f-xQSfoR9gdzcFVLjGJDLwdFAv0
1795322427381087	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3kac	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§20.7: \"The implemented version clearly lists deferred commands/features; no fake success stubs.\" and \"Release notes distinguish demonstrated guarantees from planned work.\"\n\n# Goals\nRelease notes, the public deferred list, and installable artifacts.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nA tool about bounded claims should describe itself the same way: what was demonstrated and how, what is planned, what was decided against.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Release notes in three parts: demonstrated guarantees with pointers to the audit (REL.01) and trial report (W14.08), including their limits; known limitations and open defects; planned work.\n- Deferred list from the register kept since W00.11 plus §22: for each, why and the entry condition (for example event-text purge, distributed sync, signature verification of receipts, selector exporters, PDF export, typed causal-role tags).\n- Any command named in the plan but not shipped is listed as deferred; the binary returns a clear \"not implemented in this version\" rather than a stub.\n- Packaging: reproducible release builds of the `investigation` binary for the supported platforms; checksums; licence and third-party notices from the dependency audit (W00.03); install `inv` as an alias only where the name is free.\n- No publishing step runs automatically; publishing is a separate, explicit action by the project owner.\n- Tag the release with the audited commit.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Release notes separate demonstrated, limited and planned items, each with a pointer.\n- [ ] The deferred list covers §22 and the register.\n- [ ] Built artifacts match the audited commit and have published checksums.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA release that claims exactly what its evidence supports.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): REL.01, REL.02.\nParent goal: REL. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §20.7, §22, §18.2, Header (Product) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["release","milestone-c","docs"],"parent":"bn-3ob","size":"s","title":"REL.03 Release notes, deferred-feature list and packaging"}	blake3:HqsXl1uxMn1LJp7AnK_SQY_akT1_cDYdlg4yTzsih2M
1795322531595143	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-39me	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"The companion plugin (§13) was not reviewed in depth by any reviewer. Re-review it before W15.\" And Milestone D: \"Companion earns itself through human testing, not because plugins are fashionable.\"\n\n# Goals\nA written review of §13 against what Milestone C actually shipped and what the human gate (W14.09) found, ending in a go, narrow-go or no-go ADR.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n§13 is the least-examined part of the plan and depends on Obsidian APIs that move. Building it without a fresh look would spend effort on the part with the weakest evidence. \"Only build after CLI and vault pass their offline gates.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Collect evidence of need: wrong turns and help requests from W14.09; dogfooding friction (W14.10); which of the seven §13.1 features address observed problems.\n- Re-read the current Obsidian plugin API, plugin security guidance and CLI-handler registration docs; record versions (extends the W12.02 profile).\n- Review §13.2's bridge design for safety: subprocess with argv array and JSON stdin; no shell; no local HTTP server; no `eval`.\n- Have someone who did not write §13 review it.\n- Decide scope: which features to build first, desktop-only or not, and exit criteria; or record that the native vault suffices and close the remaining W15 bones as not needed.\n- Record as an ADR.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An ADR states the decision, the evidence of need and the features in scope.\n- [ ] API facts are verified against current documentation with versions recorded.\n- [ ] An independent reviewer's comments are attached and answered.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nOptional work is started only on demonstrated need.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W15. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §13, §19.3 (Milestone D), Disposition: Not changed (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian","adr"],"parent":"bn-tjq","size":"s","title":"W15.01 Re-review the companion design and decide whether human testing justifies building it"}	blake3:XkfgPno_2dfqwLpLVvWS2Tb3Z1xhFE0hINIrZ2zcD6E
1795322531763058	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2n76	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe companion is \"a small optional TypeScript companion\" in `plugin/`, \"NOT required by CLI\". \"Plugin lifecycle handles missing binary, incompatible schema, workspace mismatch, stale revision, output limits, cancellation, and process errors.\"\n\n# Goals\nA minimal plugin that loads, finds its configured binary and workspace, and fails clearly when it cannot.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"The companion **never** implements a second planner, recorder, agent, model client, shell, benchmark, source crawler or domain-specific schema. Everything reads/writes through the same typed core API.\" Getting the boundary and failure handling right first keeps later features thin.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- TypeScript project under `plugin/`, built with Bun per the repository instructions; pinned dependencies; no network access at runtime.\n- Settings: absolute path to the `investigation` binary, workspace root, default case; validated on load.\n- Startup checks via `doctor`: binary present, schema versions compatible, workspace matches the vault's projection manifest.\n- Each failure state has a visible, specific message and disables features instead of guessing.\n- The manifest \"must truthfully declare desktop-only capabilities\".\n- No domain types redefined in TypeScript beyond generated bindings from published schemas.\n- The vault remains fully usable with the plugin disabled.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] With a missing binary, wrong schema version or wrong workspace, the plugin reports the exact condition and performs no action.\n- [ ] Disabling the plugin leaves the vault unchanged and usable.\n- [ ] The bundle contains no model client, shell or HTTP server code.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA companion that cannot become a second source of truth.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W15.01.\nParent goal: W15. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §13.1 (closing paragraph), §13.2, §18.1 (plugin/) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-tjq","size":"m","title":"W15.02 Companion plugin scaffold and lifecycle: missing binary, schema mismatch, workspace mismatch"}	blake3:hZM6VOl2U8EPtZRCqJ0e1YFwMHTIJZ0y5rn9ZuKfxnY
1795322531876788	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1zz5	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"On desktop, use a configured absolute path to `inv` and spawn it with an argv array and JSON stdin. No shell interpolation. Bind the bridge to the explicitly selected workspace, case, and expected revisions; never trust the current note to supply an arbitrary executable or workspace root.\"\n\n# Goals\nThe only channel between the plugin and canonical state.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nNotes in the vault contain untrusted text. If any note content could influence which program runs or where, opening a hostile note would be code execution. \"Do not create an unauthenticated local HTTP server.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- One function that runs a fixed executable path with a constructed argv array and JSON on stdin; arguments never pass through a shell.\n- \"Limit bridge operations to documented domain mutations/reads\": an allowlist of commands.\n- Workspace and case come from plugin settings and explicit user selection, never from note frontmatter alone; IDs read from notes are validated by format and then confirmed by a `show` call.\n- Output limits and timeouts; stderr is logged without evidence content.\n- Every mutation carries a generated command ID. \"A cancelled UI wait after submission may still have committed; resolve by command ID before offering retry\" (uses `receipt`).\n- \"A subprocess per command; a persistent stdio worker can be added only if measurements justify it.\"\n- Optional narrow `investigation:*` commands only on supported versions; never generic `eval`; `obsidian://open` as navigation fallback.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A note containing shell metacharacters, paths or executable names cannot change what is spawned.\n- [ ] A cancelled submission is resolved by command ID and never duplicated.\n- [ ] The plugin opens no listening socket.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps the trust boundary of §16 intact inside Obsidian.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W15.02.\nParent goal: W15. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §13.2 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian","security"],"parent":"bn-tjq","size":"m","title":"W15.03 Bridge: spawn the binary with an argv array and JSON stdin; recover by command ID"}	blake3:pp-3mi52lOmyMuMf64JBsnk1XW_AsSSmxYkuGBV-O6o
1795322531995271	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2x3w	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**Case sidebar:** currently selected case, read-set snapshot, effective conclusions/decisions and resumption command.\" \"**Stable-ID navigation:** never infer identity from note titles or untrusted contents.\"\n\n# Goals\nA sidebar view and navigation helpers backed by fresh canonical reads.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe generated vault can be a generation behind the journal. A sidebar that reads through the bridge shows what is true now, and tells the reader when the note in front of them is stale.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Sidebar content from `brief` at compact level: case, snapshot, effective conclusions and decisions with caveats, top recommendation, resumption command to copy.\n- Staleness indicator: compare the open note's `inv_render_sequence` with the current head; offer `vault render` as an explicit action.\n- Navigation: resolve by `inv_id` to the identity-based path; never by title match.\n- All displayed authored text is rendered as inert text (no Markdown rendering of data fields).\n- Read-only: the sidebar performs no mutation.\n- Keyboard navigable; status conveyed in words, not colour alone.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] After a commit made outside Obsidian, the sidebar shows the new effective state and marks the open note stale.\n- [ ] Two notes with the same display title navigate to the correct distinct records.\n- [ ] The sidebar works with colour disabled and by keyboard.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCurrent state at a glance, with honest staleness.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W15.03.\nParent goal: W15. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §13.1 (Case sidebar; Stable-ID navigation) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-tjq","size":"m","title":"W15.04 Case sidebar and stable-ID navigation"}	blake3:o7rdxP5pcmGUspzs1qaYv73kUA10mr3MT_05OFfLSLk
1795322532148190	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-125o	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**Alternatives matrix:** two modes, explanatory hypotheses × discriminating observations, and options × criteria with links to the actual evidence.\" §12.5 keeps the static matrix in the vault and says \"a specialized interactive view belongs in an optional companion\".\n\n# Goals\nAn interactive matrix view over a fresh snapshot.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nLarge matrices are hard to read as static tables. But the caution about ACH applies: \"the evidence-versus-hypothesis matrix is a reasoning aid, not a validated probability engine.\" The view must aid reading, not compute a verdict.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Data from the bridge (`show`, `frontier`), one snapshot per view, with the snapshot shown.\n- Explanatory mode: cells show the assessment relation and per-rival expectation; non-diagnostic evidence is visible, not hidden.\n- Evaluative mode: cells show disposition, value and unit, unknowns and stale markers; hard criteria are marked; Pareto status is shown in words.\n- No totals, scores or rankings; no colour-only encoding.\n- Each cell links to its record and evidence selector by stable ID.\n- Filtering and sorting are view state only, stored locally, never written to notes.\n- Handles the sparse 1,000 × 200 case by grouping, like `next`.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The matrix matches the static table for the same snapshot, cell for cell.\n- [ ] No computed overall score appears.\n- [ ] Every cell reaches its evidence in two steps.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nBetter reading of comparisons without changing what is known.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W15.03.\nParent goal: W15. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §13.1 (Alternatives matrix), §12.5 (comparison matrix), §2.1 (Caution about ACH) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-tjq","size":"m","title":"W15.05 Interactive alternatives matrix: hypotheses × observations and options × criteria"}	blake3:-xg_rQ7rFpVWOg7E5AL53-5hQC7SzK-0-FtQ0Y8OjYk
1795322532257301	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-zeg4	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**Measurement inspector:** aligned comparable runs, control/validity warnings, original vs derivative provenance, bounded preview.\"\n\n# Goals\nA view that places runs side by side and shows why they are, or are not, comparable.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nComparability is the hardest thing to see in a table of numbers. The inspector shows recorded conditions and the advisory flags of W10.03 together, so a reader notices a sequential-order or load-model difference before trusting a delta.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Select runs or observations; align by recorded fields: units, workload identity, window, environment, trial order, load model, sample and attempt counts.\n- Differences between columns are highlighted in words; missing fields show as unknown.\n- Validity assessments and advisory flags appear beside each value.\n- Provenance: original versus derivative, digest, selector; a bounded, inert preview through `evidence read`.\n- The inspector computes nothing statistical: no means of percentiles, no intervals, no significance.\n- Generic field names, so it also serves non-performance measurements.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] For Scenario B, the confounded, sequential and closed-loop runs each show their warning in the aligned view.\n- [ ] No derived statistic is displayed that the journal does not contain.\n- [ ] Previews are bounded and rendered as inert text.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMakes measurement validity visible to a human at the moment of comparison.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W15.03.\nParent goal: W15. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §13.1 (Measurement inspector), §17.2, §20.1 (Performance/measurement) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-tjq","size":"m","title":"W15.06 Measurement inspector: aligned comparable runs with validity warnings and provenance"}	blake3:DASUXZyMc0Ezq0Uz3fahL1vjgS-G3k8S_r_3ML_6ZW8
1795322532365435	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-el82	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**Decision review:** hard constraints, missing comparisons, sensitivity/uncertainty, tradeoff rationale and reviewer objections. **Conclusion review:** exact claim+revision, supporting/contradicting sources, method, source independence, stale basis detection.\"\n\n# Goals\nTwo review dialogs that show a reviewer exactly what they are reviewing and submit a review bound to that basis.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Review/transition dialogs fetch a fresh canonical snapshot and read-set from `inv`; they never assemble authority from potentially mixed or stale projected notes. Submit those exact revisions, show stale-view status, and require refresh/reassessment on conflict.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- On open: fetch the subject, its required read-set closure and basis digest (W03.11, W04.05) through the bridge.\n- Present the material sets: for a decision, hard-constraint dispositions, missing or unknown cells, sensitivity records, open objections; for a conclusion, supporting and contradicting evidence with origins and independence, method, open objections.\n- The reviewer fills the declarations of W10.15, including `context_isolation`.\n- Submit `review.submit` with the exact revisions fetched; on a conflict, show what changed and require the reviewer to look again — never resubmit automatically.\n- The dialog cannot validate or accept; those remain separate explicit operations.\n- A blinded mode uses the blinded packet.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Fresh canonical read-sets drive companion validation, never mixed-generation Markdown.\n- [ ] A change committed while the dialog is open causes a visible conflict on submit.\n- [ ] The submitted review's basis equals the basis displayed.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nHuman review bound to exactly what the human saw.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W15.03.\nParent goal: W15. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §13.1 (Decision review; Conclusion review), §13.2, §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-tjq","size":"m","title":"W15.07 Decision and conclusion review dialogs driven by a fresh canonical read-set"}	blake3:Yz186hcDw7ZBx_PpWyfptI9O5eOryb5A-wec8ZIlNvw
1795322532473878	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1iwm	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"**Structured actions:** add human note as annotation, submit comparison, propose/supersede decision, request review, resolve objection with fresh CAS preconditions.\"\n\n# Goals\nA small set of forms that build the standard envelope and submit through the bridge.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThese are the supported alternative to editing generated notes. \"The supported contract is **do not edit generated notes directly; use Notes or controlled mutations**.\" Each action is an ordinary catalog operation, so the plugin adds convenience and no new semantics.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Forms generated from published operation schemas where practical: `annotation.add` (including snapshotting a human note by hash, W11.14), `comparison.add`, `decision.propose` and `decision.supersede`, `conclusion.request_review`, `objection.resolve`.\n- Each fetches current revisions immediately before submit and includes them as preconditions.\n- Gate failures show the machine-readable blockers and the repair; nothing is retried silently.\n- `decision.accept` and `conclusion.validate` are deliberately not offered until W15.01's ADR says otherwise; write-side guardrails (W10.19) apply regardless.\n- After a commit, offer `vault render`; do not edit notes directly.\n- The plugin uses \"Obsidian's proper APIs for its own edits\" and makes none to generated notes.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each action commits exactly the transaction its CLI equivalent would.\n- [ ] A stale precondition yields a conflict message and no write.\n- [ ] No action modifies a generated note file.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nW15 acceptance: \"Controlled mutations/cancel/revision/stale-editor gates pass.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W15.07.\nParent goal: W15. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §13.1 (Structured actions), §12.7 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian"],"parent":"bn-tjq","size":"m","title":"W15.08 Structured actions from Obsidian with fresh CAS preconditions"}	blake3:CCvM5EtZJ-eLk-WFvuWN2GIE1ccphWr98FdNaM_Lql0
1795322532585934	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-bli2	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Do **not** silently inject or enable plugins.\" §13.3 lists four steps: `obsidian setup` to preview; an explicit `--install-companion`; \"a clear Obsidian UI step for enabling it where required; never quietly disable Restricted Mode\"; and \"an uninstall that removes only owned bundle files and settings it can restore safely.\"\n\n# Goals\nExplicit installation and clean removal of the companion bundle.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Community plugins inherit Obsidian's access rather than a fine-grained permission sandbox. That is a concrete reason to keep installation explicit and the companion small.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Extend `obsidian setup` (W12.09): `--install-companion` shows the manifest and file list, then copies a bundle embedded in or shipped beside the binary into the vault's plugin directory.\n- \"Pin bundle version/checksum and compatibility range; record installed version. Never fetch an unpinned script and execute it as part of rendering.\"\n- \"Detect custom configuration directories. Preserve unrelated plugins, themes, hotkeys, workspaces, and user settings.\"\n- Do not enable the plugin by editing configuration; print the UI step.\n- Uninstall: remove owned files; restore only settings it recorded changing; leave user data.\n- No network access in either direction.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Plugin setup/uninstall preserves unrelated configuration and custom configuration-directory settings.\n- [ ] Install without the explicit flag writes nothing.\n- [ ] After uninstall, the vault's configuration directory matches its pre-install state except for user changes.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe first useful version works without the plugin; adding it is the user's deliberate act.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W15.02, W12.09.\nParent goal: W15. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §12.9 (closing paragraph), §13.3, §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian","cli","security"],"parent":"bn-tjq","size":"s","title":"W15.09 Companion install and uninstall: previewed, pinned, reversible, never silent"}	blake3:kIMOtHYeq5_Z4iU8wBOLWdGKwAw3ooEQsQz6Dp7zk_g
1795322532688077	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-owf4	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Test with a fresh dedicated vault and an existing vault containing unrelated notes/settings. Verify cold open, rendering, aliases, backlinks, Base queries, Canvas links, edited generated note, edited human note, native property change, pending editor buffer, schema mismatch, missing binary, cancelled command, and plugin removal.\"\n\n# Goals\nAn integration suite for the companion, separate from the headless core suite.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nW15's acceptance: \"Controlled mutations/cancel/revision/stale-editor gates pass.\" These failures happen only with a live editor, so they need tests in the application at a recorded version.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Two fixture vaults: dedicated, and existing with unrelated notes, plugins and settings (reuse W12.06's).\n- Drive Obsidian through its official CLI where available (W12.07), \"always pass exact vault and path\"; otherwise a documented manual checklist with recorded results.\n- Cases from the list above; in particular: a pending unsaved editor buffer on a generated note during render (expect drift handling, no silent loss claim); a cancelled command that had committed (expect resolution by command ID); a schema mismatch and a missing binary (expect clear disabled states).\n- Plugin removal leaves the vault valid and unrelated settings intact.\n- Record Obsidian and plugin versions with each run.\n- \"No Obsidian automation is part of the core headless test suite.\"\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every item in the §13.4 list has a recorded result on the supported release.\n- [ ] Cancel, revision-conflict and stale-editor cases pass.\n- [ ] The headless core suite still passes with no Obsidian present.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe optional layer is held to the same evidence standard as the core.\n\n# Dependencies\nBlocked by (wired as `bn dep` links): W15.04, W15.05, W15.06, W15.08, W15.09.\nParent goal: W15. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §13.4, §19.2 (W15), §20.4 (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w15","milestone-d","lane-projection","obsidian","test"],"parent":"bn-tjq","size":"m","title":"W15.10 Companion integration tests in fresh and existing vaults"}	blake3:OH0ppCyDAMLDaB-NF8dDk_DaVppntztpVE98DixVysQ
1795322532864759	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-bqd6	{"description":"# Context & Background\n\"Initial scheduling remains with the external agent host or task system; `inv` has no scheduler dependency. Do not make a lease service a prerequisite for useful investigation. If local action claims are added in the second phase\", five rules apply (§14.1).\n\n# Goals\nOnly on demonstrated need: an explicit claim record that lets cooperating local agents avoid picking the same next action.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nW16 is gated on \"W14, demonstrated need\". Evidence of need would be duplicated work between agents in dogfooding or trials. The danger is over-claiming: \"An execution may succeed while the agent loses its response. Domain idempotency deduplicates records; it cannot undo or deduplicate an external request automatically.\"\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- First record the evidence of need and the decision as an ADR; if there is none, close this bone as not needed.\n- \"Claim is an explicit mutation; `next` remains read-only.\"\n- \"A claim has actor, action, expiry, and relevant revisions\" — the `action_id` from `next` plus the subject revisions it was computed on.\n- \"It prevents duplicate scheduling only among cooperating local clients.\"\n- \"Expiry of a claim associated with an external run means 'reconcile the run,' not 'safe to repeat.'\"\n- \"No distributed exactly-once execution claim is permitted\" — in code, help text or docs.\n- `next` and `frontier` show a claim as information; they never hide the gap.\n- Release is explicit or by expiry; a stale claim on changed revisions is shown as stale.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two cooperating clients asking for work see each other's live claims.\n- [ ] An expired claim with an associated Run surfaces \"reconcile the run\", never \"retry\".\n- [ ] No text anywhere promises exactly-once or duplicate-free external effects.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nW16 acceptance: \"No duplicate external effects claimed.\"\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W16. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.4, §14.1, §19.2 (W16) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w16","milestone-d","lane-investigation"],"parent":"bn-3mg","size":"m","title":"W16.01 Optional local action claims among cooperating clients, with no exactly-once promise"}	blake3:RGGsVODdhB4OaoM5Vz-NG2MHc4cy9aDdiLa0gSj96js
1795322532987821	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-yyp5	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW07 shipped the Rust `investigation-recording` crate, the JSON-CLI protocol and two example producers. W16 adds \"extension SDK conveniences\" once real consumers exist.\n\n# Goals\nSmall helpers that remove boilerplate real integrations had to write, without adding a second implementation.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe conformance suite (W07.11) is the contract. Conveniences are worth adding only where independent consumers repeated the same code — and they must stay thin wrappers so behaviour cannot diverge.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Gather evidence from actual integrations and the example producers: which steps were repeated or done wrong.\n- Candidates: a guard that finalizes a capture as partial on unwinding; a bounded local spool with reconcile for when the recorder is unavailable; a streaming attach helper that hashes while copying; a thin client package for one non-Rust language that only spawns the CLI.\n- Every helper is implemented on the public API or JSON-CLI protocol and passes the conformance suite unchanged.\n- No helper starts, schedules or retries an external action.\n- Semver the additions; keep the minimal API intact.\n- Document each helper with the failure mode it prevents.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each helper is justified by a recorded integration need.\n- [ ] A producer using the helpers passes the same conformance fixtures.\n- [ ] The core crates gain no dependency on any consumer.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEasier adoption by independent consumers, with one authoritative protocol.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W16. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §3.2, §11.3, §11.7, §19.2 (W16) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w16","milestone-d","recording","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-3mg","size":"m","title":"W16.02 Extension SDK conveniences for consumers of the recording API"}	blake3:W9xMbO08IZ0yacClMxSz4IqkMWehAVSDe3fHSEM3mqY
1795322533107475	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1k86	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW09 shipped the pack schema and example packs. §8.6: \"A later explicit, reviewed `method-pack` authoring workflow may incorporate them; that is outside v1.\" §22 defers \"Automatic method-pack self-improvement / cross-case doctrine promotion\".\n\n# Goals\nDocumentation for people writing their own packs.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\n\"Make it possible to implement another method pack without changing Rust record types.\" That is only true in practice if someone outside the project can write one from the docs. The guide also has to say clearly what a pack can never do.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Guide under `docs/`: pack file structure; writing a card (trigger, when to skip, operations to name); declaring namespaced extension properties; policy inputs and the rule that they affect promotion only; view and report template references; versioning, digests and adoption.\n- A worked example built step by step and loaded in CI.\n- Limits, stated up front: no code, no commands, no network, no authority, no change to integrity or trust rules.\n- Lessons: how a human may read admitted lessons (W10.17) and choose to write a new pack revision by hand, with review; adoption by a case remains explicit. Nothing is automatic.\n- A validation command or flag to check a pack file before use.\n- Sensitivity: a pack is shareable text; do not copy restricted case detail into cards.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A pack written by following the guide loads and pins without core changes.\n- [ ] The guide's example is executed in CI.\n- [ ] The guide states that lesson adoption is manual and reviewed.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMethod knowledge can grow outside the core, deliberately.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W16. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §2.4, §8.6 (Lessons remain case-scoped), §22, §19.2 (W16) (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w16","milestone-d","docs","lane-methodology"],"parent":"bn-3mg","size":"s","title":"W16.03 Method pack authoring guide, including how reviewed lessons may inform a pack by hand"}	blake3:3OiyIwAhf6NKeBAvixjmI8Tb8zaJivWH9QjMU5qbGhg
1795322533232870	investigation-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-xqv4	{"description":"# Context & Background\nW16 covers \"packaging/portability\", with acceptance \"platform gates correctly reported\". §18.4: \"`doctor` truthfully reports unsupported platform/filesystem capabilities and any tests unavailable in the current environment.\"\n\n# Goals\nExtend the support matrix (W00.09) beyond the platforms qualified for the first release, each backed by the durability tests.\n\n# Reasoning / Justification\nThe durability guarantees depend on filesystem behaviour: exclusive create, rename, directory fsync, advisory locks. A platform is supported only where the crash suite has actually run; elsewhere the tool should say \"untested\", not assume.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- For each candidate platform and filesystem: run the storage-fake crash matrix (W02.15), the multi-process concurrency tests (W02.17), the power-loss style job where the tooling exists (W02.16), and the clean-machine restore (W11.17).\n- Record per configuration: which publish primitive is used, lock behaviour, whether directory fsync is effective, and which tests could not run.\n- Network filesystems stay refused; document why.\n- Update `doctor` to classify the running environment as qualified, untested or refused, citing the matrix entry.\n- Packaging for newly qualified platforms follows REL.03's reproducible build and checksum process.\n- Publish the matrix with dates and versions; unqualified combinations are labeled as such, not hidden.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every \"supported\" entry in the matrix links to passing durability test runs.\n- [ ] `doctor` on an untested filesystem says so and names the tests not run.\n- [ ] No platform is described as supported on the basis of compilation alone.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDurability claims stay tied to where they were demonstrated.\n\n# Dependencies\nNo bone-level blockers of its own.\nParent goal: W16. Blockers on any ancestor goal also gate this bone.\nCodes are title prefixes; find any of them with `bn search <CODE>`.\n\n---\nPlan refs: notes/plan.md §10.2, §18.4, §19.2 (W16), §20.7, W00 support matrix (revision 4, 2026-11-21). Review rationale: \"Disposition of reviews\" at the end of the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["w16","milestone-d","test","lane-storage"],"parent":"bn-3mg","size":"m","title":"W16.04 Platform and filesystem portability qualification with truthful doctor reporting"}	blake3:Sb69nKO3xXo_-uNuCBFpN0yBaDaRZGBeF_7kMDeUGEY
